(function(){var C=[["A flame over his head","A fiery portent above the sleeping boy&#x27;s head was, according to tradition, what revealed his destiny as king.","Servius Tullius","en/d/servio-tulio.html"],["A priest brother","Eustathius of Thessalonica makes him the brother of Chryses, a priest in Chryse.","Briseus","en/d/briseo.html"],["A children&#x27;s prank","Adults often claimed he had just passed a nearby street, so children would run off to look for him.","Man of the noses","en/d/hombre-de-las-narices.html"],["Rain as tears","According to tradition, each rainfall is Rangi crying for Papa, from whom his children have parted him.","Rangi and Papa","en/d/rangi-y-papa.html"],["More than 700 statues","It is estimated that there were more than 700 statues of Sekhmet in the temple of Amenhotep III, which explains the vast number of images of the goddess that survive.","Sekhmet","en/d/sejmet.html"],["Twelve sisters","The Horae formed a group of twelve sisters, each linked to a part of the day, from sunrise to the starry night.","Sponde","en/d/sponde.html"],["A misspelt name","In some copies of Aristotle the name appears as &quot;mantichoras&quot;, a mistaken spelling that ended up becoming standard in Europe.","Manticore","en/d/manticora.html"],["A name with modern echoes","The word neraka was adopted in Malay to describe hell, even though Malaysia&#x27;s population is predominantly Muslim.","Naraka","en/d/naraka.html"],["A namesake city","The ancient city of Hypate, today Ypati in Greece, is unrelated to this Muse. Its name means &#x27;the height of Oeta&#x27;.","Hypate","en/d/hipate.html"],["A name with meaning","Her name is linked to the Greek <em>meros</em>, “part”, and <em>ops</em>, “face” or “eye”.","Merope","en/d/merope-q1555457.html"],["Meaning of her name","Bhūmi is the Sanskrit word for &#x27;earth&#x27;. In Old Awadhi the equivalent form is Puhumi.","Bhumi","en/d/bhumi.html"],["A hydrangea named after them","In Chinese, the hydrangea is called the &#x27;flower of the Eight Immortals&#x27; (八仙花, <em>Bāxiān huā</em>).","The Eight Immortals","en/d/los-ocho-inmortales.html"],["Theia of many names","Pindar calls her Theia of many names, and texts give her epithets such as Euryphaessa, Ichnaie or Basileia, which vary by author.","Theia","en/d/tea.html"],["A wife without a name","The Odyssey says Dolius&#x27;s wife was a slave from Sicily, but it never gives her name.","Dolius","en/d/dolio.html"],["A head that keeps shouting","After his death, his severed head returns to the court on his shield and repeats his warning about the raids. Only then does the king decide to act.","Súaltam","en/d/sualtam.html"],["A name for a river","In Tongva mythology, the sound heard in the upper Arroyo Seco is explained as the river laughing at Coyote after he loses the race.","Coyote","en/d/coyote.html"],["The phoenix as symbol","The phoenix appears in Aeternitas&#x27;s iconography because, according to tradition, it was reborn from its flames every 500 years, which links it to cyclical time.","Aeternitas","en/d/aeternitas.html"],["Two natures","The Prose Edda lists him first as a jötunn and then as a sea king, so the same name is used for two different kinds of being.","Hraudung","en/d/hraudung.html"],["Nine forms","Her nine appellations, known as Navadurga, are honoured during Navaratri.","Durga","en/d/durga.html"],["A changing height","According to tradition, Borevit could grow or shrink until he matched the size of a mushroom or a tree.","Borevit","en/d/borevit.html"],["A green-hued god","Although Mesopotamians usually imagined their gods in human form, Tishpak is occasionally described as green, possibly because his skin was thought to be scaly.","Tishpak","en/d/tispak.html"],["Star name","Some contemporary K&#x27;iche&#x27; speakers use his name for the seven stars of the Big Dipper.","Vucub Caquix","en/d/vucub-caquix.html"],["An animal with his name","Isidore of Seville describes the leontophonus as a small creature which, reduced to ash, was used to destroy lions.","Leontophonus","en/d/leontofono.html"],["One yellow eye","His epithet Ekaksipingala means &quot;the one with a single yellow eye&quot;. According to the story, he lost an eye for looking with jealousy at Shiva and Parvati.","Kubera","en/d/kubera.html"],["A vase with names","On the Meidias Hydria, the figures in the garden are labelled with their names, among them Asterope, Chrysothemis, Hygieia and Lipara.","Asterope","en/d/asterope.html"],["A name with numbers","In Sumerian, the name written as í-gì-gì can be read as a sum of numerals adding to 7, the number of the Great Gods, or as a product of 600, which in some traditions was the total number of gods.","Igigi","en/d/igigi.html"],["A tree as a hiding place","In one version, his mother hid him in a hollow tree at birth, a detail that ancient writers linked to his name.","Dryops","en/d/driope.html"],["Possible origin of the name","One proposed etymology for &quot;centaur&quot; links it to the meaning &quot;water-whipper&quot; or &quot;water spirit&quot;. Nessus, a ferryman on a river, fits that reading.","Nessus","en/d/neso.html"],["A related myth","Crocus&#x27;s accidental death during a discus game resembles that of Hyacinthus, whom Apollo loved. For this reason the two stories are considered likely to be related.","Crocus","en/d/croco.html"],["A horse of divine origin","Arion was born from the union of Poseidon and Demeter, who had taken the form of a mare to hide from him.","Oncius","en/d/oncio.html"],["A Mycenaean tablet","A Linear B tablet found at Knossos contains the form pa-ja-wo-ne, which some scholars consider an ancestor of Paean. It is believed that in Mycenaean Greek it named Apollo.","Paean","en/d/peon.html"],["A well-preserved temple","The Hephaisteion in Athens survived almost intact because it was converted into a Christian church, which explains its good condition today.","Hephaestus","en/d/hefesto.html"],["A name from a disguise","Pyrrhus means &quot;red&quot; or &quot;blond&quot;, and he received the name because Achilles, disguised as a woman, called himself Pyrrha.","Neoptolemus","en/d/neoptolemo.html"],["Butterfly named after him","The Old World swallowtail butterfly, Papilio machaon, carries his name in its scientific name.","Machaon","en/d/macaon.html"],["An altar with his name","The collegiate church of Goslar kept the so-called Krodo Altar, probably dating from the 11th century, which may have been moved from Harzburg Castle.","Krodo","en/d/krodo.html"],["Plants from a body","In the grandmother version, Tekawerahkwa&#x27;s body gives rise to corn, beans, squash, sunflowers, strawberries and tobacco, each from a different part of her body.","Atahensic","en/d/atahensic.html"],["Gold jewellery","According to the accounts, the animal wore gold earrings in its ears and bracelets on its forelegs.","Petsuchos","en/d/petesucos.html"],["Mother of Chariclo","According to the sources, Stilbe was the mother of the nymph Chariclo, who married the centaur Chiron.","Stilbe","en/d/estilbe.html"],["Unrecorded words","Izanagi praises what Kikurihime says to him, but the ancient text never records her words.","Kikurihime","en/d/kikurihime.html"],["An error in the dictionary","Marc Drogin noted that for half a century editions of the Oxford English Dictionary carried an incorrect page reference in a footnote on the earliest mention of Titivillus.","Titivillus","en/d/titivillus.html"],["Striking iconography","She was shown with a scorpion&#x27;s head while nursing a baby, an unusual scene that sets her apart from other deities.","Hedetet","en/d/hedetet.html"],["Stingless bees","The Melipona bees he protects are stingless species, and the Maya used these bees to obtain their honey.","Ah Muzen Cab","en/d/ah-muzen-cab.html"],["A national calendar","Until 1961, South Korea counted its years from 2333 BC, the traditional date of Dangun&#x27;s reign.","Dangun","en/d/dangun.html"],["The Salme ships","Between 2008 and 2010 two ship burials were found at Salme, Estonia, containing remains of at least 42 people, mostly men aged 30 to 40 who had been killed in battle. The ships date to about 650–700, and isotope analysis suggests the men came from central Sweden.","Ingvar","en/d/ingvar.html"],["A family of rivers and islands","Her siblings bear the names of islands, cities and springs of Greece, such as Salamis, Aegina or Peirene, showing how myths tied the landscape to divine genealogies.","Cleone","en/d/cleone.html"],["Origin of the name","Her name is linked to the Latin verb <em>cubare</em>, &quot;to lie down&quot;, which refers to the child resting in the cradle.","Cuba","en/d/cuba.html"],["A mountain named for her","Samsinbong, known as the Peak of Three Spirits, takes its name from her.","Samsin Halmoni","en/d/samsin-halmoni.html"],["The first armed soldiers","According to the sources, the Curetes are considered the first armed soldiers, as well as inventors of metalworking.","Damnameneus","en/d/damnameneo.html"],["An enormous appetite","One source describes him eating three asses&#x27; panniers of food and drinking a ten-amphora cask of wine at a time.","Lityerses","en/d/litierses.html"],["A disputed name","Stephanus of Byzantium records that some attributed the name Mytilene to a figure called Mytile, not to Myton.","Myton","en/d/miton.html"],["Orion hunting","In the Odyssey, Orion still hunts in the meadow the beasts he killed in life, carrying his unbreakable bronze club.","Asphodel Meadows","en/d/prado-de-asfodelos.html"],["A sanctuary with a bath","The Holy Garden sanctuary also had a bath, built thanks to a donation of 2,500 denarii recorded in an inscription from 182 AD.","Malakbel","en/d/malakbel.html"],["A name for the Mediterranean","The Egyptians borrowed the word <em>yammu</em> and used it for the Mediterranean Sea, although it could also function as a divine name.","Yam","en/d/yam.html"],["Different spellings","Copies of Helmold&#x27;s chronicle spell her name in different ways. Some manuscripts keep the letter w, which helps scholars reconstruct the original form.","Živa","en/d/ziva.html"],["Plesiosaur&#x27;s Lagoon","A small lake where the creature&#x27;s presence was claimed is today known as Laguna del Plesiosaurio, or Plesiosaur&#x27;s Lagoon.","Nahuelito","en/d/nahuelito.html"],["A name with meaning","His name in Proto-Norse, <em>Hrōþiwulfaz</em>, would mean &quot;famous wolf&quot;.","Hrólfr Kraki","en/d/hrolfr-kraki.html"],["A queen with a reputation for wisdom","The Roman emperor Tiberius, according to Suetonius, liked to pester scholars with obscure questions about mythology, and one of his favourites was who Hecuba&#x27;s mother had been.","Hecuba","en/d/hecuba.html"],["A name with several meanings","The word <em>dziewanna</em> also names a plant, which has led scholars to debate whether the goddess gave her name to the plant or the reverse.","Devana","en/d/devana.html"],["A spear with a crest","According to the myth, the tree split by the vel gave rise to the rooster and the peacock, two animals that accompany Murugan in his iconography.","Vel","en/d/vel.html"],["Royal crown","Pharaoh Sneferu is shown wearing the crown of Andjety, which shows how closely the god was tied to kingship.","Andjety","en/d/andyety.html"],["A goddess who may be invented","The scholar Andy Orchard suggests that Njörun may be fictitious, given how little evidence there is for her.","Njörun","en/d/njorun.html"],["A sound that scared off","According to the sources, the sound of his conch was so discordant that, when blown loudly, it put the giants to flight, who imagined it as the roar of a dark wild beast.","Triton","en/d/triton.html"],["A golden palace","According to tradition, he built a palace entirely of gold for Shiva and Parvati. Shiva later gave it to Ravana as dakshina, and so Lanka became Ravana&#x27;s residence.","Vishvakarma","en/d/vishvakarma.html"],["The seeds","According to belief, leaving seeds around the house keeps the trasno occupied, as it spends its time trying to count them.","Trasno","en/d/trasno.html"],["Born from the nose","According to the Kojiki, Susanoo came into being when Izanagi washed his nose after returning from the land of the dead.","Susanoo","en/d/susanoo.html"],["Two possible routes","In the Odyssey, Circe gives Odysseus two routes to Ithaca: one leads past the Planctae and the other to Scylla and Charybdis, and Odysseus chooses the latter.","Planctae","en/d/planctas.html"],["A Polish aircraft","The Polish utility aeroplane PZL M-15 Belphegor, built in the 1970s, was named after the demon because of its strange looks and the noise of its jet engine.","Belphegor","en/d/belfegor.html"],["Necklaces for Pandora","According to Hesiod, Peitho and the Graces adorned Pandora with golden necklaces. Jewelry was viewed with suspicion in Greek literature, as a way for women to seduce.","Peitho","en/d/peito.html"],["One more mother","In Nepal an eighth Matrika is sometimes added to represent the eight cardinal directions. In Bhaktapur, in the Kathmandu Valley, a ninth is added to represent the centre.","Kaumari","en/d/kaumari.html"],["Irregular spelling","His name is most often written in an irregular form, <em>nīvaraṇa</em>.","Sarvanivāraṇaviṣkambhin","en/d/sarvanivaranaviskambhin.html"],["An alternative origin for the name","Herodotus records a Lydian tradition that the continent was named after Asies, son of Cotys, rather than after the Oceanid.","Asia","en/d/asia.html"],["A goddess in the form of a kite","On some coffins she appears as a kite, a bird of prey that linked her to Isis and Nephthys.","Imentet","en/d/imentet.html"],["A temple with a treasury","The temple of Saturn in the Roman Forum housed the state treasury and archives of the Roman Republic and early Empire.","Saturn","en/d/saturno.html"],["Two temples","Rome had two shrines dedicated to the goddess, distinguished by the lineage of those who attended them: one patrician and one plebeian.","Pudicitia","en/d/pudicitia.html"],["Palamedes&#x27; dice","According to Pausanias, Palamedes invented the first pair of dice and offered them to Tyche.","Tyche","en/d/tique.html"],["A name with character","In local Orkney dialect, Teran means &quot;furious anger&quot;. It may derive from the Norse word for &quot;angry&quot;.","Sea Mither","en/d/sea-mither.html"],["A god-less chariot","In the Achaemenid army, Ahura Mazda&#x27;s presence was represented by an empty chariot drawn by white horses, symbolizing his company in battle.","Ahura Mazda","en/d/ahura-mazda.html"],["A city of thousands of kilometres","The measurements in Revelation, multiplied by the length of the ancient stadion, give a city about 2,222 km along each side.","New Jerusalem","en/d/nueva-jerusalen.html"],["A name with meaning","Plato links the name Thaumas with the Greek word thauma, meaning &quot;wonder&quot;, in an etymological exercise.","Thaumas","en/d/taumante.html"],["Another name","In later texts, the unnamed daughter of Opus appears under the names Cambyse or Cabia, which commentators identify with Protogeneia.","Protogeneia","en/d/protogenia.html"],["Siblings of death","Moros shares his origin with Thanatos and the Keres, who represent different aspects of death.","Moros","en/d/moro.html"],["A voice in a jar","According to Ovid, by the end of her life the Sibyl had shrunk so much that only her voice remained, kept in a jar.","Cumaean Sibyl","en/d/sibila-de-cumas.html"],["A great-grandson in Corinth","Aletes, Antiochus&#x27;s great-grandson, is the figure who conquered Corinth during the invasion of the Heraclidae.","Antiochus","en/d/antioco.html"],["A name with two meanings","Scholars suggest Vár may mean &quot;pledge&quot; or &quot;beloved,&quot; and the word <em>várar</em> referred to the oaths she protected.","Vár","en/d/var.html"],["A statue in Norway","In 1971 a bronze statue of the goddess was installed in a park in Mo i Rana, beneath a shopping centre.","Rana Niejta","en/d/rana-niejta.html"],["Lame smith gods","Like the Greek Hephaestus, Svarog and other Indo-European smith gods are depicted as lame. One hypothesis links this lameness to chronic arsenic poisoning suffered by Bronze Age smiths.","Dazhbog","en/d/dazhbog-q5117835.html"],["A lantern with a hairy man","In 1982 the paleoanthropologist Zhou Guoxing described a lantern about two thousand years old with an ornament that seems to depict a hairy man.","Yeren","en/d/yeren.html"],["A name echoed in the Goths","The terms Geats, Goths and Gutes come from related roots, according to the linguistic sources cited.","Gauti","en/d/gauti.html"],["A camel-rider in stone","In a relief from the Temple of Adonis at Dura-Europos, Arsu appears on a camel. The inscription calls him &#x27;Arsu the camel-rider&#x27; and was made by a sculptor named Oga for the life of his son.","Arsu","en/d/arsu.html"],["An island rising from the sea","According to Pindar, Rhodes rose from the sea after Helios claimed an island still submerged while the gods divided the earth.","Rhodos","en/d/rodo.html"],["An inscription beyond Aegina","A 5th-century BCE potsherd found at Bassae (Arcadia) may carry a dedication to Aphaia. If confirmed, it would be the first written mention of the goddess outside her island.","Aphaia","en/d/afaya.html"],["Variant names","His name appears in Greek as Árgynnos or Árgennos, and the Roman poet Propertius uses yet another form. The variants show how spelling differed among ancient authors.","Argynnus","en/d/argino.html"],["Twelve sons","In one tradition she had twelve sons, the same number as the country Lares, a link that reinforces her connection to their cult.","Acca Larentia","en/d/aca-larentia.html"],["Seven brothers","In the Iliad, Andromache says Achilles killed her seven brothers, which does not fit with Podes being killed by Menelaus.","Podes","en/d/podes.html"],["Minecraft translation","The Drowned monster in the video game Minecraft has the official Polish translation utopiec, the same name used for these spirits.","Topielec","en/d/topielec.html"],["A name with history","His name means the gods Vasus, since vasu means good and deva means god. The Vasus are a group of eight Vedic deities.","Vasudeva","en/d/vasudeva.html"],["A comedy as an early source","One of the earliest mentions of Melanion appears in the comedy Lysistrata, where the male chorus presents him as a misogynist who fled from women.","Melanion","en/d/melanion.html"],["A revealing name","The name combines the prefix <em>ut-</em>, which marks exclusion or exteriority, with <em>garðr</em>, meaning field, garden or district.","Útgard","en/d/utgard.html"],["Tomb with epitaph","Diodorus Siculus quotes an inscription that the Cretans said stood on Meriones&#x27; tomb at Cnossus, beside that of Idomeneus.","Molus","en/d/molus.html"],["A fish and a prophet","In some depictions of the Hindu deity Matsya, his upper body emerges from the mouth of a fish, a image comparable to the biscione. In early Christian catacomb art, the prophet Jonah appears swallowed by a serpent-like fish.","Biscione","en/d/biscione.html"],["A raven that turned black","According to the story, Lycius was turned into a white raven, which later became black after the incident involving Coronis.","Clinis","en/d/clinide.html"],["A different version","According to Hippostratus, Danaus did not have children with several wives but with a single woman, Europe, daughter of the river-god Nilus.","Pieria","en/d/pieria.html"],["Sling bullets","Roman sling bullets have been found in Kennemerland in the Netherlands, the same type as those found near the Roman fortified harbour at Velsen, which suggests where the battle may have taken place.","Baduhenna","en/d/baduhenna.html"],["Modern vote","In 2013 a regional newspaper asked its readers for their favourite woman of the Périgord, and Vesunna won 85 % of the votes.","Vesunna","en/d/vesunna.html"],["Origin of Septentrio","The name Septentrio comes from septem triones, meaning seven oxen, a reference to the seven stars of the Big Dipper.","Aquilo","en/d/aquilon.html"],["Kutkh and the mice","In a Chukchi tale, mice born from his footsteps crawl into the nose of the sleeping Kutkh. His sneezes form mountains and valleys, and attempts to stamp them out create the ocean.","Kutkh","en/d/kutkh.html"],["Guardian of the caribou","In Caribou Inuit communities, the goddess was associated with control over the herds, giving hunting a dimension of respect for the animals.","Pinga","en/d/pinga.html"],["Death at Horseleap","His wound is believed to have taken place in Horseleap, a town in County Offaly.","Conchobar mac Nessa","en/d/conchobar-mac-nessa.html"],["A white loa","Maman Brigitte is one of the few loa depicted as white.","Maman Brigitte","en/d/maman-brigitte.html"],["A magical name","The name <em>Gǫndul</em> is usually translated as &quot;wand-wielder&quot;. Rudolf Simek connects it with the Old Norse word gandr, meaning magic.","Göndul","en/d/gondul.html"],["A modern temple","In Karaikudi, in Sivaganga district, there is a temple dedicated to Tamil Thai, opened in 1993.","Tamil Thai","en/d/tamil-thai.html"],["Zeus Moiragetes","Pausanias records the supposed epithet Zeus Moiragetes, meaning leader of the Moirai, from an inscription at Olympia.","Moirai","en/d/moiras.html"],["Rain-made footprints","Photographer John A. Rennie published in a book a series of giant footprints he had found in the Spey Valley, but later discovered that rain eroding the snow had made them.","Am Fear Liath Mòr","en/d/am-fear-liath-mor.html"],["Six stars","He is physically represented by a constellation of six stars near the Big Dipper, and each star has its own name.","Wenchang Wang","en/d/wenchang-wang.html"],["An elephant face","Because of her long, prominent nose, she is sometimes called elephant-faced, and has been seen as a possible precursor of Ganesha.","Jyeshtha","en/d/jyeshtha.html"],["A month named for her","The third month of the Iranian calendar carries her name and begins around May 22.","Haurvatat","en/d/haurvatat.html"],["Seven faces and eight swords","His statue had seven heads and seven swords at the belt, plus an eighth sword in its hand.","Rugiaevit","en/d/rugiaevit.html"],["A name with meaning","Hiranyakashipu literally means one who desires wealth and material comforts, from hiranya, gold, and kashipu, soft bed. In the Puranas the name is also linked to a golden throne.","Hiranyakashipu","en/d/hiranyakashipu.html"],["Sword companion","The name is often explained by seax, the long knife of the Saxons, and by an element meaning companion, related to German Genosse.","Saxnot","en/d/saxnot.html"],["A stork","According to myth, his daughter Antigone boasted that her hair was more beautiful than Hera&#x27;s, and the goddess punished her by turning her into a stork.","Laomedon","en/d/laomedonte.html"],["Twelve pieces","According to the Bhagavata Purana, the bodies of the two asuras broke into twelve pieces. These are considered to represent the twelve tectonic plates of the Earth.","Madhu and Kaitabha","en/d/madhu-y-kaitabha.html"],["A demonic name in the Bible","In a 1776 Finnish translation of Leviticus, a form of Ajatar&#x27;s name is used to refer to demons in general, not to one specific entity.","Ajatar","en/d/ajatar.html"],["Name and cobweb","Some scholars connect his name to a Germanic root linked to loops and knots, which would tie him to the net and to his role at Ragnarök.","Loki","en/d/loki.html"],["Thursday","In English, Thursday comes from the Old English Thunresdæg, &#x27;day of Thunor&#x27;, the same god under another name.","Thor","en/d/thor-q140306282.html"],["Two places, one name","According to a Messenian tradition, the town of Leuctra took its name from Leucippus, although it is located in Boeotia rather than Messenia.","Leucippus","en/d/leucipo.html"],["A jewel for the tide","According to a legend of the Sobataka shrine, its deity drained Lake Kasumigaura with a tide-ebbing jewel to steal horses.","Futsunushi","en/d/futsunushi.html"],["Parallel with Artemis","Inara corresponds to the Greek <em>potnia theron</em>, mistress of wild animals, a figure that Greek tradition associates with Artemis.","Inara","en/d/inara.html"],["Ancharia at Faesulae","At Faesulae, today Fiesole, she was worshipped under the name Ancharia, and an altar belonging to her was discovered there in the late nineteenth century.","Angerona","en/d/angerona.html"],["A corrected name","The hero first appeared under another name in early translations. Heinrich Zimmern corrected it in 1899 to Atrahasis, meaning &#x27;exceedingly wise&#x27;.","Atrahasis","en/d/atrahasis-q530320.html"],["Chinese translation","The Chinese version was translated by Fa-hu (Dharmapāla) in the capital of the Northern Song and presented to Emperor Renzong around 1054. In China, however, the tantra never became popular.","Hevajra","en/d/hevajra.html"],["The three gods of art","Luchtaine was part of a trio of brothers, the <em>Trí Dée Dána</em>, together with Creidhne and Goibniu. Between them they shared the work of forging their people&#x27;s weapons.","Luchtaine","en/d/luchtaine.html"],["A mysterious object","The gift she receives in the poem is the <em>tudida</em>, a term presumed to mean a garment, although its exact meaning is not fully clear.","Dimpemekug","en/d/dimpemekug.html"],["An extra meal","Some versions say the cat does not eat people but steals the extra portion of Christmas food set aside for the residents of the farm.","Yule Cat","en/d/gato-de-navidad.html"],["A cycle of 18 years","Rahu and Ketu are always 180 degrees apart in their orbit, and their cycle lasts about 18 years, the same period used to predict eclipses.","Rahu","en/d/rahu.html"],["Five-headed cobra","Parshvanatha, the 23rd Tirthankara, appears with a seven-or-more-headed cobra, so the two are told apart by the number of heads.","Suparshvanatha","en/d/suparshvanatha.html"],["Oath-watching sisters","Her two sisters, Alacomenia and Thelxinoea, were collectively called the Praxidicae, a group of goddesses who watched over oaths.","Aulis","en/d/aulide.html"],["A serpent&#x27;s mouth","In one variant, Hanuman enters Surasā&#x27;s mouth and leaves through her ear.","Surasā","en/d/surasa.html"],["A hare before a rabbit","In the earliest sources the lunar figure is a hare, because rabbits had not yet arrived in China during the Western Han dynasty.","Moon rabbit","en/d/conejo-lunar.html"],["A wooden plank","The Talmud connects the name with <em>neser</em>, a plank that, according to the sages, had been part of Noah&#x27;s Ark and that Sennacherib worshipped as an idol.","Nisroch","en/d/nisroch.html"],["A town named after him","The Euboean town of Carystus took its name from this hero, according to the ancient accounts.","Carystus","en/d/caristo.html"],["Early opera","La finta pazza of 1641 was staged at the Teatro Novissimo in Venice, the first building ever built specifically for opera.","Achilles on Skyros","en/d/aquiles-en-esciros.html"],["A kinship name","His name, Guojiu, means &quot;imperial brother-in-law&quot;, since the old Chinese word jiu could also mean &quot;brother-in-law&quot;.","Cao Guojiu","en/d/cao-guojiu.html"],["A changed title","In the 1872 revision of Mussorgsky&#x27;s work, the scene originally presided over by Satan was reassigned to the black god and retitled The Glorification of Chernobog.","Chernobog","en/d/chernobog.html"],["Pebbles that change color","According to Ovid, Hercules changed the color of the pebbles with which his townspeople had voted to convict Myscellus.","Myscellus","en/d/miscelo.html"],["Seven cobras","In one myth, after swallowing seven cobras, Nehebkau became immune to any magic, fire or water.","Nehebkau","en/d/nehebkau.html"],["Risus in Latin","His Latin name, Risus, was the translation of Gelos. The sources use it for the same personification of laughter.","Gelos","en/d/gelos.html"],["Jars with heads","The lids of the canopic jars took the form of the heads of the sons of Horus. Duamutef was identified with the jackal head.","Duamutef","en/d/duamutef.html"],["A giant heron","Remains of a human-sized heron, thought to have died out around 1500 BC, were found in the United Arab Emirates and gave the species the name Ardea bennuides.","Bennu","en/d/bennu.html"],["A rock beside a river","Stephanus of Byzantium explains that Hipponous lived on the Olenian rock of a river called Peiros, and that Olenus was a city in Aetolia.","Hipponous","en/d/hiponoo.html"],["A statue of Fear","According to one of the traditions, the Corinthians dedicated to Medea&#x27;s children a statue representing Fear.","Mermerus","en/d/mermero.html"],["A name that describes his body","Kabandha means &quot;headless torso&quot;, because after losing his head Vishvavasu kept only two arms and a mouth on his belly.","Kabandha","en/d/kabandha.html"],["A harbour named after him","In the city of Corcyra, one of the two harbours bears the name of Alcinous.","Alcinous","en/d/alcinoo.html"],["Name explained","Cicero linked her name to the phrase <em>vincendi atque potiundi</em>, meaning &#x27;conquering and gaining mastery&#x27;.","Vica Pota","en/d/vica-pota.html"],["The long-haired star","In a late version, Electra let down her hair in mourning, left her sisters and became a comet.","Electra","en/d/electra-q858663.html"],["Sumedha&#x27;s hair","In the story, the future Gautama offers his hair as a carpet so that Dipankara will not soil his feet. It is one of the most depicted episodes in Buddhist art.","Dipankara","en/d/dipankara.html"],["An exceptional sculpture","American archaeologists found an exceptional sculpture of Unut, probably the only one of its kind discovered so far.","Unut","en/d/unut.html"],["A tomb near Chalcis","His tomb was shown near the city of Chalcis, on the island of Euboea.","Chalcodon","en/d/calcodonte.html"],["Very early images","They appear in the art of Mesopotamia and ancient Iran before they appear in literature, including an example from Jiroft and the Bull Lyre from Ur.","Scorpion-men","en/d/hombres-escorpion.html"],["A river with three names","The river Maratho was renamed Himerus after a young man threw himself into its waters, and later took the name Eurotas.","Lacedaemon","en/d/lacedemon.html"],["A column of fire","According to the Arunachala Purana, Shiva rose as a column of fire so intense that living beings could not bear it. He then transformed into the mountain Arunachala.","Lingodbhava","en/d/lingodbhava.html"],["A name with a double meaning","The Greek name combines «híppos», horse, with an element that can mean «destroyed», suggesting an ironic sense: «destroyed by horses».","Hippolytus","en/d/hipolito.html"],["The road of springs","According to some stories, to meet a lavandeira you must walk past 7 or 9 springs that do not dry up in summer. The encounter comes at the seventh or ninth.","Lavandeiras","en/d/lavandeiras.html"],["A name won on the track","According to the legend, the name Alabandus came from a victory in a horse race, combining the Carian words for &quot;horse&quot; and &quot;victory&quot;.","Alabandus","en/d/alabando.html"],["An epithet, not a creature","Pausanias regarded the taraxippos as an epithet rather than a single entity, which explains the variety of explanations.","Taraxippos","en/d/taraxipo.html"],["A name with meaning","Her name can be translated as &quot;the most fair&quot;. It combines pen, meaning &quot;head&quot; or &quot;chief&quot;, with arddun, used of a beautiful girl.","Penarddun","en/d/penarddun.html"],["Tears of amber","According to Ovid, the tears Phaethusa shed for her brother turned into amber.","Phaethusa","en/d/faetusa.html"],["One of the Four Perils","Qiongqi belongs, together with Hundun, Taowu and Taotie, to a group of four evil beings banished in the classical Chinese stories.","Qiongqi","en/d/qiongqi.html"],["A scene of twelve goddesses","In the Amduat, Iabet is part of a group of twelve goddesses who praise Ra as he crosses Wernes, among them Isis.","Iabet","en/d/iabet.html"],["A people with her name","The Bebryces of Bithynia, in Asia Minor, were linked to Bebryce, though other writers connected them with a hero called Bebryx.","Bebryce","en/d/bebrice.html"],["Three sisters and the golden dipper","A popular Ming-period novel presents the latrine deity as three sisters responsible for the Primeval Golden Dipper, a celestial toilet bowl from which, according to the story, all beings were born.","Zigu","en/d/zigu.html"],["Sister of an Achaean leader","Her half-brother Meges was one of the Achaean leaders in the Trojan War, placing her family at the heart of the Trojan cycle.","Eurydameia","en/d/euridamea.html"],["A goddess with a grandmother&#x27;s name","In Armenia, Nane lives on as an affectionate term for the grandmother of the household.","Nane","en/d/nane.html"],["Sacred lions","His temple at Taremu kept tame lions, which were fed daily with ox meat.","Maahes","en/d/maahes.html"],["Many names","The Sun has several Basque forms, such as Ekhi, Eguzki, Iuski or Iduzki, all referring to the same goddess.","Eki","en/d/eki.html"],["Sacred deer","In the park leading to the shrine, deer roam freely and are considered messengers of the <em>kami</em>.","Kasuga Daimyōjin","en/d/kasuga-daimyojin.html"],["A pearl as offering","According to the sutra, the pearl she offers symbolizes her life and ego, and the Buddha accepts it as a sign of her devotion.","Longnü","en/d/longnu.html"],["A different ending","In the Jain version, Ravana is killed by Lakshmana rather than by Rama, as happens in the Hindu Ramayana.","Rama (Jain version)","en/d/rama-version-jaina.html"],["A son of Apollo","Her daughter Parthenope had a son, Lycomedes, by Apollo, one of the few descendants of Samia the accounts name in detail.","Samia","en/d/samia.html"],["A meaning hard to pin down","One of the Oglala informants of ethnographer James Riley Walker defined wakan as &quot;anything hard to understand&quot; or &quot;that someone does not understand&quot;.","Wakan Tanka","en/d/wakan-tanka.html"],["A famous father","Tydeus was one of the Seven against Thebes. According to some accounts, Athena wanted to grant him immortality, but withdrew the favour after seeing him devour the brains of his enemy.","Diomedes","en/d/diomedes.html"],["Cow horns sold as fakes","In Cambodian markets, cow horns are often sold as imitations of kting voar horns.","Kting voar","en/d/kting-voar.html"],["Priam&#x27;s choice","One scholiast suggests that King Priam entrusted command to Antenor&#x27;s sons because he did not fully trust Aeneas.","Archelochus","en/d/arqueloco.html"],["A reading mix-up","In Japanese, the character li is read tanuki, so the fūri came to be considered a kind of tanuki, or raccoon dog.","Fengli","en/d/fengli.html"],["A sacred frog","For the Egyptians the frog was an ancient symbol of fertility, linked to the annual flooding of the Nile. Early frog statuettes are often thought to depict the goddess.","Heqet","en/d/heket.html"],["An inherited name","The name Ajita appears in the Vedas, although modern Indologists attribute it to shared ancient Indian naming rather than to historical proof of his existence.","Ajitanatha","en/d/ajitanatha.html"],["An exemplary punishment","Plutarch cites Termerus&#x27;s death as an example of Theseus&#x27; method of returning to his attackers the violence they used.","Termerus","en/d/termero.html"],["A statue with a false reputation","Robert Howard displayed a wooden head of Atho that he claimed was 2200 years old. The piece was stolen in 1967, and his son later admitted that Howard had carved it himself.","Horned God","en/d/dios-astado.html"],["A name that is almost a sentence","Caturix means &quot;battle king&quot;, and the same meaning appears in the name of the Caturiges, the tribe that gave its name to the Chorges area.","Caturix","en/d/caturix.html"],["A day of the week","Shanivara, Saturday in the Hindu calendar, takes its name from Shani, just as the Western Saturday takes its name from Saturn.","Shani","en/d/shani.html"],["Potatoes buried with the dead","The Incas buried potatoes with their dead as a symbol of the goddess&#x27;s blessings in the Uku Pacha.","Axo Mama","en/d/axo-mama.html"],["Four arms","In the four-armed form, a right hand may hold an axe or a trident, and a left hand a mirror or a lotus, depending on the source.","Ardhanari","en/d/ardhanari.html"],["Golden statue","Pliny the Elder reports that Mark Antony&#x27;s soldiers smashed a huge solid gold statue of the goddess, and the Armenians later erected a new golden statue at Erez.","Anahit","en/d/anahit.html"],["Storm in the strait","The story places the encounter in the Korea Strait, a passage known for its strong currents.","Shinra Myōjin","en/d/shinra-myojin.html"],["The origin of the Milky Way","According to the story, Hera pushed the infant Heracles away from her breast, and her milk sprayed across the sky, forming the Milky Way.","The Labours of Heracles","en/d/los-doce-trabajos-de-heracles.html"],["Promise to return","There is a growing belief that whoever asks for something at her temple and promises to come back and pray will have their wish granted.","Muthyalamma","en/d/muthyalamma.html"],["A monkey as emblem","The monkey or ape is his emblem, carved or stamped on the pedestal of his images, and it is what allows him to be recognised among other tirthankaras.","Abhinandananatha","en/d/abhinandananatha.html"],["Meaning of the name","The name Fiacha Cennfinnán translates as &#x27;Fiacha of the little white head&#x27;, an epithet that describes the figure.","Fiacha Cennfinnán","en/d/fiacha-cennfinnan.html"],["Winter wins the debate","Alster explains that winter prevails because it provides the water so essential to farming in the hot climate of ancient Mesopotamia.","Enten","en/d/enten.html"],["Twin of Kṣitigarbha","Some traditions present him as the twin brother of the bodhisattva Kṣitigarbha, with whom he shares a place in East Asian Buddhism.","Ākāśagarbha","en/d/akasagarbha.html"],["A name with magic","The Latin <em>carmen</em> meant song, spell or oracle, and it is the root of the English word &quot;charm&quot;.","Carmenta","en/d/carmenta.html"],["The white donkey","It is one of its best-known pranks: it offers itself as a mount and, once ridden, keeps growing without limit.","Diaño","en/d/diano.html"],["A Cynete name","According to the source, his name comes from the Cynete language and means fawn.","Habis","en/d/habis.html"],["Curse tablets","About 130 lead curse tablets have been found in the spring, most addressed to Sulis to recover stolen items such as gloves or money.","Sulis","en/d/sulis.html"],["Clawed butterfly","Her name can mean &quot;obsidian butterfly&quot; or &quot;clawed butterfly&quot;, and the second meaning seems more likely.","Itzpapalotl","en/d/itzpapalotl.html"],["A distorted name","According to rabbinic tradition, biblical scribes took the vowels of the Hebrew word boset, “shame”, to turn Asherah into Astoret.","Asherah","en/d/asera.html"],["What a tsukumogami is","These are yōkai born from everyday objects, such as kitchenware or tools, that come alive through possession by spirits or through constant use for around 90 to 100 years.","Boroboroton","en/d/boroboroton.html"],["A boar with a battle name","Freyja turns Óttar into her boar Hildisvini, a name often rendered as the Battle-Swine.","Óttar","en/d/ottar.html"],["A god with monkey traits","The anthropologist Emiko Ohnuki-Tierney notes that his name contains saru, meaning monkey, and that his features include red buttocks, a prominent trait of the Japanese macaque. The story of a hand caught in a shell also echoes Japanese folktales about monkeys.","Sarutahiko Ōkami","en/d/sarutahiko-okami.html"],["A greyhound with her name","The historian Arrian of Nicomedia owned a beloved greyhound called Horme, which he recorded in his hunting treatise, the Kynēgetikos.","Horme","en/d/horme.html"],["A famous chest","Pausanias describes a depiction of Adicia on the chest of Cypselus, where she appeared as an ugly woman punished by Dike.","Adicia (Adikia)","en/d/adicia-adikia.html"],["An arrow that returns","In the Nakime tale, Amewakahiko&#x27;s arrow pierces the bird and reaches heaven, where Takamimusubi throws it back down to earth.","Takami-musubi-no-kami","en/d/takami-musubi-no-kami.html"],["Lightning in the character","The earliest form of the character includes a component depicting lightning, a sign for ancient people that lightning was a manifestation of the divine.","Shen","en/d/shen.html"],["A distant constellation","Because she was avoiding her father, the constellation that represents her lies far from Centaurus, which stands for Chiron.","Hippe","en/d/hipe.html"],["Origin with a frog","Several traditions explain her name, meaning &quot;soft-bellied&quot;, through a story in which a frog plays a central role in her birth.","Mandodari","en/d/mandodari.html"],["A ditch in the wrong place","The ditch lies inside the bank rather than outside it, suggesting the earthwork was symbolic rather than defensive.","Emain Macha","en/d/emain-macha.html"],["Many spellings","The name appears in forms such as Bandi, Bandei, Bandu or Banduae, depending on the region and the language of the inscription.","Bandue","en/d/bandue.html"],["A variable number","The Matrikas may be seven, eight, nine or sixteen depending on the text and region.","Matrikas","en/d/matrikas.html"],["A moon of Jupiter","One of Jupiter&#x27;s moons bears her name. It is called Aoede and is also known as Jupiter XLI.","Aoide","en/d/aedea.html"],["Paphos mosaic","A mid-2nd century AD Roman villa in Nea Paphos, Cyprus, known as the House of Dionysus, holds a mosaic showing the god giving wine to Icarius as a reward for his hospitality.","Icarius","en/d/icario-q608800.html"],["Pherai and its goddess","No other Thessalian city claimed such a close relationship with a single divinity as Pherai did with Enodia.","Enodia","en/d/enodia.html"],["A doubtful name","The term &quot;Abominable Snowman&quot; arose in 1921 after a British journalist mistranslated the Tibetan word metoh, meaning &quot;man-bear&quot;.","Yeti","en/d/yeti.html"],["An egg in the sanctuary","In the sanctuary of the Leucippides in Sparta, an egg hung from the ceiling and was believed to be the one from which Leda had given birth.","Leda","en/d/leda.html"],["A meaningful name","Periboea means «surrounded by cattle», according to the ancient Greek form of her name.","Periboea","en/d/peribea-q17197552.html"],["A name with possible roots","Some scholars link his name to the Roman Tarquins. Gurney connected it to the Luwian god Tarhunt, a proposal not accepted by everyone.","Tarchon","en/d/tarconte.html"],["A nearly unknown verb","The verb hleina, from which the Prose Edda derives the goddess&#x27;s name, appears only in that passage, so its exact meaning is still debated.","Hlín","en/d/hlin.html"],["A cursed ring","The ring Andvaranaut passed from hand to hand, leaving death in its wake. According to the story, Andvari cursed it before handing it over.","Hreidmar","en/d/hreidmar.html"],["A proverbial helmet","Rabelais called it the Helmet of Pluto and Erasmus the Helmet of Orcus. Over time it became a saying about the politician who acts unseen.","Cap of Hades","en/d/casco-de-hades.html"],["The tide jewels","Kanju, the tide-lowering jewel, and Manju, the tide-raising jewel, are the objects with which Ryūjin controls the sea.","Ryūjin","en/d/ryujin.html"],["An unmistakable mark","A black arc around the eye, running down to the cheek, makes him recognisable in Maya codices.","Buluc-Chabtan","en/d/buluc-chabtan.html"],["Founder of Pellene","One son of Phorbas, Pellen, is credited with founding the city of Pellene in the region of Achaea.","Phorbas","en/d/forbas.html"],["The ants of Aegina","According to myth, the Myrmidons were born from ants transformed by Zeus, and their name comes from the Greek word myrmex, meaning ant.","Aeacus","en/d/eaco.html"],["Inherited heads","According to one account, the seven heads were offspring of the dragon itself, which fell from its mother&#x27;s head once they were fully grown.","Herensuge","en/d/herensuge.html"],["An Olympic parallel","Euthymus, an Olympic victor from Locri in the early fifth century BC, saved a woman from Temesa from a ghostly monster and then married her.","Alcyoneus","en/d/alcioneo-q9148494.html"],["An office with her name","The title cihuacoatl was given to a male high official, second in government to the tlatoani.","Cihuacōātl","en/d/cihuacoatl.html"],["A mirror from 106 CE","One of the earliest references to the pair is inscribed on a bronze mirror cast in 106 CE.","Dongwanggong (King Father of the East)","en/d/dongwanggong-rey-padre-del-este-q10344620.html"],["A shot with no effect","According to legend, Commodore Stephen Decatur fired a cannonball at a flying creature he saw at a cannonball factory, without any effect.","Jersey Devil","en/d/diablo-de-jersey.html"],["A hill on Venus","In 1997 the name Abeona Mons was approved for a hill on the planet Venus, showing how mythological names remain in use in astronomy.","Abeona","en/d/abeona.html"],["Fides and Pistis","Her Greek equivalent was Pistis, the personification of faith and trust.","Fides","en/d/fides.html"],["A farmer among goddesses","The Lebor Gabála Érenn describes her as &#x27;the she-farmer&#x27;, an epithet not expected for the mother of war goddesses.","Ernmas","en/d/ernmas.html"],["Twenty-four syllables","The mantra has twenty-four syllables, which gave the Vedic metre its name. The texts assign each one a rishi, a metre and a deity.","Gayatri","en/d/gayatri.html"],["Solar crown","Some coins from Rhodes show her wearing a solar crown, a detail that links her to her father, Helios.","Electryone","en/d/electriona.html"],["A rainbow on the move","According to some peoples, when a rainbow appears in the sky it is the serpent travelling from one waterhole to another.","Rainbow Serpent","en/d/serpiente-arco-iris.html"],["A nocturnal temple","On the Palatine Hill she had a temple as Noctiluna, the Night-Shiner, which Varro described as shining by night, though its exact purpose is unknown.","Luna","en/d/luna.html"],["A shrine named after a nut","The Lacedaemonians dedicated a temple to Artemis under the epithet Caryatis, linked to Carya, the daughter turned into a walnut tree.","Dion","en/d/dion.html"],["A name with two readings","Vingólf can be read as &#x27;pleasant hall&#x27; or, with a different accent, as &#x27;wine hall&#x27;.","Vingólf","en/d/vingolf.html"],["Hunger of two genders","The Greek word for hunger could be masculine or feminine, and the personification was imagined both ways: as a man at Byzantium and as a woman at Sparta.","Limos","en/d/limos.html"],["A name in a ruler","Tutu appears in the name of Puzur Tutu, a governor from the Ur III period.","Tutu","en/d/tutu-q458098.html"],["A lake with a name","Lake Curtius took its name from the episode in which the rider nearly sank into the mud with his horse.","Metius Curtius","en/d/metio-curcio.html"],["A much-debated photo","Critics such as Joe Nickell argue the image is a double exposure, and magician John Booth reproduced a similar effect with an assistant covered by a sheet.","The Brown Lady of Raynham Hall","en/d/dama-marron-de-raynham-hall.html"],["A name in a net","A legend about fishermen explains her name, which is linked to the Greek word for net.","Dictynna","en/d/dictina.html"],["Meaningful name","Her name, Abarbarea, means «without mud» in ancient Greek.","Abarbarea","en/d/abarbarea-q305548.html"],["An old city with his name","In Old Russian chronicles and songs from 1223 onward, Tallinn appears as Kolyvan, a name theorized to come from <em>Kalevanlinna</em>, &#x27;the burg of Kaleva&#x27;.","Kalev","en/d/kalev.html"],["A festival with keys","During the Portunalia, keys were thrown into a fire for good luck, in a very solemn and mournful manner.","Portunus","en/d/portuno.html"],["A failed expedition","The SyFy programme Destination Truth sent an expedition to search for Mamlambo, which found no evidence that the creature existed.","Mamlambo","en/d/mamlambo.html"],["Lysippos&#x27;s statue","A bronze statue of Kairos by the sculptor Lysippos stood in Sikyon and became the model for many later representations.","Kairos","en/d/cairos.html"],["Punk band","A Filipino punk rock band from the 1990s took the name Tame the Tikbalang and became part of the country&#x27;s underground music scene.","Tikbalang","en/d/tikbalang.html"],["Three heads, six eyes","He is described as a dragon with three heads and six eyes, with a thousand senses able to draw venomous animals.","Azhi Dahaka","en/d/azhi-dahaka.html"],["Meaning of the prefix","The prefix fimbul- means &#x27;mighty&#x27; or &#x27;giant&#x27;, so the name literally translates as &#x27;the mighty winter&#x27;.","Fimbulwinter","en/d/fimbulvetr.html"],["A statue for every cure","Many votive statues of people healed by the god were found near the temple, especially babies and young children.","Eshmun","en/d/eshmun.html"],["Name with meaning","Mabon derives from Maponos, meaning &quot;Great Son&quot;, and Modron from Mātronā, meaning &quot;Great Mother&quot;.","Mabon ap Modron","en/d/mabon-ap-modron.html"],["A golden elephant","According to the texts, elephants that drink from the river of Alaka turn a golden-green hue, due to the saffron the apsaras add when they bathe.","Alaka","en/d/alaka.html"],["The Water of the Alicornio","In Brañosera (Palencia) there is a tale with this title, which recounts the animal&#x27;s fierce reputation and the condition set by the virginal young woman.","Oricuerno","en/d/oricuerno.html"],["An asteroid with a tragic name","George Biddell Airy chose a name linked to tragedy for the asteroid 18 Melpomene because his daughter Elizabeth died on the day it was discovered. That day was also the anniversary of the death of an earlier son.","Melpomene","en/d/melpomene.html"],["Many versions","In some runic songs the protagonist is not Kullervo but Soini or Lemminkäinen, and in Estonia he appears as Kuller, son of Kalev.","Kullervo","en/d/kullervo.html"],["Festival every eight years","According to the sources, her festival was held every eight years, and worshippers wore animal and flower masks.","Xochiquetzal","en/d/xochiquetzal.html"],["Mother of the Buddhas","The texts call her the &quot;Mother of All Buddhas&quot; and state that the Buddhas owe their existence to her.","Prajñāpāramitā Devī","en/d/prajnaparamita-devi.html"],["An asteroid with her name","Aegina, one of the asteroids, was named after the nymph.","Aegina","en/d/egina.html"],["A philosophical example","Lucretius uses Iphianassa&#x27;s sacrifice as a case to argue that misunderstood religion can lead to great evils.","Iphianassa","en/d/ifianasa.html"],["A creation by separation","The Hittite creation myth holds that in the primordial past heaven and earth were united and later separated.","Ullikummi","en/d/ullikummi.html"],["Branches of joy","In antiquity streets, temples, doors, houses and even entire cities were decorated with green branches and leaves as a sign of joy.","Hilaritas","en/d/hilaritas.html"],["An icebreaker with his name","The icebreaker Krasin was first named Svyatogor, after the bogatyr.","Svyatogor","en/d/svyatogor.html"],["A name with several readings","When the name reached Chinese, translators such as Kumārajīva read it as &#x27;the one who perceives the world&#x27;s lamentations&#x27;, a reading that helped shape the figure of Guanyin.","Thousand-Armed Avalokiteshvara","en/d/avalokiteshvara-de-mil-brazos.html"],["A dinosaur named after him","The dinosaur <em>Igai semkhu</em> was found in the Egyptian desert and is named after this deity.","Igai","en/d/igai.html"],["Origin of the name Attica","According to tradition, the region takes its name from Atthis, the daughter of Cranaus, who gave her name to Attica after dying young.","Cranaus","en/d/cranao.html"],["A goddess with many names","Her name appears in different forms depending on language and period, such as Mullissu, Mulliltum or Mulit, which makes her harder to trace through the texts.","Mullissu","en/d/mullissu.html"],["A Sithon of two sexes","Ovid mentions in the Metamorphoses a Sithon who became of indeterminate sex, first man and then woman, as a parallel to the fate of Hermaphroditus. That episode is now lost.","Sithon","en/d/siton.html"],["Two names","The sources refer to her as Nascio or Natio, two forms of the same goddess.","Nascio","en/d/nascio.html"],["Codex ink","The black water <em>tlílatl</em> had healing properties and served as ink for making the codices.","Ixtlilton","en/d/ixtlilton.html"],["The beans of the Lemuria","The rite involved throwing black beans without looking back, an offering that, according to Fowler, symbolised a gift of life.","Lemures","en/d/lemures.html"],["A poem that made him famous","Rudolf Baumbach&#x27;s verse version, published in 1877, was what made the legend widely known, far more than earlier prose versions.","Zlatorog","en/d/zlatorog.html"],["Another Itonus","A second Itonus appears in the genealogies as the son of another Boeotus, himself a son of Poseidon. He was the father of Electryon and the grandfather of Trojan War heroes.","Itonus","en/d/itono.html"],["A fragrant name","His alternative name, Caphaurus, is linked to the word for camphor, which probably hinted at a somewhat exotic African origin.","Cephalion","en/d/cefalion.html"],["An unusual goddess","Athena is a virgin goddess, yet the punishment she imposes on Alcinoe resembles the methods of Aphrodite, as scholars have noted.","Alcinoe","en/d/alcinoe.html"],["Several names","Depending on the version, she is also known as Mauravi, Mourvi, Maurvi and Kamkanthika.","Ahilawati","en/d/ahilawati.html"],["A lacquer box","An object from the tomb of the Marquis Yi of Zeng records the names of the twenty-eight constellations and the four symbols, including the Vermilion Bird.","Vermilion Bird","en/d/ave-bermellon.html"],["Valkyrie&#x27;s horse","A Scandinavian kenning used the phrase &#x27;valkyrie&#x27;s horse&#x27; as a poetic way to refer to the wolf, suggesting these figures were linked to wolves rather than horses.","Valkyrie","en/d/valquiria.html"],["Eight legs","Sharabha, the form Shiva took, had eight legs and was mightier than a lion or an elephant.","Maa Shoolini","en/d/maa-shoolini.html"],["A name for a place","According to tradition, Disaules gave his name to the place called Celeae, linking him to the local geography.","Disaules","en/d/disaules.html"],["Divination with flour","The term alphitomanteia refers to a form of divination using flour or barley meal, linked to her name.","Alphito","en/d/alfito.html"],["A moon named for her","One of Saturn&#x27;s moons is named after Gunnlod.","Gunnlod","en/d/gunnlod.html"],["A drink with worms","Cathbad makes Ness drink river water in which two worms are floating. The story denies that this causes the pregnancy, although in many Irish legends heroes are conceived when their mothers swallow a tiny creature.","Ness","en/d/ness.html"],["A name shared with Indra","Vajra means diamond, but also thunderbolt or anything very hard. That is why the name Vajradhara is also applied to Indra, the god of thunder.","Vajradhara","en/d/vajradhara.html"],["A name that endures","The name of Mount Haemus gave way to that of the Balkan Mountains, while the old name still ties to the whole peninsula.","Haemus","en/d/hemo.html"],["Voice and word","The Sanskrit vāk, from which Vach derives, means &#x27;word&#x27;. The same Indo-European root gave the Latin word vox.","Vac","en/d/vach.html"],["Many steps","The temple is said to be reached after climbing about 1465 steps from the foot of the hill, although other sources give a noticeably lower height above sea level.","Jivdani Mata","en/d/jivdani-mata.html"],["A rakshasa with his identity","In the Ramayana, Viradha is Tumburu reborn through a curse. Rama and Lakshmana defeat him, and in death he regains his form as a gandharva.","Tumburu","en/d/tumburu.html"],["The Zab River","According to Gardizi, the channels dug in the Sawad were called the Zab River, in reference to this king.","Zaav","en/d/zaav.html"],["Ireland&#x27;s first satire","The poem Cairbre composed against Bres is considered the first satire in Ireland. According to the story, everything went wrong for the king from then on.","Bres","en/d/bres.html"],["Votive stones","Many of his cults take the form of small stone markers placed beside dikes, canals, rice paddies and springs, sometimes even in household wells.","Suijin","en/d/suijin.html"],["Eight attendants","In later iconography, the eight powers of the Ashtasiddhi appear as eight young women surrounding Ganesha. In a painting by Raja Ravi Varma, the fans show that they are attendants.","Consorts of Ganesha","en/d/consortes-de-ganesha.html"],["A grandson of Priam","Munitus, son of Acamas and Laodice, was a grandson of King Priam of Troy, making him a member of the Trojan royal family.","Acamas","en/d/acamante.html"],["Two names for one son","Depending on the source, Kari&#x27;s son is called Frosti or Jökul. Both names refer to ice and snow, which fits the wintry character of his lineage.","Kari","en/d/kari.html"],["Companion of Pomona","St. Augustine places her alongside Pomona, goddess of apples, as a deity who promoted the supply of their respective products.","Mellona","en/d/melona.html"],["A goddess with two names","The Greeks knew her as Derceto, and at Hierapolis the locals used her Syrian name while the temple was nominally dedicated to Hera.","Atargatis","en/d/atargatis.html"],["A hunt of heroes","It gathered many of the most important heroes of mythical Greece, among them several Argonauts.","Calydonian Boar","en/d/jabali-de-calidon.html"],["An uncertain etymology","The traditional meaning of her name may be a later scholarly interpretation. Originally it could have meant something like &quot;lady of the lofty wine.&quot;","Nintinugga","en/d/nintinugga.html"],["Bees born from a dead animal","According to Virgil, Aristaeus found new swarms inside the carcasses of the animals he had sacrificed. This ancient belief is known as bugonia.","Aristaeus","en/d/aristeo.html"],["Greek parallel","His mother&#x27;s desperate search has been compared to Demeter&#x27;s search for her daughter Persephone in Greek mythology.","Lemminkäinen","en/d/lemminkainen.html"],["Wounded by a bird","According to Apollonius of Rhodes, during the return from Colchis a feather from the Stymphalian Birds wounded him in the shoulder.","Oileus","en/d/oileo.html"],["A title reserved for goddesses","In the Pyramid Texts, “mother of the king” was a title that, according to scholars, was used exclusively for goddesses.","Henet","en/d/henet.html"],["A single photograph","The only photo ever linked to the creature was taken in August 1962 by Richard &#x27;Dick&#x27; Vincent while fishing near Meadow Portage.","Manipogo","en/d/manipogo.html"],["A divine ball","According to myth, Adrasteia gave the infant Zeus a marvellous ball to play with. Later, Aphrodite used it to bribe Eros.","Adrasteia","en/d/adrastea-q16737262.html"],["Offering of hair","Young girls on Delos offered Upis a lock of their hair, while boys gave her the hair growing on their cheeks.","Upis","en/d/upis.html"],["Ritual name","Some scholars link her name to camillus, the young attendant who assisted the priest in Roman sacrifices.","Camilla","en/d/camila.html"],["A volcano named after a drink","Mount Chichinauhia was renamed Popocatépetl, after the foam that pulque produces.","Tepoztécatl","en/d/tepoztecatl.html"],["A name meaning whirlwind","In Yoruba, <em>Àjìjà</em> means whirlwind, and the name is often shortened to <em>Ààjà</em>.","Ajá","en/d/aja.html"],["A shrine near Porto","A shrine dedicated to this deity existed in the vicinity of Porto in Roman times.","Durius","en/d/durius.html"],["A statue in Romania","A marble statue of Glycon was found during excavations under the former Pallas railway station in Constanța.","Glycon","en/d/glicon.html"],["Ashes from a kick","Kalayavana died after kicking a sleeping man he believed was Krishna. Muchukunda&#x27;s sleep was protected by a boon from Indra that turned anyone who woke him to ashes.","Kalayavana","en/d/kalayavana.html"],["A film-made origin","The film Demonic Beauty (2002) tells of a Khmer princess cursed after a betrayal. Traditional accounts do not mention her, so this is an invention made for commercial purposes.","Krasue","en/d/krasue.html"],["An affectionate pet name","Her neighbours called her Hecaline, a diminutive that, according to Plutarch, came from the way she treated Theseus with the affection of a grandmother.","Hecale","en/d/hecale.html"],["A throne held in trust","Aeëtes handed Ephyra to Bounos on the condition that he keep it until his own return or that of his descendants.","Bounos","en/d/bounos.html"],["Preaching hall","The samavasarana, where a tīrthaṅkara preaches after attaining omniscience, has three levels: the lowest, made of silver, is for vehicles; the middle, of gold, is for animals; and the highest, of gems, is for kings, devas and ascetics.","Tīrthaṅkaras","en/d/tirthankaras.html"],["A name with a rune","Her name in Old Norse means &quot;victory rune&quot;, a natural link to her role as a valkyrie.","Sigrún","en/d/sigrun.html"],["A very sad song","Modern literary critics consider Electra&#x27;s song in Euripides&#x27; play one of the saddest in ancient Greek tragedy.","Electra","en/d/electra.html"],["An oracle, not a demon","In the figure game <em>Monster in My Pocket</em>, Orobas (number 78) is described more as an oracle than as a demon.","Orobas","en/d/orobas.html"],["A town named after him","The town of Phlius, formerly called Araithyrea, was believed to have taken its name from Phlias.","Phlias","en/d/flias.html"],["A Lithuanian Diana","Jan Długosz compared Medeina with the Roman goddess Diana, and she was sometimes also called Diana.","Medeina","en/d/medeina.html"],["Another name","The place is also known as Idliragijenget, a term that refers both to the spirits of the dead and to the underworld.","Adlivun","en/d/adlivun.html"],["Human appearance","When he takes human form, he appears as an old man with three fingers on each hand and a hat of darkness.","Tuoni","en/d/tuoni.html"],["A hound in the sky","Some tellings say Zeus placed the hound among the stars as the constellation Canis Major.","Laelaps","en/d/lelape.html"],["The island and its name","Ovid states that the island takes its name from Paphos, the daughter of Pygmalion and Galatea.","Paphos","en/d/pafos.html"],["A goddess with several names","Aglaia also appears as Charis or Calê, names meaning &#x27;grace&#x27; and &#x27;beauty&#x27;. This is why some authors think different characters may be the same figure.","Aglaia","en/d/aglaya.html"],["Kaneyama shrines","Shrines named Kaneyama exist throughout Japan and are dedicated to these deities of the mines.","Kanayamahiko and Kanayamahime","en/d/kanayamahiko-y-kanayamahime.html"],["The uraeus of Ra","She is described as “the uraeus of Ra,” a great serpent on Horakhty&#x27;s brow that illuminates the land.","Tenenet","en/d/tenenet.html"],["An iguana, according to police","Officer Mark Matthews said the creature he shot in 1972 was a large iguana missing its tail. He suggested it was probably an escaped pet.","Loveland frog","en/d/rana-de-loveland.html"],["A play on words","The name may rest on a double meaning: <em>ami</em> means &quot;net&quot; and also a small mysid shrimp, which may have inspired Sekien.","Amikiri","en/d/amikiri.html"],["Two chronicles, two versions","The Kojiki counts twelve deities in total, while the Nihon Shoki describes three spontaneous generations followed by four of sibling pairs. It also spells the names differently.","Kamiyonanayo","en/d/kamiyonanayo.html"],["Coloured sands","The legend attributes the coloured sands of the coast to the food grains the goddess scattered.","Devi Kanya Kumari","en/d/devi-kanya-kumari.html"],["Death in the feminine","Although Death was male in Greek, the Latin <em>mors</em> is feminine, and poets imagined her as a woman, though surviving Roman art does not depict her that way.","Mors","en/d/mors.html"],["A grove named for her","The sanctuary at Luco dei Marsi was known in Latin as Lucus Angitiae, &quot;Sacred Grove of Angitia&quot;, showing how strong her cult was in the region.","Angitia","en/d/angitia.html"],["A hot temper as a young immortal","One story says that soon after becoming immortal, in a fit of anger he deformed the bank of a river.","Lü Dongbin","en/d/lu-dongbin.html"],["A rite of sharing","The distribution of food was part of the festival, since the king was seen as the father who fed the people.","Charila","en/d/carila.html"],["Olympic mascots","At the 2004 Athens Olympics, the mascots were two siblings, Athenà and Phèvos, names taken from Athena and Phoebus.","Phoebus","en/d/febo.html"],["A city without a palace","Nippur was the only Sumerian city-state that never built a palace, because its king was considered to be Enlil himself.","Enlil","en/d/enlil.html"],["Diamonds","According to the travellers Jean-Baptiste Tavernier and the historian Edward Gibbon, diamonds were exported from Sambalpur to Rome. The region was known since ancient times as Hirakhanda, the land of diamonds.","Samaleswari","en/d/samaleswari.html"],["A royal crescent","Mah&#x27;s crescent was a symbol of royalty during the Parthian and Sasanian periods.","Mah","en/d/mah.html"],["A roof that shone","Its silver roof and golden pillars were said to radiate light visible from a great distance.","Glitnir","en/d/glitnir.html"],["Sandals turned into squirrels","According to one tellings, when he went back to the sky he left his old sandals on earth, and they became the first squirrels.","Ae-oyna-kamuy","en/d/ae-oyna-kamuy.html"],["A translation error","The Trojan asteroid 5041 is named after the herald, but with an extra &#x27;e&#x27; caused by an error in a German translation of the Iliad.","Thootes","en/d/tootes.html"],["Stones with the same name","In Japan, rocks in areas with toxic volcanic gases are often called Sessho-seki.","Sessho-seki","en/d/sessho-seki.html"],["A widespread folk pattern","The story follows a pattern found widely in folklore: a seductress who, after being rejected, falsely accuses the man she desired, as in the biblical story of Joseph and Potiphar&#x27;s wife.","Astydamia","en/d/astidamia.html"],["A name that describes its body","The word Bøyg means &quot;bend,&quot; &quot;twist&quot; or &quot;curve,&quot; which fits the image of a serpent that coils and winds.","Bøyg","en/d/byg.html"],["A temple to Brahma","According to the legend, Brahma received only one temple in his honour, at Pushkar, as punishment for his lie.","Jyotirlinga","en/d/jyotirlinga.html"],["A name meaning snatchers","The Greek name Harpyia means something like &quot;snatchers&quot; or &quot;swift robbers&quot;, reflecting their role of carrying things off.","Harpies","en/d/arpias.html"],["Two Catilli","Roman legend distinguishes two characters with this name: Catillus the Arcadian, the father, and Catillus, his son.","Catillus","en/d/catilo.html"],["A memorial in Phrygia","Pausanias states that a memorial to Coroebus stood on the border of Phrygia, suggesting his memory was kept alive in the region.","Coroebus","en/d/corebo-de-frigia.html"],["A helmet with a long journey","According to the Iliad, the helmet stolen from Amyntor&#x27;s house ended up with Odysseus during the Trojan War, passed on by Meriones.","Amyntor","en/d/amintor.html"],["An unusual origin","In the Kojiki, the pair are born from Izanami&#x27;s feces, one of the most unusual births in the Japanese myths.","Haniyasu-hiko and Haniyasu-hime","en/d/haniyasu-hiko-y-haniyasu-hime.html"],["A ring from the underworld","In the Prose Edda, Baldr sends gifts from Hel to the living, and among them is a finger-ring for Fulla.","Fulla","en/d/fulla.html"],["Other names","Some sources call her Ninsiskurra, and the reference text also identifies her as Ninsutu or Ninkautu.","Ningirida","en/d/ningirida.html"],["Honorific name","He is also called Hongyuan Laozu, &quot;Great Primal Ancestor&quot;, and Hunyuan Laozu, &quot;Ancestor of Original Chaos&quot;.","Hongjun Laozu","en/d/hongjun-laozu.html"],["A leap with a name","The rock from which, according to the story, Corineus threw the giant into the sea was known for centuries as Gogmagog&#x27;s Leap.","Gogmagog","en/d/gogmagog.html"],["Stars named after him","According to beliefs in some parts of India, seven stars of the Big Dipper carry the names of the Saptarishi, Marichi included.","Marichi","en/d/marichi.html"],["Milk instead of wine","In a context where wine was the most common libation, her temple received milk as an offering, a detail that reflects the nature of the goddess.","Rumina","en/d/rumina.html"],["Ivory wands","Magical ivory wands bearing his image and that of other protective deities were placed beside newborns.","Aha","en/d/aha.html"],["A guide with three eyes","The oracle asked for a man with three eyes to lead the way. Oxylus had lost one eye, so he fit the prophecy once the count of his eyes and the animal he rode were taken into account.","Cresphontes","en/d/cresfontes.html"],["A name that means covenant","It comes from an Indo-Iranian common noun meaning covenant or agreement. In modern Sanskrit, mitra means friend.","Mitra","en/d/mitra.html"],["Smaller than its sister","The Plataea statue was somewhat smaller than the Athena Promachos on the Athenian Acropolis, also attributed to Pheidias.","Athena Areia","en/d/atenea-area.html"],["An instrument with meaning","The pipa he holds is read as a symbol of music as a way to spread joy and the Dharma.","Dhṛtarāṣṭra","en/d/dhrtarastra.html"],["Brandreth&#x27;s pills","Benjamin Brandreth sold his purgative pills as a cure for many illnesses, and his name became so well known in the United States that it appeared in Moby-Dick.","Panacea","en/d/panacea-q910154.html"],["Last sighting","The last official sighting is placed in the final days of or after World War II, in the 1940s.","Demon Cat","en/d/gato-demonio.html"],["Meaning of the name","Her Greek name, Πρωτὼ, means &#x27;the first&#x27; or &#x27;the receiver&#x27;.","Proto","en/d/proto.html"],["A name linked to magic","Bricta&#x27;s name is thought to come from a Gaulish word meaning magic, a term that also appears on the Chamalières inscription.","Bricta","en/d/bricta.html"],["A king who lends his name to wine","Tradition links him to the Greek word for wine, <em>oinos</em>, although that is not its real origin. Other accounts say the grape or its juice was named after his servants instead.","Oeneus","en/d/eneo.html"],["Seals of the first pharaohs","His name appears on the necropolis seals of the First Dynasty pharaohs Den and Qa&#x27;a, where each predecessor is named with the title &quot;Horus Khenti-Amentiu&quot;, starting with Narmer.","Khenti-Amentiu","en/d/jentiamentiu.html"],["Kodandapani","Rama is often praised as Kodandapani, &quot;the holder of the Kodanda&quot;, a way of naming him through his weapon.","Sharanga","en/d/sharanga.html"],["Ovens of Tūmatauenga","The marae, the Māori ceremonial space, is considered the fiery ovens of Tūmatauenga. Areas where battles take place are his domain.","Tūmatauenga","en/d/tumatauenga.html"],["A mountain named for her","Stephanus of Byzantium reports that a mountain in Mysia was named after Arganthone, showing how her figure became attached to the landscape.","Arganthone","en/d/arganthone.html"],["Offerings without fish","Her devotees offered her everything except fish, even though her cult was tied to fishing and seafaring.","Brizo","en/d/brizo.html"],["Cobra hood","His image usually shows a serpent hood over his head, a feature that sets him apart from other tirthankaras.","Parshvanatha","en/d/parsua.html"],["A queen and a tree","The salabhanjika pose recalls Queen Maya, mother of the Buddha, who gave birth at Lumbini while grasping a branch of an asoka tree.","Yakshini","en/d/iaksi.html"],["Heavenly Twins","According to some traditions, Dievas had two sons known as the Heavenly Twins.","Dievas","en/d/dievas.html"],["A name that means wealth","Pluto comes from the Greek <em>Plouton</em>, meaning the rich one, and in antiquity he was sometimes confused with Ploutos, the god of wealth.","Pluto","en/d/pluton.html"],["A disputed figure","Although many regard it as male, some specialists argue that Tuchulcha is female or genderless, based on its dress and skin.","Tuchulcha","en/d/tuchulcha.html"],["A name linked to the liver","In Egyptian, Imsety&#x27;s name resembled the word for &#x27;liver&#x27;, which may explain why he became linked with that organ.","Imsety","en/d/amset.html"],["A name that shines","Heiðr, the name she receives after her resurrection, means “bright” or “clear” in its adjectival form. According to Rudolf Simek, the meaning of the name remains basically the same as that of Gullveig.","Gullveig","en/d/gullveig.html"],["Familiar image","He is usually shown smiling in tattered robes, wearing a hat marked with the character Fo and often holding his shoes in one hand.","Ji Gong","en/d/ji-gong.html"],["A founding contest","The bout with Takeminakata is considered the mythical origin of sumo.","Takemikazuchi","en/d/takemikazuchi.html"],["Meaningful name","Mawu is translated as The Unsurpassable One, formed from a negative particle and a verb meaning to surpass or exceed.","Mawu-Lisa","en/d/mawu-lisa.html"],["Bronze statuette","A small bronze statuette found at Foy-Noville shows the god wearing a wolf skin, and its base is inscribed with his name in the dative case.","Intarabus","en/d/intarabus.html"],["A name meaning «red»","His name is usually interpreted as meaning «red», a term linked to the warlike character of the god.","Rudianos","en/d/rudianos.html"],["First kin-slayer","By killing his father-in-law, Ixion is regarded in the sources as the first man guilty of murdering a kinsman in Greek mythology.","Ixion","en/d/ixion.html"],["A name made of brick","His name comes from <em>later-</em>, «brick», the same root as <em>opus latericium</em>, a Roman brickwork technique.","Lateranus","en/d/laterano.html"],["Names of the vine","According to Nonnus, the names Staphylus and Botrys refer to parts of the grapevine: the cluster and the grape.","Methe","en/d/mete.html"],["An invented name","The Taiheiki does not name the bird. The name &#x27;Itsumade&#x27; was taken by Sekien from the cry the bird repeats in the story.","Itsumade","en/d/itsumade.html"],["An onomatopoeic name","Its name comes from the verb <em>drečati</em>, meaning to screech, in reference to the cries attributed to it.","Drekavac","en/d/drekavac.html"],["A silver egg","According to Ibycus, Molione gave birth after laying a silver egg, an image that recalls the birth of the Dioscuri.","Molione","en/d/molione.html"],["A city named after him","According to Pausanias, Phlegyas founded a city that bore his name, gathering there some of the finest soldiers in Greece.","Phlegyas","en/d/flegias.html"],["Poker name","Sors is a popular name in poker, as are the sayings that mention him.","Sors","en/d/sors.html"],["Name of a fawn","His name means &quot;fawn&quot;, linked to his mother&#x27;s transformation into a deer.","Oisín","en/d/oisin.html"],["A name with several forms","In Latin tradition she also appears as Erythia or Erizia, and her Greek name means &quot;the red one&quot;.","Erytheia","en/d/eritea.html"],["A lost tragedy","Euripides wrote about this story in <em>Captive Melanippe</em>, a play that has not survived. It is known only through fragments and other ancient versions.","Theano","en/d/teano.html"],["An Argonaut","Nauplius also appears among the heroes who sailed with Jason on the Argo in search of the Golden Fleece.","Nauplius","en/d/nauplio-q6038851.html"],["Twelve sisters","Dysis had eleven sisters among the Horae, including Auge, Anatole and Mesembria. Each one governed a different part of the day or of everyday life.","Dysis","en/d/dysis.html"],["Hair on fire","In Book VII of the Aeneid, while Lavinia attends a sacrifice, her hair catches fire, an omen of glorious days for her and of war for the Latins.","Lavinia","en/d/lavinia.html"],["A two-thousand-year penance","In the Lakshmi Tantra, Indra needs two thousand years of penance before the goddess appears to him.","Jaganmata","en/d/jaganmata.html"],["Doubtful place names","Brückner linked several Polish place names to Svarog, but the idea is doubtful. The modern form Swarożyn is considered a false transcription.","Svarog","en/d/svarog.html"],["The parijata tree","The Puranas state that Shachi owned the parijata tree, one of the jewels that emerged from the churning of the ocean.","Shachi","en/d/shachi.html"],["A map with a name","A map attributed to Marco Polo&#x27;s family mentions Fusang. Dated to the 15th or 16th century, it is at best a copy of a lost original.","Fusang","en/d/fusang.html"],["Fish bones","A legend from Aomori Prefecture tells of a woman considered ugly in life who returns as a beautiful skeleton. She likes fish bones and collapses when she meets a high priest.","Hone-onna","en/d/hone-onna.html"],["A temple with nine shrines","The Chennai temple arranges its eight shrines clockwise and dedicates the ninth to Vishnu and Lakshmi together, unlike the traditional layout of separate shrines.","Ashta Lakshmi","en/d/asta-laksmi.html"],["A name protected from fire","Gimlé is linked to gimr, fire, and hlé, protected place. Hollander proposed another possible reading: gem-roof.","Gimlé","en/d/gimle.html"],["A six-tusked elephant","His mount is a six-tusked white elephant. In the novel <em>Fengshen Yanyi</em>, this animal was originally an immortal defeated by Puxian in battle.","Samantabhadra","en/d/samantabhadra.html"],["Origin of a word","The English word siege originally meant seat or throne. Its military sense of a siege developed later, from the idea of an army figuratively sitting down before a fortress.","Siege Perilous","en/d/asiento-peligroso.html"],["A name with an English echo","The first element of her name, angr, gave English the word anger. Its original sense refers to constriction or distress.","Angrboda","en/d/angrboda.html"],["Runes","In the Hávamál, Dáinn appears as an elf who carved runes, alongside Odin, Dvalin and Ásvid.","Dáinn","en/d/dainn.html"],["An asteroid named after her","The asteroid 184 Dejopeja was named in her honour, so a mythological nymph now has a place in the astronomical sky.","Deiopea","en/d/deyopea.html"],["Sacred bridge","The Shinkyō crosses the Daiya River and is considered one of the three most beautiful bridges in Japan. Its legend tells of a god who appeared with two snakes that transformed into a rainbow-like bridge.","Takiribime","en/d/takiribime.html"],["Tears of red gold","In the Skáldskaparmál, gold is described with kennings that link it to Freyja&#x27;s tears, which she sheds for her husband Óðr.","Freyja","en/d/freyja.html"],["A name that describes his character","His Greek name, Δίκαιος, means &quot;just&quot;, which fits the image the texts give of him.","Dicaeus","en/d/diceo.html"],["Relics kept as one mass","The commentaries on the Buddhavamsa state that his relics stayed together in a single mass rather than being scattered.","Koṇḍañña","en/d/kondanna.html"],["Two mothers","According to manuscript U of the Prose Edda, Dagr&#x27;s mother would be the Earth, Jörð, rather than the night, Nótt. The other manuscripts keep Nótt.","Dagr","en/d/dagr.html"],["A play on words","The name of the storm may have been linked to the Greek word for goat, αἴξ, through sound similarity, which could have given rise to the idea of a goatskin.","Aegis","en/d/egida.html"],["A goddess who became a god","In the oldest texts Kakka is female, while in later literature she appears as a male god.","Kakka","en/d/kakka.html"],["Meaning of his name","His name is usually interpreted as &quot;hot red water&quot;, related to the modern Persian <em>sorkh-ab</em>, though it can also mean &quot;beautiful and shining face&quot;.","Sohrab","en/d/sohrab.html"],["A very early account","Fragments of Pherecydes of Syros, a philosopher of the 6th century BCE, are the first surviving mention of Ophion, in a story where Chronos creates the universe.","Ophion","en/d/ofion.html"],["A head crowned with ears of corn","At Alesia a fragment of Damona&#x27;s cult statue was found: a carved stone head crowned with ears of corn and a left hand coiled by a serpent. Both attributes link her to healing and fertility.","Damona","en/d/damona.html"],["A telling name","His name is usually translated as &quot;slayer of Belleros&quot;, after the Corinthian he killed by accident. Some scholars propose other explanations, such as a link to the Greek word for dart.","Bellerophon","en/d/belerofonte.html"],["A blood-red flower","In Serbian tradition, the red color of the peony <em>Paeonia peregrina</em> became a symbol of the &quot;bloodshed in the Battle of Kosovo&quot;.","Kosovo Myth","en/d/mito-de-kosovo.html"],["A thunderbolt on the coin","Hellenistic tetradrachms often showed the goddess with a thunderbolt and an aegis, a combination that identifies her in coin iconography.","Athena Alkidemos","en/d/atenea-alcidemo.html"],["Gateway to the underworld","Cape Taenarum was regarded as a conventional entrance to the underworld, so the place became associated with the dead.","Taenarus","en/d/tenaro.html"],["A spider at the loom","The scholar Thorkild Jacobsen suggested that Uttu was imagined as a spider spinning its web. The link rests on a single text and may be a learned etymology or simply the similar sound of the Akkadian word for spider, <em>ettūtu</em>.","Uttu","en/d/uttu.html"],["Two rivers, two Celtic names","The Danube begins with two small rivers draining the Black Forest, the Breg and the Brigach, both with Celtic names. The longer one, the Breg, is considered the most likely candidate for the source the ancient authors described.","Abnoba","en/d/abnoba.html"],["A nickname meaning ring","His epithet Hring means &#x27;ring&#x27; in Old Norse, which is where the name by which he is known comes from.","Sigurd Ring","en/d/sigurd-ring.html"],["A sign with no clear meaning","The hieroglyph for Ren combines a mouth sign, representing the R, and a wavy line, representing the N. It is not known for certain whether its value was purely phonetic or whether it also carried a symbolic meaning.","Ren","en/d/ren.html"],["An eye that harms","The back eye of Balar, one of the Irish Fomorians, is described as able to cause the evil eye. For this reason it is often compared to Medusa.","Olláparo","en/d/ollaparo.html"],["Serious oaths","At Haliartos, locals swore by the Praxidicae, and the oath was not taken lightly.","Praxidice","en/d/praxidice.html"],["From god to weekday","The English name Wednesday and the French mercredi preserve the association of Mercury with the Germanic god Wotan.","Mercury","en/d/mercurio.html"],["A helm that made him invisible","The Cyclopes forged a helm of invisibility for Hades during the Titanomachy. That is why the scepter, the cornucopia, and the helm are his usual attributes in art.","Hades","en/d/hades.html"],["A river that changed its name","The river Axius is known today as the Vardar, the name by which it is identified in the ancient region of Macedonia.","Axius","en/d/axio.html"],["Fifteen orishas","According to the story, fifteen orishas emerged from Yemaja after she fell, an episode that explains their origin within the pantheon.","Orungan","en/d/orungan.html"],["Hidden weapons","His arms were left hidden at home, a detail the story presents as an ominous sign of his fate.","Ancaeus","en/d/ancaeo.html"],["Forty-eight vows","The most common sutra attributes forty-eight vows to Dharmākara, which form the foundation of Pure Land doctrine.","Amitāyus","en/d/amitayus.html"],["Night-built bridges","Some beliefs hold that certain bridges in Gipuzkoa and Navarre were built at night by lamiak.","Lamia","en/d/lamia-q2577796.html"],["A game of dice","According to Apollodorus, the death of a boy during a game of dice forced Patroclus to flee from Opus.","Patroclus","en/d/patroclo.html"],["A tree with his name","In Sanskrit, <em>muchilinda</em> referred to the Pterospermum acerifolium, a tree known in other countries as karnikara or baiur. Its broad leaves are used as disposable plates, which gives it the popular name &#x27;food plate tree&#x27;.","Mucalinda","en/d/muchilinda.html"],["A hill with another name","The Quirinal in Rome was originally called &quot;Agonius&quot;, according to the sources.","Agonius","en/d/agonio.html"],["Dog coins","From 475 BCE, Segesta&#x27;s coins showed a dog on one side and a woman&#x27;s head on the other. They have been linked to the myth, but their exact meaning remains unclear.","Crinisus","en/d/crinisus.html"],["His wife","Šumugan was married to Ellamesi, regarded as the goddess of wool. Unlike Dumuzi, another agricultural god, he was rarely portrayed in romantic settings.","Šumugan","en/d/sumugan.html"],["A wind that darkens the sky","In chapter 21 his Samadhi Divine Wind is described as strong enough to shake mountains, darken the sky and disturb the gods.","Yellow Wind Demon","en/d/demonio-del-viento-amarillo.html"],["The Milky Way","According to the myth, the milk Juno spilled when she pushed away the infant Hercules formed the band of stars we now call the Milky Way.","Hercules","en/d/hercules.html"],["Name of an animal","His Greek name, <em>Φῶκος</em>, means &quot;seal&quot;, the marine mammal.","Phocus","en/d/foco-q3381352.html"],["Fire in every home","Every Aztec household kept a small permanent fire burning in honour of the god.","Xiuhtecuhtli","en/d/xiuhtecuhtli.html"],["Battlefield","The battlefield was sometimes called &#x27;the garden of the Badb&#x27;, a image that sums up her presence among the dead.","Badb","en/d/badb.html"],["Cities with similar names","It has been proposed that Cambrai and Hamburg derive their names from Gambrivium, although this remains an unconfirmed hypothesis.","Gambrinus","en/d/gambrinus.html"],["An asteroid with her name","The asteroid (163693) Atira was the first known with an orbit entirely inside Earth&#x27;s, and it gave its name to a whole class of asteroids.","Atira","en/d/atira.html"],["An ancient question","In the Chuci, Qu Yuan asks who shaped Nüwa&#x27;s body. It is one of the earliest known mentions of the goddess and has been read as a philosophical question about who created the creator.","Nüwa","en/d/nuwa.html"],["The thumb that knows","According to the tale, Fionn could access the knowledge simply by biting his thumb, the same one he burned on the fish fat.","Salmon of Knowledge","en/d/salmon-de-la-sabiduria.html"],["A bronze feather","The only direct evidence of his worship is a bronze object shaped like a feather, inscribed with a dedication to the god.","Abandinus","en/d/abandino.html"],["Meaning of the name","Daksha means &#x27;able&#x27;, &#x27;expert&#x27; or &#x27;honest&#x27;. According to the Bhagavata Purana, he got the name because he was skilled at begetting children.","Daksha","en/d/daksha.html"],["Shifting names","The same demon appears under different names depending on the grimoire: Allogor or Algor in the Liber Officiorum Spirituum, and Alloces or Allocen in other sources.","Alocer","en/d/alocer.html"],["Comparison with Erebus","The Egyptologist Heinrich Karl Brugsch linked this divine pair with Erebus, the Greek god who personified darkness and gloom.","Kek","en/d/kuk.html"],["A star in the cluster","Celaeno gives its name to one of the visible stars in the open cluster of the Pleiades.","Celaeno","en/d/celeno.html"],["Cracks in the lake","The ice cracks that form on Lake Suwa in winter are explained in folklore as Takeminakata&#x27;s crossing to visit his wife.","Yasakatome-no-Ōkami","en/d/yasakatome-no-okami.html"],["A Zeus in serpent form","On votive reliefs from Athens and Piraeus he often appears as a serpent, sometimes bearded, perhaps to match the usual depictions of Zeus.","Meilichios","en/d/meiliquio.html"],["A kingdom named after him","Hyllus, son of Melite, became the eponymous king of a Dorian tribe in northern Greece, the Hylleis, who took their name from him.","Melite","en/d/melite.html"],["A spring with a story","According to the tradition, the spring of his region appeared where Heracles struck the earth with his club, near Thermopylae.","Cragaleus","en/d/cragaleus.html"],["Half-sisters","Asia and Libya appear as Thrace&#x27;s half-sisters, joining Europa within a family of figures who give their names to territories.","Thrace","en/d/tracia.html"],["The origin of Mysore","Local tradition holds that the city of Mysore takes its name from Mahishasuramardini. According to the legend, the goddess killed him on top of the Chamundi Hills.","Mahishasura","en/d/majishasura.html"],["Fire soot","The name Eldhrímnir is interpreted as «fire-sooty», a reference to a cauldron blackened by flames.","Eldhrímnir","en/d/eldhrimnir.html"],["A telling name","His Greek name means &quot;spokesman&quot; or &quot;mouthpiece&quot;, a hint at the role the tradition gives him.","Antiphates","en/d/antifates-q581853.html"],["Coat of arms with the planets","On the 1918 coat of arms the horseman appears beneath the Seven Celestials, that is, the seven classical planets.","Tetri Giorgi","en/d/tetri-giorgi.html"],["Two names, one lineage","Erythras and Erythrios appear as spelling variants of the same figure, which is common in Greek mythological names transmitted by different authors.","Leucon","en/d/leucon.html"],["A goddess without a cult","Aergia appears very rarely in ancient sources, and there is no record of temples or rites in her honor.","Aergia","en/d/ergia.html"],["Towers and resurrection","The episode of Mushegh Mamikonian shows his relatives placing his body on a tower, hoping the aralez would bring him back to life.","Aralez","en/d/aralez.html"],["The Necklace of Harmonia","The necklace, a wedding gift to Harmonia, passed through many hands and was also called the necklace of Eriphyle because it was the bribe she received.","Eriphyle","en/d/erifile.html"],["A beautiful dwarf","According to the French romance, Oberon is stuck at the height of a three-year-old because of a curse, though his beauty makes up for it.","Oberon","en/d/oberon.html"],["Golden canoe","She was said to ride the sea in a golden canoe, and her beauty was so dazzling that no one could look at her all at once.","Moryana","en/d/moryana.html"],["A father who returns from the underworld","In the Aeneid, Aeneas sees his father again in the Elysian Fields, where Anchises encourages him to continue his journey to Italy.","Anchises","en/d/anquises.html"],["A brother who was not one","Pherecydes holds that Telamon was Peleus&#x27;s friend rather than his brother, a version that contradicts the more common account.","Endeis","en/d/endeide.html"],["Danish name","&quot;Lukøje&quot; means &quot;shut-eye&quot;, and Ole is a very common Danish first name.","Sandman","en/d/arenero.html"],["A name with weekdays","According to Záviš Kalandra, the names of the seven princes might be old Slavonic labels for the days of the week. Vojen would equal Tuesday.","Vojen","en/d/vojen.html"],["A name with meaning","His name comes from the Sanskrit root śiṣ, meaning &quot;what remains&quot;, because he stays unchanged when the universe is destroyed at the end of each kalpa.","Shesha","en/d/shesha.html"],["Possible origin of the name","The name may be related to Proto-Celtic words such as <em>lati-, &#x27;liquor&#x27;, </em>lat-, &#x27;day&#x27;, or *lāto-, &#x27;lust&#x27; or &#x27;desire&#x27;. These remain unconfirmed hypotheses.","Latis","en/d/latis.html"],["A war among the gods","In the conflict with the Bryges, Athena backed Odysseus and Ares backed their enemies, and it was Apollo who intervened to separate them.","Callidice","en/d/calidice.html"],["Born from fire","According to the account, Dhrishtadyumna and Draupadi emerged from the sacrificial fire rather than a womb, which is why they are called <em>ayonija</em>.","Dhrishtadyumna","en/d/dhrishtadyumna.html"],["Etymology","One reading of the name combines the root for &quot;human flesh&quot; or &quot;food&quot; with the possessive prefix &quot;our&quot;, hence &quot;Lady of Our Sustenance&quot;.","Tonacacihuatl","en/d/tonacacihuatl.html"],["Several possible mothers","The Bibliotheca proposes three other wives of Menoetius as possible mothers of Patroclus, showing that ancient genealogies were not uniform.","Philomela","en/d/filomela-q3381167.html"],["Raised by animals","According to the story, her father fed her with milk from cows and mares, since her mother died when she was very young.","Harpalyce","en/d/harpalice.html"],["Armenian stamp","Shivini appears on an Armenian postage stamp issued in 1993.","Shivini","en/d/shivini.html"],["A disputed name","His name has been translated as «corpse-swallower» or «shipwreck-current», depending on the preferred reading.","Hræsvelgr","en/d/hrsvelgr.html"],["A striking omission","His name does not appear on the bronze liver of Piacenza, the object used by haruspices for divination, where other deities do have their sectors.","Sethlans","en/d/sethlans.html"],["A namesake in Italy","Another character named Giges was one of Aeneas’s companions in Italy. He was killed by Turnus, Aeneas’s rival in that land.","Giges","en/d/giges.html"],["The only Gallic goddess in the Roman pantheon","Epona was the only Gallic deity fully integrated into the Roman pantheon, which was unusual for Celtic gods adopted by Rome.","Epona","en/d/epona.html"],["A war against the Yakshas","According to the Srimad Bhagavata, Dhruva declared war on the Yakshas after his half-brother Uttama was killed. When he began killing innocent Yakshas, his grandfather Manu stopped him, and Dhruva later apologised to the Yaksha king Vaishravana.","Dhruva","en/d/dhruva.html"],["The Giant&#x27;s Causeway","According to legend, Fionn built the Giant&#x27;s Causeway as a path of stones to Scotland so he would not wet his feet. It shares its hexagonal basalt columns with Fingal&#x27;s Cave.","Fionn mac Cumhaill","en/d/fionn-mac-cumhaill.html"],["A garden of roses","Herodotus describes a rose garden at the foot of Mount Bermion, which he calls the garden of Midas son of Gordias, where roses grew by themselves and bore sixty blossoms each.","Midas","en/d/midas.html"],["Mother of a traveling god","Hermes, Maia&#x27;s son, was the messenger of the gods and also a protector of travelers, merchants and thieves, traits already visible in his early mischief.","Maia","en/d/maya.html"],["Onions in worship","Sokar&#x27;s followers wore strings of onions during his festivals, a gesture tied to his role in the underworld, since onions were used in embalming.","Sokar","en/d/sokar.html"],["Origin of the name","Some modern interpretations suggest the Greek name may mean something like &#x27;mindful of her husband&#x27;.","Andromeda","en/d/andromeda.html"],["A distinctive emblem","The <em>Anzû</em> bird over two lions was the emblem associated with this god at Lagash, and it could sometimes represent him in cultic settings.","Ninĝirsu","en/d/ningirsu.html"],["A promised return","Tradition says the god left promising to return by sea in canoes called ʻAuwaʻalalua. A queen identified them with a Spanish galleon, although Pukui proposed a different translation.","Lono","en/d/lono.html"],["Hanako-san","In Japan, the legend of Hanako-san, a ghost haunting school toilets, closely parallels the Bloody Mary mythology.","Bloody Mary","en/d/veronica.html"],["Pomegranate diet","After her vow, Hariti stopped feeding on children and ate only pomegranates, a fruit that became closely associated with her image.","Hariti","en/d/hariti.html"],["A metal song","The Portuguese band Moonspell composed a song titled «Trebaruna» celebrating the goddess.","Trebaruna","en/d/trebaruna.html"],["A name with meaning","Her Greek name means « without mud », as the sources record it.","Abarbarea","en/d/abarbarea.html"],["A name linked to mountains","Its name comes from a root meaning «mountain», the same one found in the Old High German Firgunnea, referring to the Ore Mountains.","Fjörgyn","en/d/fjorgyn.html"],["A name that travels","In the Society Islands, Raiatea was once considered a sacred island known as Havai&#x27;i, and in the Tuamotu group the present-day Fakarava bore the name.","Hawaiki","en/d/hawaiki.html"],["A doubtful partner","Some medieval sources give her a male partner called Natigai, but this is thought to be a mispronunciation error.","Etügen Eke","en/d/etugen-eke.html"],["Protected plants","According to Plutarch, her descendants, the Ioxidai, were forbidden to burn wild asparagus and rushes, and treated them with respect.","Perigune","en/d/perigune.html"],["A disputed name","Traditionally the name is translated as &quot;belly spear&quot;, but Joseph Loth and Kuno Meyer linked it to Old Irish <em>bolc</em>, &quot;notch&quot;, which would connect it with Fergus&#x27;s sword Caladbolg and, in their view, with King Arthur&#x27;s Caledfwlch.","Gáe Bulg","en/d/gae-bulg.html"],["Many names","His name appears in many spellings, such as Caasimolar, Glacialabolas or Glasya Labolas, depending on the translations of the grimoires.","Glasya-Labolas","en/d/caacrinolas.html"],["A temple aligned with Sirius","According to Wells, the temple on Elephantine was aligned with the star Sirius, which the Egyptians linked to the onset of the flood.","Satet","en/d/satet.html"],["A city name","Her name points to Cnossus, the city on Crete, although the sources do not explain whether she had any direct connection to that place.","Cnossia","en/d/cnosia.html"],["Pasittu","In Atrahasis, a demon called Pasittu snatches babies from their mothers’ laps, a punishment that would affect one third of women.","Nintu","en/d/nintu.html"],["A legendary sword","In the <em>Hervarar saga</em>, Durinn and Dvalin are credited with forging Tyrfing, a magic sword of great fame in the tradition.","Durin","en/d/durin.html"],["Not Frisia","Despite its name, it should not be confused with the Frisian Islands, a real archipelago in the Wadden Sea shared by the Netherlands, Germany and Denmark.","Frisland","en/d/frislandia.html"],["A hill named after her","Apollodorus places Ate&#x27;s fall on a hill in Phrygia that bore her name, near the future site of Troy.","Ate","en/d/ate.html"],["Able to kill a god","Some accounts suggest the sword could kill a god. King Heidrek struck at Odin in disguise, forcing the god to transform into a hawk to escape the blow.","Tyrfing","en/d/tyrfing.html"],["A compound name","Harihara is also called Shankaranarayana, joining Shankara, another epithet of Shiva, with Narayana, a name of Vishnu.","Harihara","en/d/harihara.html"],["A stamp featuring Vesna","A Vesna appears on a Ukrainian stamp from 2011. A Slovenian stamp featuring her figure sold about 80,000 copies between 2005 and 2006.","Vesna","en/d/vesna.html"],["Foam that becomes dew","According to the Prose Edda, the foam falling from the bit of Hrímfaxi, Nótt&#x27;s horse, is what covers the valleys with dew in the mornings.","Nótt","en/d/nott.html"],["The origin of purple","According to Malalas, purple dye was discovered when a dog was seen with its mouth stained after eating a sea mollusc. Heracles the Tyrian made the discovery.","Phoenix","en/d/fenix-q1145956.html"],["An oni that looks like a monk","In classical yōkai drawings, such as those by Toriyama Sekien in the <em>Gazu Hyakki Yagyō</em>, Gagoze is depicted as an oni with the appearance of a monk.","Gagoze","en/d/gagoze.html"],["Mummified crocodiles","Sacred crocodiles were mummified in large numbers, and some mummies have been found with baby crocodiles in their mouths or on their backs, interpreted as a symbol of protection.","Sobek","en/d/sobek.html"],["A boy&#x27;s name","Pausanias, who confirms the story of the gate, refers to Neis as a boy rather than a girl.","Neis","en/d/neis.html"],["Historical or not","The kings in the early part of the Sumerian King List are usually not considered historical, unless they appear in contemporary documents. Etana is one of the exceptions.","Etana of Kish","en/d/etana-de-kish.html"],["A name meaning swiftness","The name comes from a Greek adjective meaning &#x27;shining white&#x27; and, figuratively, &#x27;quick&#x27; or &#x27;agile&#x27;. In Homeric epic, this epithet was commonly applied to swift dogs.","Argos","en/d/argos.html"],["A renamed creature","In the Bakemonozukushi scroll from Fukuoka, the otoroshi appears under the name ushi-kawazu, or &#x27;ox frog&#x27;, and is said to live in ponds and eat people, a detail found in no other source.","Waira","en/d/waira.html"],["A Gohonzon with a tower","Nichiren included Prabhūtaratna in the Gohonzon, his calligraphic representation of the treasure tower.","Prabhūtaratna","en/d/buda-taho.html"],["A name for a note","Her name was given to the lowest of the seven notes of the lyre, so the Muse lent her name to a specific sound.","Nete","en/d/nete.html"],["A well of two tastes","According to Athenaeus, the water of the spring at Miletus was salty on the surface but very sweet in the main stream.","Trambelus","en/d/trambelo.html"],["A translator&#x27;s name","Frazer called it the «Cadmean vixen» in his 1921 translation, although the Greek texts do not use that name.","Teumessian fox","en/d/zorra-teumesia.html"],["Meaning of the name","Borgarhjörtr literally means &quot;town hart&quot;, a name combining a reference to a town with the word for a male deer.","Þóra Borgarhjörtr","en/d/ora-borgarhjortr.html"],["A twin","Pedasus had a twin brother, Aesepus, with whom he died in the same episode of the <em>Iliad</em>.","Pedasus","en/d/pedaso.html"],["A tree still standing","The kera tree said to have split in two during the legend still stands in the temple grounds at Morkhana, lush and green.","Suswani Mata","en/d/suswani-mata.html"],["Two Emathions","The Emathion who was the brother of Dardanus and the son of Zeus and Electra may be the same figure or a different one. The sources do not settle the question.","Emathion","en/d/emation.html"],["Wolf disguise","To pass unnoticed, Dolon covered himself with a wolf skin and a marten-pelt cap, and tried to fool the Greeks by moving on all fours.","Dolon","en/d/dolon.html"],["Fairy pipes","The small clay pipes often turned up while digging or ploughing are said to belong to clurichauns, who also enjoyed smoking.","Clurichaun","en/d/clurichaun.html"],["Paired with the Alkonost","In folk tales the Sirin is linked to the Alkonost, the bird of joy. The Sirin cries in the apple orchard in the morning, and in the afternoon the Alkonost arrives to laugh, and her dew gives healing powers to the fruit.","Sirin","en/d/sirin.html"],["Cities named after siblings","The Macedonian cities of Mieza and Beroia took their names from two siblings of Olganos. Today they are known as Nausa and Veria.","Olganos","en/d/olgano.html"],["A flute with a single pipe","Unlike his brother, who played a double flute, Babys played an instrument with only one pipe, a detail that put him at a disadvantage from the start of the contest.","Babys","en/d/babys.html"],["A mushroom-shaped scepter","He holds a scepter that grants wishes, shaped like a mushroom. It symbolizes the second phase of Creation, when Yang separated from Yin.","Lingbao Tianzun","en/d/lingbao-tianzun.html"],["A tiger companion","She is often shown travelling with a companion tiger as she moves through the forest.","Budhi Pallien","en/d/budhi-pallien.html"],["Many names","Her name is written in several ways in the sources, such as Finnchóem or Fionnchaomh, which reflects the lack of a fixed spelling in the Irish tradition.","Findchóem","en/d/findchoem.html"],["A giant over the sea","According to some accounts, Poseidon granted him the ability to run across the sea as if it were solid ground, a gift that made his journeys almost unstoppable.","Orion","en/d/orion.html"],["A mountain with his name","The mountain where he was buried keeps his name, and the town of Erice stands on it.","Eryx","en/d/erice.html"],["Auroras in Poland","In Poland they were worshipped in some areas under the name zorze, meaning the auroras.","Narechnitsi","en/d/narechnitsi.html"],["A goddess with few myths","Ancient mythographers noted that Vesta had hardly any myths of her own. Her importance came mainly from being one of the oldest gods, entitled to precedence in worship and offerings.","Vesta","en/d/vesta.html"],["A human version","In Saxo&#x27;s Gesta Danorum, Nanna is not a goddess but a mortal princess, daughter of King Gevar, and the story centres on her rivalry between Baldr and Höðr.","Nanna","en/d/nanna.html"],["A Frankish name","In Waltharius, a Latin poem from around 920, Gunther&#x27;s warriors are called &quot;Nivilones&quot;, perhaps the earliest surviving form of the name.","Nibelungs","en/d/nibelungos.html"],["Falcon-headed lid","The lids of the canopic jars took the form of the sons of Horus&#x27; heads. In the New Kingdom, Qebehsenuef appeared as a falcon.","Qebehsenuef","en/d/qebehsenuf.html"],["Inheritance of the youngest","In the myth, the youngest son inherits Heracles&#x27; bow, which recalls the Celtic custom by which the youngest received the family estate, with its buildings, eight acres of land, an axe, a cauldron and a coulter.","Celtine","en/d/celtine.html"],["A temple in Mysore","In the village of Hebbya, near Nanjangud, a temple dedicated to Gandhari was built; its foundation stone was laid on 19 June 2008.","Gandhari","en/d/gandhari.html"],["Refusing gold","The twins turned down a reward of gold coins for reciting the poem, asking only for grain and forest fruit.","Lava","en/d/lava.html"],["A passage in the Vishnu Purana","The Buddha appears in the Vishnu Purana, one of the oldest Puranic texts in which he is named as an avatar.","The Buddha (Buddha Gautama in Hinduism)","en/d/buda-buda-gautama-en-el-hinduismo.html"],["An apple on the Sosias vase","In Greek art he appears with other Horae and fertility deities, holding summer fruit. On the Sosias vase, around 500 BC, he holds a red fruit.","Carpus","en/d/carpo.html"],["A name for any dog","Over time, sarama came to mean any female dog, not just Indra&#x27;s.","Sarama","en/d/sarama.html"],["A prow that spoke","The prow carried a piece of Dodona oak able to speak with a human voice and deliver oracles.","Argo","en/d/argo.html"],["A direction named after her","The adjective Āgneya is used to name the southeast, the cardinal direction ruled by Agni.","Āgneyī","en/d/agneyi.html"],["Lithobolia","The festivals at Troezen were called Lithobolia, meaning stone-throwing, in reference to the death of the virgins.","Damia","en/d/damia.html"],["A god of thieves","According to the Mahabharata, Karttikeya is also regarded as the god of thieves and swindlers, an unusual trait for a god of war.","Karttikeya","en/d/karttikeya.html"],["A name with a possible Germanic echo","Janne Saarikivi proposed that the name might connect to early Germanic forms of Odin&#x27;s name, though this is only a hypothesis.","Väinämöinen","en/d/vainamoinen.html"],["Names with meaning","Arnobius notes that the names of these deities come from the tasks they protected, as with Puta, linked to pruning.","Puta","en/d/puta.html"],["A name meaning abyss","His name may derive from a Greek term meaning &#x27;place with abysses&#x27;, related to &#x27;chaos&#x27; and &#x27;abyss&#x27;. There was also a Mount Chaon in Argolis.","Chaon","en/d/caon.html"],["A name with meaning","The Song dynasty Lushi records his family name as Wang, meaning &quot;to observe&quot; or &quot;the night of the full moon&quot;, and his given name as Huo, meaning &quot;to hunt or catch&quot;.","Tianhuang","en/d/tianhuang.html"],["Another account by Pliny","Pliny the Elder tells a different story about a Helvetian smith named Helico who, after returning home with dried figs, grapes, olive oil and wine, pushed his compatriots into war to obtain these goods.","Arruns of Clusium","en/d/arrunte-de-clusio.html"],["A city with her name","The Cypriot city of Amathus was associated with Amathusa, although tradition also offered a different explanation linked to Amathes, son of Heracles.","Amathusa","en/d/amatusa.html"],["Soup namesake","The term hundun is linguistically related to wonton, the dumpling soup, though scholars debate this connection.","Hundun","en/d/hundun.html"],["Variable spelling","The only British inscription spells the name Anextiomarus, usually taken as a variant or error for Anextlomarus.","Anextlomarus","en/d/anextlomaro.html"],["A river that appears in the landscape","Pausanias describes the river Erasinus in his <em>Description of Greece</em>, so the figure has a counterpart in a real watercourse in the Peloponnese.","Erasinus","en/d/erasino.html"],["The cherry blossoms","In one version of the Nihongi, Iwanagahime&#x27;s curse makes human life as brief as the petals of the sakura.","Iwanagahime","en/d/iwanagahime.html"],["Leontophonus","His name means Lion Slayer. According to some accounts, he is the son Odysseus had with a daughter of Thoas.","Thoas","en/d/toante.html"],["A five-part god","The sources describe Pariacaca as a god who divides into five atmospheric deities, each linked to a direction of the cosmos.","Pariacaca","en/d/pariacaca.html"],["An inventor of mills","According to tradition, Myles was the first mortal to invent the mill and grind corn, at the place called Alesiae.","Myles","en/d/miles.html"],["A voice clearer than human","According to the sources, the voice spoke in everyday Latin and with a clarity greater than that of a human.","Aius Locutius","en/d/ayo-locucio.html"],["Protector of Zeus","According to some sources, Cres hid the infant Zeus in a cave on Crete to keep him safe.","Cres","en/d/cres.html"],["Name in an ancient script","The name of this spirit is written in Old Turkic, using its own runic-type alphabet.","Chai nenesi","en/d/chai-nenesi.html"],["An uncertain name","The name Hyrrokkin has been translated as &#x27;fire-withered&#x27; or &#x27;fire-smoked&#x27;, perhaps referring to a wrinkled or dark appearance.","Hyrrokkin","en/d/hyrrokkin.html"],["Gender-neutral name","Like many other Etruscan gods, the name Laran is gender neutral.","Laran","en/d/laran.html"],["A corrected height","Revati was much taller than Balarama because she came from an earlier age. He reduced her to the height of people in his own time by tapping her shoulder with his plough.","Revati","en/d/revati.html"],["Iron throne","Her attribute was an iron throne.","Kamrušepa","en/d/kamrusepa.html"],["A twin who judges the world","In Yoruba belief, Kehinde sends his twin Taiwo ahead to check whether the world is fit and beautiful. For this reason the second-born is regarded as the elder.","Ibeji","en/d/ibeyi.html"],["A temple by the market","The temple of Spes and Fides built by Calatinus stood beside the vegetable market, just outside the Carmental Gate. It was burned down and restored twice.","Spes","en/d/spes.html"],["Eye-shaped tattoos","According to the myth, Biston had Thracians of both sexes tattooed with eye-like patterns, which served as a magical fetish.","Biston","en/d/biston.html"],["Possible origin of the name","The name might be connected to the Greek word <em>amalós</em>, meaning &#x27;soft&#x27;.","Amaleus","en/d/amaleo.html"],["A name that changed gender","The name comes from a female goddess, but in the Hebrew Bible it appears in a feminine plural form that early translations did not make clear.","Astaroth","en/d/astaroth.html"],["Bell under the water","Tradition says the bell of the sunken church rings from the depths on Saint John&#x27;s night, but only those with a good heart can hear it.","Legend of Lake Sanabria","en/d/leyenda-del-lago-de-sanabria.html"],["Grandmother of Aeneas","As the mother of Anchises, Themiste is the grandmother of the hero Aeneas in the tradition recorded by the sources.","Themiste","en/d/temiste.html"],["Many sisters","Salamis had numerous sisters, among them Corcyra, Aegina and Thebe. According to some versions, Poseidon carried off several of them from their father&#x27;s house.","Salamis","en/d/salamina.html"],["An ambiguous name","Húnaland could refer both to the territory of the Franks, also called Hugones or Hugas, and to the land of the Huns.","Sigi","en/d/sigi.html"],["A dangerous threshold","The hall&#x27;s threshold is not a simple entrance but a trap with its own name, <em>Fallandaforað</em>.","Éljúðnir","en/d/eljunir.html"],["Batō Kannon with a horse&#x27;s head","One Dōsojin form, Batō Kannon, wears a horse&#x27;s head atop its own. It also protects horses, which were vital for transport, farming and war.","Dōsojin","en/d/dosojin.html"],["A curious nickname","According to hagiography, before becoming a monk, Ilya Pechersky was nicknamed &quot;Chobotok&quot;, meaning &quot;(small) boot&quot;, after an incident in which he fought off enemies with only his boot.","Ilya Muromets","en/d/ilya-muromets.html"],["A meaningful name","Her Egyptian name can be translated as she who embraces those in need, a trait that sums up her role as a welcoming goddess.","Nehmetawy","en/d/nehmetawy.html"],["Catalogue of Ships","Homer names him in the Catalogue of Ships in the second book of the <em>Iliad</em>, a list of the Greek contingents that sailed toward Troy.","Clonius","en/d/clonios.html"],["A namesake river in the Peloponnese","Strabo mentions another river called Enipeus in the Peloponnese, which later became known as the Barniquio. Its sources lay near the ancient city of Salmone.","Enipeus","en/d/enipeo.html"],["One body, three temples","According to tradition, his remains were scattered across three places in present-day India: Bihar, Odisha and Andhra Pradesh.","Gayasura","en/d/gayasura.html"],["A name with meaning","Her name has been read as &#x27;wide justice&#x27; or &#x27;profound judgment&#x27;. This etymology appears in Fulgentius, a mythographer of the late fifth or early sixth century AD.","Eurydice","en/d/euridice.html"],["A touch that predicts the year","On New Year&#x27;s Eve, the temperature of an ovinnik&#x27;s touch revealed what the year would bring: warm meant good fortune, cold meant unhappiness.","Ovinnik","en/d/ovinnik.html"],["Grandfather of Orpheus","The Suda makes him the father of Oeagrus, which makes him the grandfather of the musician Orpheus.","Pierus","en/d/piero-q21567935.html"],["A very common surname","As of 2008, Ao is the most common surname in China, shared by about 250,000 people.","Ao Guang","en/d/ao-guang.html"],["A bird at first","Lí Ban first appears in the form of a sea bird, before revealing herself as a woman.","Lí Ban","en/d/li-ban.html"],["A shared shrine","Pausanias describes a sanctuary in Corinth where Ananke shared worship with Bia, Force, an unusual arrangement for such an abstract figure.","Ananke","en/d/ananke.html"],["Beginning with Hestia","The Greek expression for starting something well comes from the ritual of offering her the first portion at every banquet.","Hestia","en/d/hestia.html"],["Bird of Mars","The woodpecker was sacred to Mars, which is why it was seen as Picus&#x27;s prophetic animal.","Picus","en/d/pico.html"],["Lame from the fall","According to tradition, his limp comes from the fall: when he was expelled from heaven, the other devils landed on top of him.","Diablo Cojuelo","en/d/diablo-cojuelo.html"],["A son by Poseidon","According to some versions of the myth, one possible daughter of Hellen bore Cercyon to the god Poseidon.","Chthonopatra (Xenopatra)","en/d/ctonopatra-jenopatra.html"],["A name from friendship","Polyxenus named his son Amphimachus after Amphimachus, son of Cteatus, a friend who had died at Ilion.","Polyxenus","en/d/polixeno.html"],["One island from many","Hesiod spoke of them in the plural, but Pindar reduced them to a single island, showing how the image changed over the centuries.","Fortunate Isles","en/d/islas-afortunadas.html"],["A dawn god","Before becoming Itztlacoliuhqui, the god was Tlahuizcalpantecuhtli, linked to the planet Venus as the morning star.","Itztlacoliuhqui","en/d/itztlacoliuhqui.html"],["An ancestor of the Han dynasty","Legend made Liu Bang, founder of the Han dynasty, a son of the Red Emperor, giving his rule a divine origin.","Chidi (Red Emperor)","en/d/chidi-emperador-rojo.html"],["Protective tattoos","Ancient texts say the Yue people tattooed their bodies to protect themselves from these aquatic monsters.","Jiaolong","en/d/jiaolong.html"],["A plant with his name","The aquatic plant <em>Euryale ferox</em> bears the god&#x27;s name, and its edible seeds are widely eaten in Manipur.","Thangching","en/d/thangching.html"],["Bronze mirrors","Many of her scenes are known from Etruscan bronze mirrors with engraved reliefs on their backs.","Thesan","en/d/thesan.html"],["An eight-armed bell","The bronze bell has eight arms, with a small bell at each end. According to the description, it produces a majestic sound when shaken.","Three Heavenly Seals","en/d/tres-sellos-celestiales.html"],["Bone as a torch","In some legends, the arm bone of an unbaptised child, called mairu-beso, serves as a torch that lights the night and lulls the people of the house to sleep.","Mairu","en/d/mairu.html"],["A town named after her","The French town of Die preserves an echo of her cult, since its name comes from the Latin word for &#x27;goddess&#x27;.","Andarta","en/d/andarta.html"],["Greek name","The name comes from Greek and means roughly &#x27;going in two directions&#x27;, referring to its two heads.","Amphisbaena","en/d/anfisbena.html"],["Varied names","The giant is written in several forms, such as Thiazi, Thjazi or Thiassi, in anglicized spelling.","Þjazi","en/d/jazi.html"],["Admirers who stammered","According to the tale, young women of Ulster copied the quirks of their favourite heroes. Cúscraid&#x27;s followers took to stammering just like him.","Cúscraid","en/d/cuscraid.html"],["A snake with two meanings","Horapollo distinguishes between the snake that hides its tail under its body, which represents Aion, and the ouroboros, which devours its own tail and represents the cosmos.","Aion","en/d/eon.html"],["Meaning of the name","Hiʻiaka means carried egg: hii means to carry in the arms and aka means embryo.","Hiʻiaka","en/d/hiiaka.html"],["An Italian route","For some time it was thought that the story reached Russia through Italian sources, although the most likely origin is the French romance of Bevis of Hampton.","Bova Korolevich","en/d/bova-korolevich.html"],["One year, one day","Legend said that in Hyperborea the sun rose and set only once a year.","Hyperborea","en/d/hiperborea.html"],["Named after an official","The island was named after Samuel Pepys, Secretary to the British Admiralty, in whose honour Cowley christened it.","Pepys Island","en/d/isla-pepys.html"],["A vow of virginity","According to the sources, Orithyia had sworn to remain a virgin all her life, so she left no offspring.","Orithyia","en/d/oritia-q7102883.html"],["An unusual head","Despite his disorderly life, he was recognized for an unusual skull shape, which people considered remarkable under phrenology.","Jiang Ziwen","en/d/jiang-ziwen.html"],["The Tomb of Belus","Classical authors used this name for the Babylonian ziggurat. Strabo states that Xerxes demolished it.","Belus","en/d/belo-q4884837.html"],["Horse figurines","At healing sanctuaries of Apollo, such as Sainte-Sabine, small horse figurines associated with this god have been found.","Atepomarus","en/d/atepomaro.html"],["A shared sanctuary","Pausanias places on the Acrocorinth a sanctuary where Bia appears alongside Ananke, Necessity.","Bia","en/d/bia.html"],["A local companion","At Netherby he is invoked together with the local god Mogons, who shares one of his dedications.","Veteris","en/d/veteris.html"],["A cat born at sea","In some versions, the creature begins life as a kitten swept away by the waves, linking it to the aquatic realm.","Cath Palug","en/d/cath-palug.html"],["An anachronistic book","In her iconography she holds a book, although books did not exist in India until printing was introduced by the British colonisers.","Saraswati","en/d/sarasvati.html"],["A unique case","Edith Hamilton noted that in Greek mythology it is usually a god who loves an unwilling maiden, whereas here the roles are reversed.","Clytie","en/d/clitia.html"],["A misread name","For a time Bolon Tz&#x27;akab was thought to be the god&#x27;s name, but it is now known to mean «the many lords in the sequence», that is, a dynasty of rulers.","K'awiil","en/d/k-awiil.html"],["Two explanations of the name","Some scholars link the word to the root <em>mad</em>, «to rejoice», so that matsya would mean «the joyous one». The etymologist Yaska, by contrast, explained that fish are so called because they «revel in eating each other».","Matsya","en/d/matsia.html"],["A pair with the Sirin","The Alkonost almost always appears alongside the Sirin, a woman-headed bird of opposite character: the Sirin is linked to misfortune and the Alkonost to joy.","Alkonost","en/d/alkonost.html"],["Serpent that carried the sun","According to De León, the sun god Tohil was gripped in the serpent&#x27;s jaws so he could climb safely into the sky each day.","Gucumatz","en/d/gucumatz.html"],["A name that means fruit","Side means «pomegranate» in ancient Greek. Some scholars think her story is tied to the ripening cycle of this fruit.","Side","en/d/side.html"],["Children of the god","Ancient sources give different names for his children. A scholium lists Androcles, Crispus and Iocastus, while John Tzetzes offers another list.","Aeolus","en/d/eolo.html"],["Celestial city","According to tradition, the gods fixed his capital, Saubha, in the atmosphere, and some Hindus believe it can sometimes be seen in the sky.","Harishchandra","en/d/harishchandra.html"],["Etymology","The name derives from the Gaulish arduo-, &quot;height&quot;, and recurs in the names of several forested mountains in France, not only the modern Ardennes.","Arduinna","en/d/arduinna.html"],["A name with several readings","His name has been explained as a mix of Celtic and Phoenician words, and also as a possible loanword from a proto-Basque root.","Endovelicus","en/d/endovelico.html"],["A name from silence","Moniang means &quot;the silent girl&quot;, a name said to have been given because she did not cry at birth or during her first month of life.","Mazu (Matsu)","en/d/mazu-matsu.html"],["Very old","The earliest known depiction of the symbol dates from the reign of Pharaoh Den, of the First Dynasty.","Sema-tawy","en/d/sema-tauy.html"],["A memory without forgetting","According to the account, his soul forgot nothing even after death, which is why it remembered its earlier lives.","Aethalides","en/d/etalides.html"],["A disputed labour","Not all authors agree on the order of Heracles&#x27; labours. Some place the capture of the boar third.","Erymanthian Boar","en/d/jabali-de-erimanto.html"],["Priestly lists","The <em>indigitamenta</em> were catalogues of divine names that Roman priests used so they would not invoke the wrong god during rituals.","Agenoria","en/d/agenoria.html"],["A name fused with Astarte","At Pyrgi, a Phoenician and an Etruscan inscription name her as <em>Unialastres</em>, a blend of Uni and Astarte.","Uni","en/d/uni.html"],["Meaning of the name","Gram is an old Germanic adjective related to the word grim, meaning angry or fierce.","Gram","en/d/gram.html"],["A headland with a name of its own","According to the tale, Peucetius landed on the promontory of Iapygia, and the people of the area were called Peucetians after him.","Oenotrus","en/d/enotro.html"],["Three-legged crows","According to the stories, each sun he shot down turned into a three-legged crow.","Hou Yi","en/d/hou-yi.html"],["A noisy name","The name has been translated as &#x27;noisy&#x27;, possibly a reference to the roar of its waters.","Gjöll","en/d/gjoll.html"],["Thirty birds","Attar of Nishapur read the name as &quot;thirty birds&quot; in <em>The Conference of the Birds</em>, although the real etymology is different.","Simurgh","en/d/simurg.html"],["The most feared oath","Homer&#x27;s gods regarded an oath sworn by Styx as the gravest of all, and even Hera had to swear by it when she denied meddling in the Trojan War.","Styx","en/d/estigia.html"],["A temple without a trace","Texts place a temple of Wepset on the island of Biga, but no such structure has so far been located there.","Wepset","en/d/wepset.html"],["A stolen map","A map by Antonio de la Ascensión, showing California as an island, was seized by Dutch pirates on the return voyage to Acapulco. Shortly afterwards, Dutch maps appeared with the same island.","Island of California","en/d/isla-de-california.html"],["A name meaning debt","In Old Norse her name is associated with debt, lack or guilt. It shares its origin with modal verbs such as should in English and skulle in Swedish.","Skuld","en/d/skuld.html"],["A stone with cornucopias","The altar shows cornucopias on both sides, a detail that led some scholars to see the goddess as a deity of abundance.","Sandraudiga","en/d/sandraudiga.html"],["Origin of the word for king","The poem places in Konr the Young the origin of the Norse word konungr, meaning king.","Rígsþula","en/d/rigsula.html"],["Ancestor of the Memmii","Virgil describes him as the ancestral hero of the Memmii, a family of ancient Rome.","Mnestheus","en/d/mnesteo.html"],["A sun named dead egg","In the Harivamsa, Surya is called Martanda, &#x27;dead egg&#x27;, because his intense heat has disfigured his limbs and darkened his skin.","Saranyu","en/d/saraniu.html"],["Royal names","Some kings included her name in their titles, such as Meri-Maat, meaning Beloved of Maat.","Maat","en/d/maat.html"],["Meaning of his name","In Old Norse, his name means &#x27;The Waving One&#x27;. The sources spell it in several ways, such as Sveigder or Swegde.","Sveigðir","en/d/sveigir.html"],["A god with a beard of lichen","Runic songs give him a beard of lichen and eyebrows of moss, traits that link him to the Green Man archetype.","Tapio","en/d/tapio.html"],["A cave bearing his name","In Lempäälä there is a cave and a hollow called after Lempo, and local folklore says he was worshipped there before Christianity. Both were destroyed when a railway was built in the area.","Lempo","en/d/lempo.html"],["Burial place","Cumhall is reputedly buried within the grounds of Castleknock College, supposedly under a hill where an old water tower now stands.","Cumhall","en/d/cumhall.html"],["A name with meaning","Dóeltenga literally means &quot;chafer-tongue&quot; and in Old Irish it was used to describe backbiting or slander.","Dubthach Dóeltenga","en/d/dubthach-doeltenga.html"],["Cap of freedom","Newly freed slaves could go to the Terracina shrine and receive on their shaved heads the pileus, a hat that symbolized their liberty.","Feronia","en/d/feronia.html"],["Asteroid name","The asteroid 464 Megaira is named after this Erinys.","Megaera","en/d/megera.html"],["The birds of Rhiannon","The Adar Rhiannon express her enchanted power: they are far yet near, and both living and dead.","Rhiannon","en/d/rhiannon.html"],["A single known image","The only known depiction of Tychon is now in the Hatay Archaeology Museum in Turkey. It shows a boy with a semi-erect penis facing a kakodaimon, an evil spirit with the evil eye.","Tychon","en/d/ticon.html"],["A wedding with a wooden statue","To win back Hera, Zeus announced a wedding to Plataea and presented a wooden image as if it were the bride. The ruse is the best-known anecdote about this figure.","Plataea","en/d/platea.html"],["Uncertain etymology","A popular explanation links her name to pūta, meaning pure, and na, meaning not, but scholars do not consider it certain. Another connects it to a word for a hell associated with parents and children.","Pūtanā","en/d/putana.html"],["A visitor at Sunpu","In 1609 a blob of flesh about the size of a small child appeared in the courtyard of Sunpu Castle. Tokugawa Ieyasu ordered it driven out, and the servants took it into the mountains.","Nuppeppō","en/d/nuppeppo.html"],["Origin of the river&#x27;s name","According to the story attributed to Pseudo-Plutarch, the Meander river received its name from the god who threw himself into its waters after keeping his vow.","Meander","en/d/meandro.html"],["The Moon and the month in one word","The Greek <em>mēnē</em> means both the Moon and the lunar month, reflecting how the lunar cycle marked the passage of time.","Mene","en/d/menas.html"],["A horse-like nickname","He is sometimes given the epithet faxi, meaning &quot;the one with a mane&quot;, which is also a word for horse. Some scholars link it to the army of the dead said to roam Norway at Yule.","Guðmundr","en/d/gudmund.html"],["A name meaning «a hundred»","His name comes from a Greek word meaning «a hundred», although his role in the stories is very slight.","Hecaterus","en/d/hecatero.html"],["A name with meaning","The name Themis comes from the Greek verb <em>títhēmi</em>, &#x27;to put&#x27;, so it refers to what is established rather than to human authority.","Themis","en/d/temis.html"],["Heracles at the Crossroads","The only ancient story centred on Arete was told by the sophist Prodicus, and Christian writers such as Clement of Alexandria later retold it.","Arete","en/d/arete-q265823.html"],["Egyptian origin","The Egyptians claimed that Peteus had originally been Egyptian and later became a citizen of Athens.","Peteus","en/d/peteo.html"],["A god who changes shape","His power to take any appearance is the trait that defines him in the stories, and it reflects his role as a god of change.","Vertumnus","en/d/vertumno.html"],["A stone fallen from the sky","The god&#x27;s most revered object was a black, cone-shaped meteorite, kept in a temple on the Palatine Hill.","El-Gabal","en/d/el-gabal.html"],["Spring of Pegasus","The winged horse Pegasus was said to have made the spring of Aganippe burst forth with his hooves, as he did with Hippocrene on the same mountain.","Aganippe","en/d/aganipe.html"],["An asteroid with her name","The minor planet 465 Alekto is named in her honour, showing how her figure lives on well beyond literature.","Allecto","en/d/alecto.html"],["A butterfly named after her","The name Philotes was given to a genus of butterflies native to North America, which contains a single species, Philotes sonorensis.","Philotes","en/d/filotes.html"],["A pond without sun","The pond at Vallimalai, from which Valli drew water for Murugan, is still there and, according to tradition, receives no sunlight although it lies in the open.","Valli","en/d/valli.html"],["The death of Oleg","The motif of mocking the prophecy and dying from a horse&#x27;s skull has parallels in the Primary Chronicle, which describes the death of Oleg of Novgorod.","Örvar-Oddr","en/d/orvar-oddr.html"],["A crab&#x27;s name","The name Acragas likely comes from the Greek <em>karkinos</em>, meaning crab, because of the large population of crabs in its waters.","Acragas","en/d/acragante.html"],["Separate pieces","The toy consists of two papier-mâché pieces: a hollow body, and a head and neck that hang from a string and bob when moved.","Akabeko","en/d/akabeko.html"],["Many names","She goes by many names depending on the region, such as yuki-musume, &quot;snow daughter,&quot; or tsurara-onna, a name related to icicles.","Yuki-onna","en/d/yuki-onna.html"],["A disputed name","The name sadāparibhūta can be understood as «never despising» or as «always despised». According to some scholars, the first reading was popularised by Kumārajīva.","Bodhisattva Never Disparaging","en/d/bodhisattva-fukyo.html"],["An Oscan dedication","At Aeclanum, in Irpinia, an inscription in the Oscan language was found naming Fatuveís, the genitive of Fatui, which shows that the concept was Italic.","Fauna","en/d/fauna.html"],["A name of colour","Her name comes from the whitish sulphur waters of her spring and is linked to the Latin <em>albinus</em>, meaning &#x27;white&#x27;.","Albunea","en/d/albunea.html"],["A giant with a thousand heads","The Purusha Sukta describes the primordial being with a thousand heads and a thousand feet, sacrificed by the gods to create the world.","Purusha","en/d/purusha.html"],["A chain of destruction","At the Chennakeshava temple in Belur, Sharabha is shown devouring a lion, and in turn is destroyed by Gandaberunda in the final scene of a sequence of animals devouring one another.","Sharabha","en/d/sharabha.html"],["Blindness from birth","According to the Shiva Purana, Andhaka received his name because he was born blind, after Parvati&#x27;s game with Shiva&#x27;s eyes.","Andhaka","en/d/andhaka.html"],["Old Norse spelling","In Old Norse it was written Nástrǫnd, and in modern Icelandic the letter ǫ is replaced by ö, so the name becomes Náströnd.","Náströnd","en/d/nastrond.html"],["Disputed measurements","The Bhagavata Purana gives the serpent a length of one yojana, about 13 kilometres, while the English version of the same story puts it at about 8 miles.","Aghasura","en/d/aghasura.html"],["Buddha of the south","His cardinal direction is the south, and he is linked with earth and the spring season.","Ratnasambhava","en/d/ratnasambhava.html"],["A name that describes part of a ship","Prymnô means &quot;undermost&quot; or &quot;root&quot;, and her link to the stern places her in the part of the ship where the steering was handled.","Prymno","en/d/prymno.html"],["A name with several meanings","The queen&#x27;s name is associated with an Ionic word meaning &quot;sacred,&quot; though some sources link it to righteousness or virtue, and others to the god Ares.","Arete","en/d/arete.html"],["The path of Mag Mell","The sea route to the island is called Mag Mell, the &quot;Plain of Honey&quot;. It is the golden path the sun lays across the ocean.","Tír na nÓg","en/d/tir-na-nog.html"],["A constellation named for a kneeling pose","The Greek name Engonasin comes from a phrase meaning &#x27;on the knees&#x27;. It refers to Heracles&#x27; posture while praying to Zeus.","Alebion","en/d/alebion.html"],["A failed bribe","Xerxes tried to exploit the shared ancestry with the Greeks to bribe the Argives during his invasion of Greece, without success.","Perses (son of Perseus)","en/d/perses-hijo-de-perseo.html"],["A river with two names","The Jhelum, called Vitasta in Sanskrit, is the river the Greeks named Hydaspes. In classical accounts, a river of the Indus also took the name.","Hydaspes","en/d/hidaspes.html"],["An eagle as guide","Zacatzontli is accompanied by an eagle that serves as his guide on the road.","Zacatzontli","en/d/zacatzontli.html"],["Catfish in its waters","According to Pausanias, the Hermus was a place where catfish were raised, large freshwater fish.","Hermus","en/d/hermo.html"],["A day with his name","Macuiltochtli gave his name to one of the days of the 260-day calendar used in central Mexico. The coefficient five of that day was read as a sign of excess and lost control.","Macuiltochtli","en/d/macuiltochtli.html"],["A stag as a royal symbol","Its name means &quot;stag&quot;. In Anglo-Saxon finds, such as the sceptre from Sutton Hoo, stags are associated with royalty.","Heorot","en/d/heorot.html"],["Banned dogs","According to tradition, after Thasos was devoured by dogs, keeping dogs on Delos was prohibited.","Anius","en/d/anio.html"],["A sword that is a spirit","Motoori Norinaga, in his commentary on the <em>Kojiki</em>, read the sword as the spirit of the blade. Basil Hall Chamberlain, in his translation, noted that the text contains nothing that supports this reading.","Ame no Ohabari","en/d/ame-no-ohabari.html"],["A shared shrine","Archaeological remains of a small shrine on the island of Kos show that Hemera was worshipped alongside Helios, the sun god.","Hemera","en/d/hemera.html"],["A tomb in Megara","Pausanias places Autonoë&#x27;s tomb in the village of Ereneia, in the territory of Megara, where she withdrew after leaving Thebes.","Autonoë","en/d/autonoe.html"],["An ivory shoulder","According to Pindar, Pelops&#x27;s missing shoulder, eaten by Demeter during the feast, was replaced with ivory.","Clotho","en/d/cloto.html"],["A name for the drink","In Akkadian texts her name was used as a metonym for beer itself, written without the divine determinative.","Siris","en/d/siris.html"],["Imperial treasure","Emperor Wu of the Han dynasty declared the pixiu the &quot;Treasure of the Emperor.&quot; It is said that only the royal family could own one, and the ban lasted until the end of the Qing dynasty.","Pixiu","en/d/pixiu.html"],["The colour black","In Indo-Caribbean communities, Sangani Baba is associated with the colour black, which symbolises his fierce and protective nature.","Karuppu Samy","en/d/karuppu-samy.html"],["A boat tied to a fish","In the story, the giant fish&#x27;s horn serves as the mooring for the boat, and the serpent Shesha acts as the rope.","Vaivasvata Manu","en/d/vaivasvata-manu.html"],["A painting that saved a city","According to Pliny, the admiration for Protogenes&#x27; painting of Ialysus protected the Rhodians from an attack by Demetrius.","Ialysus","en/d/ialiso.html"],["An unflattering name","His name is usually translated as &quot;dung man&quot;, from the Greek word for dung. It may, however, originally have been linked to the care of cattle.","Copreus","en/d/copreo.html"],["Frogs and snakes","When the Nile floods receded in the Delta marshes, frogs and snakes appeared. For this reason, frogs were associated with the male deities of the Ogdoad and snakes with the female ones.","Naunet","en/d/naunet.html"],["A name that became an adjective","The name Gersemi gave rise to gersamr, a word used for anything precious or charming.","Gersemi","en/d/gersemi.html"],["Born from jewels","One version has him born from the jewels in Amaterasu&#x27;s hair, linking him to the goddess&#x27;s sacred objects.","Amatsuhikone","en/d/amatsuhikone.html"],["Three kings in one birth","In the Westcar Papyrus, Meskhenet appears after each of the three sons said to be born at once, and prophesies that they will become kings of Egypt: Userkaf, Sahure and Neferirkare Kakai.","Meskhenet","en/d/mesjenet.html"],["Stones turned into sheep","Legend credits Wong with the ability to transform stones into sheep, a feat that symbolises his power over nature.","Wong Tai Sin","en/d/wong-tai-sin.html"],["A revealing name","Henwen means &quot;Old White&quot;, a name that suggests an ancient, respected figure rather than an ordinary farm animal.","Henwen","en/d/henwen.html"],["A link to sacred fire","His name is related to the Old Irish word gobae, &#x27;smith&#x27;, and to the Lithuanian gabija, &#x27;sacred home fire&#x27;, suggesting an ancient link between the craft and the sacred.","Gofannon","en/d/gofannon.html"],["Three names in Southeast Asia","In the Laotian Phra Lak Phra Lam she is called Punukay, in the Thai Ramakien Benyakai, and in the Malay Hikayat Seri Rama, Devi Seri Jali.","Trijata","en/d/trijata.html"],["Origin of the name","The Latin word diēs comes from the Proto-Italic <em>dijēm, based on the Proto-Indo-European root </em>dyeu-, which denoted the daytime sky or the brightness of day. The same root gives the name of the Proto-Indo-European day god *Dyeus.","Dies","en/d/dies.html"],["A possible explanation","It has been suggested that these tales tried to describe the deadly effects of mosquitoes and malaria, whose transmission was not understood at the time.","Adze","en/d/adze.html"],["A name made of sand","In Sanskrit, Renuka means &#x27;fine grains of sand&#x27;, a simple name for a goddess who is so widely worshipped.","Renuka","en/d/renuka.html"],["A 100 euro coin","The Sculpture gold coin, issued in 2002, is the most recent coin with Providentia as its main motif.","Providentia","en/d/providentia.html"],["Twelve swift foals","According to Homer, the twelve foals born to Erichthonius&#x27;s mares were so light that they could run over ripe wheat without bending it.","Erichthonius of Dardania","en/d/erictonio-de-dardania.html"],["A sympathetic portrait","In the Harivamsha, Dantavakra recognises Krishna as God and wishes for his friendship, very different from his role as an enemy in other texts.","Dantavakra","en/d/dantavakra.html"],["A name that gets confused","Kui, the hero who invents music in another account, is often confused with a one-legged monster bearing the same name.","Ling Lun","en/d/ling-lun.html"],["A patronymic name","Ismenis is not a given name but a patronymic, derived from her father Ismenus.","Ismenis","en/d/ismenis.html"],["Two Cleopatras","Danaus had two daughters named Cleopatra, born to different mothers. Each belongs to a different strand of the story.","Cleopatra (daughter of Danaus)","en/d/cleopatra-hija-de-danao.html"],["A name that changed meaning","The adjective Edonic came to mean &quot;Thracian&quot; and, by extension, &quot;Dionysiac&quot; or &quot;Bacchantic&quot;, because of Thrace&#x27;s importance in the worship of Dionysus.","Edonus","en/d/edono.html"],["An asteroid named after him","The asteroid 8405 Asbolus keeps the centaur&#x27;s name in the sky. It belongs to a group of bodies that orbit between Jupiter and Neptune.","Asbolus","en/d/asbolus.html"],["The largest ship","The sources describe it as the greatest ship ever built, so heavy that not even the gods could move it without help.","Hringhorni","en/d/hringhorni.html"],["A statue at Olympia","A statue showing Ekecheiria crowning Iphitos was installed beside the Temple of Zeus in 460 BC.","Ekecheiria","en/d/ekecheiria.html"],["A cyclops&#x27;s mother","Homer says that Thoosa, mother of Polyphemus, was a daughter of Phorcys, though he does not name her mother.","Phorcys","en/d/forcis.html"],["A poisonous eye","According to the Cath Maige Tuired, the power of Balor&#x27;s eye came from the fumes of a magic potion brewed by his father&#x27;s druids.","Balor","en/d/balor.html"],["Crocodile god","He is described as a crocodile god of the Ganges, an origin far removed from the Japanese coast.","Konpira Gongen","en/d/konpira-gongen.html"],["A river&#x27;s name","Her name is the same as that of a mythical river, which suggests that she began as two different deities that later became one.","Anahita","en/d/anahita.html"],["An oracle with a catch","In the version recorded by Pausanias, Aegeus did not understand the Delphic answer about not opening the wineskin until reaching the height of Athens. It was King Pittheus who deciphered it.","Aegeus","en/d/egeo.html"],["A large family","Pseudo-Apollodorus names more than fifty sons of Priam, counting those by Hecuba and those by other women.","Pammon","en/d/pamon.html"],["A wounded eye","An old story tells that Midir lost an eye in a quarrel between groups of youths, and that Dian Cecht or Étaín healed it.","Midir","en/d/midir.html"],["An Italian adjective","Modern Italian keeps the adjective <em>egea</em>, meaning &#x27;Aegean&#x27;. Classical Latin had no equivalent.","Egea","en/d/egea.html"],["Night matches","For fun, these creatures play shinty on clear, bright nights.","Blue men of the Minch","en/d/hombres-azules-de-el-minch.html"],["A shared name","Asterion was also the name of Minos&#x27;s foster-father, so the monster and that figure shared the same name.","Minotaur","en/d/minotauro.html"],["Charm for mothers","Her name appeared on ivory knives used as charms to protect pregnant and nursing mothers.","Urethekau","en/d/urethekau.html"],["The scroll of life","He carries a scroll on which the lifespan of all living things is written. The scroll is sometimes identified as a Buddhist sutra.","Jurōjin","en/d/jurojin.html"],["Origin of cremation","According to tradition, Heracles&#x27;s gesture with Oeonus&#x27;s ashes gave rise to the Greek custom of cremation.","Oeonus","en/d/oneo.html"],["Aphrodisiac and remedy","The ancients believed marjoram was a strong aphrodisiac and that it could also cure snakebites.","Amaracus","en/d/amaraco.html"],["Well lit","Despite being underground, Youdu may be the best-lit area of Diyu, with torches and candles.","Youdu","en/d/youdu.html"],["A much-debated name","The origin of Yaldabaoth has generated decades of hypotheses. In 1974 Gershom Scholem refuted the etymology that dominated from the late 19th to the mid-20th century.","Yaldabaoth","en/d/yaldabaoth.html"],["A shared name","Podarces was also the original name of Priam, king of Troy. The same name thus belongs to two different figures connected with the war.","Podarces","en/d/podarces.html"],["Expression for the new moon","The Maya expression &#x27;the Moon has gone to her well&#x27; refers to the new moon.","Maya Moon Goddess","en/d/diosa-de-la-luna-maya.html"],["A name with a hand","Dutch <em>hand werpen</em> is related to Old English <em>hand</em> and <em>wearpan</em>, meaning to throw. Over time the verb evolved into today&#x27;s <em>warp</em>.","Druon Antigoon","en/d/druon-antigoon.html"],["Disguised as a shieldmaiden","During one of his Baltic campaigns, Frotho dressed as one of his own shieldmaidens to deceive the enemy.","Frotho I","en/d/frotho-i.html"],["A priest of Babylon","The colophon credits Kabti-ilâni-Marduk, a priest of Esagila, who presents himself only as the scribe of a dream in which Erra himself revealed the text.","Epic of Erra","en/d/epopeya-de-erra.html"],["River names","Several rivers in northern Spain bear similar names, such as the Navia and the Nalón, although their link to the goddess is not proven.","Navia","en/d/navia.html"],["Mother of the fourth sun","Cicero mentions a woman named Acantho who became the mother of the so-called fourth sun in Rhodes.","Acantha","en/d/acantha.html"],["A stone with a name","A holed stone in Ardudwy is still known as Llech Ronw, Gronw&#x27;s Stone.","Blodeuwedd","en/d/blodeuwedd.html"],["Meaning of the name","Maha means great and Gauri means purity, which gives the goddess her epithet.","Mahagauri","en/d/mahagauri.html"],["Protective figurine","A clay mould found in the Athenian agora, dating to the late 4th century BC, was used to make figurines of a woman lifting her dress to reveal male genitals, a gesture believed to be protective.","Aphroditus","en/d/afrodito.html"],["A god with a mast","His predecessor Babi was said to use his penis as the mast of a boat to carry the dead to the Egyptian paradise.","Isdes","en/d/isdes.html"],["A stolen thunderbolt","In Nonnus&#x27;s account, Typhon&#x27;s theft of Zeus&#x27;s thunderbolts sets off a cosmic battle between the two.","Pluto","en/d/pluto-q2099776.html"],["A disputed episode","The scene in which Krishna saves Draupadi with an endless supply of cloth appears in several versions of the text. The scholar Franklin Edgerton concluded that it is a later interpolation.","Krishna","en/d/krishna-q4241433.html"],["A name with scent","The feminine name Ilmr resembles the masculine <em>ilmr</em>, &quot;pleasant scent&quot;. According to Grimm, no other Norse deity is associated with smell.","Ilmr","en/d/ilmr.html"],["A name of fire","<em>Yan</em> literally means &quot;flame&quot;. K. C. Wu suggests the title may relate to using fire to clear fields in slash-and-burn agriculture.","Yandi","en/d/yandi.html"],["A relief with a dragon&#x27;s head","The ancient Arabic god Saḥar is depicted in reliefs with the symbol of a dragon&#x27;s head.","Shahar","en/d/sahar.html"],["A girl left behind","According to Apollodorus, Hermione was nine years old when her mother Helen eloped with Paris, and she was left behind in Sparta.","Hermione","en/d/hermione.html"],["Sandals and an umbrella","In the Mahabharata, Jamadagni becomes annoyed with the sun god Surya for the heat he causes. Surya appears to him as a Brahmin and gives him sandals and an umbrella to shield himself.","Jamadagni","en/d/yamadagni.html"],["Ancient origins","Some of these shrines are thought to date back to the 5th century or even to the pre-Christian era.","Tukh Manuk","en/d/tukh-manuk.html"],["A reversal of roles","Ovid reverses the Hesiodic genealogy and makes Hippodamas the father of Perimede, whom he calls Perimela.","Perimede","en/d/perimede.html"],["A stone that sounds","The Megarians claimed a stone near the acropolis kept Apollo&#x27;s lyre, and that striking it with a small pebble made it sound like an instrument.","Alcathous","en/d/alcatoo.html"],["Panacris","Panacris was one of the nymphs of Dicte, described as a bee that gave sweet honey to the infant Zeus.","Melissae","en/d/melisas.html"],["Hinomisaki Shrine","Legend credits this deity with founding Hinomisaki Shrine.","Ame no Fuyukinu","en/d/ame-no-fuyukinu.html"],["Storks carrying souls","In Slavic tradition, storks brought to earth the souls that had waited in Vyrai before birth.","Iriy","en/d/iriy.html"],["A translation with consequences","Livius Andronicus translated Homer&#x27;s Odyssey into Latin and used the word Camena to render Μοῦσα. This created the link between the Camenae and the Muses.","Camenae","en/d/camenas.html"],["A name that lasts","The region of Magnesia in Thessaly was named after the hero, according to the tradition recorded in the reference text.","Magnes","en/d/magnes-q10323051.html"],["A name on the map","Strabo suggests that Cebriones gave his name to the region of Cebrenia, its city and the Cebreni people.","Cebriones","en/d/cebriones.html"],["Twelve sisters","Acte was one of twelve Horae, each linked to a particular moment of the day or of nature.","Acte","en/d/acte.html"],["An island dedicated to goddesses","According to Cicero, in his Against Verres, the entire island of Sicily was dedicated to Ceres and Libera, the goddesses who, in that account, first brought cereal crops to the island.","Proserpina","en/d/proserpina.html"],["A ritual punishment","According to tradition, Hippolyta was punished for refusing to take part in the yearly dance in honour of Artemis.","Otrera","en/d/otrera.html"],["A necessary funeral","In the <em>Aeneid</em>, Misenus&#x27;s burial is a precondition for Aeneas to descend to the Underworld, showing how central funeral ritual was.","Misenus","en/d/miseno.html"],["A name in the sea","The Nereid&#x27;s name was applied to a genus of mud shrimp, Callianassa, and to their family, Callianassidae.","Callianassa","en/d/calinasa.html"],["A mace that speaks","Ninurta&#x27;s mace Sharur could speak and, according to some accounts, transform into a winged lion.","Ninurta","en/d/ninurta.html"],["Name of colour","His name means &#x27;black&#x27; or &#x27;dark&#x27;, which is why he is often depicted with dark or blue skin, although tradition describes him as dark-complexioned.","Krishna","en/d/krishna.html"],["Lady of Byblos","The Egyptians called Hathor the “Lady of Byblos”, a reflection of the Phoenician goddess’s name.","Baaltis (Baalat Gebal)","en/d/baaltis-baalat-gebal.html"],["Three associated animals","He was linked to the baboon, regarded by the Egyptians as a lunar animal, and in other depictions he appears as a bull or double bull, a symbol of fertility.","Khonsu","en/d/jonsu.html"],["A Jupiter called Bil","In Mandaean cosmology, Jupiter is named Bil, which derives from Bel.","Bel","en/d/bel.html"],["A name that screams","His name has been translated as &#x27;screamer&#x27; and relates to the Old Norse verb <em>gjalla</em>, &#x27;to scream, yell&#x27;.","Gilling","en/d/gilling.html"],["Festival of the heat","The Neptunalia fell during the peak of summer, when freshwater sources were at their lowest. Participants drank spring water and wine under branch huts.","Neptune","en/d/neptuno.html"],["From river to heraldry","Fleeces used to catch gold flakes in Georgia are thought to have inspired the myth, which later entered the region&#x27;s heraldry.","Golden Fleece","en/d/vellocino-de-oro.html"],["The stolen earrings","According to tradition, the asura Naraka stole a pair of earrings from her. Krishna, together with his consort Satyabhama, returned them after slaying the asura.","Aditi","en/d/aditi.html"],["Shivaji&#x27;s sword","According to local tradition, Bhavani gave the Chandrahasa sword to Shivaji, founder of the Maratha Empire, for his battle against evil.","Bhavani","en/d/bhavani.html"],["Three thousand sisters","The Oceanids were said to be some three thousand daughters of Oceanus and Tethys, which makes Acaste one of a vast family of nymphs.","Acaste","en/d/acaste.html"],["An island with his name","Stephanus of Byzantium writes that an island of Euboea was named Amarynthus after this hunter.","Amarynthus","en/d/amarinto.html"],["Another island with the same name","Calypso was also said to have lived on an island called Aeaea, located in the straits between Italy and Sicily.","Aeaea","en/d/eea.html"],["Name variants","Manuscripts of the Prose Edda record the name in different forms, such as Býleistr, Blýleistr or Býleiptr.","Býleistr","en/d/byleistr.html"],["A gift from dwarves","Draupnir was made by dwarves as part of a wager set up by Loki, who claimed they could not outdo the Sons of Ivaldi.","Draupnir","en/d/draupnir.html"],["Inisfail","The Tuatha Dé Danann were said to have called Ireland Inisfail, &#x27;island of Fáil&#x27;, after the stone, so Fáil became another name for the island.","Lia Fáil","en/d/lia-fail.html"],["A pine and some violets","In some versions, Attis castrates himself under a pine tree and violets grow from his blood.","Attis","en/d/atis.html"],["A misread name","For a long time the god’s hieroglyphs were read as Anti or Anty, a reading that later studies have shown to be incorrect.","Nemty","en/d/nemty.html"],["Sacred bird","The peafowl was her sacred animal, and in some versions Juno had Argus in her service, along with Iris, the messenger she shared with Jupiter.","Juno","en/d/juno.html"],["A constellation named after an inventor","Some ancient authors thought the constellation Auriga was the starry image of Trochilus, with which he was honored for his invention.","Trochilus","en/d/trocilo.html"],["A creature in the cup","According to the story, Deichtine drank from a cup and a tiny creature leapt from it into her mouth, an episode that foreshadows her pregnancy by Lug.","Deichtine","en/d/deichtine.html"],["The bell pond","Lake Kanegabuchi, whose name means &quot;bell abyss&quot;, lies in Niiza and is linked to the legend of the bell thief.","Noderabō","en/d/noderabo.html"],["Names of the Sun","In the Alvíssmál, the dwarf Alvíss gives different names for the Sun depending on the world: sól for humans, sunna for the gods and fagrahvél, &#x27;lovely wheel&#x27;, for the elves.","Sól","en/d/sol.html"],["A name that is an animal","Crius means &quot;ram&quot; in Greek. Nonnus linked him with the constellation Aries, although the myth does not place him in the sky.","Crius","en/d/crio.html"],["A sea of fire","Hans Christian Andersen mentions &quot;Surt&#x27;s sea of fire&quot; in his fairy tale &quot;The Marsh King&#x27;s Daughter&quot;.","Muspelheim","en/d/muspelheim.html"],["A goddess with several mothers","Cicero distinguishes up to five Minervas, each with different parents, and only one of them is born of Jupiter.","Minerva","en/d/minerva.html"],["Meaning of the name","Euroswydd is interpreted as &quot;the one of the gilded seat,&quot; from Welsh <em>euro</em>, &quot;to gild,&quot; and <em>swydd</em>, &quot;seat, position, office.&quot;","Euroswydd","en/d/euroswydd.html"],["Two spellings","The Second Vatican Mythographer uses the Latinized form Prophasis, while the Greek original is written Πρόφασις.","Prophasis","en/d/profasis.html"],["The horses of Zeus","The horses Zeus gave to Tros were so swift that, according to the myth, they could run over water and over standing grain. Later, Diomedes yoked them to his chariot.","Tros","en/d/tros.html"],["A handle that is too short","In the story of its forging, Mjölnir&#x27;s handle is a little short and not set at a right angle to the head, because it was meant to be used with one hand.","Mjölnir","en/d/mjolnir.html"],["Red beer","To stop Sekhmet, Ra had beer dyed with ámbar until it looked like blood, and the goddess, intoxicated, stopped her killing.","Ra","en/d/ra.html"],["Father of metal","Some sources credit him with inventing metal weapons and forging the first swords of bronze and copper.","Chiyou","en/d/chi-you.html"],["Calauria, today Poros","According to Plutarch, the island of Calauria was once called Eirene. It is today identified with Poros, at the entrance to the Saronic Gulf, off the coast of Argolis.","Melantheia","en/d/melantea.html"],["A name with several forms","The word appears as tékmor or tékmar in Greek, two variants of the same term meaning end, limit or goal.","Tecmor","en/d/tecmor.html"],["A city named after him","The city of Cyzicus, on the shore of the Propontis, was named after the king according to the tradition recorded in the texts.","Cyzicus","en/d/cicico.html"],["Coins with togas","On one coin, a Roman holds out his toga to catch the coins poured by Liberalitas, while the emperor watches from an elevated seat.","Liberalitas","en/d/liberalitas.html"],["A temple at Samye","A chapel within the Samye complex bears his name, Pekar lha khang, or Pekar Temple.","Gyalpo Pehar","en/d/gyalpo-pehar.html"],["Mother of Cows","One of her titles is Govu māte, &#x27;Mother of Cows&#x27;, which links her to the care of livestock.","Māra","en/d/mara-q1302527.html"],["Sweet hope","Pindar gives Elpis the adjective &quot;sweet&quot;, showing that the Greek view of her was not only negative.","Elpis","en/d/elpis.html"],["Name of a people","According to the tradition, the inhabitants of the Gargano region took their name, Daunians, in honor of Daunus.","Daunus","en/d/daunio.html"],["A name with meaning","Marpesia is usually translated as &quot;snatcher&quot;, according to the sources that record her name.","Marpesia","en/d/marpesia.html"],["Ma and Dionysus","According to Stephanus of Byzantium, Ma raised the infant Dionysus and, to hide his identity from Hera, said he was the son of Ares. This gave rise to the epithet &quot;Masaris&quot;.","Ma","en/d/ma.html"],["An inscription on a statuette","His name appears on the back of a Neo-Assyrian statuette of Pazuzu, now in the Louvre, where the demon presents himself as the son of Hanpa.","Hanbi","en/d/hanbi.html"],["A cup in the sky","According to the myth, the wine bowl was placed among the stars and gave its name to the constellation Crater.","Demophon","en/d/demofonte-q3705305.html"],["A ship in a field","Scholars Joseph S. Hopkins and Haukur Þorgeirsson connect Fólkvangr and Sessrúmnir with the stone ships of Scandinavian burial sites, an image that may reflect a ship in a field of the dead.","Fólkvangr","en/d/folkvangr.html"],["A melody that sends you to sleep","The song sung by Aengus and Caer put anyone who heard it to sleep for three days and three nights.","Aengus","en/d/angus.html"],["Seven heads","In Zulu folklore from Howick Falls, the Inkanyamba is described as a creature with seven heads.","Inkanyamba","en/d/inkanyamba.html"],["The mirror of wisdom","His wisdom is described as a mirror that reflects a rose or a bloodstained dagger exactly as they are, without being affected by either.","Akshobhya","en/d/akshobhya.html"],["Wooden figures","Two wooden figures found in the Braak bog in Germany depict a nude man and woman. Some scholars think they may be connected to the tradition of Ask and Embla&#x27;s creation.","Ask and Embla","en/d/ask-y-embla-q16724339.html"],["A reported case","Swift Runner, a Plains Cree trapper from Alberta, killed and ate his wife and five surviving children in 1878, even though food supplies were nearby. He confessed and was executed.","Wendigo","en/d/wendigo.html"],["Another name","Dionysius of Halicarnassus calls him Aegestus, a name different from the one usually used for this character.","Lausus","en/d/lauso-q3219705.html"],["A name with a bear","Bjarki means &#x27;little warrior&#x27; and is tied to the bear, which also appears in his father&#x27;s name, Björn.","Bödvar Bjarki","en/d/bodvar-bjarki.html"],["The case of Linus","Pausanias says Apollo sent Poine to punish the Argives for the death of Linus. The legend presents her as a threat to children until Coroebus killed her.","Poine","en/d/poine.html"],["Athenian oath","Athenians swore by the formula &quot;in the name of the Unknown God&quot;, a sign of how common this cult was in everyday life.","Unknown God","en/d/dios-desconocido.html"],["Emblem of Chios","The ancient city-state of Chios used the sphinx as its emblem on seals and on the obverse of its coins.","Sphinx","en/d/esfinge.html"],["Two Dionysuses","The historian Diodorus Siculus claims there were two gods named Dionysus, an older one, son of Zeus and Persephone, and a younger one, son of Zeus and Semele, and that people confused them because they shared a name.","Zagreus","en/d/zagreo.html"],["Impossible tasks","To get rid of him, people give him jobs he cannot finish, such as collecting water in a perforated basket. Having failed, he leaves in shame.","Trastolillu","en/d/trastolillu.html"],["Link to Jain Ambika","Isakki corresponds to the Jain yakshini Ambika, who is usually shown with one or two children beneath a tree.","Isakki","en/d/isakki.html"],["A name in Beowulf","The Old English poem Beowulf uses the form frea Ingwina to refer to the Danish king Hroðgar, a close echo of the same name.","Ingunar-Freyr","en/d/ingunar-freyr.html"],["The Tower of the Winds","In an octagonal tower in Athens, built in the Roman era, Notus is carved as a young man pouring water from a jar. It is one of the best-known depictions of this god.","Notus","en/d/notos.html"],["A Homeric remedy","In the Odyssey, Odysseus resists Circe&#x27;s enchantments thanks to the herb moly, which some interpretations link to sophrosyne.","Sophrosyne","en/d/sofrosina.html"],["A name about wandering","Foito comes from the Greek word foitos, which means wandering, especially of the mind.","Samian Sibyl","en/d/sibila-samia.html"],["A mother who served Artemis","According to Callimachus, as a young woman Anticlea served the goddess Artemis and joined her in the hunt, carrying a bow and quiver.","Anticlea","en/d/anticlea.html"],["A ten-day vow","The <em>Dashama Vrata</em> is observed in Gujarat and Diu, and in some cases also in the month of Chaitra, in March.","Momai","en/d/momai.html"],["A name that lasts","According to the legend, the place where Bianna vanished took her name, and it later gave rise to the Allobroges&#x27; capital.","Bianna","en/d/bianna.html"],["A centuries-old temple","Hong Kong&#x27;s first Shing Wong temple was built in 1877. It has undergone renovations and name changes, but it still stands.","City God","en/d/divinidad-de-ciudad.html"],["An easily confused name","Melanippe, Abas&#x27;s wife, is a little-known figure and should not be confused with other women of the same name in Greek myth, such as Poseidon&#x27;s lover.","Abas","en/d/abante.html"],["Train visits","He reportedly visited Sendai Station almost daily and rode trains to Shiroishi and Shirakawa, though sometimes he could not pay the fare when getting off.","Sendai Shirō","en/d/sendai-shiro.html"],["A successful prophecy","According to <em>Duxing</em>, written by Zeng Minxing in the Song dynasty, General Di Qing consulted He Xiangu before suppressing a rebellion. She predicted that the enemy would flee before any battle took place, and that is what happened.","He Xiangu","en/d/he-xiangu.html"],["An otter with a hound&#x27;s name","Dobhar-chú translates as water hound. In modern Irish, dobharchú is the word for otter, while cú means hound.","Dobhar-chú","en/d/dobhar-chu.html"],["The Telchines","Before the Heliadae, Rhodes was inhabited by the Telchines, dog-headed daimones believed to control rain and other sky phenomena.","Ochimus","en/d/oquimo.html"],["A chrysalis with snakes","In one of his forms, Chi Songzi appears as a silkworm chrysalis, with a red snake and a green snake coming out of his ears.","Yu Shi","en/d/yu-shi.html"],["A possible name","Some scholars propose that the goddess was called Njörun, a name that appears in some skaldic kennings and in the Nafnaþulur appended to the Prose Edda.","Sister-wife of Njörðr","en/d/hermana-esposa-de-njorr.html"],["A forest from a walking stick","According to the myth, the wooden club Kuafu carried grew into the Deng Forest of peach trees.","Kuafu","en/d/kuafu.html"],["A game named after it","The cucaña pole game, in which players climb a greased pole, takes its name from this imaginary land.","Cockaigne","en/d/pais-de-cucana.html"],["A plant with a mythological name","The heliotrope takes its name from Clytia, who according to the story was transformed into this flower by turning toward the sun.","Orchamus","en/d/orcamo.html"],["A possible Egyptian name","The name may derive from pꜣ-rwtj, meaning the high doors of a temple, one of the titles of the Egyptian king.","Proteus","en/d/proteo-q7251707.html"],["Cursed sword","Dáinsleif, Högni&#x27;s sword, caused wounds that never healed and had to kill a man each time it was drawn.","Hjaðningavíg","en/d/hjaningavig.html"],["Rivers flowing southeast","Because the sky pillar was damaged, the Chinese land tilted southeast, and its rivers flow in that direction.","Buzhou","en/d/buzhou.html"],["Untranslated name","Hyginus left the name in its Latinized Greek form, unlike other abstractions that he did translate into Latin.","Epiphron","en/d/epifron.html"],["A city named after a son","Idrias was named after Idrieus, another son of Car. Carian cities often took the names of their legendary founders.","Car","en/d/car.html"],["The Medes","According to tradition, her son Medus gave his name to the region of Media. Herodotus also links Medea to the Aryans who came to be called Medes.","Medea","en/d/medea.html"],["Scamander&#x27;s daughters","Apollodorus names Callirrhoe and Strymo as daughters of Scamander. Both married key figures of the Trojan line: Tros and Laomedon.","Idaea","en/d/idea-q2007963.html"],["A journey there and back","According to a story from the River Honddu, a water horse carried a man from a Roman camp near Brecon to the Towy. Three days later it brought him back, battered after being dragged through mire, brambles and briars.","Ceffyl Dŵr","en/d/ceffyl-dwr.html"],["Cloth from the land of Tait","According to funerary texts, mummy bandages came from the &quot;land of Tait&quot;, a town named after the goddess, of which she was the protective deity.","Tait","en/d/tait.html"],["An invented tale","&#x27;The Soul Cages&#x27;, with its male merrow, turned out to be fiction based on a German tale, although Keightley claimed similar stories circulated in Cork and Wicklow.","Merrow","en/d/merrow.html"],["A name with a European echo","The term ribhu is related to European words for work, such as Latin <em>labor</em>.","Ribhus","en/d/ribhus.html"],["A cauldron that never empties","It is one of the Four Treasures of the Tuatha Dé Danann. It was said that no assembly left unsatisfied after feeding from it.","The Dagda","en/d/dagda.html"],["A name meaning affliction","In Irish, Cessair is linked to words meaning lament or affliction, a fitting name for a figure who arrives with her companions in a hard new land.","Cessair","en/d/cessair.html"],["First appearance on a coin","Securitas first appears on a coin in 62 AD and then becomes a common coin motif in the following centuries.","Securitas","en/d/securitas.html"],["A etymology from Plato","In the Cratylus, Plato proposes a folk etymology linking the name of Zeus to life, although modern scholars do not accept it.","Zeus","en/d/zeus.html"],["Music-hall name","The colonial name may come from an English music-hall song heard in Vernon in 1924, although a version of the song already appeared in a Vancouver newspaper in 1912.","Ogopogo","en/d/ogopogo.html"],["A kitten or a ball of cotton","Accounts describe it as something soft, like a kitten or a wad of cotton, that is felt wrapping around a person&#x27;s legs without usually being seen.","Ashimagari","en/d/ashimagari.html"],["The Milky Way","According to Anania Shirakatsi, the Milky Way formed when Vahagn dropped the straw he had stolen. In Armenian it is popularly known as &#x27;the way of the straw-thief&#x27;.","Vahagn","en/d/vahagn.html"],["A boy turned into a serpent","In the legend, the child who gives the deity his name turns into a serpent in the middle of battle, and this prodigy is what routs the Arcadian army.","Sosipolis","en/d/sosipolis.html"],["Boxing champion","At the funeral games held in Thebes for Oedipus, Mecisteus won every boxing contest.","Mecisteus","en/d/mecisteo.html"],["A revealing name","The name Nyctimene means &#x27;of the night&#x27;, which fits her fate of living away from the light of day.","Epopeus","en/d/epopeo-q2365262.html"],["A pit with a thousand objects","At Cupra Marittima excavators uncovered a votive pit cut into bedrock containing about a thousand objects, including incense burners and miniature vessels.","Cupra","en/d/cupra.html"],["Two genealogies","The <em>Mahabharata</em> and the <em>Puranas</em> give Pratipa different parents. Ancient authors did not agree on his lineage.","Pratipa","en/d/pratipa.html"],["The French dahu","France has an equivalent imaginary animal, the dahu, and the <em>chasse au dahu</em> is a traditional joke.","Gamusino","en/d/gamusino.html"],["An enigmatic emblem","The emblem she wears on her head is described as a flower, a star, or a rosette beneath a crescent, and its exact meaning remains unclear.","Seshat","en/d/seshat.html"],["An annoying fly","According to the story, a fly stung Brokkr while he worked the bellows, and it is sometimes thought to have been Loki in disguise.","Eitri","en/d/eitri.html"],["An inscription on a liver","The Piacenza Liver, a bronze model used in divination, bears the inscription &quot;Uni Mae,&quot; which is linked to Juno.","Maia","en/d/maia.html"],["A name with a vessel","His character combines the sign for a tripod ritual vessel with three hollow legs, typical of the Bronze Age in northern China. David Hawkes linked the name to ancient Chinese ceramic technology.","Zhurong","en/d/zhu-rong.html"],["Female serpent","Her name is often translated as woman serpent, although its etymology suggests female serpent, since the word cihuatl marks the animal&#x27;s sex.","Cihuacoatl","en/d/cihuacoatl-q2888346.html"],["A golden eagle","Xenophon describes a golden eagle as the standard of the Achaemenids, a different emblem from the one tradition attributes to the blacksmith Kaveh.","Derafsh Kaviani","en/d/derafsh-kaviani.html"],["A sacred grove","The sanctuary included a circular monument that was probably used for dances or processions around a grove of sacred trees.","Hylates","en/d/hilates.html"],["A river that makes you forget","According to the legend, the people of Achaea bathed in the waters of the Selemnus to rid themselves of erotic passions.","Selemnus","en/d/selemno.html"],["Chandigarh","The city of Chandigarh, joint capital of Punjab and Haryana, takes its name from the goddess.","Chandi","en/d/chandi.html"],["Erda in Wagner","Richard Wagner brought her back in <em>Das Rheingold</em> under the name Erda, a figure who knows the fate of the gods.","Jörð","en/d/jor.html"],["A widow goddess","According to Varro&#x27;s quotation, Fulgora was listed among divinities described as widows, alongside Populonia and Rumina.","Fulgora","en/d/fulgora.html"],["A drum against the worm","Mackerle built a motorised device that thumped the ground, inspired by the sandworms of the novel <em>Dune</em>, and even used small explosions to try to bring the creature to the surface.","Aka Allghoi Khorhoi","en/d/aka-allghoi-khorhoi.html"],["Origin of the pilot fish","According to the tale, the sailor Pompilus was transformed into a pilot fish, a marine species known today as Naucrates ductor.","Ocyrhoë","en/d/ocirroe-q6048794.html"],["Animal forms","In statuary they often have animal heads, and many have a bird as their vahana, or animal vehicle. They are associated with the power of flight.","Yogini","en/d/yogini.html"],["A city named after her","The city of Bura in Achaea bears the name of this heroine, whom tradition presents as its eponym.","Bura","en/d/bura.html"],["An Odin disguise","The name Grímnir suggests &#x27;mask&#x27; or &#x27;hood&#x27;, one of the many guises in which Odin travels the world.","Geirröð","en/d/geirro.html"],["A book and a brush","In Taoist painting and sculpture she holds a book and a brush, reflecting the Chinese custom of recording newborns in family lineage records.","Songzi Niangniang","en/d/songzi-niangniang.html"],["Samadhi fire","His main weapon is the <em>True Samādhi Fire</em>, which ordinary water cannot extinguish. Samādhi is a Sanskrit word meaning concentration.","Red Boy","en/d/nino-rojo.html"],["An ignored ancestor","The Moktsevay Kartlisay chronicle and the cleric Arsen of Iqalto actively ignored and omitted him, alongside the Ancient Hebrews.","Kartlos","en/d/kartlos.html"],["A name with rice","His name is interpreted as &quot;rice ear advancing.&quot; Its first character now means &quot;flame&quot; in modern Japanese.","Honosusori","en/d/honosusori.html"],["Three gods, three stars","Each god personifies a celestial body: Jupiter, Mizar and Canopus.","Fu (Fuxing)","en/d/fu-fuxing.html"],["An unusual cap","His only certain depiction shows him wearing a cap of bear or cat hide, a detail that sets him apart from his possible Roman counterpart.","Selvans","en/d/selvans.html"],["Raised beds","In some rural areas, beds are raised about two metres on bricks to keep the tikoloshe away.","Tokoloshe","en/d/tikoloshe.html"],["A Sabine name","Aulus Gellius claimed that the goddess&#x27;s name was a Sabine word meaning strength and fortitude.","Nerio","en/d/nerio.html"],["A kinship name","“Amm” means paternal uncle in Arabic, so his name points to a family bond rather than a divine title.","ʿAmm","en/d/amm.html"],["A 1990 video game","Damocles is a 16-bit video game from 1990 in which the player tries to stop the comet Damocles from destroying a planet.","Damocles","en/d/damocles.html"],["Alternative names","Ivaldi also appears as Ölvaldi or Allvaldi, two parallel forms of the name that may refer to the same giant.","Ivaldi","en/d/ivaldi.html"],["A Swedish word","In modern Swedish, the word öde, meaning fate, derives from the same root as the name Urðr.","Urðr","en/d/urr.html"],["An island with a name","According to the account, the island where she was buried bears the name of Prochyte, the kinswoman of Aeneas to whom Procas&#x27;s name is linked.","Procas","en/d/procas.html"],["A lover of two gods","Some accounts state that Philonis was loved by Apollo and Hermes, and that she bore Philammon and Autolycus to them respectively.","Philonis","en/d/filonis.html"],["Gwri of the golden hair","Teyrnon named the boy Gwri Wallt Euryn, &#x27;Gwri of the golden hair&#x27;, because his hair was as yellow as gold.","Pwyll Pen Annwn","en/d/pwyll-pen-annwn.html"],["Two fathers","Hyginus gives Dryas both Ares and Iapetus as his father, the latter probably an epithet of Ares himself.","Dryas","en/d/dryas.html"],["A name among a thousand","The Rudrayamala-Uttaratantra lists Kaushiki among the thousand names of Kumari, and simply reciting them is said to bring spiritual rewards even without formal worship.","Kaushiki","en/d/kaushiki.html"],["A dragon in the sky in 1091","Prince Vsevolod Yaroslavich reportedly saw a dragon-meteorite fall near Vyshgorod, as recorded in the chronicle. Today it is read as a meteorite fall.","Fiery serpent","en/d/serpiente-de-fuego.html"],["A name in pop music","Paul Simon mentions Olorun in the song “The Rhythm of the Saints,” from his 1990 album of the same name.","Olorun","en/d/olorun.html"],["Grannus and Mars","At Chassenon he appears as Mars Grannus Victor, the only inscription linking him to Mars, alongside the local goddess Cobrandia.","Grannus","en/d/grannus.html"],["Three eyes","Because of his divine origin he is born with a third eye, and he is therefore also known as Sanyan Lingguan, the Three-Eyed Official.","Emperor Huaguang","en/d/emperador-huaguang.html"],["A royal wedding","According to the tradition, the first royal wedding in Cusco joined Sinchi Roca, the couple&#x27;s 20-year-old son, with Mama Coca, daughter of the lord of Sannon.","Mama Ocllo","en/d/mama-ocllo.html"],["The Nine Ways","According to the tradition, Phyllis returned nine times to the shore to wait for Demophon, and the place takes its name from this.","Phyllis","en/d/filis.html"],["Growing beside his brother","According to Porphyry, Eros grew when Anteros was near and shrank back when he went away.","Anteros","en/d/anteros.html"],["A comic catchphrase","The exclamation &quot;By Toutatis!&quot; is one of the most recognisable phrases in the Asterix comics.","Teutates","en/d/teutates.html"],["A very particular diet","The Irvings said they left biscuits, chocolates and bananas on a tray hung from the ceiling, and that Gef took them when he thought no one was watching.","Gef","en/d/gef.html"],["Devotional seals","On Old Babylonian cylinder seals from Sippar appears the formula &quot;servant of Enki and Damgalnuna&quot;, showing the joint devotion to the divine couple.","Damgalnuna (Damkina)","en/d/damgalnuna-damkina.html"],["An altar underground","According to legend, the altar was found about 6 metres down while foundations were being dug. After three days of games, it was buried again.","Dis Pater","en/d/dis-pater.html"],["Epithets of the great gods","The names Domiduca and Domiducus appear as epithets of Jupiter and Juno, showing that their domestic role was also associated with the chief deities.","Domiduca","en/d/domiduca.html"],["A refuge refused","When Alcon demanded his son back, the Chalcidians of Euboea refused to hand him over, according to the tradition.","Phalerus","en/d/falero.html"],["A club inherited","Theseus kept Periphetes&#x27; club for himself, a gesture that recalls Heracles and the skin of the Nemean lion.","Periphetes","en/d/periphetes.html"],["Classic illustration","Toriyama Sekien drew her in the third volume of his <em>Konjaku Gazu Zoku Hyakki</em>.","Amanozako","en/d/amanozako.html"],["A drum against the monster","According to the story, the drummer Kanroku survived by beating his drum with all his strength until his boat broke free from the akugyo&#x27;s back.","Akugyo","en/d/akugyo.html"],["A toy for Zeus","According to Apollonius, Adrasteia made the infant Zeus a small ball, in Greek <em>sphaira</em>.","Adrasteia","en/d/adrastea.html"],["A shield against demons","According to the Testament of Solomon, the two interlaced triangles confused the demons, so they could not harm the king.","Seal of Solomon","en/d/sello-de-salomon.html"],["Bamboo baskets","To drive it away, some families hung or placed bamboo baskets beside their doors.","Hitotsume-kozō","en/d/hitotsume-kozo.html"],["A sacred number","Anu was associated with the number 60, a sacred figure for the Sumerians, and the numeral could stand for his name.","Anu","en/d/anu.html"],["A lady of the marshes","In a Slovenian tale, Mokoška is a witch who lives in a castle surrounded by wet, marshy land. She helped people in exchange for gifts, but also stole children.","Mokosh","en/d/mokos.html"],["Origin of the name","The root <em>īś</em> means to be powerful or master, and <em>vara</em> adds the idea of what is most excellent or valuable.","Ishvari","en/d/ishvari.html"],["The ao","His right foot rests on an ao, a giant turtle or fish. The expression standing alone on the ao&#x27;s head means coming first in an examination.","Kui Xing","en/d/kui-xing.html"],["A name of fire","Her name means &quot;glowing embers&quot;, and her daughters bear names related to fire and embers.","Glöð","en/d/glod.html"],["Food offerings","Villagers were said to feed the cuélebre with boroña and rye bread so it would not attack their livestock.","Cuélebre","en/d/cuelebre.html"],["Disputed numbers","Sources disagree on how many sons he had: twelve in the most common list and twenty according to Diodorus Siculus, who gives no names.","Hippocoön","en/d/hipocoonte.html"],["Protective head","Brân&#x27;s head, which keeps speaking during the journey, was buried on the White Hill and was believed to protect against foreign invasion.","Manawydan fab Llŷr","en/d/manawydan-fab-llyr.html"],["Names of the heads","According to the Mahabharata, each head had its own name: Somapītha, Surāpītha and Annāda, according to what it drank or ate.","Trishiras","en/d/trisiras.html"],["A name with Phoenician roots","Elissa, her alternative name, seems related to El, the Phoenician creator god. In Greek it was rendered as Theiossô.","Dido","en/d/dido.html"],["A chariot with one wheel","His chariot had wings and a single wheel, which some scholars read as the circle in which oxen turn when threshing grain.","Triptolemus","en/d/triptolemo.html"],["A dragon in Beowulf","In the Anglo-Saxon poem <em>Beowulf</em>, Sigemund kills a serpent guarding a hoard and carries the treasure away by ship while the monster melts.","Sigmund","en/d/sigmund.html"],["A statue that was never built","The original plan for the Powiśle statue was a 20-metre glass sculpture on a pillar in the middle of the Vistula channel. For financial reasons it was abandoned in favour of a more modest version surrounded by fish and seagulls.","Mermaid of Warsaw","en/d/syrenka-de-varsovia.html"],["National coin","In 1997 the National Bank of Ukraine minted a coin showing Cossack Mamai smoking a pipe and playing the kobza.","Cossack Mamai","en/d/cosaco-mamai.html"],["Eyes on the chest","Headless, Xingtian used his nipples as eyes and his navel as a mouth, an image that stands out among descriptions in early Chinese literature.","Xingtian","en/d/xingtian.html"],["A disputed homeland","Different versions of the bylina place the duke&#x27;s homeland in different places, such as India, Volyn, Korela or Galich.","Duke Stepanovich","en/d/dyuk-stepanovich.html"],["Ancestors of Bucephalus","According to some sources, these mares were ancestors of Bucephalus, the horse of Alexander the Great.","Mares of Diomedes","en/d/yeguas-de-diomedes.html"],["Greek name","The Greeks called him Tithoes, and in Ancient Egyptian his name was written <em>twtw</em>, a term linked to the idea of image.","Tutu","en/d/tutu.html"],["A name with variants","In many manuscripts the brother appears as Uar or Uair, but modern editors have corrected it to Brian, the form found in the older texts.","Iuchar","en/d/iuchar.html"],["A Hittite servant","A Hittite scribe named Ḫanikkuili described himself as a servant of Enbilulu in the colophon of a text about Naram-Sin that he copied.","Enbilulu","en/d/enbilulu.html"],["A lame river","After his clash with Zeus, the river was left lame and flows very slowly. People said bits of burnt coal turned up in its bed.","Asopus","en/d/asopo.html"],["A flag with a god","The Assyrian flag shows Ashur alongside the god Shamash, who in a modern interpretation of the pair appears as the Son.","Ashur","en/d/ashur.html"],["A daughter with her name","Ramesses II named his daughter Bint-Anat, meaning «Daughter of Anat», and she later became his wife.","Anat","en/d/anat.html"],["A warrior goddess","In the Golden Light Sutra she is described with a bow, arrow, sword and spear, and is said to fight on battlefields and always win.","Benzaiten","en/d/benzaiten.html"],["Two springs","According to Homer, the river rose from two springs: one gave warm water and the other cold water, whatever the season.","Scamander","en/d/escamandro.html"],["The princes in a fresco","One theory about the number of princes rests on the frescoes in the rotunda of Znojmo, in Moravia. The historian Anežka Merhautová argued that they depict all members of the Přemyslid dynasty, including the younger Moravian princes.","Hostivít","en/d/hostivit.html"],["Possible identity with Hel","The philologist Hjalmar Falk argued that Sinmara might actually be Hel, daughter of Loki, and compared her to the Roman goddess Proserpina because of her pale or half-blue appearance.","Sinmara","en/d/sinmara.html"],["A name tied to the underworld","The name Lethaea comes from the Latin Lethaeus, which in turn derives from Greek. Both terms are linked to the underworld.","Lethaea","en/d/letea.html"],["A plant with his name","A glossary and an Old English leechbook mention the plant forneotes folm, which may derive from his name, though this is not confirmed.","Fornjót","en/d/fornjot.html"],["A sharp name","His name is written with the hieroglyph for something pointed, a sharp triangle, and its literal translation is &#x27;the sharp ones&#x27;.","Sopdu","en/d/sopdu.html"],["A name also used for a king","In the Heimskringla, the name also appears as that of a Norse king, showing it was used for both beings and rulers.","Gandalf","en/d/gandalf.html"],["Two women with the same name","Molpadia is also the name of a second woman, daughter of Staphylus and Chrysothemis and sister of Parthenos and Rhoeo, sometimes called Hemithea. The scholar Otto Gruppe suggested the name may have come from Boeotia.","Molpadia","en/d/molpadia.html"],["The colour red","Because red symbolises happiness and longevity in China, Canopus is also called the Star of Old Age in China and neighbouring Korea, Japan and Vietnam, although it is barely visible in northern China.","Old Man of the South Pole","en/d/anciano-del-polo-sur.html"],["An echo in Njáls saga","In the Icelandic saga, the hero Gunnar sings joyfully inside his own burial mound, a scene scholars compare with Eggthér&#x27;s.","Eggthér","en/d/eggther.html"],["A statuette in Bern","Artio&#x27;s best-known image is a small bronze group found at Muri, near Bern, which is now held in the Bern Historical Museum.","Artio","en/d/artio.html"],["Sibylline oracles","The fata Sibyllina were the Sibylline oracles. In this way, the word fatum was associated with prophecy as well as with destiny.","Fatum","en/d/fatum.html"],["A name with warlike sense","His name comes from perthō, meaning to sack or destroy, a meaning that contrasts with the wisdom Hesiod credits him with.","Perses","en/d/perses.html"],["A moon of Saturn","One of Saturn&#x27;s moons is called Suttungr, a name originally spelled Suttung.","Suttungr","en/d/suttung.html"],["Origin of Rahu and Ketu","According to the tale, Rahu&#x27;s severed head and body became two celestial bodies floating in space.","Mohini","en/d/mohini.html"],["Gold is not metal","In the <em>Cratylus</em>, Plato clarifies that Hesiod did not mean the people were literally made of gold, but that they were good and noble.","Golden Age","en/d/edad-de-oro.html"],["Guardian at the threshold","The scholar John Lindow compares him to the outer guardian type, like the herdsman in <em>Skírnismál</em>, whom a character must confront before the main confrontation.","Eldir","en/d/eldir.html"],["A deme named after a heroine","The Attic deme of Melite took its name from this figure, and it housed a temple of Heracles Alexikakos.","Melite","en/d/melite-q4289514.html"],["An eye for sale","In one tale, the merchant does not flee from the screen watching him: he pries off its eyes and sells them to an eye surgeon.","Mokumokuren","en/d/mokumokuren.html"],["The lost ring","The royal ring was swallowed by a fish in the river, and it was a fisherman who found it when he cut open his catch, allowing the king to remember Shakuntala.","Dushyanta","en/d/dushianta.html"],["A star in Sanskrit","Her name, tara, means star in Sanskrit and is written तारा in Devanagari.","Tara","en/d/tara-q2462108.html"],["An asteroid bears his name","The asteroid 3794 Sthenelos is named after the hero, one of the few modern traces of his figure.","Sthenelus","en/d/estenelo-q1060267.html"],["A sword in the tree","According to the saga, only Sigmund managed to pull the sword Odin drove into the Barnstokkr, the great tree in Völsung&#x27;s hall.","Völsung","en/d/volsung.html"],["Ancient tattoos","In the New Kingdom, images of Bes were tattooed on the thighs of dancers, musicians and servant girls.","Bes","en/d/bes.html"],["Twelve moon daughters","Each of the twelve moons was said to complete a full journey across the sky in a single day, according to the ancient tradition.","Changxi","en/d/changxi.html"],["Name of Bangkok","The ceremonial name of Bangkok alludes to Sakra. It begins with the Thai word <em>krung</em>, meaning &#x27;capital&#x27;.","Śakra","en/d/sakra.html"],["Meaning of his name","His Old Norse name has been translated as &#x27;ring-shaped&#x27;.","Baugi","en/d/baugi.html"],["A Cornish name","In Cornish, the lost land was called Lethowsow, meaning &#x27;the milky ones&#x27;. The name refers to the constant white water surrounding the Seven Stones reef, which lies on the supposed site of its capital.","Lyonesse","en/d/lyonesse.html"],["A surviving son","Dagr survives the battle because he surrenders and swears oaths of loyalty to Helgi.","Högne","en/d/hogne.html"],["A telling name","Her name comes from <em>śāka</em>, &quot;vegetable,&quot; and <em>bharī</em>, &quot;bearer,&quot; so she is understood as the bringer of food.","Shakambhari","en/d/shakambhari.html"],["A name that almost was different","The society Mensa International nearly took the name mens, but chose Mensa to avoid linguistic confusion.","Mens","en/d/mens.html"],["A name with variants","The name Sreng is often misinterpreted as Streng, a reading that appears frequently in modern sources.","Sreng","en/d/sreng.html"],["A sacred tree","The spear Romulus threw onto the Palatine was said to have turned into the Corniolus, the sacred tree of Rome.","Founding of Rome","en/d/fundacion-de-roma.html"],["A god once thought to be female","For much of the twentieth century Ruḍaw was thought to be a female deity. More recent inscriptions revised this view, showing him as masculine.","Ruḍaw","en/d/rudaw.html"],["The Strophades","The Floating Islands where Iris halted the sons of Boreas were later called the Strophades, the Turning Islands, after the place where the Boreads turned back.","Phineus","en/d/fineo.html"],["A name of wind","Aello means &quot;stormy wind&quot; in Greek, a name that fits her connection with the winds.","Aello","en/d/aelo.html"],["Dwarf names","Dáinn and Dvalinn are also dwarf names in Norse mythology, a link scholars point to when interpreting the stags as winds.","Duraþrór","en/d/duraror.html"],["A namesake sage","The Rig-veda mentions a sage named Sahadeva, with the patronymic Varsha-Guira, meaning voice of a bull.","Sahadeva","en/d/sahadeva.html"],["A name with meaning","Amphimachus comes from the Greek amphi, &#x27;on both sides&#x27;, and mache, &#x27;battle&#x27;. The name can be roughly rendered as &#x27;one who fights on both sides&#x27;.","Amphimachus","en/d/anfimaco-q5676469.html"],["A monster without a description","The ancient sources do not describe her appearance, and modern commentators suppose she was a dragoness because of her resemblance to the serpent Python, who also plagued the Delphi region.","Sybaris","en/d/sybaris.html"],["Place in the indigitamenta","The scholar W. H. Roscher includes her among the indigitamenta, the list of deities Roman priests kept so that the correct divinity would be invoked in rituals.","Stata Mater","en/d/stata-mater.html"],["The Hall of Drunkenness","Mut&#x27;s temple had a hall with this name, confirmed by inscriptions from the time of Hatshepsut. There the goddess was linked to rituals of drinking and dancing.","Mut","en/d/mut.html"],["Vanished ingredients","The sources say the six ingredients of the ribbon ceased to exist in the world so that they could become part of it.","Gleipnir","en/d/gleipnir.html"],["A city named after a widow","According to the myth, the city of Tingis, today Tangier, took its name from Tingis, Antaeus&#x27;s widow, at the suggestion of her son Sophax.","Antaeus","en/d/anteo.html"],["God of Tahiti","On Tahiti, Tāne was revered as the god of peace and beauty, a role different from his figure in New Zealand.","Tāne","en/d/tane.html"],["The Wednesday pedestal","According to Burmese mythology he was born on a Wednesday, so he occupies the pedestal of that day.","Koṇāgamana","en/d/konagamana.html"],["Clothes and weight","The weight of the clothes hung on the branch shows the gravity of the sins, since the worst sinners carry more water when crossing the river.","Keneō","en/d/keneo.html"],["A trident on a flag","The broken tip of the trident appears on the flag of Barbados, symbolising the end of British colonial rule.","Trident of Poseidon","en/d/tridente-de-poseidon.html"],["A name with several meanings","Melia can be translated as ash tree, sweet or honey. In ancient Greek it is written Μελία or Μελίη.","Melia","en/d/melia.html"],["A river with two names","According to Plutarch, the Inachus River was previously called Carmanor or Haliacmon. It took the name Inachus after the story of his daughter Io.","Inachus","en/d/inaco.html"],["A Phrygian cap","His head was always shown covered by a cowl or Phrygian cap, a feature that sets him apart in images.","Telesphorus","en/d/telesforo.html"],["A name meaning &quot;mortal life&quot;","The Avestan name breaks down into &quot;life&quot; and &quot;mortal&quot;, an etymology that defines him as the human being par excellence.","Gayōmart (Keyumars)","en/d/gayomart-keyumars.html"],["The hand of Ningal","In medical treatises, the term &#x27;hand of Ningal&#x27; referred to an unidentified skin disease.","Ningal","en/d/ningal.html"],["Origin of its name","According to folklorist Walter Traill Dennison, its name comes from the Orcadian dialect word <em>knoggelvi</em> and means &quot;Devil of the Sea&quot;.","Nuckelavee","en/d/nuckelavee.html"],["The Chindits","The British long-range forces in Burma took their name from the chinthe during the Second World War.","Chinthe","en/d/chinthe.html"],["A royal seal","The seal of the crown prince Mursili III showed Šarruma embracing him, reflecting the god&#x27;s close link to the Hittite royal family.","Šarruma","en/d/sarruma.html"],["Walls of Telegonus","Latin poets used this name for Tusculum, as Propertius does, and the Mamilia clan of Rome claimed descent from him and Penelope.","Telegonus","en/d/telegono.html"],["Mysterious roads","Medrano claimed the Muisca had long, straight roads that, according to tradition, Bochica had cleared as he walked. The people did not dare to use them because they revered them.","Bochica","en/d/bochica.html"],["An Amazonian quiver","According to Hyginus, Elymus, under the name Helymus, finished second in the funeral footrace and received an Amazonian quiver as his prize.","Elymus","en/d/elimo.html"],["The bet on the figs","According to Tzetzes, Calchas tested Mopsus by asking how many figs a nearby tree bore. Mopsus answered ten thousand and one, and when the figs were counted, he was proven right.","Mopsus","en/d/mopso-q1887300.html"],["A goddess of cholera","Among the sisters is Olabibi, who in these beliefs is the goddess of cholera, a disease that for centuries struck the riverside areas of Bengal.","Asanbibi","en/d/asanbibi.html"],["Meaning of the name","Oscar is linked to the old word <em>oscara</em>, usually translated as deer friend or god friend.","Oscar","en/d/oscar.html"],["A rooster on a tapestry","The Överhogdal tapestries show a central tree with a bird at the top. Some researchers propose that the bird is Gullinkambi and that the tree is Yggdrasil.","Gullinkambi","en/d/gullinkambi.html"],["A god with chains","In the image Lucian describes, Ogmios&#x27;s followers are bound by gold chains attached to the god&#x27;s tongue.","Ogmios","en/d/ogmios.html"],["Sri Chakra earrings","According to tradition, Adi Shankara installed earrings bearing the Sri Chakra to calm the goddess&#x27;s fury, as she was once worshipped only from outside the temple.","Akhilandeshwari","en/d/akhilandeshwari.html"],["A name for every face","Parvati has over a thousand names in the regional mythologies of India, and each one expresses a different aspect of the goddess, such as Gauri.","Devi","en/d/devi.html"],["Hill of the two horns","The sanctuary at Jebel Boukornine, near Carthage, takes its name from the hill, which worshippers linked to the god&#x27;s horns.","Baal Hammon","en/d/baal-hammon.html"],["Diamonds in the eyes","Some versions say the monster has diamonds set in its eye sockets, a detail that Green considers a narrative exaggeration.","Grootslang","en/d/grootslang.html"],["A name with several etymologies","His name has been explained as Greek, Hebrew or Latin in origin, and no explanation is definitive.","Mephistopheles","en/d/mefistofeles.html"],["A distorted name","The word «indrik» is a distorted form of the Russian word for unicorn, the animal the folklore links it to.","Indrik","en/d/indrik.html"],["An example in ethics","Aristotle mentions Merope in the <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em> as an example of an action done unknowingly, because she did not realise the circumstances: she could have taken her own son for an enemy.","Merope of Messenia","en/d/merope-de-mesenia.html"],["An irreverent list","Aristophanes included in two comedies the same list of foul-smelling objects, which mentioned the testicles of Lamia, making her gender ambiguous in that passage.","Lamia","en/d/lamia.html"],["Broken horns","Ovid calls him <em>Rhenus cornibus fractis</em>, an image interpreted as a symbol of defeated peoples.","Rhenus Pater","en/d/rhenus-pater.html"],["Name of a space programme","NASA&#x27;s Apollo programme, which sent astronauts to the Moon, took its name from the god.","Apollo","en/d/apolo.html"],["Two myths in one","Some critics think the story combines two separate submyths: an astronomical one tied to the star Capella, and another about the goat that raised Zeus on Crete.","Ega","en/d/ega.html"],["Bodiless heads","Their images showed no bodies, only heads, and the sacrifices were limited to those same heads.","Alalcomenia","en/d/alalcomenia.html"],["A game of names","The family is given the names of birds and plants, and the myth depends on Greek wordplay linking the two.","Erodius","en/d/erodio.html"],["A being in transit","In Buddhism, the term gandharva also denotes a being in the intermediate state between death and a new birth, according to a commentary on a sutta.","Gandharva","en/d/gandharva.html"],["A name meaning snow","Chione comes from the Greek word for snow, <em>chiōn</em>, related to <em>kheîma</em> (snow) and <em>kheimōn</em> (winter).","Chione","en/d/quione.html"],["Flamingo goddess","At Buto, alongside Shu, she was worshipped in the form of a flamingo, one of her most unusual depictions.","Tefnut","en/d/tefnut.html"],["Zep Tepi","The term zp tpj, &quot;the first time&quot;, denotes the moment of transition from primordial chaos to the world as it exists.","Ancient Egyptian creation myths","en/d/mitos-egipcios-de-la-creacion.html"],["Name of the river","The names Leir, Lerion and Ligora derive from the old Brittonic name of the River Soar, reconstructed as <em>Ligera or </em>Ligora.","Leir of Britain","en/d/leir-de-bretana.html"],["A bird linked to lightning","Among some African tribes the lightning bird is believed to be the hammerkop, a small wading bird.","Impundulu","en/d/impundulu.html"],["Flayed tortoise","Pausanias says Mount Chelydorea, near Cyllene, means flayed tortoise. There Hermes is said to have made a lyre from a tortoise&#x27;s shell.","Chelone","en/d/quelona.html"],["The vultures","In the accounts of Plutarch and Livy, the dispute is settled by birds, and the number of vultures decides who gains command.","Romulus and Remus","en/d/romulo-y-remo.html"],["Rivers that run dry","Pausanias explains that the riverbeds of these rivers are dry in summer, except those of Lerna.","Cephissus","en/d/cefiso.html"],["A sacred oath","Zeus made the waters of the Styx the source of his irrevocable oath, and Iris was in charge of collecting the water in a krater.","Titanomachy","en/d/titanomaquia.html"],["A god in women&#x27;s names","Although his feminine counterpart was the goddess Astarte, among the Aramaeans his name always appeared in the masculine, even in women&#x27;s names such as <em>&#x27;Attar-ramat</em>.","Attar","en/d/attar.html"],["A third eye","He is depicted with a third eye in his forehead, called the Eye of Heaven, which lets him see through deceptions and transformations.","Erlang Shen","en/d/erlang-shen.html"],["Astrologer of whole lives","He is credited with the Bhrigu Samhita, considered one of the first books of astrology, with predictions covering a whole life.","Bhrigu","en/d/bhrigu.html"],["A proverb born from a myth","Herodotus records the phrase &quot;Lemnian crime&quot; as a synonym for any cruel deed, showing how famous the story had become.","Hypsipyle","en/d/hipsipila.html"],["Battle crow","Her name may derive from the Proto-Celtic <em>Katu-bodwā, made of </em>katu (&quot;war, battle&quot;) and *bodwā (&quot;crow&quot;).","Cathubodua","en/d/cathubodua.html"],["River of ash","In East Asia the Buddhist river is known as the Ash River Hell, a name adapted from the Sanskrit.","Vaitarani","en/d/vaitarani.html"],["The number nine","Her number is 9 and its multiples, and she carries nine otá stones gathered from the river. Her attributes include nine copper bracelets.","Oyá","en/d/oya.html"],["A personified region","One reference holds that Naucrate personifies the western region of the Nile Delta.","Naucrate","en/d/naucrate.html"],["A nightingale without song","According to Hyginus, the female nightingale has no song, a detail linked to the transformation of Philomela into this bird.","Tereus","en/d/tereo.html"],["Beer without a hangover","In reliefs at Edfu, Dendera and Behbeit, Nephthys receives beer offerings from the pharaoh, which she returns so that he may enjoy it without a hangover.","Nephthys","en/d/neftis.html"],["Ivory shoulder","According to the myth, Hephaestus made him an ivory shoulder to replace the one Demeter had eaten.","Pelops","en/d/pelope.html"],["Name of a region","Punjab comes from panj-āb, &#x27;five waters&#x27;, a word related to the Vedic term for water.","Āp (Apah)","en/d/ap-apah.html"],["A name with several readings","The meaning of Mótsognir is uncertain. Readings such as &quot;he who drinks in courage&quot; or &quot;the mead-sucker&quot; have been proposed, and the name appears in variant spellings across manuscripts.","Mótsognir","en/d/motsognir.html"],["A crocodile in the sky","On some ancient Greek and Egyptian star charts, the constellation Scorpius is shown as a crocodile, and Schlegel linked it with the Azure Dragon.","Azure Dragon","en/d/dragon-turquesa.html"],["A shifting genealogy","Depending on the source, Hellen was the son of Deucalion, Zeus or Prometheus, and in some accounts he appears as Deucalion&#x27;s brother.","Hellen","en/d/helen.html"],["Possible meaning of her name","Guðbrandur Vigfússon linked her name to the Old Norse word róta, meaning &#x27;sleet and storm&#x27;, and saw her as a goddess who sent storms and rain.","Róta","en/d/rota.html"],["One eye","According to the story, Chandi burned one of her eyes, which is why she is known as &quot;the goddess of one eye&quot;.","Manasa","en/d/manasa.html"],["A banana that is not eaten","The Kluai Tani banana is not edible, yet its leaves are used to wrap local sweets and its flower cluster is used in traditional medicine to treat ulcers.","Nang Tani","en/d/nang-tani.html"],["Mongoose variant","A version recorded by the scholar David Lewis-Williams tells how the mongoose, ǀKaggen&#x27;s grandson, spies on his grandfather and reveals to his meerkat brothers where the eland was hidden.","ǀKaggen","en/d/kaggen.html"],["A bronze goat","The people of Elyrus sent Delphi a bronze statue of a goat that, according to legend, nursed the children Phylacides and Phylander.","Acacallis","en/d/acacalide.html"],["Kites and Disney","A legend about Rongo flying the first kite is told in the waiting room of Walt Disney&#x27;s Enchanted Tiki Room.","Rongo","en/d/rongo.html"],["Dew","Her name means dew, which is why she was revered as a protector of agriculture.","Herse","en/d/herse.html"],["Incestuous twins","In the Rigveda, Yami is the first woman and her brother Yama the first mortal. The dialogue in which she asks him to have children with her is one of the most debated passages of the text.","Yami","en/d/iami.html"],["A different Amphion","He is easy to confuse with Amphion, son of Zeus and Antiope, whose story is much better known. They are two different characters.","Amphion (son of Iasus)","en/d/anfion-hijo-de-yaso.html"],["An estate with his name","In the Saqqara tomb of the official Raemka, a list of royal estates includes one called &quot;the Work of Iunmutef&quot;, linked to the 9th Upper Egyptian nome.","Iunmutef","en/d/iunmutef.html"],["Varied names","His name has been transcribed in several ways, such as Geshtu-(E), Geshtu, Gestu or We-ila. Today the name Ilawela is preferred.","Geshtu-E (Ilawela)","en/d/geshtu-e.html"],["Many spellings","The name appears in several forms, such as Adramelec, Adramelech, Adrammelech or Adar-malik, depending on the language and the source.","Adramelech","en/d/adramelec.html"],["A tower over a head","Alfonso X tells how Heracles raised a tower over Geryon&#x27;s head. The present Tower of Hercules in A Coruña is a working lighthouse rebuilt on a Roman one.","Geryon","en/d/gerion.html"],["The Echinades","They are a group of islands at the mouth of the Achelous river, according to the story in which Perimela becomes one of them.","Perimela","en/d/perimela.html"],["An apple as a message","Acontius did not declare himself out loud. He wrote his oath on an apple and threw it to the girl during a sacrifice to Artemis.","Cydippe","en/d/cidipe-q5723319.html"],["A genus of spiders","The name Poltys is also used for a genus of spiders, a scientific use unrelated to the legend.","Poltys","en/d/poltis.html"],["Gaia and the sickle","According to Hesiod, Gaia herself forged the sickle with which Cronus castrated Uranus, making her the instigator of her husband&#x27;s overthrow.","Gaia","en/d/gea.html"],["Full name","Her full name, Apemerukoyan-mat Unamerukoyan-mat, is usually rendered as something like “Rising Fire Sparks Woman”.","Kamuy-huci","en/d/kamuy-huci.html"],["A god with sacred bulls","Bulls were sacred to Almaqah, which is why he was known as the &quot;Bull of the land of the Lord&quot;.","Almaqah","en/d/almaqah.html"],["A biblical echo","The lottery that picks who must jump into the sea is linked by scholars to the biblical story of Jonah.","Sadko","en/d/sadko.html"],["A portico with terracotta figures","Pausanias describes a portico in the Athenian Ceramicus with baked clay figures on its roof, among them Hemera carrying off Cephalus.","Cephalus","en/d/cefalo.html"],["A fly-shaped god","Rabbinic tradition equates him with Beelzebub, the god of Ekron, who was represented as a fly.","Baal-berith","en/d/baal-berith.html"],["An eating contest","In one of the contests, Lepreus and Heracles competed to see who could eat a whole bull faster. Pausanias is the source that says Lepreus matched the hero.","Lepreus","en/d/lepreo.html"],["Two versions of the family","While the usual tradition makes Deucalion a son of Minos, Diodorus presents him as the brother of Molus, with their sons as leaders of the Cretans at Troy.","Deucalion of Crete","en/d/deucalion-de-creta.html"],["Many names","Según las fuentes, this figure appears under several names, such as Iasion, Iasus, Yaso or Eetion, reflecting the different traditions that recorded him.","Iasion","en/d/yasion.html"],["Meaning of the name","Litr derives from a reconstructed Proto-Germanic form *ulituz, related to Gothic and Old English words meaning &#x27;shape&#x27; or &#x27;brightness&#x27;.","Litr","en/d/litr.html"],["A name meaning visible","Phantes comes from the Greek Φάντης, meaning visible, a name that suggests something that can be seen.","Phantes","en/d/fantes.html"],["A disputed name","Scholars do not agree on how her name was read. Bau is the conventional form, but Baba, Bawu and Babu have also been proposed.","Bau","en/d/baba-bau.html"],["A word that also means orca","The character 鯱 is a kokuji, a kanji created in Japan, and when read shachi it can mean orca.","Shachihoko","en/d/shachihoko.html"],["A very slow tree","According to the stories, the tree puts forth leaves once every thousand years and needs another three thousand to ripen its fruit.","Peaches of Immortality","en/d/melocotones-de-la-inmortalidad.html"],["A reworked passage","In the Táin, the encounter with Cúchulainn is awkwardly reassigned to Fedelm&#x27;s handmaid, an alteration that looks like an attempt to soften the episode.","Fedelm Noíchrothach","en/d/fedelm-noichrothach.html"],["A name with a journey","According to the sources, the name did not come straight from Greece but through a Latin centre, probably Praeneste.","Apulu","en/d/apulu.html"],["Family rights","The Cheerappanchira family holds the right to set off fireworks and light lamps at the temple, and to collect half of the coconuts donated by devotees.","Malikapurathamma","en/d/malikapurathamma.html"],["A parallel story","Comaetho&#x27;s betrayal resembles that of Samson and Delilah, and that of Scylla and Nisus, where a woman also betrays her father for love.","Pterelaus","en/d/pterelao.html"],["Children before birth","Some scenes show Ganesha and Kartikeya, who are not yet born. They appear in the Rameshvara Cave at Ellora and in a 9th-century sculpture from Uttar Pradesh.","Kalyanasundara","en/d/kalyanasundara.html"],["Coins of Imbros","His figure appears on coins from Imbros, where he is shown naked, bearded and with an erect phallus, next to an incense burner.","Orthanes","en/d/ortanes.html"],["Names","The names mean something like &quot;teeth-baring snarler&quot; and &quot;teeth-grinder&quot;. According to scholar Rudolf Simek, they may have been invented by Snorri.","Tanngrisnir and Tanngnjóstr","en/d/tanngrisnir-y-tanngnjostr.html"],["A fishermen&#x27;s dedication","The Beetgum inscription was dedicated by a group of fishermen, showing that her cult reached coastal communities too.","Hludana","en/d/hludana.html"],["The drug in Brave New World","Aldous Huxley took the name of the Vedic ritual drink soma for the dystopian drug the state uses to control its population.","Chandra","en/d/chandra.html"],["Mountain name","Her name means &#x27;daughter of the mountain&#x27;, alluding to her origin as the daughter of King Himavat.","Shailaputri","en/d/shailaputri.html"],["Snakes in the ears","The ancient text describes a green snake in her left ear and a red one in her right, an unusual detail among rain figures.","Yu Shiqie","en/d/yu-shiqie.html"],["Popular sayings","According to the Prose Edda, &#x27;to loose from Leyding&#x27; or &#x27;to strike out of Dromi&#x27; became sayings for an achievement won with great effort.","Fenrir","en/d/fenrir.html"],["A bull and a horse","In early Indian religious art he appears either as a red bull spewing lightning or as a black horse adorned with pearls, which symbolize the stars.","Dyaus Pita","en/d/dyaus-pita.html"],["Origin of the shimenawa","The straw rope that marks sacred spaces in Japan is, according to the myth, traced to Futodama&#x27;s gesture of closing off Amaterasu&#x27;s cave.","Futodama","en/d/futodama.html"],["A name with meaning","Her name means &quot;divine family&quot; and is formed from <em>dion</em> and <em>patra</em>.","Diopatra","en/d/diopatra.html"],["A goddess of many names","The texts call her Nidaba, Nisaba or Nanibgal. It has been suggested that Nunbarsegunu was another name for Nisaba, goddess of writing.","Nunbarsegunu","en/d/nunbarsegunu.html"],["A warrior princess","Apollodorus describes her driving a chariot and practicing the art of war, an unusual image for a princess of the period.","Deianira","en/d/deyanira.html"],["The meaning of his name","Vipassī comes from vi (well) and passī (saw), meaning roughly one who has seen clearly. The word belongs to the same family as vipassanā, or contemplation.","Vipassī","en/d/vipassi.html"],["An imperial figurine","A Ming figurine of Amoghasiddhi was made during the reign of the Yongle Emperor, in the early 1400s.","Amoghasiddhi","en/d/amoghasiddhi.html"],["Britomartis","According to Cretan tradition, Britomartis was born to Zeus and Carme, the daughter of Eubuleus. She was a huntress much loved by Artemis, until Minos began to pursue her.","Carmanor","en/d/carmanor.html"],["Kinship with Ares","Her maternal grandfather was Ares, god of war, and her grandmother Tereine was a daughter of the river god Strymon.","Polyphonte","en/d/polifonte.html"],["A version with other names","Antoninus Liberalis calls the maiden Arsinoe and her suitor Arceophon, while Plutarch tells a variant with the Cretans Gorgo and Asander.","Anaxarete","en/d/anaxareta.html"],["A year in five variants","Each sixty-year cycle includes five Pig years, one for each element: Wood, Fire, Earth, Metal and Water.","Pig","en/d/cerdo.html"],["Origin of the word maelstrom","The word maelstrom, now used for a great sea whirlpool, is explained in the sources as coming from mal (mill) and ström (stream).","Grottasöngr (Song of Grótti)","en/d/grottasongr-cancion-de-grotti.html"],["A rarely visited shrine","The hidden statue at Mōtsū-ji is displayed only once every thirty-three years.","Matarajin","en/d/matarajin.html"],["A fable by Aesop","In an Aesop fable, a crow invites a dog to a banquet and makes a sacrifice to Athena. The dog says it is pointless because the goddess dislikes her, but the crow replies that she will offer it anyway to make amends.","Corone","en/d/corone.html"],["A constellation named after him","The constellation Cygnus, visible in the northern hemisphere sky, recalls this figure transformed into a bird.","Cycnus","en/d/cicno.html"],["An asteroid named after her","The main-belt asteroid 5 Astraea is named after her.","Astraea","en/d/astrea.html"],["A Greek word","The term <em>metis</em> described an intelligence combining wisdom and cunning, a quality highly admired in heroes such as Odysseus.","Metis","en/d/metis.html"],["A decisive oath","Phyleus witnessed the oath between Augeas and Heracles, and that role is what led him to testify at the hearing.","Phyleus","en/d/fileo.html"],["Several names","Some accounts also call him Attalus or Calus, variant forms of his name in the tradition.","Talos","en/d/talos-q2365581.html"],["Endless fields","The Mahavastu states that there are thousands of buddha-fields beyond those it lists, and that their end cannot be discerned.","Pure land","en/d/tierra-pura.html"],["A late name","Ancient texts do not name the statue. The name Galatea was established by eighteenth-century writers.","Galatea","en/d/galatea.html"],["Two mandalas, one Chinese union","The two mandalas are believed to have evolved separately in India and were first joined in China, perhaps by the master Huiguo.","Diamond Realm","en/d/reino-de-diamante.html"],["One of four great legends","The 1920s Folklore Movement included this story among China&#x27;s Four Great Folktales.","Zhinü","en/d/zhinu.html"],["A left-handed god","The sources say Tyr was left-handed, which in that era was associated with bad luck.","Tyr","en/d/tyr-q19921982.html"],["Meaning of the name","In Mandarin, «shui» means water and «mu» means mother, so she is known as the Old Mother of Waters.","Shuimu","en/d/shuimu.html"],["A name with meaning","His Greek name, Παλαίχθονος, means «ancient earth», fitting the idea of ancestors born from the soil.","Palaechthon","en/d/palecton.html"],["A name with biblical echoes","The Hebrew Bible mentions a sea monster called tannin, which the Greek Septuagint rendered as ketos. The great fish that swallowed Jonah was also given that name in the Greek version.","Cetus","en/d/ceto-q3269950.html"],["A snail as offering","In Brazil the snail Achatina fulica is offered to Obatala, because it shares the local name igbin with the giant African snail used in Yorubaland.","Obatala","en/d/obatala-q48854763.html"],["A name with history","Some versions hold that her name comes from that of a sly prince called Patrikas.","Kuma Lisa","en/d/kuma-lisa.html"],["A disputed name","The Iranian etymology of the name, which links it to the Persian word for sun, is rejected today by linguists.","Khors","en/d/khors.html"],["Poseidon in Libya","Herodotus regarded Poseidon as an ancient Libyan god that the Greeks adopted, as they did with Athena.","Tingis","en/d/tingis.html"],["Stag of the ash tree","The Prose Edda places him among the four stags that eat the leaves of Yggdrasil, while the Poetic Edda mentions only one stag.","Dvalinn","en/d/dvalin.html"],["A ship of nails","Åke Ohlmarks proposed that nagl also means peg or nail, so Naglfar could refer to clinker-built ships joined with metal nails, used as funeral ships in the Migration period.","Naglfar","en/d/naglfar.html"],["Two words, one sound","Dhara means &quot;support&quot; in Sanskrit, while the word dhārā, with a long vowel, refers to the flow of a stream or waterfall.","Dhara","en/d/dhara.html"],["A temple built with butter","A regional legend says the foundations of the Bhandasar Temple in Bikaner were poured with clarified butter instead of water to withstand the desert climate.","Sumatinatha","en/d/sumatinatha.html"],["Wine as blood","The Egyptians offered red wine as &quot;the blood of the gods&quot;, and its dark color may explain Shesmu&#x27;s link with blood.","Shezmu","en/d/shesmu.html"],["An eastern name","Also called Anatolia or Antolia, her name in Greek means &quot;eastern,&quot; referring to the place where the sun rises.","Anatole","en/d/anatole.html"],["Kama Dahanam","In South India the same date is linked to Shiva burning Kamadeva with his third eye. In rural Tamil Nadu, mime performances of Kamadeva are staged and his effigies are burnt.","Holika","en/d/holika.html"],["A figure on a shield","Hesiod includes her among the figures decorating the Shield of Heracles, next to the Keres and the Moirai.","Achlys","en/d/aclis.html"],["An Antarctic lake","A lake in Antarctica is named after this god, a modern reminder of a deity who has almost been forgotten.","Volturnus","en/d/volturno.html"],["A rocket with her name","The third stage of the ELDO Europa rocket was named Astris.","Astris","en/d/astris.html"],["Age at death","The chronicler Malalas describes her, at the time of her sacrifice, as eighteen years old.","Polyxena","en/d/polixena.html"],["A computer project","A simulation engine from the University of Chicago is named after him, in connection with farming in ancient settlements.","Enkimdu","en/d/enkimdu.html"],["An axe with a Lydian name","According to some sources, the Lydians called the battle-axe attributed to Hippolyta a <em>labrys</em>, and the statue of Zeus Labrandeus in Caria holds it.","Hippolyta","en/d/hipolita.html"],["Inherited name","His name comes from an uncle also called Agnarr, whom Geirröd betrayed and sent to die on the high seas.","Agnarr Geirröðsson","en/d/agnarr-geirrosson.html"],["The Liver of Piacenza","This bronze model of a sheep&#x27;s liver carries the names of 27 deities. Usil&#x27;s name is on its convex side, next to that of Tiur.","Usil","en/d/usil.html"],["A name with many theories","The word may come from Latin <em>gradalis</em>, a serving dish. In the 15th century the English writer John Hardyng invented an etymology linking it to royal blood.","Holy Grail","en/d/grial.html"],["Nomos basileus","The phrase «the law is king» sums up the idea that no one should submit to a man, only to the law.","Nomos","en/d/nomos.html"],["Contemptor divum","Romans called him <em>contemptor divum</em>, &quot;the despiser of the gods&quot;, which made him especially hateful to a Roman audience.","Mezentius","en/d/mecencio.html"],["The cup of wine","In the story, Gyptis&#x27;s choice is expressed through a ritual gesture: she hands Protis the ceremonial cup of wine during the banquet.","Gyptis","en/d/gyptis.html"],["Her father&#x27;s bulls","According to tradition, Krishna won Nappinnai by subduing seven ferocious bulls belonging to her father.","Nila Devi","en/d/nila-devi.html"],["A name meaning &quot;hate&quot;","Hati, the name of this wolf, means &quot;He Who Hates&quot; or &quot;Enemy&quot;.","Mánagarmr","en/d/managarmr.html"],["A goddess with a statue","On kudurru stones, most deities appear only through symbols, while Gula is shown with a statue of her own.","Gula","en/d/gula.html"],["A willow name","The Greek name Ἴτωνος can be translated as &quot;willow&quot;, a tree associated with damp ground and riverbanks.","Itonus","en/d/itono-q17635079.html"],["Triads of mothers","Many representations show her in groups of three, known as triads of divine mothers.","Dea Matrona","en/d/dea-matrona.html"],["Other names","In some sources the brother is called Ligys, and Dercynus appears as Bergion or Beergios.","Dercynus","en/d/dercino.html"],["A name that changed meaning","At first the name referred to a specific beast. Over time it extended to mythical creatures in general and to violent people.","Taowu","en/d/taowu.html"],["One immortal head","According to the sources, the Hydra&#x27;s only immortal head was buried under a great rock beside the road from Lerna to Eleutes.","Lernaean Hydra","en/d/hidra-de-lerna.html"],["An incomparable name","One interpretation of her name splits it into parts meaning &#x27;not to be measured&#x27; or &#x27;incomparable&#x27;, alluding to her exceptional beauty.","Menaka","en/d/menaka.html"],["Phoebaean Lagoon","The sanctuary stood in a shallow, marshy lagoon that the Greeks called the Phoebaean Lagoon.","Saron","en/d/saron.html"],["Lettuce or barley","According to the comic poet Cratinus, Aphrodite hid Phaon among lettuces so she could keep him for herself. Marsyas of Philippi, however, said it was unripe barley.","Phaon","en/d/faon.html"],["A possibly Cretan name","The name may derive from the ancient city of Hyrtacina in southwestern Crete.","Hyrtacus","en/d/hirtaco.html"],["Golden apples","The poets&#x27; name for the &quot;golden apples&quot; would come from the golden sheep owned by Hesperus and Atlas, according to Diodorus.","Hesperis","en/d/hesperis.html"],["The head of Abydos","Abydos kept a sacred relic identified as the god&#x27;s head, one of the reasons for the city&#x27;s prestige.","Osiris","en/d/osiris.html"],["Angkor Wat relief","The 12th-century relief at Angkor Wat is the most famous depiction of the churning and forms part of this Cambodian temple&#x27;s gallery.","Mount Mandara","en/d/monte-mandara.html"],["Three sworn sisters","Chen Jinggu, Lin Jiuniang and Li Sanniang were sworn sisters. Together they are called the &quot;Three Ladies&quot;.","Chen Jinggu","en/d/chen-jinggu.html"],["A disputed nurse","Servius reports that there was no agreement about whom Caieta had nursed: besides Aeneas, she was also assigned to Creusa or Ascanius.","Caieta","en/d/caieta.html"],["Many names","The Mahabharata gives her numerous names, such as Pannagī or Uragātmajā, which refer to her serpent origin.","Ulupi","en/d/ulupi.html"],["Offering after a wish","Devotees whose wish is granted offer a bhandara, a community meal, at the Goran temple.","Baba Shivo","en/d/baba-shivo.html"],["A black crown","According to tradition, a dakini gave the black crown to the third Karmapa, Rangjung Dorje, when he was three years old. The crown became an emblem of his lineage.","Dakini","en/d/dakini.html"],["Early archaeology","The oldest archaeological remains naming Ephialtes are Attic black-figure vases, dated to around 575-525 BC.","Ephialtes","en/d/efialtes.html"],["Only Gaulish inscription","A potsherd found at Lezoux, in Puy-de-Dôme, is the only known inscription in the Gaulish language that names her.","Rosmerta","en/d/rosmerta.html"],["Legendary cook","He is credited with the cookbook Pakadarpanam, and he is said to have been able to prepare a full meal without lighting a fire.","Nala","en/d/nala.html"],["Shown on atlases","The reef still appeared in the 2015 edition of the National Geographic Atlas of the World, even though its existence was never confirmed.","Ernest Legouvé Reef","en/d/arrecife-ernest-legouve.html"],["Name of her worshippers","Her followers were called baptes, &quot;bathers&quot;, after the bathing ritual that preceded worship.","Kotys","en/d/cotis.html"],["The origin of the games","According to tradition, the funeral games held in honour of Opheltes gave rise to the Nemean Games.","Lycurgus","en/d/licurgo-q368157.html"],["Breasts and belly","Although male, Hapi was shown with drooping breasts and a large belly, an image of the fertility the flood brought.","Hapi","en/d/hapi.html"],["A statue by Praxiteles","Pausanias attributes the image of Paregoros in the temple of Aphrodite at Megara to the sculptor Praxiteles.","Paregoros","en/d/paregoro.html"],["Annual pilgrimage","Every 17 December, tens of thousands of devotees visit the San Lázaro sanctuary in El Rincón, on the outskirts of Havana.","Babalú-Ayé","en/d/babalu-aye.html"],["A namesake town","Aricia was also a town near Rome, sacred to Diana, where the nymph Egeria shared with the goddess the protection of childbirth.","Aricia","en/d/aricia.html"],["A telling name","His Greek name, Ναυσίθοος, means «impetuous sailor», an etymology that fits his father&#x27;s life as a seafarer.","Nausithous","en/d/nausitoo.html"],["Origin of thunder","In the story, the rising sound of thunder comes from his mother carrying him up and down a ladder to get him to sleep as a baby.","Ajisukitakahikone","en/d/ajisukitakahikone-q11634943.html"],["A given name","Ondine is also used as a female given name, especially in French.","Undine","en/d/ondina.html"],["Father of Euphorbus","Tzetzes presents him as the father of the hero Euphorbus, who in other sources is the son of Panthous and Phrontis.","Bucolion","en/d/bucolion.html"],["Anchor stone","Across tropical Polynesia, related words refer to a coral stone that was also used as an anchor.","Punga","en/d/punga.html"],["A dream before birth","The mother of the poet Li Bai dreamed of Taibai Jinxing while pregnant, and so believed her son was his reincarnation.","Taibai Jinxing","en/d/taibai-jinxing.html"],["Ossetian parallel","The scholar James R. Russell sees a parallel between Covinar&#x27;s conception and a similar episode involving the Ossetian figure Satanaya in the Nart sagas.","Tsovinar","en/d/tsovinar.html"],["Two mountains named after him","According to Tzetzes, one of the two mountains of Euboea was called Opheltes in his honour. The other was called Zarex.","Opheltes","en/d/ofeltes.html"],["A famous poem","Kuzunoha&#x27;s farewell poem has become well known. The folklorist Kiyoshi Nozaki translated it as an invitation to come and find her in the great wood of Shinoda.","Kuzunoha","en/d/kuzunoha.html"],["A name for the alphabet","According to the Suda, Actaeus named the Phoenician letters in honour of his daughter Phoenice, who had died a virgin.","Actaeus","en/d/acteo.html"],["A cobra on the brow","His image showed him as a falcon crowned with the solar disk and a cobra, called Uadyet, on the forehead.","Khor-akhti","en/d/horajti.html"],["A humorous name","His name translates as &quot;great honker&quot; or &quot;great cackler&quot;, a title that stresses his bird-like appearance.","Gengen-Wer","en/d/gengen-wer.html"],["Two genealogies","The same name can refer to a son of Poseidon or to one of the Oceanids, daughters of the Titan Oceanus. Both figures serve to explain the island&#x27;s name.","Chios","en/d/quios.html"],["Name with variants","The manuscripts record several spellings of her name, such as Norcia or Nyrtia, which makes the exact form hard to fix.","Nortia","en/d/nortia.html"],["A race on pottery","On the François Vase, dated to around 565 BC, Automedon appears in a version of the chariot race held at the funeral games of Patroclus, finishing second.","Automedon","en/d/automedonte.html"],["A possible Etruscan origin","It has been proposed that the name Capua derives from an Etruscan word for the hawk. The idea is a linguistic hypothesis, not a proven fact.","Capys","en/d/capis.html"],["Origin of the name","Varro linked the name Venilia to the verb <em>venire</em> (&quot;to come&quot;) and to the word <em>ventus</em> (&quot;wind&quot;), and Plautus quotes a line that plays on that idea.","Venilia","en/d/venilia.html"],["A sign, not a goddess","In the Rig Veda, the word lakshmi refers to a mark of gratitude or good omen. The divine figure appears later, in subsequent texts.","Lakshmi","en/d/lakshmi.html"],["A sphere, not a disc","The scholar Hugh Nibley argued that &#x27;globe&#x27;, &#x27;orb&#x27; or &#x27;sphere&#x27; is a more accurate translation of aten than &#x27;disc&#x27;, since in reliefs the Aten appears curved.","Aten","en/d/aton.html"],["The beer of Hesat","The milk the goddess gave was called &quot;the beer of Hesat&quot;, a poetic expression tied to her role as nourisher.","Hesat","en/d/hesat.html"],["Capricorn","One version says Zeus placed Aegipan among the stars, and his image is linked to the constellation Capricorn.","Aegipan","en/d/egipan.html"],["Presence in art","Her name appears on the Heritage Floor of Judy Chicago&#x27;s art installation The Dinner Party.","Ḫannaḫanna","en/d/hannahanna.html"],["Several spellings","In Irish it is written in several forms, such as <em>Claidheamh Soluis</em> or <em>cloidheamh solais</em>, and in Scottish Gaelic it is rendered as &quot;glaive of light&quot;.","Claíomh Solais (Sword of Light)","en/d/claiomh-solais-espada-de-la-luz.html"],["Reincarnation in the Vedas","Svetaketu&#x27;s story is, according to the sources, the first time reincarnation appears in the Vedas.","Svetaketu","en/d/svetaketu.html"],["A hero of unmixed wine","In Munichia the hero Akratopotes, the &quot;Drinker of unmixed wine&quot;, was honored, according to the same account that mentions Ceraon.","Ceraon","en/d/ceraon.html"],["A matter of pigs","In her form as Vajravārāhī she appears with a sow&#x27;s head, a symbol of ignorance in Buddhism that she transforms into wisdom.","Vajrayogini","en/d/vajrayoguini.html"],["A greeting in Vrindavan","In the city of Vrindavan, people greet each other in the street with the cry Radhe Radhe, a custom that keeps her devotion alive.","Radha","en/d/radha.html"],["A link to Rome","According to Plutarch, this sense of moderation also lay at the root of Roman religion.","Eulabeia","en/d/eulabeia.html"],["Son of a god","He was the son of Hermes, the divine messenger, and was raised by his grandfather Phylas after his mother married Echekles.","Eudoros","en/d/eudoro.html"],["Bell-shaped ears","His name comes from ghanta (bell) and karna (ear), referring to his bell-shaped ears.","Ghantakarna Mahavira","en/d/ghantakarna-mahavira.html"],["Indian and Greek relatives","Scholars compare Ūsiņš with the Indian Ashvini and the Greek Dioscuri, since all of them transport the sun.","Ūsiņš","en/d/usins.html"],["A spared house","The Achaeans spared Demoleon&#x27;s house because his family had given hospitality to Odysseus and Menelaus.","Demoleon","en/d/demoleon.html"],["Another name","It is also called the Tryambakam Mantra, after the epithet of Rudra that appears in the verse.","Mahamrityunjaya Mantra","en/d/maja-mritiun-yaia-mantra-del-maja-mritiun-yaia.html"],["A similar being in Somalia","Somali mythology has a similar creature that can kill with a single touch. It was believed to be the offspring of a jinn called shiqq and a human being.","Nasnas","en/d/nasnas.html"],["A fresco at Delphi","Pausanias described a painting by Polygnotus at Delphi in which Epeius appeared naked, tearing down the walls of Troy.","Epeius","en/d/epeo.html"],["The Sun from an egg","According to the <em>Kalevala</em>, a teal laid its eggs on the knee of Ilmatar, the daughter of the air. When the egg fell, the white became the Moon and the yolk became the Sun.","Päivätär","en/d/paivatar.html"],["A Javanese volcano","According to the Tantu Pagelaran, the gods moved part of Meru to Java to steady the island, which was drifting on the ocean. That fragment is said to be today&#x27;s Mount Semeru, the tallest peak on Java.","Mount Meru","en/d/monte-meru.html"],["A roofless temple","The Roman shrine had no roof, as oaths were believed to belong under the sky.","Dius Fidius","en/d/dius-fidius.html"],["Wind jewel","Quetzalcoatl wears a spirally voluted breastplate called the ehecacozcatl, a cut conch shell linked to whirlwinds and other natural forces. Similar shells have been found in burials across Mesoamerica.","Quetzalcoatl","en/d/quetzalcoatl.html"],["An old-fashioned Christmas","In 1616 Ben Jonson dressed his Old Christmas in round hose, long stockings and white shoes, styles that were already out of date.","Father Christmas","en/d/father-christmas.html"],["A drained lake","The Acherusia lake in Thesprotia no longer exists: it was drained in the 1960s by the British company Boots Ltd, and today a network of irrigation canals crosses the plain.","Acherusia","en/d/aquerusia.html"],["Historicity","No contemporary sources from the 8th century BC mention this king, so he is probably a mythical figure.","Acron","en/d/acron.html"],["Temple in the agora","In Sicyon, Peitho had a temple in the agora, the civic centre of the city.","Suadela","en/d/suadela.html"],["A gem on his head","He is described with a gem called nagamani, or serpent&#x27;s ornament, on his head.","Vasuki","en/d/vasuki.html"],["The sacred lettuce","Its milky sap, which the ancients compared to semen, led to lettuce being linked with him and offered during his festivals.","Min","en/d/min.html"],["A closed lotus","Those who doubt Amitābha are reborn in a lotus that stays closed. They live inside it as in a palace, but without the Buddha&#x27;s presence, until they dispel their doubts.","Amitābha","en/d/amitabha.html"],["Several names","Hindu scriptures also call him Himavant (&quot;icy&quot;), Himavān (&quot;snowy&quot;), Himaraja (&quot;king of snow&quot;) and Parvateshwara (&quot;god of mountains&quot;), depending on the text and the aspect emphasized.","Himavat","en/d/himavat.html"],["Terracotta horses","In the Rarh region countless terracotta and wooden horses are used as symbolic offerings, something scholars consider peculiar to Dharmaraj.","Dharmaraj","en/d/dharmaraj.html"],["Indo-European root","Her name is linked to the Manes, Mana Genita and Manius. The Greek and Latin forms of Mania derive from the Proto-Indo-European root *men-, meaning «to think».","Mania","en/d/mania.html"],["Father of Þrúðr","In the Prose Edda, Thor can be given the poetic epithet &quot;father of Þrúðr&quot;, a phrase the poet Eysteinn Valdason used in a poem about the god.","Þrúðr","en/d/rur.html"],["Two lineages","Some accounts place Abarta among the Tuatha Dé Danann and others among the Fomorians, which makes him hard to classify.","Abarta","en/d/abarta.html"],["A father born of the earth","In some accounts his father was Palaechthon, whose name means roughly &quot;ancient earth&quot;. Pelasgus presents himself as a child of the earth that brought him forth.","Pelasgus","en/d/pelasgos.html"],["Meaning of the name","Her Greek name, Kymopoleia, can be translated as «strength of the waves», which fits her link with the sea.","Cymopoleia","en/d/cimopolea.html"],["A monster in a list","In Ovid&#x27;s poem, the ophiotaurus is one of the lesser monstra that threaten both men and gods, set apart from the more terrifying monsters.","Ophiotaurus","en/d/ofiotauro.html"],["A month named after him","In Lagash, the month of Dumuzid was the sixth month of the year. The month and its festival passed to the Babylonians under the name Tammuz.","Tammuz","en/d/tammuz.html"],["Six months of each sex","Tradition assigns Logun Ede an alternation of six months as a man and six months as a woman, which sets him apart from most orishas.","Logun Ede","en/d/logun-ede.html"],["Altar at Phaleron","The Athenians dedicated an altar to him at Phaleron, showing that he received heroic cult.","Androgeus","en/d/androgeo.html"],["An Antarctic peak","A peak on the Trinity Peninsula in Antarctica is named after the goddess.","Bendis","en/d/bendis.html"],["An asteroid named after her","The asteroid Fedra was named in her honour after the Cretan princess.","Phaedra","en/d/fedra.html"],["Alternative names","Apollodorus considered several names for this Harpy: Ocypete, Ocythoe and Ocypode, all referring to speed.","Ocypete","en/d/ocipete.html"],["Three colours, three energies","The pindis differ in colour: white stands for Sattva, yellowish red for Rajas and black for Tamas, each representing a different cosmic energy.","Vaishno Devi","en/d/vaishno-devi.html"],["Root with a human shape","The mandrake was described as a plant with human features, since its roots resembled a pair of legs.","Euresis","en/d/euresis.html"],["A god of serpents","His most frequent symbol on the kudurru stones is the serpent, and some texts suggest he could be shown with a partly serpentine body.","Ishtaran","en/d/ishtaran.html"],["A bush that speaks","In the Aeneid, the spears that killed Polydorus took root in the ground and turned into plants, so the bush bleeds when uprooted.","Polydorus","en/d/polidoro.html"],["A god with many heads","He is shown with the head of a falcon, ram, lion or cat. In some images the falcon head is combined with ram&#x27;s horns.","Ra-Horakhty","en/d/ra-horajty.html"],["Two spellings","The name of this goddess is also written Semia, and sources sometimes use Semla.","Semla","en/d/semia.html"],["A bow with a borrowed name","The legendary bow Ichaival in the video game Fire Emblem: Genealogy of the Holy War probably takes its name from Ydalir. Ichii is the Japanese word for yew.","Ydalir","en/d/ydalir.html"],["A name with a horse&#x27;s voice","According to the Adi Parva, at birth he let out a cry resembling the neighing of the celestial horse Uchchaihshravas, which gave rise to his name.","Ashvatthama","en/d/ashuatama.html"],["A riddle about a leek","In the riddle that Odin, in disguise, poses in the Hervarar saga, the phrase &quot;Delling&#x27;s door&quot; is part of the clues. The answer is the leek, whose head is fixed in the ground while its leaves seek the sun.","Dellingr","en/d/delling.html"],["The Dog Days","The Greeks called the hottest days of summer the Sirian period and linked them to certain illnesses. The Egyptians also connected this period with sicknesses.","Sopdet (Sothis)","en/d/sopdet-sotis.html"],["Mix-up with Nanook","Christian missionaries identified Nanook, the polar bear spirit, as the supreme deity of the Inuit. According to the sources, Silap Inua is much closer to that role.","Silap Inua","en/d/silap-inua.html"],["Doors of war","The doors of the Temple of Janus in Rome were opened in wartime and closed when peace arrived.","Janus","en/d/jano.html"],["A goddess without a tongue","According to Ovid, Jupiter ripped out her tongue, and that is why her name refers to silence.","Dea Tacita","en/d/dea-tacita.html"],["A Lisbon viewpoint","The Miradouro de Santa Catarina in Lisbon is known as Adamastor because of its large statue, and offers views of the Tagus, the 25 de Abril Bridge and the Cristo-Rei.","Adamastor","en/d/adamastor.html"],["Related name","Her name comes from a Proto-Indo-European earth goddess and is related to Thracian Zemele, meaning &quot;mother earth&quot;.","Žemyna","en/d/zemyna.html"],["Shifting spellings","After Długosz, several writers repeated the name with different spellings: Dzidzilia, Zizilia, Zyzylia or Zezylia.","Dzidzilela","en/d/dzidzilela.html"],["A name without a clear author","Although the English name is credited to T. E. Lawrence, it never appears in his published writings.","Atlantis of the Sands","en/d/la-atlantida-de-las-arenas.html"],["A name with two readings","Her name was written dNIN.GUL, and the sign GUL could be read as sun or sumun in Sumerian, both meaning wild cow.","Ninsun","en/d/ninsun.html"],["A name in Bleach","Bleach&#x27;s commander general, Genryusai Shigekuni Yamamoto, takes his name from Yama and plays a similar role.","Enma","en/d/enma.html"],["Samba Dashami","In Odisha, Samba Dashami is celebrated on the tenth day of the waxing fortnight of the month of Pausha.","Samba","en/d/samba.html"],["Three fingers","In the illustrations, the uwan has only three fingers on each hand. It has been suggested that this refers to oni, which also have three fingers.","Uwan","en/d/uwan.html"],["Two gods, one name","Cicero explained that Saturn, called Cronus in Greek, bore that name because Krónos is the same as chrónos, meaning a span of time. The similarity of the names has caused confusion ever since.","Chronos","en/d/crono-chronos.html"],["A lost bronze statue","The original Athenian statue, made of bronze, is lost, but Roman marble copies survive and show how it was composed.","Eirene","en/d/irene.html"],["The letter yud","Each of the Four Worlds corresponds to one letter of God&#x27;s four-lettered divine name. Adam Kadmon is represented by the transcendental tip of the first letter, yud.","Adam Kadmon","en/d/adam-kadmon.html"],["A possible echo in Tacitus","If the name is emended to <em>Ið[is]avǫllr</em>, it would match Idisiaviso, the site on the Weser river where, in 16 CE, the forces of Arminius and Germanicus fought, according to Tacitus&#x27; <em>Annals</em> book II. It is a philological hypothesis, not a certainty.","Iðavöllr","en/d/iavollr.html"],["Infinity in modern Hebrew","In present-day Israel, &quot;ein sof&quot; is used in everyday speech with the mathematical sense of infinity, without any reference to God.","Ein Sof","en/d/ein-sof.html"],["A word with several etymologies","Du Cange derived it from the Latin guirriar, &#x27;to laugh&#x27;. Other scholars prefer guerrire, &#x27;to frolic&#x27;, because of the leaps these figures made.","Guirrio","en/d/guirrio.html"],["A stone that still stands","The Sessho-seki split in two on March 5, 2022, though cracks visible for several years earlier make a natural cause highly likely.","Tamamo-no-Mae","en/d/tamamo-no-mae.html"],["A name with two possible meanings","Kára may derive from Old Norse afkárr, «wild», or from kárr, «curl». According to Otto Höfler, the second option connects with the Odinic cult name Odinkar, «the one with Odin&#x27;s curls».","Kára","en/d/kara.html"],["Roman coins","She frequently appears on Roman-era coins, identified as Concordia.","Homonoia","en/d/homonoia.html"],["A name that sounds like a paradox","Virbius means &quot;twice a man&quot;, a label that sums up the hero&#x27;s fate: killed and brought back to life.","Virbius","en/d/virbio.html"],["A medical term","In medicine, <em>crepitus</em> refers to the creaking or popping sounds made by the joints.","Crepitus","en/d/crepitus.html"],["A corrected finger","In the church of Székelyderzs, a painted ceiling showed a figure with six fingers. When it was renovated, the picture was &#x27;corrected&#x27; to five.","Táltos","en/d/taltos.html"],["A name with European echoes","Related forms of the name appear elsewhere in Europe, such as the Black Forest (Schwarzwald), suggesting it may have been a general term for dark, dense forests.","Myrkviðr","en/d/myrkvir.html"],["A constellation with scales","According to Aratus, the scales Dike carried became the constellation Libra, placed beside Virgo.","Dike","en/d/dice.html"],["Two seas","The Arabic name of Bahrain means &#x27;two seas&#x27;. The region, where fresh groundwater mixes with salt water, is linked to Dilmun, the original site of creation for the Sumerians.","Tiamat","en/d/tiamat.html"],["Traditional protections","To stop a strzyga returning, the corpse was beheaded and the head burned, or it was buried face down with a sickle around its head. Other measures included placing a flint in the mouth of the dead.","Strzyga","en/d/estrige.html"],["Double-headed axe","His characteristic weapon is the oxê, a double-bladed axe that identifies him in iconography and on altars.","Shango","en/d/chango.html"],["Name of a serpent","His name is usually derived from Basque words meaning serpent and male, that is, male serpent. The idea that it comes from fire and flame is considered folk etymology.","Sugaar","en/d/sugaar.html"],["A constellation","The chen, or Dragon star, is an asterism in traditional Chinese constellations, associated with the east and spring.","Shen","en/d/shen-q1077710.html"],["A horse head","A star named Tiranna may have been shown on kudurru stones as a horse head surrounded by a gate, although scholars disagree about its meaning.","Manzat","en/d/manzat.html"],["A transparent name","Her epithet Amaterasu Ōmikami literally means &quot;The Great August Goddess Who Shines in Heaven,&quot; with none of the etymological opacity of many names in the ancient texts.","Amaterasu","en/d/amaterasu.html"],["Cosmic form","His image is compared to Krishna&#x27;s <em>visvarupa</em>, the revelation of the god&#x27;s universal form in the <em>Bhagavad Gita</em>.","Mada","en/d/mada.html"],["Single source","Libertalia comes almost exclusively from an 1724 book that includes genuine accounts of other pirates alongside this episode, which raises doubts about its truth.","Libertalia","en/d/libertalia.html"],["A calf etymology","One ancient explanation of the name Italy links it to a word meaning calf, an idea recorded by Timaeus, Varro and Festus.","Italus","en/d/italo.html"],["A stone instead of a statue","In Phrygia, the goddess was worshipped as an unshaped stone of black meteoric iron, which was later taken to Rome.","Magna Mater","en/d/magna-mater.html"],["Swan name","His Greek name, Kýknos, means &quot;swan&quot;, and tradition explains it by the bird the fishermen saw above the abandoned boy.","Cycnus","en/d/cicno-q1066344.html"],["A modern origin","Although linked to ancient history, the gashadokuro first appeared in print in the second half of the twentieth century, through magazine writers and yōkai encyclopedias.","Gashadokuro","en/d/gashadokuro.html"],["A pierced eye","According to Tibetan legend, Padmasambhava pierced her right eye so she could subdue Tibetan demons more effectively.","Ekajaṭī","en/d/ekajati.html"],["Unlocked doors","In Bhaguda, it is said, residents do not lock their shops or homes out of respect for the goddess.","Mogal Maa","en/d/mogal-maa.html"],["A giant with a telling name","Celsclan, whose name means &quot;son of Cel&quot;, appears on a 5th-century BC bronze mirror confronting Laran, the god of war.","Cel","en/d/cel.html"],["A name with a story","His name means &#x27;joined by Yara&#x27;, after the cannibal demoness who found him. According to the Vishnu Purana, Yara was a daughter of Mritu, Death.","Jarasandha","en/d/yarasandha.html"],["Other names","When he made cattle fertile he was called <em>Inuus</em>. Some scholars have also identified him with Favonius, one of the Roman wind gods.","Faunus","en/d/faunus.html"],["A reconstructed name","The form Thesis never appears in ancient texts. The attested form is Tetis, and the modern name comes from interpreting the Greek word thesis, meaning &quot;arrangement, ordering&quot;.","Thesis","en/d/thesis.html"],["Hathor&#x27;s headdress","During the New Kingdom, Isis took on traits that had originally belonged to Hathor, the preeminent goddess of earlier periods, and began to be shown wearing her headdress: a sun disk between the horns of a cow.","Isis","en/d/isis.html"],["A name shared with Odin","The name Helblindi appears as one of the many epithets of Odin in the Grímnismál and the Gylfaginning, which complicates whether he was a distinct figure.","Helblindi","en/d/helblindi.html"],["Name explained","His name comes from Sanskrit: ghata means pot or jar, and utkacha means bald, referring to his head.","Ghatotkacha","en/d/gatotkacha.html"],["First Filipino horror film","The silent film Manananggal (1927), directed by José Nepomuceno, is considered the first Filipino horror film. Actress Mary Walter plays the creature with its upper torso detached.","Manananggal","en/d/manananggal.html"],["Hybris or Hubris","The name appears in sources with two spellings, Hybris and Hubris. The second is the form that reached English with the sense of excessive pride.","Hybris","en/d/hybris.html"],["Miraculous sake","According to an old local story, the woman who lived with the elderly couple brewed a sake said to cure every ailment.","Toyouke Ōmikami","en/d/toyouke-omikami.html"],["An annual festival","The story remains present in a New Year&#x27;s ceremony that recalls the escape from the valley.","Ergenekon","en/d/ergenekon.html"],["The scars","The <em>twa màk</em> are the marks that, according to some legends, Èzili Freda left on her during their dispute over Ogou.","Èzili Dantò","en/d/ezili-danto.html"],["A spring at Cyphanta","According to one tradition, a spring rose at Cyphanta where Atalanta struck a rock with her javelin when she was thirsty.","Atalanta","en/d/atalanta.html"],["A river with her name","The name was also given to a river in northwestern India, mentioned in the Mahabharata.","Urvashi","en/d/urvashi.html"],["A mother&#x27;s name","The name Manungal may be a contraction of <em>ama Nungal</em>, meaning &quot;mother Nungal&quot;.","Nungal (Manungal)","en/d/nungal-manungal.html"],["A temple in Madrid","The temple of Debod, which contains the chapel where Arensnuphis appears, was moved from Egypt and now stands in Madrid.","Arensnuphis","en/d/arensnufis.html"],["A name linked to mountains","In Proto-Germanic, Fjörgyn is related to a word meaning mountain. Its Old High German cognate Firgunnea gave its name to the Ore Mountains, on the border between Germany and today&#x27;s Czech Republic.","Fjörgynn","en/d/fjorgynn.html"],["A name with many traces","The root uellauno- appears in Celtic place names, tribal names and personal names, such as Cassivellaunos, the British leader who opposed Julius Caesar.","Vellaunus","en/d/vellaunus.html"],["Animal heads on human bodies","Chinese figures in the Victoria and Albert Museum&#x27;s collection show the zodiac animals with human bodies and animal heads.","Horse","en/d/caballo.html"],["A bird with a human head","The <em>ba</em> was often depicted as a human-headed bird that flew out of the tomb to join the <em>ka</em> in the afterlife.","Components of the human being in ancient Egypt","en/d/componentes-del-ser-humano-en-el-antiguo-egipto.html"],["Name and tree","The princess&#x27;s name matches the Greek word for walnut tree, <em>karya</em>, and the myth uses this to explain her transformation.","Carya","en/d/carya.html"],["A city bearing his name","Pharaoh Amenemhat I founded a city called Heh near the third cataract of the Nile, and Senusret III consolidated it a century later.","Heh","en/d/heh.html"],["A giant brought back to life","In a Latin text in the Book of Armagh, Saint Patrick raises from the dead a giant who says he was killed during Cairbre&#x27;s reign.","Cairbre Nia Fer","en/d/cairbre-nia-fer.html"],["Five faces","His five faces are associated with the four directions and upward, and are named the panchabrahmas, the five creators.","Sadashiva","en/d/sadashiva.html"],["An auspicious animal","The Chinese generally regard sheep and goats as lucky animals, so their year is linked with promise and prosperity.","Goat","en/d/cabra.html"],["A shared name","The name Attis was also given to the two birds into which Philomela and Procne were transformed, according to the story.","Attis","en/d/atis-q495133.html"],["A starship","In the series <em>Stargate SG-1</em>, an Asgard ship takes its name from Thor&#x27;s hall and appears as the Beliskner.","Bilskirnir","en/d/bilskirnir.html"],["A name shaped like a letter","Her name was linked to the shape of her legs, which resembled the Greek letter lambda. According to ancient grammarians, that letter was originally pronounced labda.","Labda","en/d/labda.html"],["Equivalence at Emar","A lexical text from Emar presents her as analogous to Lelluri, a mountain goddess of Hurrian tradition.","Ninmena","en/d/ninmena.html"],["A disc of Iphitos","According to Aristotle, an ancient disc linked Lycurgus to the founding of the Olympic Games. It is now considered probably a forgery from the fourth century BC.","Lycurgus","en/d/licurgo.html"],["Yellow colour","Umay is associated with the sun, so yellow is her colour and she is called the Yellow Maiden.","Umay","en/d/umay.html"],["Greek and Latin name","The older form of the goddess&#x27;s name passed into Greek as Iernē and into Latin as Hibernia, the classical name of the island.","Ériu","en/d/eriu.html"],["A blind man as model","The tenome takes the form of a zatō, a masseur or biwa tutor, trades that in the Edo period were often reserved for blind people through guilds.","Tenome","en/d/tenome.html"],["A change of animal","Until the middle of the Eighteenth Dynasty he was shown as a ram, and later as a bull.","Bata","en/d/bata.html"],["A proposal from 1939","In 1939 the scholar William F. Albright proposed that Qetesh was a form of the &#x27;lady of Byblos&#x27;, a Canaanite goddess. Later, René Dussard linked her to Asherah.","Qetesh","en/d/qadesh.html"],["A disputed name","The meaning of Embla is unclear. &quot;Elm&quot; or &quot;vine&quot; are proposed, and some linguists link it to a word meaning something like &quot;the fire-maker&quot;.","Ask and Embla","en/d/ask-y-embla-q10272315.html"],["Pot-egg","One possible etymology links their name to “pot-egg,” a euphemism for testicles, which explains their image with organs as big as pots.","Kumbhanda","en/d/kumbhanda.html"],["A giant black bull","According to one legend, Khenti-kheti could take the form of a giant black bull called Kemwer.","Khenti-kheti","en/d/jentijeti.html"],["The flying throne","The Shahnameh describes a wooden and gold throne with four long poles at its corners, tipped with meat that eagles chained to its feet strained to reach, lifting the structure into the air.","Kay Kavus","en/d/kay-kavus.html"],["A primate with his name","The gray langur carries the scientific name Presbytis entellus, in honor of this figure from the Aeneid.","Entellus","en/d/entelo.html"],["Odin&#x27;s boy","In the Codex Regius, considered the best manuscript, he is called sveinn Óðins, &#x27;Odin&#x27;s boy&#x27;, which may also mean &#x27;Odin&#x27;s servant&#x27;.","Hermóðr","en/d/hermor.html"],["Name with a folk etymology","The Homeric Hymn to Aphrodite links his name to an adjective meaning &#x27;terrible&#x27;, for the &#x27;terrible grief&#x27; that his mortality causes the goddess.","Aeneas","en/d/eneas.html"],["Missing hands","The hands that once held her divine attributes on the Muri statuette are lost, so we do not know what the goddess carried.","Naria","en/d/naria.html"],["A name meaning «truly glorious»","His name comes from <em>eteós</em> («true») and <em>kleos</em> («glory»), so it can be rendered as «truly glorious».","Eteocles","en/d/eteocles.html"],["An island with several namesakes","Ancient sources offer up to three different Naxos as possible eponyms of the island, each with a different genealogy.","Naxos","en/d/naxos.html"],["A telling name","The Old Norse name can be translated as &#x27;the one making an unlucky journey&#x27; or &#x27;unlucky traveller&#x27;, a reference to the horse&#x27;s misfortune during the night of the chase.","Svaðilfari","en/d/svailfari.html"],["Three hundred and sixty-five cantons","According to Polyaenus, the territory was split into 365 cantons, one for each day of the year, each with its annual tax.","Pandaie","en/d/pandaia.html"],["Other names","Her name also appears as Aegialeia or Aegialia, and one account calls her Euryaleia.","Aegiale","en/d/egiale.html"],["Statue with a monkey","A temple in Tokyo has a binbōgami statue with a monkey riding its head, a pun since saru means both to leave and monkey.","Binbōgami","en/d/binbogami.html"],["Several identities","Her name appears as Leanira or Laodamia, and Arcas&#x27;s wife may be given other names depending on the source.","Leanira","en/d/leanira.html"],["An ocean named after a king","The Atlantic Ocean takes its name from the &quot;Sea of Atlas&quot;, a designation that links the ocean to this monarch.","Atlas of Mauretania","en/d/atlas-de-mauritania.html"],["Carved in stone","She appears with a lion and a blackbuck in a rock-relief panel at the Varaha Mandapam in Mahabalipuram.","Korravai","en/d/korravai.html"],["A name that means horse","In Aramaic, the word raḵšā, related to the name Rakhsh, simply means «horse».","Rakhsh","en/d/rakhsh.html"],["Home on Mount Fuji","Tradition places Kuninotokotachi on the summit of Mount Fuji, one of Japan&#x27;s most iconic mountains.","Kuninotokotachi","en/d/kunitokotachi.html"],["A single weak point","Talos was invulnerable except for a vein running down to his ankle. In Apollonius, only a thin layer of skin protected it.","Talos","en/d/talos.html"],["A euphemistic name","Euonymus means &#x27;well-named&#x27; in ancient Greek. It is a euphemistic epithet, a way of naming someone with kind words.","Euonymus","en/d/euonimo.html"],["Two stones on the Areopagus","According to Pausanias, the accused and their accusers stood on two rough stones, called Hybris and Anedea.","Anaideia","en/d/anaideia.html"],["Son of Rohini","Balarama is given the matronymic epithet Rauhineya, meaning son of Rohini.","Rohini","en/d/rohini.html"],["A name made of numbers","His name contains a numeral that could be read as 40. A modern proposal reads it as &quot;servant of two thirds&quot;, alluding to Gilgamesh being two thirds god.","Urshanabi","en/d/urshanabi.html"],["Three hares, one motif","One of the best-known medieval images is the three hares motif, which appears in a stained-glass window in Paderborn Cathedral in Germany.","Easter Bunny","en/d/conejo-de-pascua.html"],["A star with a season&#x27;s name","Homer calls Sirius &#x27;the star of the opora&#x27;, although its rising does not coincide exactly with the season.","Opora","en/d/opora.html"],["A family breastplate","The breastplate that saved his life had been a gift from Euphetes of Ephyra to his father, Phyleus.","Meges","en/d/meges.html"],["A region of Pluto","Meng-p&#x27;o Macula, one of the equatorial dark regions of Pluto, is named in her honor.","Meng Po","en/d/meng-po.html"],["A mural at Teotihuacan","Centuries later, a mural discovered at Teotihuacan was found to seem to depict Tlaloc&#x27;s paradise, offering a visual image of what the chronicles described.","Tlalocan","en/d/tlalocan.html"],["Shadow of fortune","It was believed that merely catching a glimpse of its shadow would make a person happy for the rest of their life.","Huma","en/d/homa.html"],["A name that endures","The hill where the trial was held still bears the name Areopagus, derived from the god Ares.","Alcippe","en/d/alcipe.html"],["Butter for the sun","In spring, butter was smeared on doorposts because it melts in sunshine. People believed this helped the goddess regain her strength.","Beaivi","en/d/beiwe.html"],["A broken jaw","In the childhood story, Indra struck him with his thunderbolt after he mistook the sun for a fruit, and his name is said to come from the wound to his jaw.","Hanuman","en/d/hanuman.html"],["A river with her name","The Rigveda uses the word asikni to describe the Chenab River, a name linked to the sense of dark.","Asikni","en/d/asikni.html"],["Spelling variants","His name is also spelled Ronové, Ronwe and Ronoweh.","Ronove","en/d/ronove.html"],["The last of the suitors","Leodes is the last suitor Odysseus kills on his return. His death closes the slaughter in the palace.","Leodes","en/d/leodes.html"],["Imperial rank","In the former Shinto ranking system it held imperial second-rank status, placing it among the officially most important shrines.","Ikasuri no Kami","en/d/ikasuri-no-kami.html"],["A name meaning will","Vili means &quot;will&quot; in Old Norse, a word related to Gothic <em>wilja</em> and Old English <em>willa</em>.","Vili","en/d/vili.html"],["Two names for one princess","Some sources call her Eurycyda and others Eurypyle, a variant that appears in the account of Endymion&#x27;s genealogy.","Eurycyda","en/d/euricida.html"],["A god without a temple","Despite his importance, Geb never received a temple of his own. His oldest known image is a fragmentary relief from the Third Dynasty, found at Heliopolis.","Geb","en/d/geb.html"],["A cup with his name","An Attic cup in the Louvre shows Eos holding the body of her son, with inscriptions that include the words &quot;Memnon&quot; and &quot;kalos&quot;, meaning &quot;beautiful&quot;.","Memnon","en/d/memnon.html"],["Household amulet","The ancient Egyptians carried small statuettes of Nefertem as good-luck charms.","Nefertem","en/d/nefertum.html"],["Cicero and the wandering stars","Cicero used the star as an argument: if the Moon was a goddess, so were Lucifer and the other wandering stars.","Lucifer","en/d/lucifer.html"],["Absent swallows","According to the Phocians, swallows in their territory neither nest nor lay eggs, because even Philomela, once a bird, feared Tereus and stayed away from his country.","Daulis","en/d/daulide.html"],["A meaningful name","His Old Norse name means &quot;sudden day.&quot; In the poem, he gives the watchman the false name Vindkald, &quot;wind-cold.&quot;","Svipdagr","en/d/svipdagr.html"],["A possible human origin","Some researchers link Vipunen to Ikämieli, a Sámi shaman of the 16th or 17th century who appears in Finnish folktales. This is a hypothesis, not an established fact.","Antero Vipunen","en/d/antero-vipunen.html"],["Two mothers for Ganesha","According to the Skanda Purana, Ganesha has two mothers, Parvati and Ganga, which is why he is called Gangueia, &#x27;son of Ganga&#x27;.","Ganga","en/d/ganga.html"],["A shared reign","The three brothers took turns holding power, each ruling for one year, in a cycle lasting between twenty-nine and thirty years depending on the source.","Mac Gréine","en/d/mac-greine.html"],["One portion too many","Gandhari asked for a daughter besides the hundred sons, and Vyasa set aside an extra piece of the flesh so that Duhsala would be born.","Duhsala","en/d/duhsala.html"],["A video game with his name","Abzû is an underwater adventure released in 2016 that takes its name and inspiration from Sumerian mythology.","Apsu","en/d/apsu.html"],["Two names for one river","According to Plutarch, the Ismenus was once called the Foot of Cadmus, in honour of Cadmus, founder of the Cadmea at Thebes.","Ismenus","en/d/ismeno.html"],["Origin of the name","The name Sobrarbe is explained by the Latin phrase <em>super arborem</em>, which the legend reads as &#x27;above the tree&#x27;, referring to the cross that appeared over the oak.","Kingdom of Sobrarbe","en/d/reino-de-sobrarbe.html"],["The mouth of time","Aken is described as the &#x27;mouth of time&#x27;, an unusual image that links him directly to the passing of hours and cycles.","Aken","en/d/aken.html"],["South African rock painting","At Brakfontein Ridge in South Africa there is a rock painting of an unknown creature that some link to the dingonek, including its walrus-like tusks.","Dingonek","en/d/dingonek.html"],["An island that kept moving","As maps of the Indian Ocean improved, the imagined location of Argyre shifted further and further east, to the edge of the known world.","Argyre","en/d/argyre.html"],["A mix of goddesses","On the Birrens relief, the goddess wears Minerva&#x27;s gorgon and Victoria&#x27;s wings, yet her mural crown ties her to Brigantian territory.","Brigantia","en/d/brigantia.html"],["Meaning of the name","Her Greek name, Eurydikē, is usually rendered as &#x27;wide justice&#x27;, combining <em>eurys</em>, &#x27;wide&#x27;, and <em>dike</em>, &#x27;justice&#x27;.","Eurydice of Troy","en/d/euridice-de-troya.html"],["Diomedes&#x27; necessity","The Greek proverb arose from the episode in which Diomedes forced Odysseus to walk ahead of him at sword point. It describes doing something under compulsion rather than by choice.","Palladium","en/d/paladio.html"],["Comparison with Himiko","Some scholars link her with Queen Himiko, mentioned in 3rd-century Chinese sources, since both were princesses or priestesses and their names are associated with the sun. Other authors propose different historical figures.","Yamatohime-no-mikoto","en/d/yamatohime-no-mikoto.html"],["Many names","His name appears in several spellings, such as Elotha, Elier or Elada. Some sources write Elathan, a form that is actually the genitive and means &#x27;of Elatha&#x27;.","Elatha","en/d/elatha.html"],["A name with a stomp","Kazimieras Būga linked the name to the root <em>trimp-</em>, related to the Lithuanian verb <em>trempti</em>, meaning to trample.","Potrimpo","en/d/potrimpo.html"],["A name with meaning","Teyrnon derives from the Common Brittonic <em>tigernonos</em>, meaning “great lord”, which reflects his high rank as a nobleman.","Teyrnon Twryf Liant","en/d/teyrnon-twryf-liant.html"],["An oak that speaks","According to the myth, a piece of sacred oak set into the ship could speak to warn Jason in moments of danger.","Argus","en/d/argos-q1075014.html"],["A name meaning “mouth”","His name is interpreted as “lord of the mouth,” an allusion to grain as the sustenance of the people.","Neper","en/d/neper.html"],["Coins of Larissa","Drachmas from the city of Larisa showed the nymph seated, holding a hydria with a spring nearby.","Larissa","en/d/larisa.html"],["Wolf&#x27;s heart","According to the saga, the remedy Svipdag gave to Ingjald&#x27;s shame was a roasted wolf&#x27;s heart, a symbolic cure remembered as the origin of his fierce temper.","Ingjald Illråde","en/d/ingjald-illrade.html"],["Knife-eared calves","The folklorist John Gregorson Campbell described the calves of these unions as knife-eared, with ears that looked sliced off.","Water bull","en/d/toro-de-agua.html"],["A name born from tears","Ravana means «the one who cried», a name he received after crying out in pain beneath the mountain.","Ravananugraha","en/d/ravananugraha.html"],["A name with several readings","It has been suggested that her name comes from a Greek verb meaning to shout or call. Another hypothesis links it to the Aeolic form of <em>hebe</em>, meaning youth.","Aba","en/d/aba.html"],["A scallop shell","Many Venus Anadyomenes include a scallop shell, which is read in some interpretations as a symbol of the female vulva.","Venus Anadyomene","en/d/venus-anadiomena.html"],["A namesake on Lesbos","Another Hicetaon was a prince of Methymna on the island of Lesbos, son of King Lepetymnus. According to the account, Achilles killed him when he attacked the islands close to the mainland.","Hicetaon","en/d/hicetaon.html"],["Medieval spelling","In the fifteenth century, the sh sound was written ss, so the name appears in that form in the sources. Today it is written Jesza or Jasza.","Jesza","en/d/jesza.html"],["Museum in Samarkand","The Afrasiab Museum in Samarkand holds finds from the ancient city that bears his name, including the city&#x27;s well-known murals.","Afrasiab","en/d/afrasiab.html"],["Gifts of money","During the Principate, the New Year&#x27;s gifts called <em>strenae</em> often took the form of money.","Strenua","en/d/strenua.html"],["Witness to a birth","In the Homeric hymn, the goddesses come to Delos to bear witness that Apollo&#x27;s birth is lawful.","Ichnaea","en/d/icnea.html"],["Consolation prize","In the <em>Aeneid</em>, Salius receives a lion skin as a consolation prize for losing the race after being tripped.","Salius","en/d/salio.html"],["Tibetan name","In Tibetan he is called Pak Kyepo, meaning &#x27;Noble Birth&#x27;.","Virūḍhaka","en/d/virudhaka.html"],["Disputed legions","The grimoires do not agree on how many legions he commands. Some give him thirty-one, while the Livre des Esperitz gives thirty-six.","Agares","en/d/agares.html"],["A blood-filled cucumber","The insult ordered by Doña Lambra is to throw a cucumber filled with blood at Gonzalo González. Some modern scholars read a sexual allusion in this scene.","The Seven Lara Princes","en/d/los-siete-infantes-de-lara.html"],["Name linked to Mitra","His name is related to Mitra, an ancient Vedic and Iranian god, and to the Sanskrit word maitri, meaning friendship.","Maitreya","en/d/maitreya.html"],["A word with history","The English word syringe, meaning a hypodermic device, derives from the nymph&#x27;s name.","Syrinx","en/d/siringa.html"],["A vegetarian goddess","Over time, Khodiyar and other goddesses of western India became vegetarian, and their fierce character softened.","Khodiyar","en/d/khodiyar.html"],["Origin in a snail","The sazae-oni is believed to originate from turban snails that lived around thirty years.","Sazae-oni","en/d/sazae-oni.html"],["A renamed city","The city formerly called Pityussa was renamed Lampsacus in her honor.","Lampsace","en/d/lampsace.html"],["Sacred wood","The idol is carved from neem wood and replaced with a new one roughly every 12 or 19 years.","Jagannath","en/d/jagannath.html"],["Seven trunks","The sources describe Airavata with several trunks and tusks, though the number varies by text, from three heads to seven trunks.","Airavata","en/d/airavata.html"],["Xuan Cabritu","In Asturias, the nuberu is also known as Xuan Cabritu. Some versions place his home in distant places such as Egypt, though there is no evidence of Phoenician traders in the area.","Nuberu","en/d/nuberu.html"],["A name with meaning","Admete comes from the Greek Ἀδμήτη, linked to the idea of being unbroken and not tamed.","Admete","en/d/admete.html"],["A medieval giraffe","Some Arthurian scholars see the beast as a reflection of medieval ideas about giraffes, whose Latin name, camelopardalis, suggests a hybrid of camel and leopard.","Questing Beast","en/d/questing-beast.html"],["An echo among the Vandals","Paul the Deacon, writing in the 7th century, mentions the Vandal kings Assi and Ambri, whose names may derive from the same root as Embla.","Ask and Embla","en/d/ask-y-embla.html"],["Mask against tigers","Some tribes tie a mask with his face to the back of their heads. The aim is to confuse or scare an approaching tiger and ward off its attack.","Dakshin Rai","en/d/dakshin-rai.html"],["The Pityusic Islands","The Greek word <em>pitys</em> also named these islands in the western Mediterranean.","Pitys","en/d/pitis.html"],["Swedish place names","The Swedish place names Lytisberg and Lytislunda may contain elements of the god&#x27;s name.","Lytir","en/d/lytir.html"],["The Feast of Lamps","Herodotus describes an annual celebration in honour of Neith in which participants lit numerous lamps throughout the night.","Neith","en/d/neit.html"],["A different Herse","There is another Herse, a heroine and daughter of King Cecrops, whom the Athenians worshipped. She should not be confused with the goddess of dew.","Ersa","en/d/herse-q180325.html"],["The name of the sage","Shukra means &quot;bright&quot; and, in another sense, &quot;semen&quot;, in reference to the way he emerged from Shiva&#x27;s body in a Purana story.","Shukra","en/d/sukra.html"],["A god who arrived late","According to Plutarch, Penthus was absent when Zeus handed out the gifts, and when he arrived he had to settle for an honour no one else wanted.","Penthus","en/d/pento.html"],["A name with two meanings","The Greek name Macaria can be translated as &quot;happiness&quot; or &quot;blessed one&quot;, but it can also mean &quot;foolishness&quot;.","Macaria","en/d/macaria-q3274055.html"],["A name with meaning","Plato comes from the Greek Plátōn, meaning «broad-shouldered». The same name was later borne by the famous philosopher, although the mythological figure has no connection with him.","Plato (son of Lycaon)","en/d/platon-hijo-de-licaon.html"],["A royal mausoleum","The Mausoleum of the Yellow Emperor was established in Shaanxi to commemorate the ancestral legend.","Three Sovereigns and Five Emperors","en/d/tres-augustos-y-cinco-emperadores.html"],["A fish that keeps growing","In the story, the fish Matsya grows without stopping: first it fits in a copper vessel, then a pond, a lake and the ocean. It is the avatar of Vishnu who guides Manu through the flood.","Manu","en/d/manu.html"],["A nurse with two names","Some sources also give her the name Macris as an equivalent.","Nysa","en/d/nisa.html"],["An early oracle","If the Delphic oracle on the division of land at Aigys is genuine, it would be the oldest known oracle from Delphi.","Charilaus of Sparta","en/d/carilo-de-esparta.html"],["A reluctant warrior","In the <em>Iliad</em>, Agamemnon finds him in the rear ranks, apparently avoiding combat, and in another episode Menestheus trembles before Sarpedon and calls on Ajax and Teucer for help.","Menestheus","en/d/menesteo.html"],["A name shaped like an abyss","The Greek χάος is related to a verb meaning to gape or open wide, so its original sense is an opening rather than disorder.","Chaos","en/d/caos.html"],["A king with two possible fathers","Depending on the version, the father of Saon, Rhene&#x27;s son, could be either Hermes or Zeus. The first king of Samothrace thus had a disputed divine origin.","Rhene","en/d/rene.html"],["Death in Japan","In Japanese Buddhism it was believed that Amida appeared on a purple cloud at the moment of death to lead the departed to the Western Paradise.","Sukhavati","en/d/sukhavati.html"],["Link to the atlatl","His name seems to derive from the possessive form of <em>ahtlatl</em>, the spear-thrower, which is why he is linked to arrows.","Atlahua","en/d/atlahua.html"],["Eye of the lotus","Vishnu is called Padmaksha, «lotus-eyed», because he offered to pluck out one of his eyes to complete his worship of Shiva.","Sudarshana Chakra","en/d/sudarsana-chakra.html"],["A mountain range named after her","The Rhodope Mountains are now shared between Bulgaria and Greece, and their name comes from the legend of the queen turned into a mountain.","Rhodope","en/d/rodope.html"],["The pain rock","The stone she sits on is said to have a hole through which pains can be banished.","Kivutar","en/d/kipu-tytto.html"],["Goddess on coins","Eleutheria appeared on coins of Alexandria, where she had a brief presence as a personification.","Eleutheria","en/d/eleutheria.html"],["An epithet of Apollo","Pausanias explains that the epithet of Apollo &quot;Carneus&quot; might come from sacred cornel trees in a grove of the god on Mount Ida, which the Greeks cut down to build the horse.","Trojan Horse","en/d/caballo-de-troya.html"],["Sheep or apples","In Greek, the word <em>mēlo</em> can mean either «sheep» or «apple», so the golden sheep have also been read as golden apples.","Atlas","en/d/atlante.html"],["A name with a foot","Folk etymology links the name empusa to the Greek for &quot;one foot&quot;, although her epithets refer more to the donkey&#x27;s leg.","Empusa","en/d/empusa.html"],["A name of power","His name is explained as &#x27;the preeminently powerful&#x27;, formed from pra, meaning increase, and diumna, meaning splendour, strength or majesty.","Pradyumna","en/d/pradiumna.html"],["A synonym for battle","In Skáldskaparmál, her name is used as a synonym for battle, an unusual use for a personal name.","Svipul","en/d/svipul.html"],["A striking synchronism","Eusebius of Caesarea placed Criasus&#x27;s reign at the same time as the birth of Moses in Egypt, showing how chronographers linked the histories of different peoples.","Criasus","en/d/criaso.html"],["An asteroid with his name","The asteroid (7543) Prylis, discovered in 1973, was named after this seer.","Prylis","en/d/prilis.html"],["A name that speaks of the trade","Some interpretations read <em>Amatsumara</em> as «eye divination» and connect it with a one-eyed blacksmith, reflecting the dangers of working hot metal.","Amatsumara","en/d/amatsumara.html"],["Protected snakes","The tradition arising from her deed required that snakes found near Byzantium not be killed, as they were considered benefactors.","Phidalia","en/d/fidalia.html"],["A mandala in stone","The Buddhist complex of Borobudur in Indonesia is considered a mandala on the scale of an entire temple.","Womb Realm","en/d/reino-del-vientre.html"],["A bird&#x27;s name","The painted bunting carries the scientific name Passerina ciris, which Linnaeus chose in memory of Scylla.","Scylla of Megara","en/d/escila-de-megara.html"],["A labour with a hostage","Heracles&#x27; ninth labour aimed at Hippolyta&#x27;s belt, but in the story the hero ended up taking Melanippe, her sister, hostage.","Melanippe","en/d/melanipa.html"],["A lion named after its place","The Suda calls it the Thespian lion and the Ravine lion, because it lived in a place called <em>charadra</em>, or ravine.","Lion of Cithaeron","en/d/leon-de-citeron.html"],["A sukkal recognized by his face","Most sukkal are identified in art by their staff of office. Isimud is an exception, recognized by his two faces, although he does not always hold the staff.","Isimud","en/d/isimud.html"],["A temple in Malta","In Mtarfa, a suburb of ancient Melite, there was a temple dedicated to Proserpina. Its ruins were quarried away between the 17th and 18th centuries.","Libera","en/d/libera.html"],["Priam&#x27;s father-in-law","Altes was the father of Laothoe, one of many wives of the Trojan king Priam, which makes him the father-in-law of one of the best-known rulers of the Trojan War.","Altes","en/d/altes.html"],["Sister or wife","In the Yajurveda she appears as Rudra&#x27;s sister, while the Taittirīya Āraṇyaka calls her Rudra&#x27;s wife.","Ambikā","en/d/ambika.html"],["A legal saying","According to the Prose Edda, her name gave rise to an expression: a person who proved an accusation false was said to &#x27;make a syn&#x27;.","Syn","en/d/syn.html"],["A disputed name","The editors of the Library attributed to Apollodorus split the name into two figures, although the manuscripts show a different form: Hestia Eretusa.","Hesperethusa","en/d/hespere-q5896560.html"],["Seven names, many lists","The Puranas do not agree on the names of the seven realms. The Shiva Purana replaces Mahatala with Tala, and the Vayu Purana uses different names such as Gabhastala or Sritala.","Patala","en/d/patala.html"],["The White Hare of Inaba","In the Kojiki, a flayed hare receives help from Ōnamuji and predicts his marriage to the princess. It is the character&#x27;s best-known episode.","Ōkuninushi","en/d/okuninushi.html"],["A lost message","Karbusický proposed that, combined, the ancestral names form an Old Slavic text with the phrase «Krok kazi tetha lubossa premisl», which would be a message of peace to the Franks.","Vnislav","en/d/vnislav.html"],["Ronier leaves","The costume is usually made from ronier leaves, a palm whose fronds completely cover the person performing the Kumpo.","Kumpo","en/d/kumpo.html"],["A death in her grove","Gaius Gracchus was killed in the Grove of Furrina, showing that the site was significant in late Republican Rome.","Furrina","en/d/furrina.html"],["A name with meaning","His name is usually interpreted as &#x27;half-speech&#x27; or &#x27;half-language&#x27;, though the meaning is not entirely certain.","Llŷr","en/d/llyr.html"],["Name of a mountain","Mount La Verna, in Tuscany, held an ancient sanctuary of the goddess. In 1213 Count Orlando of Chiusi gave it to St Francis, who chose it as the site for his religious order.","Laverna","en/d/laverna.html"],["A difficult epithet","The epithet Gricenchos has an uncertain meaning. Macalister renders it as &quot;clapperleg&quot;, while Comyn gives &quot;of withered feet&quot;; O&#x27;Donovan left it untranslated.","Cichol Gricenchos","en/d/cichol-gricenchos.html"],["A wife of Theseus","Plutarch says that Iope, daughter of Iphicles, was one of the wives of Theseus, linking this hero to another great Athenian tradition.","Iphicles","en/d/ificles.html"],["A spider turned octopus","In one version, the monster was a net-weaving spider before the gods transformed it into an octopus.","Akkorokamui","en/d/akkorokamui.html"],["Easter witches","In Sweden and Swedish-speaking Finland, children dress as witches, old women and old men at Easter to commemorate the witches&#x27; journey to Blåkulla.","Blockula","en/d/blockula.html"],["Shared name","According to some sources, mother and daughter could both be called Agraulos, and the form Aglauros was more commonly used for the daughter.","Aglaurus","en/d/aglauro.html"],["The meaning of his name","His Greek name, Χρύσης (Khrýsēs), means “golden.”","Chryses","en/d/crises.html"],["A name that survives","The island still bears the ancient name Thera, although it is better known today as Santorini.","Theras","en/d/teras.html"],["Three forms in Uruk","In archaic Uruk she was worshipped in three forms: morning Inanna, evening Inanna and princely Inanna. The first two partly reflected the phases of Venus.","Inanna","en/d/inanna.html"],["A moon of Saturn","In 1847 his name was chosen for the moon of Saturn closest to the planet, one of the best-known bodies in the Solar System.","Mimas","en/d/mimas.html"],["A place named after him","Pausanias reports that in his time the Reitoi area was still called the Palaces of Crocon, although he could not find the hero&#x27;s tomb.","Crocon","en/d/crocon.html"],["Immortal","Some versions say Vishnu granted him the status of Chiranjivi, one of seven immortals.","Bali (Mahabali)","en/d/bali-mahabali.html"],["Two different Enkis","Sources distinguish between Enki of Eridu and a primordial god with a similar name, written differently in the Emesal dialect. Some scholars think Enki was originally an epithet that eventually replaced the god&#x27;s original name.","Enki","en/d/enki.html"],["A boneless son","According to scholars, Ragnar insisted on bedding Aslaug right after the wedding, against her advice, and their first son, Ivar, was born weak and boneless.","Aslaug","en/d/aslaug.html"],["A severed arm as a weapon","On a 4th-century BC Etruscan mirror, Menrva fights the giant Akrathe using the arm she has torn from him. This image appears only in works from Etruria.","Menrva","en/d/menrva.html"],["A famous piece of advice","According to Herodotus, Solon advised Croesus not to call anyone blessed before they died. The saying has been quoted ever since.","Cydippe","en/d/cidipe-q5796492.html"],["A giant from a single source","Alpos appears only in Nonnus of Panopolis&#x27;s Dionysiaca and had no known cult, so some scholars consider him an invention of the poet himself.","Alpos","en/d/alpo.html"],["A name related to Welsh","The root ritu- survives in Welsh rhyd, Cornish rid and Breton rit, all meaning ford.","Ritona","en/d/ritona.html"],["Mother of Ninkasi","A hymn to Ninkasi states that her parents were Ninti and Enki, although she was raised by Ninhursag.","Ninti","en/d/ninti.html"],["A festival for a victory","The Apaturia was a celebration of the Ionian cities linked to Melanthus&#x27; victory over Xanthus. Ephesus and Colophon stayed out of it.","Xanthus","en/d/janto.html"],["Footprints in stone","Tradition holds that the footprints he left dancing after his cure are still visible on a rock at Dōgo.","Sukunabikona","en/d/sukunabikona.html"],["A name that is a trade","Dictys means &#x27;net&#x27; in Greek, a term that fits his role as a fisherman.","Dictys","en/d/dictis.html"],["A god almost always paired","Belobog appears in the sources almost always alongside Chernobog, which makes him hard to study on his own.","Belobog","en/d/belobog.html"],["Meaning of the name","The Greek name Alcidice suggests something like &#x27;powerful justice&#x27;.","Alcidice","en/d/alcidice.html"],["A name from nets","Diodorus Siculus explains that Dictynna comes from the Greek word diktya, meaning nets, because she was said to have invented hunting nets.","Britomartis","en/d/britomartis.html"],["Unusual hairstyle","At the Srirangam temple, Ranganayaki wears an unusual hairstyle: her hair is tied in a side-bun (kondai) positioned to her left.","Ranganayaki","en/d/ranganayaki.html"],["Compared to a mermaid","In 1888, the Journal of American Folklore described the Apotamkin as similar to a mermaid.","Apotamkin","en/d/apotamkin.html"],["A word with distant relatives","Erebus is etymologically linked to words in Sanskrit, Armenian and Old Norse, all tied to darkness or evening.","Erebus","en/d/erebo.html"],["A god who extends lives","According to one account, Shou can change a person&#x27;s age recorded on his tablet, for example from 18 to 81, if offerings are made to him.","Sanxing (Fushoulu)","en/d/sanxing-fushoulu.html"],["Enemy in Kirby","In the Kirby games <em>Dreamland 3</em> and <em>UHFR9D</em> there is an enemy called Jumpershoot that is based on the karakasa.","Karakasa-obake","en/d/karakasa-obake.html"],["Curse on Ravana","In a Puranic story, Nandi cursed Ravana for his impatience, and the prophecy came true when Hanuman set Lanka on fire.","Nandi","en/d/nandi.html"],["Origin of coconuts","Coconuts got their name from the three indentations on the shell, which look like a hairy, dark face. Portuguese sailors linked them to the coco, a ghost of the same kind.","Bogeyman","en/d/bogeyman.html"],["An ancient relief","A sixth-century BC metope from the temple of Apollo at Thermos shows Chelidon and Aëdon plotting together. It is one of the oldest depictions of the episode.","Chelidon","en/d/quelidon.html"],["A Germanic temple","Tacitus stated in the <em>Germania</em> that the Germanic peoples had no temples, yet in the <em>Annals</em> he describes one dedicated to Tanfana.","Tanfana","en/d/tanfana.html"],["A disputed name","Plutarch proposed that Mana came from <em>fluere</em>, &quot;to flow&quot;, an explanation the grammarian Verrius Flaccus also connected with the <em>manes</em>, the souls of the dead.","Mana Genita","en/d/mana-genita.html"],["A sixth buddha","Some sources, such as the Vajrasekhara Sutra, mention Vajradhara as a sixth buddha, seen as the source of the other five.","The Five Tathāgatas","en/d/los-cinco-tathagatas.html"],["Another name","According to the Wakan Sansai Zue, tenaga is also known as chōhi, a name that roughly means long arm.","Ashinaga and Tenaga","en/d/ashinaga-y-tenaga.html"],["A mother for Niobe","Some late sources say Europa was the mother of Niobe, but not all versions record this.","Europa","en/d/europa-q11921267.html"],["Sister of Dirce","Strophia shared a father with Dirce, and the two presided over the region&#x27;s two main springs near Thebes.","Strophia","en/d/estrofia.html"],["Two arrows","In the Thidrek saga, Egil keeps a second arrow in case he misses, meant for the king.","Agilaz","en/d/agilaz.html"],["Symbol in the crown","She is often depicted with a small image of Parshvanatha placed in her crown.","Padmavati","en/d/padmavati-q7123883.html"],["A red spring","According to an apocryphal tradition, Perseus washed the monster&#x27;s blood off in a spring near Joppa, which turned red as a result.","Cepheus","en/d/cefeo.html"],["The Sibylline Oracles","The Suda attributes the Sibylline Oracles to the Hebrew Sibyl. They should not be confused with the Roman Sibylline Books.","Persian Sibyl","en/d/sibila-persica.html"],["A lyre with a history","According to Diodorus, the lyre Hermes gave ended up at Lyrnessus, and Achilles took it when he sacked the city.","Corybas","en/d/corybas.html"],["A name in motion","Verðandi is the present participle of the Old Norse verb verða, &#x27;to become&#x27;, which is why it is usually translated as &#x27;what is happening&#x27;.","Verðandi","en/d/verandi.html"],["A goddess dressed like maize","Sahagún compares her headdress and ornaments to a maize plant, with quetzal feathers arranged like an ear of corn.","Huixtocihuatl","en/d/huixtocihuatl.html"],["Fine snow","Gói, his daughter according to the Orkneyinga saga, has a name meaning &#x27;thin snow&#x27;, in the same vein as her father&#x27;s name.","Þorri","en/d/orri.html"],["Origin of the name","Her name is said to come from her loud way of speaking, expressed in Malayalam as <em>uchathil</em>.","Uchitta Bhagavathy","en/d/uchitta-bhagavathy.html"],["Demon-trapping bowls","Hundreds of Aramaic incantation bowls from the Sasanian Empire mention the lilim, and they were buried upside down.","Lilin","en/d/lilim.html"],["A name with an echo","It has been proposed that her name may have given rise to the root that became Mexico, though this remains a hypothesis.","Metztli","en/d/metztli.html"],["Two Lugaids","The Cóir Anmann tells of Lugaid Loígde, one of five sons of Dáire Doimthech, all named Lugaid. A druid prophesied that one would rule, and the winner was the son who caught a fawn appearing at Tailtiu. Keating doubts this is the same man, since he has a different father.","Lugaid Laigde","en/d/lugaid-laigde.html"],["No ancient precedents","No mentions have been found in works older than the Edo period, so its appearance is relatively late.","Chōchin-obake","en/d/chochin-obake.html"],["Antlered females","In nature it is rare, but some female deer grow antlers when they experience unusually high levels of testosterone. In Greek literature such animals are usually linked to Artemis.","Ceryneian Hind","en/d/cierva-de-cerinea.html"],["A golden book","According to tradition, Aisyt keeps a golden book in which she records each person who is born, as if noting their destiny from the very first moment.","Aisyt","en/d/aisyt.html"],["A name with meaning","His name translates as &quot;the wisdom and thoughtfulness of many people&quot;, an idea that sums up his role as a collective counselor.","Omoikane","en/d/omoikane.html"],["The banana peels","Absorbed in Krishna, Sulabha peeled bananas and offered him the peels instead of the fruit. When Vidura came home, he found Krishna eating them.","Vidura","en/d/vidura.html"],["The Oracle of the Potter","An Egyptian nationalist text predicts the fall of Alexandria, with Kneph and Agathos Daimon leaving the city for Memphis.","Agathodaemon","en/d/agatodemon.html"],["Name for the underworld","The name Calu was used as a synonym for the underworld itself, much as Hades was in Greek.","Calu","en/d/calu.html"],["Spear names","Many valkyrie names allude to the spear, a weapon closely associated with Odin.","Valkyrie","en/d/valquiria-q10655862.html"],["One eye","In two of the German accounts Hagen has only one eye, a trait that reinforces his image as a grim and violent figure.","Hagen","en/d/hagen.html"],["A demon named Martín","In tale XLV of <em>El conde Lucanor</em>, the demon serving a man is called Martín, a name used for the devil in the Middle Ages.","Martinico","en/d/martinico.html"],["Olympic victory","In the story, Strophius returns home honored after winning at the Olympic Games, a detail that underlines his prestige before he meets Electra.","Strophius","en/d/estrofio.html"],["Raised by Astraia","According to the story, the Virgin Astraia, lady of justice, fed her honey and taught her words of law.","Beroe","en/d/beroe.html"],["A goddess named for skis","She is called Öndurguð and Öndurdís, meaning &#x27;ski god&#x27; and &#x27;ski goddess&#x27;, because she travelled over the mountains on skis.","Skaði","en/d/skai.html"],["Two versions of one name","Athenaeus and Diodorus do not agree on who named the island: for one it was the god Glaucus, and for the other her own son Chthonius.","Syme","en/d/sime.html"],["A translation error","In most English translations of Jujutsu Kaisen, the name Makora is mistakenly rendered as Mahoraga, and that form is the best known among Western fans.","Mahoraga","en/d/mahoraga.html"],["Seven serpents","In the calendrical language, the number seven was linked to seeds, and &#x27;seven serpents&#x27; was a secret name for maize.","Chicomecoatl","en/d/chicomecoatl.html"],["First of 108 pithas","The Devi Bhagavata Purana lists Viśālākṣī of Varanasi as the first of its 108 Shakti Pithas.","Viśālākṣī","en/d/visalaksi.html"],["An island or a coastline","For a long time Conand&#x27;s tower was placed on Tory Island. Henry Morris instead proposed a small island off the coast of Sligo, called Derinish, derived from Tor-Inis.","Conand","en/d/conand.html"],["A crossed upbringing","Ilione raised her brother as if he were her son and her own son as if he were her brother. As a result, Deipylus and Polydorus grew up as brothers.","Ilione","en/d/ilione.html"],["A possible origin for the Danube","The ancient name of the Danube river, <em>Danuuius</em>, has been linked to the same root as the goddess&#x27;s name, although its Celtic origin is not certain.","Dana (Danu)","en/d/dana-danu.html"],["A bird without a certain name","The Latin term <em>mergus</em>, later used as the name of a genus of diving ducks, does not reveal with certainty which bird was meant. A cormorant or Scopoli&#x27;s shearwater have both been proposed.","Aesacus","en/d/esaco.html"],["A vow of long hair","During the voyage of the Argonauts, Eurytion vowed to let his hair grow until he was home again.","Eurytion","en/d/eurition.html"],["Three and a half pillars","The Danish legend explains why the church in Kalundborg has only three and a half pillars: the troll hurled the last one into the air.","Fin","en/d/fin.html"],["Sicania","According to tradition, Sicily was first called Sicania, after the legendary king Sicanus, before taking the name linked to the Sicels.","Siculus","en/d/siculo.html"],["A name meaning speed","According to the etymology in the sources, <em>raghu</em> originally meant &#x27;swift&#x27; in the Rig Veda, and the name may have been given to the king for his skill as a chariot driver.","Raghu","en/d/raghu.html"],["A name with meaning","The name Rangda means widow in Old Javanese, a clue to her origin in the story of Calon Arang.","Rangda","en/d/rangda.html"],["A god added by translators","Many translations add «the god of stupidity» to Demosthenes&#x27; speech, but that phrase does not appear in the original Greek.","Koalemos","en/d/coalemo.html"],["Other mothers","Some accounts give Libya or Lysianassa as the mother of Busiris instead of Anippe.","Anippe","en/d/anipe.html"],["An animal without a name","Egyptologists have not been able to identify with certainty the animal that represents Set, with its curved snout and squared-off ears. Proposals include the aardvark, the donkey and the greyhound.","Set","en/d/set.html"],["A name with many forms","Her name is spelled in dozens of different ways. Some variants arose from confusion with the genitive, which was often taken for a nominative.","Ethniu","en/d/ethniu.html"],["A bull with servants","The Buchis bull, regarded as an embodiment of Montu, had dozens of attendants and wore precious crowns and bibs.","Montu","en/d/montu.html"],["Origin of the name","The Greek name of the myth means &#x27;ant&#x27;, a word derived from the Proto-Indo-European root *morwi-, meaning the same.","Myrmex","en/d/mirmex.html"],["A river named after her","A river in Thrace or Moesia took its name from the goddess, and the Zlatna Panega in modern Bulgaria still carries that Greek root.","Panacea","en/d/panacea.html"],["Honored in antiquity","Her sacrifice was widely celebrated in antiquity, and she appears on the Chest of Cypselus in a scene showing the funeral rites of Pelias.","Alcestis","en/d/alcestis.html"],["A birth-bath pond","Near Inada Shrine in Okuizumo lies a pond called Ubuyu-no-Ike, where she is said to have received her first bath after birth.","Kushinadahime","en/d/kushinadahime.html"],["A name meaning good omen","Shiva means auspicious or benevolent, and the name replaced Rudra, the terrible god of the Vedic texts, as a way of softening his figure.","Shiva","en/d/shiva.html"],["A name popularised","Outside Inuit languages, the name Sedna became common thanks to Franz Boas, who recorded her stories.","Sedna","en/d/sedna-q1971615.html"],["Heads that grow back","In one variant, the severed heads of the Chudo-Iudo regrow if someone draws a line on them with the dragon&#x27;s fiery finger.","Zmei Gorynich","en/d/zmei-gorynich.html"],["Foundation and shepherds","Florus links the founding of Rome to April 21, the day of the Parilia, an ancient shepherds&#x27; festival.","Pales","en/d/pales.html"],["Long title of the sutra","One of its longer Sanskrit titles is <em>Sarvadurgatipariśodhana Uṣṇīṣa Vijaya Dhāraṇī Sūtra</em>, which refers to the purification of all unfortunate paths of rebirth.","Uṣṇīṣavijayā","en/d/usnisavijaya.html"],["Another Cleta","A nurse of Penthesilea, queen of the Amazons, was also called Cleta. According to one account, her ship was blown off course to Italy, where she founded the city of Clete.","Cleta","en/d/cleta.html"],["Deadly cry","According to the sources, Nemain&#x27;s war cry could kill a hundred men at once.","Nemain","en/d/nemain.html"],["An unfinished roof","The child&#x27;s name is explained by the cormorant-feather roof that was not complete when he was born. The image sums up the episode of the birth on the beach.","Ugayafukiaezu","en/d/ugayafukiaezu.html"],["A city named after a brother","According to the account, the town Brangas founded was named Olynthus, in honour of his brother killed by the lion.","Brangas","en/d/brangas.html"],["A ghat named after him","Akrura Ghat, in Vrindavan, is one of the region&#x27;s sacred bathing sites and is linked to the revelation of Krishna and Balarama&#x27;s divine forms.","Akrura","en/d/akrura.html"],["Origin of the name","His name seems to derive from Latin <em>foras</em>, meaning &#x27;outside&#x27;, though its origin is not firmly established.","Foras","en/d/foras.html"],["A trinity of fates","The Roman Parcae were also called the tria Fata. Each presided over a stage of life: Nona over birth, Decima over marriage and Morta over death.","Morta","en/d/morta.html"],["Sword of the serpent","In Old Japanese, «kusa» means sword and «nagi» means serpent, so an alternative theory reads its name as &#x27;serpent sword&#x27;.","Kusanagi-no-tsurugi","en/d/kusanagi-no-tsurugi.html"],["A tech company","The name of the company Nvidia comes from Invidia, the Roman goddess of envy.","Invidia","en/d/invidia.html"],["A unit of time","In the traditional Hindu system, one kala corresponds to 144 seconds.","Kala","en/d/kala.html"],["A name for a moon","Deimos is the smaller of the two moons of Mars. Like Phobos, its name was chosen by Asaph Hall, who discovered it in 1877.","Deimos","en/d/deimos.html"],["A golden roof","According to the Gylfaginning, the Gjallarbrú bridge has a roof that shines like gold.","Modgud","en/d/modgud.html"],["A name with a chariot","His name combines bṛhat, &#x27;great&#x27;, and ratha, &#x27;chariot&#x27;, so it is interpreted as &#x27;the one with a great chariot&#x27;.","Brihadratha","en/d/brihadratha.html"],["A pagoda that traps","His pagoda can capture any spirit, demon or god within its walls.","Li Jing","en/d/li-jing.html"],["A rationalist version","Palaephatus, who sought to explain Greek myths rationally, rejected the hollow horse and described a horse too large to pass through the city gates.","Sinon","en/d/sinon.html"],["Name","Alakshmi combines the negative prefix a- with Lakshmi, meaning &quot;not Lakshmi&quot; or &quot;anti-Lakshmi&quot;.","Alakshmi","en/d/alaksmi.html"],["Husband of five princesses","According to one source, Yadu married five daughters of a Naga king, a serpent-like being of Hindu mythology.","Yadu","en/d/iadu.html"],["The Star of the West","Saturn was called the “Star of the West”, a name that fitted Horus’s association with the western sky.","Horkapet","en/d/horkapet.html"],["A dinosaur named for him","The dinosaur Erlikosaurus was named in honour of this god of death.","Erlik","en/d/erlik.html"],["Coins with a Latin name","In the Middle Ages, <em>moneta</em> still meant both &#x27;money&#x27; and &#x27;die&#x27;. The phrase <em>moneta nova</em> appears on coins from the Low Countries and the Rhineland in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries.","Moneta","en/d/moneta.html"],["A name that says it all","Atropos means something like &#x27;without turn&#x27; or &#x27;inexorable&#x27;. Servius distinguished her from Mors: Atropos would be natural death or the dissolution of things, while Mors was violent death.","Atropos","en/d/atropos.html"],["Hansi hoard","Excavations at Asigarh Fort in Haryana uncovered 58 bronze idols, many depicting him, buried to protect them from Ghaznavid invasions.","Chandraprabha","en/d/chandraprabha.html"],["A plain named after him","The Teneric plain, northwest of Thebes, was named after him, according to Pausanias.","Tenerus","en/d/tenero.html"],["Distinctive colour","In art he is shown with a reddish complexion, which sets him apart in his statues.","Padmaprabha","en/d/padmaprabha.html"],["Dressed as a woman, voiced as a man","In Chinese theatre, Lan appears in feminine clothes, but the actor speaks in a masculine voice without attempting a feminine style of speech.","Lan Caihe","en/d/lan-caihe.html"],["Etymology","His name derives from <em>mārta</em>, &#x27;dead or undeveloped&#x27;, and <em>ānda</em>, &#x27;egg or bird&#x27;, so it denotes a dead sun or one sunk below the horizon.","Martanda","en/d/martanda.html"],["A storybook name","In children&#x27;s literature the cobbler appears as Dratewka, the hero of a play by Maria Kownacka published in 1935.","Wawel Dragon","en/d/dragon-de-wawel.html"],["A river that changed course","According to ancient geographers, the Simois changed its course so much that it no longer flows into the Scamander and now empties directly into the Hellespont.","Simois","en/d/simois.html"],["A telling name","Haemon comes from the ancient Greek <em>Haímôn</em>, meaning &#x27;bloody&#x27;, a name that fits the tragic fate of the character.","Haemon","en/d/hemon.html"],["Favourite word","Will Shortz, editor of The New York Times crossword, has said that Ucalegon is his favourite word in English.","Ucalegon","en/d/ucalegon.html"],["Temple at Tiruverkadu","The Karumariamman Temple in Tiruverkadu, India, is considered her abode.","Karumariamman","en/d/karumariamman.html"],["An accidental find","In 2000, a prolonged drought shifted the course of the Halil river in southern Iran and exposed pottery that triggered a massive looting spree until the Iranian government stepped in during 2001.","Aratta","en/d/aratta.html"],["An island named after a dog","The island where the couple lived was named Inis Saimera after Saimer, Dealgnaid&#x27;s dog.","Dealgnaid","en/d/dealgnaid.html"],["The missing Pleiad","One tradition holds that the star that cannot be seen is not Merope but Electra, who supposedly covered her face on seeing Troy destroyed.","Merope","en/d/merope.html"],["The inventor of applause","Crotus clapped when the Muses sang, and others copied the gesture. For this he is considered the creator of applause.","Crotus","en/d/croto.html"],["&quot;Cream of nectar&quot;","Polyphemus calls the wine Odysseus brings him &quot;cream of nectar&quot;, praising its quality.","Nectar","en/d/nectar.html"],["Euhemerist version","Palaephatus records a version in which Cottus and Briareus are mortal men who lived in the city of Hecatonchiria, from which they would have taken their name.","Cottus","en/d/coto.html"],["A goat&#x27;s name","His name is linked to the male goat, because in one version he was suckled by a goat after being abandoned.","Aegisthus","en/d/egisto.html"],["A war cry with an owl&#x27;s call","Athenians were said to have adopted the cry to imitate the hoot of the owl, the bird of their goddess Athena.","Alala","en/d/alala.html"],["Clever thief","The thief managed to get past the guards by plying them with the wine from the wineskins he carried on donkeys.","Rhampsinitus","en/d/rampsinito.html"],["Horus on the crocodiles","In the Late Period, his close link with Horus the Child led to the so-called &#x27;Horus on the crocodiles&#x27; stelae, where he appears.","Shed","en/d/shed.html"],["A unique case","Scholars regard the story of Iphis and Ianthe as the only Greco-Roman myth that deals with female same-sex desire.","Iphis","en/d/ifis.html"],["A messenger bee","The mother goddess Hannahanna sent a bee to locate the missing god, and it was the bee that found him.","Telipinu","en/d/telipinu.html"],["A city bearing his name","Agrinio, the largest city in Aetolia, takes its name from Agrius, keeping his memory alive in modern Greek geography.","Agrius","en/d/agrio.html"],["An asteroid named for her","The asteroid 15 Eunomia, and a whole family of asteroids, take their name from the goddess.","Eunomia","en/d/eunomia.html"],["A name with several readings","Yggdrasil is usually read as &quot;Odin&#x27;s horse&quot;, alluding to Odin riding between the worlds. Others translate it as &quot;tree of terror&quot; or &quot;gallows of the hanged&quot;, and some scholars propose an even older origin.","Yggdrasil","en/d/yggdrasil.html"],["Osogbo festival","The annual festival in the Osogbo sacred grove draws devotees for two weeks and is one of the major events of Yoruba worship.","Òṣun","en/d/osun.html"],["An invented witness","Captain John Nisbet, who in 1674 claimed to have seen the island, was in fact a character in a story written by Irish author Richard Head.","Hy-Brasil","en/d/isla-brasil.html"],["The abbreviation EO","On the liver of Piacenza her name is abbreviated as EO. Ethausva appears alongside Thalna, but Thanr is not included.","Ethausva","en/d/ethausva.html"],["A bronze boat","Abcán is described with a bronze boat and a tin sail, an unusual detail in stories of this kind.","Abcán","en/d/abcan.html"],["Double-meaning name","Her name combines <em>chalcos</em>, meaning both &#x27;copper&#x27; and &#x27;bronze&#x27;, with <em>medousa</em>, meaning &#x27;guardian&#x27; or &#x27;protectress&#x27;.","Chalcomedusa","en/d/calcomedusa.html"],["An asteroid named for her","The asteroid 466 Tisiphone is named after this Erinys.","Tisiphone","en/d/tisifone.html"],["Blancaflor in the ballads","In oral ballads from Spain and Latin America, Procne is usually called Blanca Flor or Blancaflor, and Tereus often takes other names or appears as a foreigner.","Procne","en/d/procne.html"],["Divination bones","The term vala is linked to the knucklebones used in divination.","Völva","en/d/volva.html"],["A mosaic in Mérida","Pontus appears on a polychrome Roman mosaic from Mérida, where only some parts of his body survive, such as his feet and one hand.","Pontus","en/d/ponto.html"],["Trees as shelter","Zhuangzi describes the Youchao gathering nuts during the day and resting in trees at night to escape predators.","Youchao","en/d/youchao.html"],["A disputed name","From 1726 onward, some scholars proposed reading OLLOVBO instead of OLLOVDIO on the Antibes inscription. Several accepted this reading and took it as a place-based surname.","Olloudios","en/d/olloudios.html"],["Fons Perennis","In Mithraic sanctuaries, inscriptions were dedicated to Fons Perennis, a water treated as divine and able to hear, like the nymphs.","Fontus","en/d/fonto.html"],["A divided kingdom","The legend says Argos was split into three parts among Melampus, his brother Bias, and the king&#x27;s descendants.","Anaxagoras","en/d/anaxagoras.html"],["A river named after a goddess","The Yewa River runs parallel to the Nigerian coast until it reaches the Lagos lagoon, and it gives its name to the area and its people.","Yewá","en/d/yewa.html"],["Twin rivers","In Purgatory, Lethe makes one forget wrongs and Eunoë makes one remember good deeds. Dante must drink from both before following Beatrice toward Paradise.","Eunoë","en/d/eunoe.html"],["A name with double meaning","The word <em>nenia</em> could mean a funeral song, but also a lullaby or a repeated chant.","Nenia","en/d/nenia.html"],["A jewel wrapped up","The <em>Object of Devotion for Observing the Mind</em> says the Buddha wrapped the gem of the doctrine in the five characters of <em>Myōhō Renge Kyō</em> and hung them as an adornment on the necks of ignorant people of the latter age.","Viśiṣṭacāritra","en/d/visistacaritra.html"],["A Dublin place name","A popular legend holds that Isolde may have lived in Chapelizod, a suburb of Dublin, from which the place takes its name. Her remains are also believed to lie there.","Isolde of Ireland","en/d/isolda-de-irlanda.html"],["Exalted palace","Her main temple in Ur was called Egalmah, &quot;exalted palace&quot;, a name that reflects her role as protector of palaces.","Ninegal","en/d/ninegal.html"],["A seal in Oryol","In November 2024, a seal was found in central Oryol that, according to archaeologist Oleg Radyush, belonged to Putyata Vyshatich, a grandson of Dobrynya. His link to Prince Sviatoslav II, who ruled Kiev from 1093 to 1113, made it possible to date the find.","Dobrynya Nikitich","en/d/dobrynya-nikitich.html"],["Indian origin","His figure was inspired by Nalakuvara, a Hindu yaksha who entered Buddhism through the Jataka tales and reached China.","Nezha","en/d/nezha.html"],["A typhoon with her name","Thailand proposed the name Mekkhala for the typhoon naming list, and it has been used in 2002, 2008, 2015, 2020 and 2026.","Manimekhala","en/d/manimekhala.html"],["A misleading word","According to analysis of the logbook, Taber alternated between breaker and breacher, two words that are easily confused and that may have produced a reef that never existed.","Tabor Island","en/d/isla-tabor.html"],["An island as a reward","In a rare account, Euneus gave part of Lemnos to Philoctetes as a reward for his help, and Philoctetes named it Akesa.","Euneus","en/d/euneo.html"],["Clíodhna&#x27;s birds","Her three birds do more than sing: in the Voyage of Teigue they also guide the heroes across the sea.","Clíodhna","en/d/cliodhna.html"],["The Bears","According to Diodorus Siculus, Zeus rewarded Ida and Adrasteia by turning them into the constellations Ursa Major and Ursa Minor.","Ida","en/d/ida.html"],["Different mothers","The accounts do not agree on Alcmenor&#x27;s mother: some name an Arabian woman, others the naiad Eurryroe or Isaie, daughter of the king of Tyre.","Alcmenor","en/d/alcmenor.html"],["A mirage","Lafcadio Hearn notes that Hōrai is also called Shinkiro, meaning mirage, the vision of the impossible.","Mount Penglai","en/d/monte-penglai.html"],["The Mourning Fields","Virgil places Laodamia in the Mourning Fields, a region of the underworld that Aeneas crosses in Book VI of the Aeneid.","Laodamia","en/d/laodamia.html"],["A root in trembling","The Greek <em>phríkē</em> shares its root with the verb <em>phríttō</em>, &#x27;to tremble&#x27;.","Horror (Frice)","en/d/horror-frice.html"],["Three thousand daughters","Hesiod states that Oceanus and Tethys had three thousand Oceanids, though the Theogony names only a few of them.","Camarina","en/d/camarina.html"],["Healing dogs","Lydney yielded a dozen figures of dogs, an animal associated with healing in antiquity. One has a human face, suggesting the god might have been imagined in animal form.","Nodens","en/d/nodens.html"],["Boiling fish","According to the poem, his mere presence made fish boil alive in the rivers.","Asag","en/d/asag.html"],["A lost tragedy","The tragedian Sophocles may have written a play titled Oicles dealing with this episode, but it has not survived.","Oecles","en/d/ecles.html"],["Names by role","When both father and daughter appear, the daughter is called Varuni and the wife Varunani; in some texts the roles are swapped.","Varuni","en/d/varuni.html"],["A cicada as punishment","In the tradition, Eos turned Tithonus into a cicada after he kept aging without being able to die. The Greek word for cicada was tettix.","Eos","en/d/eos.html"],["Restricted access","Her temple recently banned foreigners from entering, citing their clothing and conduct as objectionable.","Vijayadurga","en/d/vijayadurga.html"],["A wind named after a region","The Euro-Auster was linked to Egypt, while the Austro-Africus came from Sidra, showing the geography the ancients associated with each wind.","Auster","en/d/austro.html"],["An ancient name","Emain Macha, a place in Ireland, appears as &#x27;Isamnion&#x27; in Ptolemy&#x27;s Geography of the 2nd century AD. Emain Ablach&#x27;s name may be related to it, though its origin is uncertain.","Emain Ablach","en/d/emain-ablach.html"],["Another name","In some versions she also appears under the name Palanto.","Amata","en/d/amata.html"],["A tree-man","Simek compares the story with Örvar-Oddr, who becomes young again after living as a tree-man, according to Örvar-Odds saga.","Hoddmímis Holt","en/d/bosque-de-hodmimir.html"],["A physician among the Argonauts","Among the heroes who sailed with Jason, Eribotes stands out for his healing skills, since he tends to Oileus after a wound caused by a feather of the Stymphalian birds.","Eribotes","en/d/eribotes.html"],["Pillar of the Boatmen","Smertrios appears on the Pillar of the Boatmen, a monument discovered in Paris that brings together several Gaulish gods.","Smertrios","en/d/smertrios.html"],["A morsel for the gods","At every meal a portion was tossed into the hearth fire for the Penates, a way of sharing the food with the household gods.","Penates","en/d/penates.html"],["A famous logo","The well-known lips-and-tongue logo of the Rolling Stones was inspired by the image of Kali, according to the sources.","Kali","en/d/kali.html"],["Son or little Svarog","The name&#x27;s suffix allows two readings: the patronymic &quot;son of Svarog&quot; and the diminutive &quot;little Svarog&quot;. The second is supported by parallels such as the Serbo-Croatian name Djurdjić.","Svarozhich","en/d/svarozhich.html"],["Identified with Hecate","Apollonius of Rhodes states that Crataeis was another name for Hecate, a way of reconciling the conflicting versions.","Crataeis","en/d/crateis.html"],["Meaning of the name","The Nahuatl name Tlilhua is usually interpreted as &quot;one that has ink.&quot;","Tlilhua","en/d/tlilhua.html"],["The green nickname","According to Michael O&#x27;Clery, a snakebite left a green ring on him, which gave him the nickname <em>Glas</em>. Geoffrey Keating offers a different explanation for the same name.","Goídel Glas","en/d/goidel-glas.html"],["A mallet as attribute","Aristophanes describes him carrying a huge mallet, a detail that sets him apart from other figures of war.","Kydoimos","en/d/cidoimos.html"],["A flayed pony","In one version of the <em>Nihongi</em>, Susanoo&#x27;s attack on the weaving hall involves a flayed pony thrown at the goddess.","Wakahirume","en/d/wakahirume.html"],["A winged dog","Simargl is often depicted as a winged dog, an unusual image that sets it apart from other Slavic deities.","Simargl","en/d/simargl.html"],["A disputed name","The meaning of the name is uncertain. The translation &quot;outpour of water&quot; is now considered a mistake, because the homophone <em>arūru</em> means &quot;outlet of a canal&quot;.","Aruru","en/d/aruru.html"],["The name of the Atlantic","The ocean surrounding the island took its name from Atlas, the eldest son of Poseidon and Cleito, according to Plato&#x27;s account.","Atlantis","en/d/atlantida.html"],["Four heads","His four heads represent the four Vedas and face the four cardinal directions.","Brahma","en/d/brahma.html"],["A shrine in a Roman port","A shrine to Dushara has been discovered in Puteoli, the ancient Roman port of Italy, showing that his cult reached places far from Petra.","Dushara","en/d/dushara.html"],["Indra&#x27;s eyes","According to the story, the curse of the sage Gautama covered Indra&#x27;s body with vulvas, which were later turned into eyes. This is why he is shown with many eyes.","Indra","en/d/indra.html"],["A crafty wish","Taraka asked to be slain only by a son of Shiva, a wish considered clever because Shiva was an ascetic unlikely to have children.","Tarakasura","en/d/tarakasura.html"],["Colonnades that repeat the voice","Pausanias describes a colonnade at Olympia that repeats a human voice seven times or more, and another at Hermione that repeats it at least three times. Locals called both of them Echo Colonnades.","Echo","en/d/eco.html"],["Several spellings","Her name appears in cuneiform texts with several spellings, such as ma-ma or ma-mi-tum.","Mamitu","en/d/mamitu.html"],["A weapon no one knew how to recall","Ashvatthama launched the brahmastra without knowing the full teaching needed to recall it, which led to the tragedy in the tale.","Brahmastra","en/d/brahmastra.html"],["A mistake that made a word","Her original name was written without an initial aspirate, but a false etymology linked it to the sea and it was spelled with an H. That mistaken form led to the English word halcyon.","Alcyone","en/d/alcione.html"],["A ring held to the face","According to the Dictionnaire Infernal, the summoner should wear a silver ring on the middle finger of the left hand, held against the face as a sign of respect for his rank as king.","Beleth","en/d/beleth.html"],["A puzzling name","An inscription of Gudea from the third millennium BCE describes Sin as a god whose name nobody can explain, suggesting its meaning was already debated in his time.","Sin","en/d/sin.html"],["A sun turned to stone","According to a late tradition, a sun fell onto Mount Tiantai and became a stone, the Sun Stone, from which his essence later emerged.","Zhao Gongming","en/d/zhao-gongming.html"],["A name meaning iron","Her name comes from a Greek word meaning &quot;of iron&quot;, and Sophocles links it to the harshness of her character.","Sidero","en/d/sidero.html"],["A triad with Harpocrates","Together with Montu she formed a triad with Harpocrates, a child deity, at sanctuaries such as Medamud.","Raet-Tawy","en/d/raet-tawy.html"],["A duel settled by trickery","According to the Suda, Melanthus killed Janthus by using a ruse, fighting in place of Timetes.","Andropompus","en/d/andropompo.html"],["Aušrinė","In Lithuanian tradition, Aušrinė, personification of the morning star, was said to light a fire for the sun at the start of each day.","Hausos","en/d/hausos.html"],["Two Butes","Ancient sources distinguish this hero from another Butes, a son of Poseidon, although in some versions the two appear as the same figure.","Butes","en/d/butes.html"],["A king in between","Aletes appears in the lists of Achaean kings after Clitus, son of Temenus, and before Tisamenus, son of Orestes.","Aletes","en/d/aletes.html"],["A sacred tripod","Pausanias describes her image on a sacrificial tripod at the sanctuary of Amyclae.","Sparta","en/d/esparta.html"],["Midwife of Asclepius","According to some accounts, Lachesis assisted at the birth of Asclepius in Apollo&#x27;s temple, while Apollo eased the pains of the mother, called Aegle or Coronis.","Lachesis","en/d/laquesis.html"],["A coded allusion in Joyce","James Joyce mentions this bogatyr in Finnegans Wake under a coded name: «Wassaily Booslaeugh of Riesengeborg».","Vasily Buslayev","en/d/vasili-buslaev.html"],["An island named after a swim","A very small island near Lemnos was called Neai, from a Greek verb meaning to swim, because Heracles was said to have swum there.","Heracles","en/d/heracles.html"],["A dolphin constellation","According to the story, the dolphin that found Amphitrite was rewarded with a place among the stars, in the constellation Delphinus.","Amphitrite","en/d/anfitrite.html"],["Ingredients of Gleipnir","The ribbon that finally bound Fenrir was made from impossible ingredients, such as the sound of a cat&#x27;s footfall or a woman&#x27;s beard.","Ámsvartnir","en/d/amsvartnir.html"],["A name centered on the earth","His name means risen land, and in ancient inscriptions he appears as Tanenu or Tanuu, the inert land.","Tatenen","en/d/tatenen.html"],["An uncertain kinship","Pindar says Ixion was the first to shed the blood of a kinsman, which has led some to suggest Eioneus may have been his brother.","Eioneus","en/d/eyoneo.html"],["A rooster of cold fire","The fire the Basan breathes is cold: it glows, but does not burn or ignite combustible material.","Basan","en/d/basan.html"],["Name in video games","In Dungeons &amp; Dragons the monster is spelled with two r&#x27;s, Tarrasque, and is one of the most powerful creatures in the game.","The Tarasque","en/d/la-tarasca.html"],["God and day, relatives","The Spanish words dios and día both descend from the root that also gave Dyēus, linked to brightness and the sky.","Dyēus","en/d/dyeus.html"],["Only for children with living parents","Only children whose two parents were still alive could carry the branch, a requirement that set this ritual role apart for young participants.","Eiresione","en/d/iresiona.html"],["A necklace with a bad reputation","The necklace passed through Jocasta, Polynices, Eriphyle, Alcmaeon and others, and it almost always brought a death with it.","Harmonia","en/d/harmonia.html"],["A name that sounds like a storm","Brontes means &#x27;thunderer&#x27;, while Arges and Steropes refer to brightness and lightning. The names reflect their role as forgers of the thunderbolt.","Brontes","en/d/brontes.html"],["Two different visitors","Amazake-babaa only asks for the drink, while amazake-banbaa tries to sell it, so each is repelled in a different way.","Amazake-babaa","en/d/amazake-babaa.html"],["A president, not a king","The title President of Hell has been compared to that of a university or assembly president, though those uses are only premodern and do not clarify the original meaning.","Caim","en/d/caim.html"],["An imperial coin","Euthenia appeared on Roman coins minted in the Greek East, where she served as the Greek equivalent of the Roman personifications Annona and Abundantia.","Euthenia","en/d/eutenea.html"],["A flying ship","The <em>roth rámach</em> is described as a flying machine of great destructive power. A prophecy attributed to Saint Columba links it to an omen of the Last Judgement.","Mug Ruith","en/d/mug-ruith.html"],["A woodpecker","Circe turned Picus, a Latian king, into a woodpecker for rejecting her love. In this way an Italian episode became part of her legend.","Circe","en/d/circe.html"],["A disputed tomb","Her gravestone is dated 1574, but it may have been made in the early 20th century.","Lim Ko Niao","en/d/lim-ko-niao.html"],["Chucky","In the Child&#x27;s Play franchise, the villain Charles Lee Ray uses Damballa&#x27;s name in a voodoo incantation to transfer his soul into a Good Guy doll. Damballa also appears in the third season of the Chucky series as Chucky&#x27;s heavenly judge.","Damballa","en/d/damballa.html"],["A planet with the same name","The novel <em>A Wrinkle in Time</em> describes Camazotz as a dark planet ruled by a telepathic mind called IT.","Camazotz","en/d/camazotz.html"],["The first iron","According to the runic songs, Ilmarinen was the first to forge iron. The metal promised not to harm anyone, but broke its word when a hornet carried snake poison into the quenching water.","Ilmarinen","en/d/ilmarinen.html"],["An egg hung from the ceiling","In the sanctuary of the Leucippides in Sparta, an egg hung from the ceiling by ribbons, believed to be the one from which Helen had been born.","Helen","en/d/helena.html"],["Parallel with Blodeuwedd","Scholars compare her story with that of Blodeuwedd, the woman made of flowers in the Mabinogion, because of her floral name and her plot against her husband.","Bláthnat","en/d/blathnat.html"],["The milk that formed a galaxy","In one version, Rhea had Cronus suckle the stone disguised as a child. The milk that spilled as she pressed her breast formed the Milky Way.","Rhea","en/d/rea.html"],["A lubok","Her image was captured in a Russian lubok, a popular print from the 17th or 18th century.","Meduza","en/d/meduza.html"],["A heaven above the earth","In the Pyramid Texts, the Duat was a celestial world above the earth, not a subterranean realm.","Duat","en/d/duat.html"],["Telling name","His name derives from caecus, &quot;blind&quot;, and is explained by the smoke that damaged his eyes in the story.","Caeculus","en/d/ceculo.html"],["A river with a divine name","Snorri identifies the Don River with the ancient Tanais, which he says was once called Tanakvísl or Vanakvísl.","Vanaheim","en/d/vanaheim.html"],["Seven temples","At Mahabalipuram, local belief holds that six of seven temples are submerged. In 2002, the Scientific Exploration Society recorded stone remains and walls between 5 and 8 metres deep.","Kumari Kandam","en/d/kumari-kandam.html"],["A lovers&#x27; festival","Her holy day coincides with Sepandārmazgān, a celebration of eternal love in which Iranians give each other gifts.","Spenta Armaiti","en/d/spenta-armaiti.html"],["A Yo-kai Watch character","In the Yo-kai Watch series it appears as a strip-shaped yōkai called So-Sorree in the English dub.","Ittan-momen","en/d/ittan-momen.html"],["A name meaning lightning","The name Sterope derives from the Greek <em>steropē</em>, meaning &#x27;lightning&#x27;.","Sterope","en/d/esterope-q15849193.html"],["Golden rods","In Styria, the birch rod bundles Krampus gives to families are painted gold and displayed throughout the year.","Krampus","en/d/krampus.html"],["Candalus&#x27;s brothers","In the English reference, Candalus appears alongside Triopas, Macar and Actis as brothers involved in the death of Tenages, the fifth brother.","Candalus","en/d/candalo.html"],["Link to pigs","The name Banba resembles <em>banb</em>, the Irish word for piglet, and pigs were closely associated with prosperity in Ireland. For this reason they are sometimes considered a symbol of the goddess.","Banba","en/d/banba.html"],["A violet name","His name is linked to the Greek word for violet, the flower among which he was found according to the story.","Iamus","en/d/yamo.html"],["A name with many forms","Her name appears as Ceithlinn, Céithlionn or Kethlenda depending on the source and period.","Cethlenn","en/d/cethlenn.html"],["Enchanted fire","According to the <em>Dindsenchas</em>, his name may mean &quot;enchanted fire&quot; or &quot;fire shape(d).&quot;","Delbáeth","en/d/delbaeth.html"],["A name with variants","His name also appears as Eneo in some sources, and the city of Cebrene is sometimes spelled Kebrene or Kevrin.","Cebren","en/d/cebren.html"],["A deliberate vocalization","In the Masoretic text of Amos 5:26 the name is vocalized so that it sounds like the Hebrew word <em>shiqqùts</em>, «a repugnant thing».","Kajamānu","en/d/kajamanu.html"],["A name with meaning","Her name is rendered as &quot;the calm one&quot; or &quot;the charmer&quot;, and has even been compared to the refreshing coolness of a shady stream.","Galaxaura","en/d/galaxaura.html"],["A county&#x27;s coat of arms","The aegishjalmur is the official symbol on the coat of arms of the Icelandic county of Strandasýsla.","Aegishjalmur","en/d/aegishjalmur.html"],["A revised date","The altar bears the year 161, but until the 1990s it was believed to date from 399.","Erudinus","en/d/erudino.html"],["A wrestling champion","According to a tradition recorded in the sources, Echemus won the wrestling event at the first Olympic Games, established by Heracles.","Echemus","en/d/equemo.html"],["A mountain with a mythic name","The Norwegian mountain range Jotunheimen takes its name from the world of the giants. Its highest peak, Galdhøpiggen, is the tallest mountain in Scandinavia.","Jötunheim","en/d/jotunheim.html"],["No seashells","The image of mermaids covering their chests with seashells, popularised by films such as The Little Mermaid, does not fit the logic of traditional legends.","Sirenuca","en/d/sirenuca.html"],["A trickster","He is classed as a trickster, a deceiver who can protect travelers on their way to the afterlife or push them to reveal the worst of themselves.","Supay","en/d/supay.html"],["Giant fruits","According to the Vishnu Purana, the fruits of the jambu tree are as large as Asian elephants.","Jambudvipa","en/d/yambuduipa.html"],["A disputed name","Richard Haly proposes that the name derives from <em>omitl</em>, &quot;bone,&quot; rather than <em>ome</em>, &quot;two,&quot; so the pair would be linked to the creation of humans from bones.","Ometeotl","en/d/ometeotl.html"],["A god named in his own language","Lucan is one of the few Greco-Roman authors who names Gaulish gods by their native names instead of Greek or Roman equivalents.","Esus","en/d/esus.html"],["A temple with a museum","According to Cicero, the temple of Felicitas also served as an art gallery, housing statues looted from Greece, including a group of the Muses.","Felicitas","en/d/felicitas.html"],["Ignoring pain","According to the saga, Sinfjötli says pain seems trifling to a Völsung, and he proves it against the cowardly sons of Siggeir.","Sinfjötli","en/d/sinfjotli.html"],["A hidden son","In the account preserved by Hyginus, Antigone has a son with Haemon, named Maeon, who is recognized at funeral games by a dragon mark on his body, a sign borne only by descendants of the Spartoi, the first people of Thebes.","Antigone","en/d/antigona.html"],["The Pylos tablets","On the Mycenaean Linear B tablets from Pylos, <em>potnia</em> almost always appears with an epithet indicating the goddess&#x27;s place or function.","Potnia Theron","en/d/potnia-theron.html"],["A shared name","Greek mythology has several women named Cleopatra, including a wife of Meleager who is also called Alcyone, which makes them easy to confuse.","Cleopatra","en/d/cleopatra.html"],["A god without myths","According to the sources, Honos and other personifications of virtues such as Fides or Spes had no associated myths; they are known mainly through their symbols and temples.","Honos","en/d/honos.html"],["Name and pigs","The tale explains his name as &#x27;sow run&#x27;, although philologists think this is a folk etymology.","Culhwch","en/d/culhwch.html"],["A shade in the Underworld","Odysseus meets her among the shades of heroines when he descends to Hades, alongside other female figures of Homer&#x27;s account.","Iphimedeia","en/d/ifimedia.html"],["A name from a sighting","The island takes its name from Yakov Sannikov, who in 1811 reported a new land north of Kotelny Island.","Sannikov Land","en/d/tierra-de-sannikov.html"],["A cave with a double meaning","The cave at Amnisos had stalactites linked to the goddess&#x27;s double role, as she could both hasten and delay labour.","Eileithyia","en/d/ilitia.html"],["A confusing name","The term applies to the Japanese badger, but traditionally also to the tanuki, and even to an introduced palm civet.","Mujina","en/d/mujina.html"],["Another name","Aepytus is also known by the name Telephus, a variant that links him to other stories in the tradition.","Aepytus","en/d/epito-q406443.html"],["Seven heads, seven mountains","According to the text, the beast&#x27;s seven heads stand for seven mountains and also for seven kings.","The Beast of Revelation","en/d/bestia-del-apocalipsis.html"],["A name for the wolf","On the Rök Stone, the earliest known mention of her name appears in a kenning, a poetic circumlocution that calls the wolf &quot;the horse of Gunnr&quot;.","Gunnr","en/d/gunnr.html"],["A possible Virgilian model","The classical scholar Alison Keith suggests that Athis&#x27;s death was modelled on the killing of Ebysus by Corynaeus in Virgil&#x27;s Aeneid, where a burning firebrand taken from an altar is also thrown.","Athis","en/d/atis-q3514860.html"],["Etymological pair","Culsu shares an etymological root with Culsans, her male counterpart. Culsans is the Etruscan equivalent of the Roman god Janus, who also has two faces.","Culsu","en/d/culsu.html"],["A bird&#x27;s name","The lexicographer Hesychius of Alexandria glossed the word pēnelops as some kind of bird, and the heroine&#x27;s name is traced to it.","Penelope","en/d/penelope.html"],["Water serpent or dragon","Her name is explained as &quot;water snake woman&quot; or &quot;water dragon woman&quot;, an image that links the goddess with water creatures.","Mizuhanome","en/d/mizuhanome.html"],["One of the earliest goddesses","Nüba is counted among the first goddesses to appear in Chinese literature, already in the Shijing, an ancient collection of poetry.","Nüba","en/d/nuba.html"],["Uar or Uair","In many surviving manuscripts the character appears as Uar or Uair, and modern editors have changed the name to Brian.","Brian","en/d/brian.html"],["Melqart behind the name","Makeris, Sardus&#x27;s father, is identified with Melqart, the deity that Egyptians and Libyans associated with Heracles.","Sardus","en/d/sardo.html"],["A bird called oknos","According to Pausanias, diviners who watched birds called the heron oknos, the largest and most beautiful of its kind.","Ocnus","en/d/ocno.html"],["Eleven","In later texts, the word rudra can simply mean the number eleven.","Rudra","en/d/rudra.html"],["A poetic kenning","A poetic kenning calls poetry the &#x27;cup of the son of Billing&#x27;, which works whether Billing is a dwarf or a giant.","Billingr","en/d/billingr.html"],["A horse and a goddess","Pausanias tells that Poseidon united with Demeter in the form of a horse, and from this union were born Arion and Despoina.","Poseidon","en/d/poseidon.html"],["Flying head","In popular belief, the leyak flies at night with its head and entrails trailing from the body, which is left behind looking like an ordinary person.","Leyak","en/d/leyak.html"],["A city named after her son","Nauplius, son of Amymone and Poseidon, gave his name to the port city of Nafplio (Nauplia) in the Argolid.","Amymone","en/d/amimone.html"],["Meaning of the name","The name has been translated as something like &#x27;broad gleam&#x27; or &#x27;the far-shining one&#x27;, alluding to the brightness of the place.","Breiðablik","en/d/breiablik.html"],["A tablet found in the 17th century","The inscription turned up in the foundations of a ramp near the Grand Séminaire of Langres, in 1642 or 1645.","Moccus","en/d/moccus.html"],["A sea in one nostril","According to the account, all the seas of the world would fit in one of the fish&#x27;s nostrils like a mustard seed in the desert.","Bahamut","en/d/bahamut.html"],["Bells in the Well of Sacrifice","Dredging the Well of Sacrifice at Chichén Itzá recovered many copper and sometimes gold bells, thought to have been thrown in with the victims.","Ah Puch","en/d/ah-puch.html"],["A medical eponym","His deformity has given its name to a medical term, according to the sources.","Thersites","en/d/tersites.html"],["A town named after a god","Glanum took its name from the god Glanis, which was unusual among towns in Gaul.","Glanis","en/d/glanis.html"],["A work with a Latin genealogy","Cicero gives a similar parentage, from Erebus and Night, to the personification of the Latin concept labor, suggesting a parallel between the two traditions.","Ponos","en/d/ponos.html"],["Parallel with Theseus","Connla&#x27;s story resembles that of Theseus, who was raised far away by his mother, takes up the tokens left by his father Aegeus, and is recognised by him after a series of combats.","Connla","en/d/connla.html"],["A disputed name","The origin of the name is uncertain. Carnoy linked it to a word meaning &quot;ugly, coarse&quot;, though he found this unlikely, while Höfer proposed that the syllable si- was a dialectal form of &quot;god&quot;.","Siproites","en/d/sipretes.html"],["A telling name","The name Himalia is linked to a Greek word for a miller, a term tied to grain and flour.","Himalia","en/d/himalia.html"],["A possibly mistaken name","Gram may be a misreading: the Old Norse word gramr meant &#x27;king&#x27;, and no other ancient source mentions a monarch by that name.","Gram","en/d/gram-q5593587.html"],["A luxury commodity","In his time incense was extremely expensive, and the trade that came from Nubia enriched the region and made Dedun a god of prosperity.","Dedun","en/d/dedun.html"],["An accidental find","The monolith turned up in February 1978 while electric company workers were digging to lay underground cables in central Mexico City.","Coyolxauhqui","en/d/coyolxauhqui.html"],["A telling name","His name could mean &quot;He who subdues the divine&quot; or &quot;He who masters the divine&quot;.","Theiodamas","en/d/tiodamante.html"],["Protective amulets","Byzantine mothers used amulets to protect their newborns from Gello. The first-century Cyranides recommended hyena eyeballs in a purple pouch as protection.","Gello","en/d/gelo.html"],["A name meaning &quot;young&quot;","Neaera&#x27;s name is linked to the idea of youth, as are those of her daughters, alluding to the sun that is reborn each morning.","Neaera","en/d/neaera.html"],["An agricultural couple","Beyla and Byggvir are interpreted as personifications of farming: she stands for the manure that softens the soil, and he for the chaff left over from the mill.","Byggvir","en/d/byggvir.html"],["From name to element","The chemical element tellurium takes its name from the Latin tellus, meaning Earth.","Tellus","en/d/tellus.html"],["A computer named after her","The program Kaissa won the first World Computer Chess Championship in 1974.","Caïssa","en/d/caisa.html"],["Meaning of the name","The name Pheidas comes from Greek and means &quot;sparing&quot; or &quot;thrifty.&quot; It is a descriptive name that does not refer to a specific deed.","Pheidas","en/d/pheidas.html"],["Epithets of Zeus","The names he was worshipped under, Epopsios (&quot;Overlooker of All&quot;) and Meilichios (&quot;Gracious&quot;), are epithets otherwise applied to Zeus.","Periphas","en/d/perifante.html"],["Speos Artemidos","The Greek name of the cave, &#x27;Cave of Artemis&#x27;, is still used even though Artemis was not an Egyptian goddess.","Pakhet","en/d/pajet.html"],["Prodigious runner","Accounts said Iphiclus could run over asphodels and wheat without harming the plants. His speed was proverbial in Greek tradition.","Iphiclus","en/d/ificlo.html"],["A name with variants","He appears under forms such as Ane, On, One or Aukun, depending on the language and the source.","Aun the Old","en/d/aun-el-viejo.html"],["A fig tree as a gift","Demeter thanked Phytalus for his hospitality with a fig tree, a gift that links him to this plant in the story.","Phytalus","en/d/fitalo.html"],["A cave with Classic scenes","The Naj Tunich cave contains Classic Period scenes showing a naked, sexually excited male creature embracing a nude Maya nobleman, possibly as part of an initiation.","Chin","en/d/chin.html"],["Virgin priestess","In some accounts, the daughter who rejected Heracles was condemned to lifelong virginity and to serve the hero as his priestess.","Procris","en/d/procris.html"],["Four rivers of milk","According to Snorri, four rivers of milk flowed from Auðumbla&#x27;s udders, and the giant Ymir fed on them.","Auðumbla","en/d/auumbla.html"],["A name tied to happiness","Nandini is linked to <em>nandin</em>, &#x27;the one who gives happiness&#x27;, although the latter is a masculine term.","Nandini Mata","en/d/nandini-mata.html"],["Four Chaacs","Each of the Chaacs of the cardinal directions had a colour: red in the east, white in the north, black in the west, and yellow in the south.","Chaac","en/d/chaac.html"],["A figure often confused","Basilea is often mistaken for Theia, the more widely accepted mother of Selene, Helios and Eos, and it is unclear whether the two are the same figure.","Basilea","en/d/basilea.html"],["A lady of the steppe","Her best-known title, &#x27;lady of the steppe&#x27;, could also refer to the land of the dead, since the word ṣēru also meant the underworld.","Belet-Seri","en/d/belet-seri.html"],["A brother who opposes","Yuyutsu, another Kaurava, defected to the Pandavas at the start of the Kurukshetra War. Vikarna is often contrasted with him for choosing a different path.","Vikarna","en/d/vikarna.html"],["Serpent of shadow","At the equinoxes, light and shadow on the stairway of Chichén Itzá create the illusion of a serpent descending the pyramid.","Kukulcán","en/d/kukulcan.html"],["Eyes that awaken","On Peng Chau, the lions receive the &#x27;eye-dotting&#x27; (點睛) ceremony to wake them, presided over by local officials.","Lady Kam Fa (Golden Flower)","en/d/lady-kam-fa-flor-de-oro.html"],["Ear in the sky","Mesopotamian astronomers called Spica &quot;the barley ear of Shala,&quot; linking the goddess to the sky of the sowing season.","Shala","en/d/shala.html"],["A tanuki on screen","In the 1968 film the ungaikyō can inhale and then puff out its belly to project scenes from different places. Its tanuki look later spread through children&#x27;s books.","Ungaikyō","en/d/ungaikyo.html"],["Victory at Marathon","Tradition holds that a soldier ran from Marathon to Athens to announce the victory with the cry &quot;Nike!&quot; and then collapsed.","Nike","en/d/nike.html"],["A two-faced tree","White poplar leaves are dark on top and white underneath, which Servius read as a symbol of Heracles&#x27; labours in the upper and the underworld.","Leuce","en/d/leuce.html"],["Elephant head","In the Shahnameh, Akvan has long hair, blue eyes and an elephant&#x27;s head with tusks in place of teeth.","Aka Manah","en/d/aka-manah.html"],["A dragon on the flag","The druk appears on the flag of Bhutan, holding jewels that represent the wealth of the country.","Druk","en/d/druk.html"],["A name in different languages","The character appears as Gunther in the Nibelungenlied and as Gunnar in the Norse versions, reflecting the difference between the Germanic and Scandinavian traditions.","Gunther","en/d/gunter.html"],["A name with several meanings","His name can be written with the character 女 (woman) or with 如, which in Buddhism translates the Sanskrit term tathāgata.","Zennyo Ryūō","en/d/zennyo-ryuo.html"],["A mother named after a lioness","A Purana calls him a son of Siṃhikā, whose name means &quot;little lioness&quot;, though the text also associates her with the cat.","Svarbhānu","en/d/svarbhanu.html"],["An egg on the stove","In Czech folklore, the raróg is hatched by keeping an egg warm on a stove for nine days and nine nights.","Raróg","en/d/rarog.html"],["A name with several meanings","The adjective ogygian could mean primeval or gigantic, which led to different interpretations of the name.","Ogyges","en/d/ogiges.html"],["An oath beside a spring","Herodotus tells how Cleomenes planned to gather the Arcadian leaders near Nonacris to swear oaths on the waters of the Styx, believed to be the water gods swore by. In reality it was a spring flowing from a rock above a walled pool.","Nonacris","en/d/nonacris.html"],["A name meaning &quot;giver&quot;","Her Greek name is understood to mean &quot;giver,&quot; and it was linked to the gift of a safe voyage.","Doto","en/d/doto.html"],["April festival","The Robigalia was held every year on 25 April in Rome, in a sacred grove north of the city.","Robigo","en/d/robigo.html"],["The winged disc","The well-known Zoroastrian winged disc is traditionally read as a symbolic image of the fravashi.","Fravashi","en/d/fravashi.html"],["An inscribed vase","A fifth-century BCE vase found near a sanctuary at San Giovenale bears the inscription &quot;I am Lurs, of Laran.&quot;","Lur","en/d/lur.html"],["An island born from a drop","The island of Onogoro arises from the drops of salt water that fall from the spear when it is lifted out of the sea.","Japanese creation myth","en/d/mito-japones-de-la-creacion.html"],["More copies than Gilgamesh","At least thirty-six copies of the Epic of Erra have been recovered, more than of the <em>Epic of Gilgamesh</em>, according to the Assyriologist Luigi Giovanni Cagni.","Erra","en/d/erra.html"],["Festival and demon","The same word names both the festival and a spirit that shortens life, showing how closely the solstice was linked to death.","Koročun (Kračun)","en/d/korocun-kracun.html"],["The nickname&#x27;s origin","Lodbrok is explained by the protective clothing he wore when facing the serpent guarding Thora. The sources describe the garment, but do not give a secure etymology for the name.","Ragnar Lodbrok","en/d/ragnar-lodbrok.html"],["Parasol and wheel","Although she is famous for her white parasol, she is also often associated with a golden wheel, a symbol of the Buddhist teaching.","Sitātapatrā","en/d/sitatapatra.html"],["Horse herder","He tended his father&#x27;s horses at Abydos, a position of trust for a son of Priam.","Democoon","en/d/democoonte.html"],["Statues in the dining halls","According to Polemon, in Sparta cooks raised statues of heroes such as Maton and Ceraon in the communal dining halls.","Deipneus","en/d/dipneo.html"],["A name with a point","Some scholars link the name Rinnal to the Old Irish <em>rind</em> or <em>rinn</em>, meaning point, in connection with the spears attributed to him.","Rinnal","en/d/rinnal.html"],["Golden and bronze armour","Glaucus gave up golden armour, said to be worth one hundred oxen, in exchange for Diomedes&#x27; bronze armour, valued at nine oxen.","Glaucus","en/d/glauco-q1081453.html"],["A single source","Almost everything known about Phthius comes from one ancient author, Dionysius of Halicarnassus, who mentions him while describing the Pelasgian migration.","Phthius","en/d/ftio.html"],["Four Agnis","The Shatapatha Brahmana tells how the first three priestly fires died and how the fourth hid in the waters out of fear, until the gods found him.","Agni","en/d/agni.html"],["A town named after him","The town of Orneae, in Argolis, is believed to be named in his honor, although the place name has another explanation linked to a nymph.","Orneus","en/d/orneo.html"],["Horns in question","An oracle from the Mari archives asked how many horns his crown should have; two, four or eight were considered possible answers.","Lagamal","en/d/lagamal.html"],["A popular name element","The character peng is common in personal names in China, Hong Kong and Taiwan, and several politicians from those regions carry it.","Peng","en/d/peng.html"],["A god with the same name","Urash was also the name of a male agricultural god worshipped at Dilbat. In some texts it is unclear which of the two is meant.","Uras","en/d/uras.html"],["A fashion named after her","The Yamanba Gyaru fashion of the 1990s took its name from this legend, though it has no connection to the spirit beyond the name.","Yama-uba","en/d/yama-uba.html"],["Útgarða-Loki&#x27;s cat","Thor tried to lift a colossal cat at the court of the giant Útgarða-Loki and managed only to raise one of its paws. Later it was revealed that the animal was the serpent itself in disguise.","Jörmungandr","en/d/jormungandr.html"],["A much-debated name","Scholars have proposed Greek, North Germanic, Sami and even Christian origins for the name Ahti, without agreeing on one.","Ahti","en/d/ahti.html"],["A ghost in a wolfskin","In some versions the spirit wears a wolfskin, which led scholar David Ogden to see it as a possible classical precursor of werewolf stories.","Polites","en/d/polites-q838767.html"],["A field without death","Odinsaker, the name of this place, is linked to the idea of a land where old age and death do not reach.","Glæsisvellir","en/d/glsisvellir.html"],["A bell on her forehead","Her name roughly means « she who bears the bell-shaped moon ». The crescent moon on her forehead is shaped like a bell.","Chandraghanta","en/d/chandraghanta.html"],["Origin of &#x27;cereal&#x27;","Her name gave rise to the word &#x27;cereal&#x27;, which refers to cultivated grains.","Ceres","en/d/ceres.html"],["A colour with a name","The Greek word kyanos meant bluish-green and referred to the water of lakes and streams. The colour cyan takes its name from it.","Cyane","en/d/ciane.html"],["Two deaths for one hero","The Iliad describes Adrastus&#x27;s death in one passage at the hands of Agamemnon, and in another credits Diomedes with the fatal blow.","Adrastus","en/d/adrasto-q4057953.html"],["Two Boeotian towns","Some traditions credit the sons of Thersander with giving their names to Haliartus and Coronea, two towns in Boeotia.","Thersander","en/d/tersandro-q10380878.html"],["Village goddess","Her most common worship name is Pidari Amman, and she is considered a goddess of villages rather than of great temples.","Pidari","en/d/pidari.html"],["A pig with a serpent&#x27;s head","In the Hellenic period Shai could be shown as a pig with a serpent&#x27;s head, an image Egyptologists know as the &quot;Shai animal&quot;.","Shai","en/d/shai.html"],["A borrowed ankle bone","According to one account, Chiron exhumed the body of the swift giant Damastes and removed the astragalus from his foot, which he implanted in the heel of Achilles.","Gigantomachy","en/d/gigantomaquia.html"],["Stags who were winds","In 1824, Finnur Magnússon proposed that the four stags stood for winds, based on their names. Dáinn and Dvalinn would be calm winds, and Duneyrr and Duraþrór heavy ones.","Duneyrr","en/d/duneyrr.html"],["Lord of the holy sea","Some texts call him &quot;the lord of the holy sea&quot;, and in a hymn to Nanshe he is described as &quot;the tax collector of the sea&quot;, perhaps because of the port city where he was worshiped.","Nindara","en/d/nindara.html"],["Dussehra","Each year, during the Hindu festival of Dussehra, a large effigy of the ten-headed Ravana is burned to mark his defeat.","Ravana","en/d/ravana.html"],["A region named after him","The region of Cestrine, north of the River Thyamis, took its name from him according to the tradition recorded by Pausanias.","Cestrinus","en/d/cestrino.html"],["Worship on Samothrace","Pliny writes that on the island of Samothrace Pothos was worshipped with especially solemn rites.","Pothos","en/d/pothos.html"],["Invoked before every undertaking","Pliny the Elder wrote that in Africa no one began anything without first calling on the goddess.","Africa","en/d/africa.html"],["Origin of Normandy","Heimskringla tells how Rolf Ganger reached Valland, subdued it and settled it with men from the north, and that the name Normandy comes from them.","Valland","en/d/valland.html"],["A culinary weakness","She is described as craving fish, which in Bengal was considered a especially suitable food for married women.","Shakchunni","en/d/shakchunni.html"],["The Moon&#x27;s mark","According to the myth, a rabbit thrown at Tecciztecatl&#x27;s face left a dark mark that, by tradition, can still be seen on the Moon.","Nanahuatzin","en/d/nanahuatzin.html"],["Twelve children in Italy","In Italy it was said that Loviatar had twelve children rather than nine.","Loviatar","en/d/loviatar.html"],["A masculine name","In Nahuatl, Tlaltecuhtli takes a grammatically masculine form, yet most of her representations are female.","Tlaltecuhtli","en/d/tlaltecuhtli.html"],["An attempt against Delos","Porphyrion tried to hurl the island of Delos at the gods, a gesture that shows the scale of the giants&#x27; rebellion.","Porphyrion","en/d/porfirion.html"],["A mountain on Venus","Milda Mons, a mountain on Venus, is named after this Lithuanian goddess.","Milda","en/d/milda.html"],["The rhinoceros emblem","In Jain art he is identified by the rhinoceros, which is usually carved on the pedestal of his images.","Shreyansanath","en/d/shreyansanath.html"],["Dedications at Périgueux","Several inscriptions dedicated to Telo have been found at Périgueux, showing that his worship reached beyond Toulon.","Telo","en/d/telo.html"],["A crater named for her","The Annona crater, in the southern hemisphere of the dwarf planet Ceres, is named after this goddess.","Annona","en/d/annona.html"],["A male lover","Although the figure is usually female, there is at least one reference to a male leannán sídhe who troubles a mortal woman.","Leannán sídhe","en/d/leannan-sidhe.html"],["A mountain on Venus","One of the mountains on Venus is named after the goddess, Idunn Mons.","Iðunn","en/d/iunn.html"],["One wife, two versions","The sources that name Anaxibia as Nestor&#x27;s wife are a minority; most give that place to Eurydice.","Anaxibia","en/d/anaxibia-q488626.html"],["Anklets with different gems","Kannagi proves her husband&#x27;s innocence by breaking her anklet and revealing rubies, unlike the pearls in the queen&#x27;s anklet.","Kannagi","en/d/kannagi.html"],["A temple named after Anu","The name of his main temple, E-ibbi-Anum, means «Anu has called it into being». Temple names that credit another god with creating the temple are unusual.","Urash","en/d/uras-q1802064.html"],["Eleven heads","According to the annals, Dihuang had eleven heads and eleven brothers, supernatural traits that emphasize his exceptional nature.","Dihuang","en/d/dihuang.html"],["Goddess of Thursday","Tara is linked to Brihaspati&#x27;s day, Thursday, and in Cambodia she is regarded as the protector of that day.","Tara","en/d/tara-q2625996.html"],["Field of dharma","Kurukshetra is also called Dharmakshetra, the field of dharma. The text says that sins committed in this place would be absolved.","Kurukshetra War","en/d/guerra-de-kurukshetra.html"],["Leopard skin","He was shown wearing a priestly cloak of leopard or cheetah skin and the side lock of youth. From the New Kingdom he was theologically drawn close to Iunmutef.","Sameref","en/d/sameref.html"],["A forge at Doire-na-tuan","A smith named Gaibhlen, identified as the smith of the Tuatha Dé, melted ore at Doire-na-tuan, according to folklore. Tradition holds that a forge has stood there ever since.","Goibniu","en/d/goibniu.html"],["A disputed name","Some readers believe Homer names two Charites, Pasithea and Kale, while others think the words form a single phrase.","Kale","en/d/kale.html"],["A shared shrine","At Odero Lal, Hindus and Muslims pray in the same place, which some Muslims regard as the tomb of Sheikh Tahir.","Jhulelal","en/d/jhulelal.html"],["A famous parallel","Her story parallels that of Cydippe and Acontius, another couple bound by an oath written down for them.","Ctesylla","en/d/ctesila.html"],["A name that is also a month","In Ugaritic, yarikh means both the celestial body and &quot;month&quot;, a word close to the terms for the moon in other Semitic languages.","Yarikh","en/d/yarij.html"],["Five faces","The described statue had four faces on its head and a fifth on its chest, an unusual image in the sources about Slavic gods.","Porenut","en/d/porenut.html"],["A Sumerian name","His name is usually linked to Sumerian terms such as kíĝ, meaning &quot;work&quot;, and kingal, meaning &quot;leader&quot;, which fits his role as a commander.","Kingu","en/d/kingu.html"],["Three names, one mother","The same wife of Nauplius appears under three different names depending on the author: Philira in the Nostoi, Hesione in Cercops and Clymene in the tragic poets.","Clymene","en/d/climene-q2980439.html"],["A forbidden precinct","Plutarch says the ban on women entering held even in emergencies such as earthquakes.","Eunostus","en/d/eunosto.html"],["A name with meaning","The name Pefredo is linked to the word &#x27;alarm&#x27;, a hint at the watchful or warning character attributed to her in the story.","Pefredo","en/d/pefredo.html"],["Five Suns","Cicero records up to five different figures called Sol, each with its own genealogy, showing how much this god could vary depending on the author.","Sol","en/d/sol-q1199937.html"],["Land recovered from the sea","According to a coastal legend, Parashurama threw his axe into the sea and the waters receded, creating the western strip of India.","Parashurama","en/d/parashurama.html"],["A name in royalty","The Hittite queen Puduḫepa carries a form of the goddess&#x27;s name, showing how widespread her worship was at court.","Ḫepat","en/d/hepat.html"],["A mountain with his name","A mountain on Euboea was thought to have been named after Zarex.","Zarex","en/d/zarex.html"],["An adjective from her name","According to Snorri, her name gave rise to <em>snotr</em>, a word used to call any wise man or woman clever.","Snotra","en/d/snotra.html"],["A name with a double meaning","The name Odysseus is linked to a Greek verb meaning to be hated, which Autolycus himself explains when he chooses it.","Autolycus","en/d/autolico.html"],["From pest to mount","According to the sources, the rat was seen as a pest that damaged crops, and its subjection symbolizes Ganesha&#x27;s mastery over obstacles.","Mushaka","en/d/mushaka.html"],["An altar with a Roman dedicator","The altar was dedicated by a Roman citizen, yet its imagery follows a traditional Roman sacrifice, reflecting how officials honored local gods.","Vagdavercustis","en/d/vagdavercustis.html"],["A shared name","Actor is often confused with another Actor, king of Phthia and son of Myrmidon, who shares his name.","Actor","en/d/actor.html"],["A wooden image","The ancient images of Eileithyia in Athens were made of wood, and some were covered down to the feet.","Erysichthon of Attica","en/d/erisicton-del-atica.html"],["A recurring sculptor","The same sculptor, Sulinus son of Brucetus, dedicated works to the Suleviae both at Bath and at Cirencester.","Suleviae","en/d/suleviae.html"],["A very short reign","Eusebius of Caesarea assigns him a single year on the throne, a figure modern scholars consider hard to reconcile with the deeds attributed to him.","Agis I","en/d/agis-i.html"],["Broken cable","In 1983, contractors laying telephone cable measured a large shifting mass near the shore. When they retrieved the cable, they found it broken exactly where the anomaly had been.","Lagarfljót Worm","en/d/gusano-de-lagarfljot.html"],["Deaf coach","The Dullahan&#x27;s carriage is called Cóiste Bodhar, or \\","Dullahan","en/d/dullahan.html"],["Link to Ebisu","In some traditions Hiruko is associated with Ebisu, one of the deities connected with good fortune.","Hiruko","en/d/hiruko.html"],["Two names for one city","The city could be called Lacedaemon or Sparta, and both names were used interchangeably in the accounts.","Cleocharia","en/d/cleocaria.html"],["Meaning of the name","Vasoorimala literally means chain of smallpox pustules, since vasoori is the Malayalam word for smallpox.","Vasoorimala","en/d/vasoorimala.html"],["June&#x27;s beans","The 1 June festival was called the Bean-Kalends because ripe beans were used in her ceremonies that month.","Cardea","en/d/cardea.html"],["A telling name","Her Greek name points to abundance and flourishing life, fitting for a sea nymph.","Thaleia","en/d/talia-q1241338.html"],["A star&#x27;s name","His name comes from the Greek word for star, inherited from a Proto-Indo-European root with the same meaning.","Astraeus","en/d/astreo.html"],["Poene, punishment personified","An alternate version says Apollo sent Poene, the personification of punishment, who stole children from their mothers until Coroebus killed her.","Linus","en/d/linos.html"],["A horse with the same name","The name Gullfaxi reappears in an Icelandic folk tale collected by Jón Árnason, which has no direct connection to the giant’s horse.","Gullfaxi","en/d/gullfaxi.html"],["The last hieroglyphic inscription","At Philae, the last known hieroglyphic inscription, dated AD 394, was dedicated to him, and he appears in human form on a wall beside it.","Mandulis","en/d/mandulis.html"],["A crow with a history","Jayanta is one of the few gods told of as a crow, and his encounter with Rama explains why he loses an eye.","Jayanta","en/d/jayanta.html"],["Sea crow","His name means «sea crow», formed from <em>môr</em> (sea) and <em>brân</em> (crow) in Welsh.","Morfran","en/d/morfran.html"],["An ironic ending","Cabrakan is defeated after eating magic earth, which scholars read as an irony for a god of earthquakes.","Cabrakan","en/d/cabrakan.html"],["An undefined gender","The ancient texts do not specify whether Ukanomitama is male or female. Its interpretation as a goddess came later.","Ukanomitama","en/d/ukanomitama.html"],["A celestial cow","Her most common image is a cow with a sun disk between her horns. More rarely she appears as a woman with a cow&#x27;s head.","Mehet-Weret","en/d/meheturet.html"],["A dog named after a mouse","Luch Donn means «brown mouse», an ironic name for such a ferocious dog.","Celtchar","en/d/celtchar.html"],["A name with several explanations","Romans and Christian writers gave different interpretations of the name, from myrtle to laziness.","Murcia","en/d/murcia.html"],["A Sicilian temple","Some ancient sources place a temple to Adephagia on the island of Sicily, which is unusual for such a poorly documented deity.","Adephagia","en/d/adefagia.html"],["A frog with a dragon&#x27;s tradition","Although the creature is called a frog, the tale follows the pattern of dragon-slayer stories found in many European cultures.","The Dragon of the North","en/d/la-rana-del-norte.html"],["A tangled marriage","In some versions Cleola was married to her cousin Pleisthenes; in others, to her uncle Atreus. Both options place her within the same family of the Atreidai.","Cleola","en/d/cleola.html"],["Sister of the stars","In some versions she belongs to a family of four sisters, each linked to a different star depending on the time of day.","Poludnitsa","en/d/poludnitsa.html"],["Mountains named after her","The people of Arcadia believed the Nomian Mountains had been named after the nymph Nomia.","Nomia","en/d/nomia.html"],["Origin of the word","The name Huracán may come from a Carib word, and it is the source of the English words hurricane and orcan, a European windstorm.","Huracán","en/d/huracan.html"],["A play on <em>inire</em>","Rutilius Namatianus puns on the phrase <em>Faunus init</em>, &#x27;Faunus enters&#x27;, when describing the statue at the gate of Castrum Inui.","Inuus","en/d/inuus.html"],["Death whistle","The Aztec death whistle was found at his temple. Researchers still debate how it was used in rituals.","Ehecatl","en/d/ehecatl.html"],["A nickname explained by the sagas","The nickname Vidfamne is tied to his conquering zeal, and the Ynglinga saga cites it as the reason for his epithet, &quot;the long-armed&quot;.","Ivar Vidfamne","en/d/ivar-vidfamne.html"],["A kiss at the centre of the world","According to the Rigveda, Dyaus and Prithvi are described as kissing the central point of the world, an image of the union between sky and earth.","Prithvi","en/d/pritivi.html"],["A raven in the grimoires","In demonological texts his usual form is that of a raven, though he can take human form if the conjurer asks for it.","Malphas","en/d/malphas.html"],["A different mother","A scholion states that her mother was called Telephe, not Perimede, showing that ancient authors did not agree on her genealogy.","Astypalaea","en/d/astipalea.html"],["Transformed hounds","Fionn&#x27;s hounds, Bran and Sceolan, were also humans transformed into animals, which is why they did not harm Sadhbh when they found her.","Sadhbh","en/d/sadhbh.html"],["A body carried to Dionysus","On two Etruscan mirrors a woman carries a body labeled Esia before Minerva and Fufluns. It may recall a Greek myth in which Artemis brings Ariadne to Dionysus to be revived.","Artume","en/d/artume.html"],["Another Archelaus","Besides the hero, tradition knew another Archelaus, son of Electryon, father of Alcmene, who was the mother of Heracles. This is why the name seemed naturally tied to that family.","Archelaus","en/d/arquelao.html"],["A name with two meanings","His Nahuatl name can be understood as &#x27;the Toltec&#x27; or as &#x27;the skilled artisan&#x27;, two readings the sources give without settling which is correct.","Toltécatl","en/d/toltecatl.html"],["Born from the eyes","While he bathed, Izanagi gave birth to Amaterasu from his left eye, Tsukuyomi from his right eye and Susanoo from his nose.","Izanami","en/d/izanami.html"],["Torn mountains","According to tradition, the goddess tore up the Yatsugatake mountains because one of them was higher than Mount Fuji.","Konohanasakuya-hime","en/d/konohanasakuya-hime.html"],["An asteroid named after her","The asteroid 271 Penthesilea, discovered in 1887, was named in her honor.","Penthesilea","en/d/pentesilea.html"],["Twin serpents","He is shown with two serpents growing from his shoulders, making him one of the earliest known examples of this symbol.","Ningishzida","en/d/ningizzida.html"],["Nyctimus, the brother saved","According to some versions, the child Maenalus proposed sacrificing was his brother Nyctimus. Zeus brought him back to life and gave him the throne of Arcadia.","Maenalus","en/d/menalo.html"],["An asteroid named after him","The asteroid (11885) Summanus, discovered in 1990, commemorates this Roman god.","Summanus","en/d/summano.html"],["An asteroid with his name","The Trojan asteroid 1867 Deiphobus carries his name, like other Trojan-group asteroids that are named after heroes of the Trojan War.","Deiphobus","en/d/deifobo.html"],["Avian origin","Her origin is linked to the mythical nine-headed bird, from which her animal form is said to derive.","Jiutou Zhiji Jing","en/d/jiutou-zhiji-jing.html"],["Eight arms","Some versions describe him as a giant with eight arms, and in Hervarar saga he uses four swords at once in a duel.","Starkad","en/d/starkad.html"],["A hundred-year reign","According to Chinese chronicles calculated by Jesuit missionaries, his traditional reign lasted exactly one hundred years, beginning around the late third millennium BC.","Yellow Emperor (Huangdi)","en/d/emperador-amarillo-huangdi.html"],["Meaning of her name","Her name, <em>Moria</em>, means &#x27;sacred olive-tree&#x27; in Ancient Greek.","Moria","en/d/moria.html"],["A Mycenaean name","His name appears on Linear B tablets found at Pylos, dated to around the 13th century BC, linked to offerings of wine.","Dionysus","en/d/dioniso.html"],["Epithet of the Ganges","The goddess Ganga is called Jahnavi because the sage Jahnu let the river out through his ear.","Bhagiratha","en/d/bhagiratha.html"],["A birth saved by an eagle","According to Aelian, Gilgamesh&#x27;s grandfather kept his mother under guard to prevent pregnancy, because an oracle had said his grandson would overthrow him. The guards threw the child from a tower, but an eagle caught him mid-fall.","Gilgamesh","en/d/gilgamesh.html"],["Protector against scorpions","Her vahana is the scorpion, so believers call on her after a sting.","Anjana","en/d/anjana.html"],["A moon named after her","One of Jupiter&#x27;s moons is called Arce, taken from this muse.","Arche","en/d/arque.html"],["Name of the axolotl","The axolotl takes its name from this god. The Nahuatl word atl, water, combined with xolotl, is often translated as &quot;water monster&quot;.","Xolotl","en/d/xolotl.html"],["Three infallible arrows","Tradition credits the goddess Durga with giving Barbarika three arrows that never miss.","Barbarika","en/d/barbarika.html"],["An incomplete statue","Of the statue dedicated to Lenus at Caerwent only a pair of human feet and those of a goose survive, the goose being a bird linked with war in Celtic imagery.","Lenus","en/d/lenus.html"],["Poemandria","The city founded by Poemander was called Poemandria, while Tanagra took its name from his wife, a naiad.","Poemander","en/d/pemandro.html"],["A sculpture group at Olympia","Pausanias describes a work donated by the Phliasians showing Asopus&#x27;s daughters, placed in the sanctuary of Hippodamia at Olympia.","Harpina","en/d/harpina.html"],["The only one to wound Achilles","Asteropaios is the only Trojan in the Iliad who draws blood from Achilles, though he dies shortly afterwards at the hands of the Achaean hero.","Asteropaios","en/d/asteropeo.html"],["One wife, two identities","Some sources treat Rohini as the birth name of the queen Jambavati, while others consider them two different women.","Rohini","en/d/rohini-q14590114.html"],["Her name","“Shield-hand” refers to the way she caught arrows with her bare hand.","Chimalman","en/d/chimalma.html"],["Five faces","The five faces of Pashupati represent forms of Shiva and are associated with the five elements: earth, water, air, fire and ether.","Pashupati","en/d/pasupati.html"],["Brother-in-law of Hiranyakashipu","By marrying Holika, sister of Hiranyakashipu, Viprachitti became the brother-in-law of this powerful demon.","Viprachitti","en/d/viprachitti.html"],["A name that means poppy","Mecon comes from a Greek word meaning &quot;poppy&quot;, which explains the direct link between the character and the flower.","Mecon","en/d/mecon.html"],["A river of memory","In the underworld there was a river of Mnemosyne, opposed to Lethe. While ordinary souls drank from Lethe to forget, initiated Orphics chose to drink from the river of remembrance.","Mnemosyne","en/d/mnemosine.html"],["Tail and diadem","Some interpretations hold that the royal diadem of the Aztec emperors represented the serpent&#x27;s tail.","Xiuhcoatl","en/d/xiuhcoatl.html"],["A tool of creation","In the cosmological version, the land is formed from sand scattered from a snail shell with a rooster, and Obàtalá and Odùduwà are symbolically represented by a calabash, one taking the top and the other the bottom.","Odùduwà","en/d/oduduwa.html"],["Eleven names for the Moon","In the Alvíssmál, the dwarf Alvíss gives a different name for the Moon depending on the world: humans call it “moon”, elves call it “year-counter” and dwarves call it “the shiner”.","Máni","en/d/mani.html"],["A name with three eyes","The popular etymology of his name links it to the Greek for &quot;he who has three eyes&quot;, although its ending suggests a origin older than Greek.","Triopas","en/d/triopas.html"],["Onikuma stones","Large rocks in the Kiso Valley are called &quot;onikuma stones&quot; and are credited to these creatures because of their size and placement.","Onikuma","en/d/onikuma.html"],["A name meaning &quot;Moon&quot;","His name, written <em>jꜥḥ</em>, simply means &quot;Moon&quot;, which explains his role in the pantheon.","Iah","en/d/iah.html"],["A one-eyed being","In some tales the basajaun has a single eye in the middle of his forehead, a trait also attributed to Tartalo and often confused with him.","Basajaun","en/d/basajaun.html"],["A name with several readings","A late text offers another reading of her name: «Lady of Babylon, the saviour», based on an uncommon alternate name of that city.","Shuzianna","en/d/suzianna.html"],["A name that describes its role","Fragarach is known as &quot;The Answerer&quot; or &quot;The Retaliator&quot;, names that refer to its nature as a weapon that strikes back at whoever wields it against its bearer.","Fragarach","en/d/fragarach.html"],["Worms or epidemic","A 2nd century inscription was read as a response to worm infections, but a Spanish veterinary scientist proposed that it reflected an epidemic affecting both people and animals.","Verminus","en/d/verminus.html"],["Son of three hounds","His epithet mac Trí Con literally means &quot;son of three hounds&quot;. Its interpretation varies between genealogical traditions, some of which give him a multiple or symbolic parentage.","Lugaid mac Con Roí","en/d/lugaid-mac-con-roi.html"],["A name with several meanings","Amba is also a name of the goddess Parvati or Durga, and in astrology it denotes a condition arising from a conjunction of planets.","Amba","en/d/amba.html"],["Thai name","In Thailand he is known as Phra Phrom and is associated with education, the police and the law.","Brahmā","en/d/brahma-q289162.html"],["Three times sixty","The Rig Veda, in hymn 8.96.8, speaks of three times sixty Maruts, one of the highest figures the sources give for their number.","Maruts","en/d/marut.html"],["A name that changed","At Alalah, the original /m/ of the name shifted to /p/, so forms such as Shamshu and Shapshu both appear. At Ugarit only Shapshu is attested.","Shapshu","en/d/shapshu.html"],["A pact with Death","According to tradition, Orunmila was the only deity who made a pact with Ikú, Death, so that she would spare his children.","Orunmila","en/d/orunmila.html"],["A whole hymn","Aranyani has one of the most descriptive hymns in the Rigveda dedicated to a single deity.","Aranyani","en/d/aranyani.html"],["A name meaning river","The name Potamon comes from Greek and means &quot;river&quot; or &quot;stream&quot;, which fits his status as the son of a naiad, a nymph of fresh waters.","Potamon","en/d/potamon.html"],["A very old name","Hadad is already attested at Ebla around 2500 BCE, long before his cult reached Mesopotamia through the Amorites.","Adad","en/d/adad.html"],["A disputed name","The most accepted etymology links his name to pondering or melancholy, in the sense of being deep in thought.","Calchas","en/d/calcas.html"],["Five heads","Each head is linked to one of the five aspects of Shiva: Ishana, Tatpurusha, Aghora, Vamadeva and Sadyojata.","Heramba","en/d/heramba.html"],["A sea named after him","The Tyrrhenian Sea, between Italy, Corsica, Sardinia and Sicily, takes its name from the Romans, who extended the term from the region of Etruria.","Tyrrhenus","en/d/tirreno.html"],["A Roman tomb","The Tomb of Vibia, a Roman hypogeum of the second half of the 4th century on the Appian Way, shows her in painted panels beside Dis Pater, presiding over the tribunal of the dead.","Erecura","en/d/erecura.html"],["Kohbar paintings","Kohbar Mithila paintings include this goddess and are kept in the nuptial chamber of newlyweds.","Lajja Gauri","en/d/lajja-gauri.html"],["A goose or swan foot","Several medieval languages have a Bertha with a large foot. Grimm linked it to the flat foot of the spinning women who worked the loom&#x27;s treadle.","Perchta","en/d/perchta.html"],["A robot with his name","The remote-controlled robot that explored the shafts of the Queen&#x27;s Chamber in the Great Pyramid was named Wepwawet, meaning &quot;opener of the ways&quot;.","Wepwawet","en/d/upuaut.html"],["The first chariot races","According to the myth, the chariot races at Azan&#x27;s funeral games were the first held in Greece, and it was there that Apis died accidentally.","Aetolus","en/d/etolo.html"],["A name in doubt","Although his name seems to be of Sanskrit origin, Myōken is absent from early Buddhist literature and first appears in apocryphal Chinese sutras.","Myōken","en/d/myoken.html"],["Other names","In the <em>Vishnu Purana</em> she is called Shaibya or Shaivya, meaning descendant of King Shibi. The commentator Ratnagarbha identifies her with Kalindi, another chief queen of Krishna.","Mitravinda","en/d/mitravinda.html"],["Conflicting numbers","Homer and Apollodorus do not agree on how many suitors came with Demoptolemus from Dulichium: 51 according to the former and 56 according to the latter.","Demoptolemus","en/d/demoptolemus.html"],["The largest bird sculpture","The Jatayu Earth&#x27;s Center park in Chadayamangalam holds a statue of Jatayu that is considered the world&#x27;s largest bird sculpture.","Jatayu","en/d/jatayu.html"],["A hard-to-see star","Alcor is so faint that, according to a belief, someone near death can no longer make it out.","Arundhati","en/d/arundhati.html"],["A protective trio","Deverra was one of three deities who protected midwives and women in labour, together with Pilumnus and Intercidona.","Deverra","en/d/deverra.html"],["Gaulish root","The Gaulish word taruos means ‘bull’. It has relatives in Celtic languages such as Old Irish tarb and Welsh tarw.","Tarvos Trigaranus","en/d/tarvos-trigaranus.html"],["An imperial visit","The emperor Caligula is said to have visited the sacred grove of Clitumnus before his campaign against Germany, presumably to consult the oracle.","Clitumnus","en/d/clitumno.html"],["A hymn without repetitions","The Lalita Sahasranama is the only sahasranama that never repeats a name, and it avoids the filler particles other hymns use to keep the meter.","Tripura Sundari","en/d/tripura-sundari-q1722818.html"],["Sacred elephants","In some regions of eastern India, wild elephants are worshipped as manifestations of Mahakala.","Mahakala","en/d/mahakala.html"],["A bee with his name","According to the story, a species of bees bears the name of Meliteus, in memory of the child who was nurtured by them.","Othreis","en/d/otreide.html"],["A name meaning “child”","His name combines pilli, “child,” the diminutive tzintli and tecuhtli, “lord,” so it is usually rendered as “the young lord.”","Piltzintecuhtli","en/d/piltzintecuhtli.html"],["Another name","Antoninus Liberalis calls her Hypermestra and her father Aethon, a name tied to the hunger that consumed him.","Mestra","en/d/mestra.html"],["Born from a staff","Among the gods born from the objects Izanagi discarded, Tsukitatsufunado arose from the staff he threw.","Chimata-no-kami","en/d/chimata-no-kami.html"],["A name with a double meaning","Tuisto may derive from the Proto-Germanic root *twai, &#x27;two&#x27;, and mean &#x27;double&#x27;. Linking it to a gender is only conjecture, since the same root gives words such as &#x27;dispute&#x27; in other Germanic languages.","Tuisto","en/d/tuisto.html"],["Meaning of the name","The name is usually translated as &quot;dark fields&quot; or &quot;downward fields&quot;, since its first element may relate to niðr, meaning &quot;down&quot;.","Niðavellir","en/d/niavellir.html"],["Three days of battle","Milton places the war in heaven over the course of three days, following the proclamation of Jesus Christ as the Son of God.","War in Heaven","en/d/guerra-en-el-cielo.html"],["An ancient name","His name first appears as an ordinary personal name in documents from the Ur III period.","Humbaba","en/d/humbaba.html"],["A Vedic term","In the Rig Veda, the word diti means generosity and may personify that virtue, which would make it a goddess.","Diti","en/d/diti.html"],["A domestic tiebreaker","To decide which of the twins was the elder, the story turns to an everyday detail: their mother fed and cleaned Eurysthenes first.","Eurysthenes","en/d/euristenes.html"],["Diomede&#x27;s lovely curls","The Catalogue of Women describes her as &quot;the one with lovely curls&quot;, an epithet that highlights her beauty.","Diomede (daughter of Lapithes)","en/d/diomede-hija-de-lapites.html"],["Wheels of joy","The hollow balls the lioness plays with can be shown in Tibetan art as three-coloured wheels of joy (dga&#x27; &#x27;khyil).","Snow Lion","en/d/leon-de-las-nieves.html"],["A name with a double meaning","The word “kāne”, on its own, means “man”, and it is also the name of one of the four major Hawaiian gods.","Kāne Milohai","en/d/kane-milohai.html"],["Buddhist murals","His story is depicted in the caves of Ajanta and in those of Mogao.","Shibi","en/d/shibi.html"],["A disputed place","Cabesos, Othryoneus&#x27;s homeland, has no clear location. Some seek it in the Troad and others at Kavissos, near Feres, by the Evros.","Othryoneus","en/d/otrioneo.html"],["A name with several readings","The name is usually translated as &quot;Lord of Our Sustenance&quot;, but some read its root as &quot;human flesh&quot; or &quot;food&quot;, and others as &quot;abundance&quot;.","Tōnacātēcuhtli","en/d/tonacatecuhtli.html"],["A disputed name","Her name is usually rendered as « Lady Greenery », but scholars disagree on its reading, and its meaning bears no clear link to her known roles.","Ninšar","en/d/ninsar.html"],["A city with two names","Chaeronea, in Boeotia, was formerly called Arne. Its eponymous hero, Chaeron, gave the town the name it keeps today.","Chaeron","en/d/queron.html"],["Borrowed name","The Etruscans took her name from the Greeks, reflecting the influence of Greek culture on the Etruscan world.","Persipnei","en/d/persipnei.html"],["A difficult letter","Her name is spelled in several ways, including Đirona, because Latin could not easily represent its initial sound, possibly a Celtic combination of s or t.","Sirona","en/d/sirona.html"],["A gate named after her","The Porta Pandana, beside which her sanctuary stood, gave access to the Capitoline hill. The gate kept her name.","Empanda","en/d/empanda.html"],["A temple for the mice","Teucer is remembered for the first temple to Apollo Sminthius, the &quot;destroyer of mice&quot;, linked to the plague that struck his camp.","Teucer","en/d/teucro.html"],["An early inscription","The earliest known evidence of his name is an inscription on the strap of a soldier&#x27;s shield, found at Olympia and dated to around 600–575 BC.","Mopsus","en/d/mopso.html"],["The helmet of Amyntor","Meriones inherited from his father the helmet of Amyntor. He later gave it to Odysseus when Odysseus set out as a night spy into the Trojan camp.","Meriones","en/d/meriones.html"],["Name","Kripa means &quot;pity&quot; or &quot;mercy&quot; in Sanskrit, and the name refers to the compassion with which he was adopted.","Kripa","en/d/kripa.html"],["Origin of the name","His name has a disputed etymology. Sarmiento de Gamboa linked it to sea foam, while others connect it to the lake and the origin of all things.","Viracocha","en/d/huiracocha.html"],["A Japanese meme","His ghost-like image spread across Japanese social media after the 2012 exhibitions.","Medjed","en/d/medjed.html"],["River of the underworld","Some rivers far from Sumer were called the «river of the underworld». This suggests that one tradition placed the realm in a remote area, perhaps in the west.","Kur","en/d/kur.html"],["A cursed chest","According to Pausanias, the chest that drove Eurypylus mad had been abandoned by Aeneas when he fled Troy, or left deliberately by Cassandra, who cursed it against whichever Greek opened it.","Eurypylus","en/d/euripilo-q1588440.html"],["Two names for one colonist","Stephanus of Byzantium, Conon and Ovid call the colonist of Andros Andros, son of Anius, rather than Andreus.","Andreus","en/d/andreo.html"],["Three stumps","The name is read as &#x27;three stumps&#x27;, possibly referring to the alignment of stars in the Southern Cross.","Trishanku","en/d/trishanku.html"],["A Sogdian stirrup","When Nana rides a lion, Sogdian artists show her astride with a visible stirrup, a detail not attested earlier in her iconography.","Nana","en/d/nana.html"],["A god on coins","From the Flavian dynasty on, Serapis appeared on imperial coinage alongside the reigning emperor.","Serapis","en/d/serapis.html"],["A honey cake","A fragment of Archilochus, quoted by Athenaeus, tells that Aithiops, a member of the expedition, traded his plot in the new colony for a honey cake.","Archias of Corinth","en/d/arquias-de-corinto.html"],["A ship named after a city","Jason&#x27;s ship, the Argo, takes its name from the city of Argos. According to tradition, the name honours the Danaids who lived there.","Sthenele","en/d/estenele.html"],["A drained spring","In 1822 Jean-Baptiste Jollois artificially drained the spring to see whether it could still supply the town&#x27;s public fountains, and he found the inscription in a former cesspit.","Acionna","en/d/acionna.html"],["Confusion in art","According to Edward H. Schafer, in East Asian religious art the kinnara is often confused with the kalaviṅka, another half-human, half-bird hybrid, although the two are distinct and unrelated.","Kinnara","en/d/kinnara.html"],["Several fathers","Carme is presented as a daughter of Arabus and Phoenix in some versions and of Eubuleus in others, showing that ancient authors did not agree on her family tree.","Cassiopeia","en/d/casiopea.html"],["A play on words","The lotus and the box are pronounced the same as the Chinese word for harmony, which is why they serve as symbols of union.","He He Er Xian","en/d/he-he-er-xian.html"],["A bird&#x27;s name","Gerana takes her name from the Greek word for crane, the bird she was transformed into.","Gerana","en/d/gerana.html"],["A drill with a name","In the Prose Edda, the auger is given the name Rati, which is unusual for a tool in Norse stories.","Rati","en/d/rati-q7295695.html"],["Athena&#x27;s flutes","Pindar says Athena invented flutes to imitate the shrill cry of Euryale as she chased Perseus.","Stheno","en/d/esteno.html"],["The sun crow","Takamimusubi sent the Yatagarasu, a three-legged crow associated with the sun, to help Emperor Jimmu.","Kotoamatsukami","en/d/kotoamatsukami.html"],["An omen in the cellar","An owl driving bees away from a wine cellar in Minos&#x27; palace led Polyidus to Glaucus&#x27; body.","Polyidus","en/d/poliido.html"],["A distinctive mount","Meldi Mata stands apart from other Gujarati goddesses by her mount: Bahuchar Mata rides a rooster and Khodiyar Mata a crocodile.","Meldi Mata","en/d/meldi-mata.html"],["Origin of the term Saracen","In the Lancelot-Grail Cycle, the name Sarras gives rise to the word used for Muslims. Historians note that &quot;Saracens&quot; was in fact a Greek designation for Arab tribes of the Sinai Desert.","Sarras","en/d/sarras.html"],["Owl or seabird?","Some propose a horned owl because its name resembles words for owl, while Celoria thought of a seabird.","Byssa","en/d/byssa.html"],["Clouds at the funeral","In the Mimibukuro story, dark clouds cover a funeral and, when they clear, the corpse is gone from the coffin.","Mōryō","en/d/moryo.html"],["Half white, half black","In Cleopatra&#x27;s <em>Chrysopoeia</em>, the ouroboros is divided into a white half and a black half. Some have related this duality to Gnostic thought or to the Taoist yin and yang.","Ouroboros","en/d/uroboro.html"],["A borrowed name","Titania derives from Ovid, who uses it as an epithet of the Roman goddess Diana.","Fairy Queen","en/d/reina-de-las-hadas.html"],["A meaningful name","Kishar&#x27;s name is usually translated as &quot;Whole Earth&quot;, which reinforces her link to the material world set against the sky.","Kishar","en/d/kishar.html"],["A male Alpan","On one of the Etruscan mirrors, Alpan is depicted as a man, which differs from most of her images.","Alpanu","en/d/alpanu.html"],["A mortal Hecate","Diodorus describes a Hecate, daughter of Perses, as a mortal priestess and sorceress, distinct from the Titan Hecate, goddess of witchcraft. She shares the name and some traits with her.","Perses","en/d/perses-q2071913.html"],["Discovery at Güímar","According to tradition, an image of the Virgin was found on a stone on the beaches of Güímar between 1390 and 1401.","Chaxiraxi","en/d/chaxiraxi.html"],["Magic cauldron","In Preiddeu Annwfn, the cauldron of the lord of Annwn is finished with pearl and will not boil the food of a coward.","Annwn","en/d/annwn.html"],["Origin of the name","The name may come from rokuro, a word for a potter&#x27;s wheel, a well&#x27;s pulley or an umbrella handle, all of which lengthen.","Rokurokubi","en/d/rokurokubi.html"],["A horse as emblem","In Jain art, the horse carved on the pedestal is what identifies Sambhavanatha among the other tirthankaras.","Sambhavanatha","en/d/sambhavanatha.html"],["A disputed name","His name has been translated as bear-yeller or mountain-yeller. The linguist Jan de Vries proposed reading it as one who roars like a bear.","Bergelmir","en/d/bergelmir.html"],["Several Greek spellings","In Greek inscriptions the name appears as Κολάνθης, Κολάνθας or Κολάνθος, and the hieroglyphs in the temple of Athribis vary from scene to scene.","Kolanthes","en/d/colantes.html"],["A name with several readings","Her name could mean &quot;lone she-wolf&quot;, perhaps referring to the jaws of the waves that sank ships or broke against the shore.","Oiolica","en/d/oiolica.html"],["A wooden cow","Daedalus built a hollow cow-shaped device covered with real hide so that the bull would approach Pasiphaë. This deception is central to the myth.","Pasiphaë","en/d/pasifae.html"],["Days of the week","Záviš Kalandra believed the names of the princes were disguised ancient Slavic days of the week.","Mnata","en/d/mnata.html"],["A court case","In 1852, a reaper who claimed to be cursed with lycanthropy was arrested by the Guardia Civil near Escalona (Toledo), after years of crimes across several regions.","Enchanted Wolf","en/d/lobo-hechizado.html"],["From Arcadia to Acadia","The explorer Giovanni da Verrazzano applied the name «Arcadia» to the North American Atlantic coast in the 16th century. Over time the word evolved into Acadia.","Arcadia","en/d/arcadia.html"],["A moon of Saturn","One of Saturn&#x27;s moons is named after Mundilfari, an example of how Norse mythology still appears in modern astronomical naming.","Mundilfari","en/d/mundilfari.html"],["Identification with Mars","In five inscriptions he is explicitly called Mars Belatucadrus, merging him with the Roman god of war.","Belatucadros","en/d/belatucadros.html"],["A necklace with a past","The necklace and robe at the heart of the plot pass from Harmonia to Eriphyle depending on the version, and the quest for them sets the whole story in motion.","Callirhoe","en/d/calirroe-q1722503.html"],["A fierce head","The rear face, called kapila, may have a moustache, bulging eyes and fangs, and is read either as wrathful or as the sage Kapila.","Vaikuntha Chaturmurti","en/d/vaikuntha-chaturmurti.html"],["Scottish whirlpool","In Orcadian and Shetlandic folklore, the witches Grotti Finnie and Grotti Minnie create a whirlpool in the Pentland Firth called the Swelkie.","Grótti","en/d/grotti.html"],["An island with his kin","Thasus, whom some sources make a son or nephew of Cilix, gave his name to the island of Thasos in the northern Aegean.","Cilix","en/d/cilix.html"],["Oldest god image in the Americas","A depiction found in 2003 on gourd fragments, dated to around 2250 BCE, is the oldest known image of a deity on the continent.","Staff God","en/d/dios-de-los-baculos.html"],["A name from dogwood trees","According to another legend, the Greeks cut dogwood trees from Apollo&#x27;s sacred grove on Mount Ida to build the wooden horse. When they learned the god was angry, they appeased him and gave him the epithet Carneius after those trees.","Carnus","en/d/carno.html"],["Shaming monuments","Herodotus says Sesostris had pillars carved with a female image on the monuments of peoples who surrendered, to shame them.","Sesostris","en/d/sesostris.html"],["Late three legs","Neither the Kojiki nor the Nihon Shoki says he has three legs. The first reference to that figure appears in the Wamyō Ruijushō, written around 930.","Yatagarasu","en/d/yatagarasu.html"],["An eating contest","In Snorri&#x27;s account, Logi ate not only the meat but also the bones and the wooden board it was served on.","Logi","en/d/logi.html"],["An arrow that returns","His death gave rise to a Japanese saying about an arrow that comes back to the one who shot it.","Ame no Wakahiko","en/d/ame-no-wakahiko.html"],["Two births","In the <em>Kojiki</em> he is born from Izanagi and Izanami, while in the <em>Nihon Shoki</em> he emerges when Izanagi splits his son Kagutsuchi in two.","Ōyamatsumi","en/d/oyamatsumi.html"],["Widespread belief","A 2004 study of 1695 women from the Nigerian states of Ondo and Ekiti found that more than half believed in abiku children.","Abiku","en/d/abiku.html"],["Names for goats","In the Prose Edda, the names of both goats also appear in a list of names for goats.","Tanngnjóstr and Tanngrisnir","en/d/tanngnjostr-y-tanngrisnir.html"],["A name made of numbers","In the Greek numerical system, the letters of Abraxas add up to 365, the number of days in the solar year.","Abraxas","en/d/abraxas.html"],["Possible Canaanite origin","The writer Jacques Collin de Plancy wondered whether Bael was the same as the Canaanite god Baal, and considered this a reasonable assumption.","Bael","en/d/bael.html"],["A disputed name","The origin of the name Sovij is debated. Some consider it a borrowing from Arabic or the name of a Lithuanian tribe, but most etymologists link it to an Indo-European root meaning sun.","Sovij","en/d/sovij.html"],["Origin of the name","His name is thought to combine Tarku, an ancient Anatolian deity of fertility, and Angegh, a god worshipped mainly in the province of Angegh in south-western Armenia.","Tork Angegh","en/d/tork-angegh.html"],["An uncertain name","Its etymology is unclear, although it has been proposed to mean something like &quot;torch or staff bearer who goes in front&quot;.","Hendursaga","en/d/hendursaga.html"],["A meaningful name","Anasuya combines the negative prefix an with asuya, meaning jealousy, hence her sense of being free from envy.","Anasuya","en/d/anasuya.html"],["Thumb-sized","To ask King Nala for help, Karkotaka shrank himself to the size of a thumb.","Karkotaka","en/d/karkotaka.html"],["Cook and a human sacrifice","In 1777 the captain James Cook witnessed a human sacrifice made to Oro. The victim, held on a platform, was struck on the skull with a sacred mace.","Oro","en/d/oro.html"],["A rat of the twelve signs","According to the Keizai Zasshisha, written in the Azuchi-Momoyama period, the Nezumi no Hokura shrine at Hiyoshi Taisha actually honors the Rat of the Twelve Earthly Branches.","Tesso","en/d/tesso.html"],["A very early statue","Ur-Nanshe had a statue of the goddess erected, and the inscription commemorating it is considered by scholars to be the oldest known text mentioning anthropomorphic statues of deities.","Gatumdug","en/d/gatumdu.html"],["Keeper of keys","He received the epithet Kleidouchos, &#x27;key-bearer&#x27;, because he was thought to hold the key to hearts.","Eros","en/d/eros.html"],["An inscription at Mathura","An old stupa at Mathura bears an inscription dated 157 CE that mentions an image of Aranatha. Somadeva Suri and Jinaprabha Suri instead attribute the stupa to Suparshvanatha.","Aranatha","en/d/aranatha.html"],["A name with ancient echoes","The name Lotherus may share its origin with Hlǫðr in the Hervarar saga and the Hlöðskviða, or with Hliþe in the Old English poem Widsith.","Lotherus","en/d/lotherus.html"],["Thirteen days and nights","According to the legend, Thalestris stayed with Alexander for thirteen days and nights, a period as precise as her wish to have a daughter.","Thalestris","en/d/talestris.html"],["A god with twelve rays","His solar halo traditionally had twelve rays, symbolising the twelve months of the year.","Helios","en/d/helios.html"],["Name of the island","According to the legend, the three queens asked that the island bear their names. Ériu is the origin of Éire, while Banba and Fódla remain poetic names for Ireland, much as Albion does for Great Britain.","Amergin Glúingel","en/d/amergin-gluingel.html"],["A stolen golden idol","Phosop&#x27;s idol at Wat Siri Wattanaram in Bang Phrom was once believed to be made of gold, but it was stolen in the 1970s by three men from outside the area and never recovered.","Phosop","en/d/phosop.html"],["A wolf that hunts alone","Unlike wolves, which hunt in packs, the amarok is described as a solitary hunter that attacks anyone who goes out alone at night.","Amarok","en/d/amarok.html"],["A nearly forgotten wind","Hesiod discusses the principal winds without naming Eurus, and the Dionysiaca is one of the few sources that gives him a family.","Eurus","en/d/euro.html"],["Two mothers for Devayani","The <em>Mahabharata</em> considers Jayanti the mother of Devayani, but the <em>Devi Bhagavata Purana</em> assigns her to Urjjasvati, another wife of Shukra.","Jayanti","en/d/jayanti.html"],["Three daughters, three versions","The ancient sources do not agree on the name of Atrax&#x27;s daughter: Ovid mentions Hippodamia, Antoninus Liberalis Caenis and Pseudo-Plutarch Damasippe.","Atrax","en/d/atrax.html"],["Latin name","In Latin, <em>murmur</em> means noise, whisper, murmur and also the sound of a trumpet, meanings that fit his role as a herald.","Murmur","en/d/murmur.html"],["A travelling name","When he appears in disguise, Gylfi goes by the name Gangleri.","Gylfi","en/d/gylfi.html"],["A bird of many names","It is also known as Anqa al-Mughrib, where <em>mughrib</em> suggests the strange, the distant and the west.","Anqa","en/d/anqa.html"],["A much-debated video","Critics of Kozak&#x27;s footage note that the camera never pans left, which could hide a boat, and that the creature moves in a straight line.","Lake Van Monster","en/d/monstruo-del-lago-van.html"],["A station shaped like a palace","Katase-Enoshima Station in Fujisawa has a design reminiscent of Ryūgū-jō, although it is a modern building inspired by the legend.","Ryūgū-jō","en/d/ryugu-jo.html"],["A lost tragedy","The term Phorcydes also gives its name to a lost tragedy by Aeschylus.","Phorcydes","en/d/forcides.html"],["Statues in major temples","Their statues can be seen in heavily visited temples such as those at Tirumala, Puri and Srirangam.","Jaya and Vijaya","en/d/jaya-y-vijaya.html"],["A name with several etymologies","Ancient writers linked his name to <em>eris</em> (strife) and <em>chthon</em> (earth), or to <em>erion</em> (wool), though the modern view treats these as folk etymologies.","Erichthonius","en/d/erictonio.html"],["Changes in modern editions","Some modern editions, such as Guðni Jónsson&#x27;s 1954 edition and Henry Adams Bellows&#x27;s 1923 English translation, have replaced the name Narfi with Váli to match the account in the Prose Edda.","Narfi","en/d/narfi-q1194706.html"],["Possible origin in Zhurong","Modern scholars believe Hung Shing derives from the worship of Zhurong, the fire deity and god of the southern seas. That worship predates the Tang dynasty.","Hung Shing","en/d/hung-shing.html"],["An imperial temple","Emperor Hong Wu of the Ming dynasty wrote a calligraphy dedicated to the goddess for the Xiaotong Temple.","Longmu","en/d/longmu.html"],["Hemp as currency","According to the sources, hemp was used as a means of exchange from the Joseon period onward.","Mago Halmi","en/d/mago-halmi.html"],["Told by Nestor","Ovid does not tell the episode directly, but within the words of Nestor, who recounts the battle between the Lapiths and the centaurs.","Hylonome","en/d/hylonome.html"],["A metal name","The name Chalcis was linked to the Greek word for bronze, χαλκόν. That is why the nymph was said to have invented bronze weapons.","Combe","en/d/combe.html"],["Day of the week","The English name Thursday means &#x27;day of Thunor&#x27;, the Old English form of Thor, and derives from a weekday name calqued from Latin &#x27;day of Jove&#x27;.","Thor","en/d/thor.html"],["Runic mention","Hading appears in the Runekrønike section of the <em>Codex Runicus</em> as the father of King Froþe, although the fragment is incomplete.","Hadingus","en/d/hadingus.html"],["A creative New Year","According to a tradition recorded in the sources, the world was created at Chinese New Year, and animals, people and many deities came into being during its fifteen days.","Chinese creation myths","en/d/mitos-de-creacion-chinos.html"],["A name meaning prosperity","Auðr means &quot;prosperity&quot; in Old Norse, a name that contrasts with how little is told about him.","Auðr","en/d/aur.html"],["The surgeon&#x27;s photograph","The 1934 photograph showing a long neck above the water was attributed to a surgeon, R. K. Wilson. In 1994 Marmaduke Wetherell&#x27;s son-in-law claimed it was a fake.","Loch Ness Monster","en/d/monstruo-del-lago-ness.html"],["Statue taken to Rome","After the Roman conquest of Sinope, Lucullus carried Autolycus&#x27;s statue to Rome.","Autolycus","en/d/autolico-q4056272.html"],["A sword that splits shields","In Hervarar saga, the sword Tyrfing cuts through Arngrim&#x27;s shield and sinks into the ground.","Arngrim","en/d/arngrim.html"],["A disputed name","One traditional etymology links it to a verb meaning to take charge or to care for. Since Wilamowitz, many scholars prefer a different reading, connected to phallos and to the verb meaning to marry.","Cedalion","en/d/cedalion.html"],["Place among the Mahavidya","She is the fourth of the ten Mahavidya goddesses, a group revered within Shaktism.","Bhuvaneshvari","en/d/bhuvaneshvari.html"],["A temple born of a promise","The temple was built to fulfill a vow made during the storm off Corsica, an event recorded on Scipio&#x27;s epitaph.","Tempestas","en/d/tempestas.html"],["A name with several meanings","Besides a goddess, the term Dhisana came to name knowledge, intelligence and speech, as well as a ritual vessel.","Dhisana","en/d/dhisana.html"],["The burner","Aillen was called “the burner,” a nickname that sums up his role in the stories: he set Tara ablaze with his fiery breath.","Aillen","en/d/aillen.html"],["A name that states her role","Her name derives from the Latin bos, meaning ox or cattle, like those of other minor Roman deities.","Bubona","en/d/bubona.html"],["A name with a landscape","Her name is interpreted as &quot;lady of the pure reed&quot;. Jacobsen deduced from this that she and her daughter were associated with reeds and marshes.","Ningikuga","en/d/ningikuga.html"],["A treasury with a trap","According to Pausanias, the treasury of Hyrieus stood in Orchomenus and was attributed to Trophonius and Agamedes, whose story gives rise to a legend of its own.","Hyrieus","en/d/hirieo.html"],["A telling name","Her name means &#x27;Gorgon slayer&#x27;, a tribute to her father Perseus&#x27;s killing of Medusa.","Gorgophone","en/d/gorgofone.html"],["A treasury with an Egyptian echo","Herodotus tells an almost identical story about the treasury of the Egyptian king Rhampsinitus, which has led scholars to ask which tale came first.","Agamedes","en/d/agamedes.html"],["A narrator dog","The Victorian poet George Augustus Simcox wrote the poem My Master Mordred, narrated from the point of view of the character&#x27;s dog.","Mordred","en/d/mordred.html"],["The Nemean Games","According to Hyginus, the abduction of Chrysippus took place during the Nemean Games, while Laius was teaching him to drive a chariot.","Laius","en/d/layo.html"],["Vīr and pīr","Gogaji combines two identities: that of a <em>vīr</em>, or warrior, and that of a <em>pīr</em>, or saint.","Gogaji","en/d/gogaji.html"],["A crater in his name","In 1970 the International Astronomical Union named a crater on the far side of the Moon Wan-Hoo after him.","Wan Hu","en/d/wan-hu.html"],["The Liver of Piacenza","This bronze liver model, used for divination, mentions Fufluns twice among the gods of the Etruscan astrological houses.","Fufluns","en/d/fufluns.html"],["Asteroid named after her","The main belt asteroid 427 Galene takes its name from her.","Galene","en/d/galene.html"],["A name meaning oppression","Scholars link Narfi to the Old Norse adjective <em>nǫrr</em>, meaning “narrow.” This would give the giant a sense of something closed-in and oppressive, fitting for the night.","Narfi","en/d/narfi.html"],["Temple guardian","In China his figures flank temple and monastery gates as guardians.","Vajrapani","en/d/vajrapani.html"],["Three thousand sisters","Ianthe was one of the three thousand Oceanids, daughters of Oceanus and Tethys, which shows how large the family of water nymphs in Greek mythology was.","Ianthe","en/d/ianthe.html"],["Four hundred breasts","She was also called the one of the Four Hundred Breasts, in reference to the countless leaves a maguey produced and the many products it provided.","Mayahuel","en/d/mayahuel.html"],["A name tied to helping","Her Latin name, Iuturna, may come from a verb meaning to help, iuvaret, which fits her role as a helper of mortals.","Juturna","en/d/yuturna.html"],["A hooded spirit","Among the few votive statuettes found at the Möhn sanctuary is one of a genius cucullatus, similar to those offered to the Xulsigiae at the Lenus Mars temple complex in Trier.","Ancamna","en/d/ancamna.html"],["Rival brothers","According to tradition, the twins Acrisius and Proetus were already fighting in their mother&#x27;s womb, an image ancient authors used to stress the family rivalry.","Abas","en/d/abas.html"],["A river with a hellish name","According to the story, the river where the tyrant&#x27;s body was thrown became known as Tartarus, the same name the citizens had given the tyrant.","Aspalis","en/d/aspalis.html"],["A dated statue","A statue of Shiva and Swasthani was consecrated in 1674 by King Pratap Malla and is identified by its inscription.","Swasthani","en/d/swasthani.html"],["A volcano on a moon","A volcano on Jupiter&#x27;s moon Io bears Pele&#x27;s name, officially approved by the International Astronomical Union.","Pele","en/d/pele.html"],["Tree of awakening","Kassapa attained enlightenment under a banyan, which the sources name as his bodhi tree.","Kassapa Buddha","en/d/buda-kassapa.html"],["Kuhikugu","After Fawcett&#x27;s presumed death, Westerners discovered the Kuhikugu site in 1925, near the headwaters of the Xingu River. It holds the ruins of about twenty cities and villages that may have housed up to 50,000 people.","The Lost City of Z","en/d/ciudad-perdida-de-z.html"],["Link to Mars","Some authors have suggested that her Slovak form, Marmuriena, may be related to the Roman god of war Mars, although the idea is debated.","Morana","en/d/morana.html"],["A forbidden spear","The Gáe Bolga was a barbed spear that Scáthach taught only Cú Chulainn to use, which made it decisive in the duel&#x27;s outcome.","Ferdiad","en/d/ferdiad.html"],["A sparrow as spy","In the story, the sparrow that kept Dag informed about the world is the reason for his final military campaign.","Dag the Wise","en/d/dag-el-sabio.html"],["Guardian of the bathroom","In Japan he is regarded as a guardian of the bathroom, and his effigy is often placed there.","Ucchuṣma","en/d/ucchusma.html"],["Three pups in a skull","According to the tale, Culann&#x27;s pup was found in the skull of Conganchnes mac Dedad, along with two other pups belonging to the hounds of Celtchar and Mac da Tho.","Culann","en/d/culann.html"],["A name of uncertain origin","The origin of the name Dymas is obscure and probably non-Greek, which suggests a root older than the Hellenic language.","Dymas","en/d/dimas.html"],["A constellation","Hyginus says Apollo placed his daughter Parthenos among the stars, where she became the constellation Virgo.","Chrysothemis","en/d/crisotemis-q9191502.html"],["A nickname in manga","In the manga Hoshi no Samidare, the dog knight Shinonome Hangetsu was nicknamed «Fūjin» for his combat skills.","Fūjin","en/d/fujin.html"],["A horse that treads the sea","Hófvarpnir can move through the air and over the sea. His name is usually translated as &quot;he who throws his hoofs about&quot; or &quot;hoof-thrower&quot;.","Gná","en/d/gna.html"],["A horse with her name","Cúchulainn&#x27;s chariot horse was called Liath Macha, &#x27;Macha&#x27;s Grey&#x27;. In the tale, twin colts were born on the same day as the hero.","Macha","en/d/macha.html"],["Two Conans","Conán mac Lia shares his name with Conán mac Morna, the other Conán of the Fianna, who is much better known.","Conán mac Lia","en/d/conan-mac-lia.html"],["A modern name","&quot;Serpopard&quot; is a modern compound of &quot;serpent&quot; and &quot;leopard&quot;. No ancient text gave the creature a name.","Serpopard","en/d/serpopardo.html"],["Kingfisher-feather robes","According to Du Guangting&#x27;s text, she wears garments of nine colours made of kingfisher feathers and rides a cinnabar phoenix.","Xuannü","en/d/xuan-nu.html"],["A character with two meanings","The same character 天 denotes both the physical sky and a religious force. That is why the term can refer to a personal being or to an impersonal principle.","Tian","en/d/tian.html"],["A name with meaning","Her name derives from <em>śakunta</em>, a word for a bird or a bird of prey, and is explained as one protected by the birds.","Shakuntala","en/d/shakuntala.html"],["Adinkra symbol","The Gye Nyame symbol remains very present in today&#x27;s Akan culture, although its meaning changed under the influence of Christianity.","Nyame","en/d/nyame.html"],["Mentor and Athena","In Book II of the Odyssey, Athena also takes the guise of Mentor, which adds to the confusion between the two names.","Mentes","en/d/mentes.html"],["Two Polyphemuses","Euphorion and Socrates mistakenly called him a son of Poseidon, confusing him with the Cyclops who bears the same name.","Polyphemus","en/d/polifemo-q2627407.html"],["Blood and mountains","Snorri interprets Brimir&#x27;s blood as the sea and his bones as the mountains, when the world was made from Ymir&#x27;s body.","Brimir","en/d/brimir.html"],["The ‘bunyip bird’","The Australasian bittern, a marsh bird, produces a low booming call during the breeding season, which has earned it the nickname.","Bunyip","en/d/bunyip.html"],["An extinct wolf","The figure is believed to have been inspired by the Honshū wolf, a now-extinct subspecies that was said to hunt children in large packs.","Okuri-ōkami","en/d/okuri-okami.html"],["A name with a double origin","According to the Suda, Delphi was named after Delphyne, although her own name seems to have been formed from Delphi.","Delphyne","en/d/delfina.html"],["Swallows named after a myth","The swallow genus Progne takes its name from Procne, the sister who in Latin tradition becomes a swallow.","Philomela","en/d/filomela.html"],["Paul Bunyan","More recent Ojibwe legends tell of his clash with the folk lumberjack Paul Bunyan, who originated among Canadian loggers in the 1800s. In these versions, the fight lasts forty days and forty nights, and Nanabozho ends it by slapping Bunyan across the face with a giant walleye.","Nanabozho","en/d/nanabozho.html"],["Unusual images","As Kamado-gami he is sometimes depicted as a woman, and some representations give him three heads.","Kōjin","en/d/kojin.html"],["A creature with no body","According to the Lüshi Chunqiu, the taotie had a head but no body, and when it ate people it did not swallow them but harmed them.","Taotie","en/d/taotie.html"],["A disguised carpenter","According to the Skanda Purana, the carpenter who shaped the wood for the Puri images was in fact Vishvakarman in disguise.","Indradyumna","en/d/indradyumna.html"],["A net that caught everyone","In the Prose Edda, the gods discover that Rán catches everyone who goes to sea. That story explains why gold could be called &#x27;fire of Ægir&#x27;.","Rán","en/d/ran.html"],["An earlier model","The tale resembles that of Pelops, who beats King Oenomaus in a chariot race to win Hippodamia, after bribing a servant to sabotage the king&#x27;s chariot.","Pallene","en/d/palene.html"],["An oar over the grave","Elpenor asked that his grave be marked with the oar he had rowed with in life, and Odysseus placed it on the mound as requested.","Elpenor","en/d/elpenor.html"],["Hell, but not quite","Scholars note that the term is usually translated as hell, though it is closer to purgatory, since the punishment does not last forever.","Naraka","en/d/naraka-q3595292.html"],["Flight like a valkyrie","Saxo says Lagertha flew around to the rear of the enemy. According to Judith Jesch, this trait links her to the valkyries.","Lagertha","en/d/lagertha.html"],["Two names","He appears as Eante or Eas in Spanish and as Aeas in Latin. Both forms refer to the same river god.","Eanthes","en/d/eante.html"],["Double of the Cumaean Sibyl","Some authors believe the Cimmerian and Cumaean sibyls were in fact the same figure, while other sources treat them as two distinct prophetesses.","Cimmerian Sibyl","en/d/sibila-cimeria.html"],["A name with meaning","His name is derived from the Proto-Celtic <em>uɸo-</em> (&quot;under, below&quot;) and <em>segos</em> (&quot;force, victory&quot;), although its full meaning remains debated.","Vosegus","en/d/vosego.html"],["A temple in two parts","According to one account, one had to pass through the temple of Virtus before reaching that of Honos, symbolising that honour is earned through military success.","Virtus","en/d/virtus.html"],["Origin of a word","The adjective «stentorian», used for a loud, booming voice, comes directly from this herald&#x27;s name.","Stentor","en/d/estentor.html"],["Laundry left out overnight","According to the <em>Kii zōdan shū</em>, the <em>ubume</em> flies at night and leaves bloodstains on children&#x27;s clothes, so people avoided leaving garments hanging out overnight.","Ubume","en/d/ubume.html"],["Several names","He is also known as Tectaphus, Teutamus, Tectauus and Tectaeus, depending on the ancient versions.","Tectamus","en/d/tectamo.html"],["A minor god","In the story he is a sea deity of secondary rank, hired as a navigator for a mission that is not part of his main story.","Azumi-no-isora","en/d/azumi-no-isora.html"],["A name possibly about offspring","The name may mean &quot;the one striving for sons&quot;, though philologists are not entirely certain of this.","Mögþrasir","en/d/mograsir.html"],["Two sacred forms","Many temples house both the linga, the symbol of Shiva, and the human-like image of Khandoba on horseback.","Khandoba","en/d/khandoba.html"],["The rabbit mark","According to the story, the gods threw a rabbit at Tecciztecatl, and its shape was left on the Moon. This is why some depictions show a rabbit-shaped mark on its surface.","Tecciztecatl","en/d/tecciztecatl.html"],["An atheist Brihaspati","The Barhaspatya sutras, materialist texts that reject the Vedic gods, carry his name. Today they are traditionally attributed to the philosopher Charvaka, although an atheist Brihaspati distinct from the mythical sage may have existed.","Brihaspati","en/d/brihaspati.html"],["Volcanoes as messengers","Tradition explains volcanic eruptions as the moment these dragons burst from the ground to report to heaven.","Fucanglong","en/d/fucanglong.html"],["A name with many forms","They are known as vetal, baital, Betal or Vetoba depending on region and language.","Vetala","en/d/vetala.html"],["A harbour named after him","According to the Odyssey, there is a harbour on Ithaca named after Phorcys, whom the poem also calls the Old Man of the Sea.","The Old Man of the Sea","en/d/el-viejo-del-mar.html"],["Meaning of the name","In Old Norse, Dyggvi means something like &quot;useful&quot; or &quot;effective&quot;.","Dyggvi","en/d/dyggvi.html"],["A hall that shines","Glitnir, Forseti&#x27;s hall, has golden pillars and a silver roof, and its glow could be seen from far away.","Forseti","en/d/forseti.html"],["Four forms in one temple","The four gateway towers of Chidambaram Temple in Tamil Nadu each hold a niche with one of four forms of Shiva, among them Kankalamurti.","Kankalamurti","en/d/kankalamurti.html"],["A pioneering ladder","Vegetius remembers him as the first to use ladders in a siege, at the very moment Zeus struck him down.","Capaneus","en/d/capaneo.html"],["A survivor","He was the only son of Karna to survive the Kurukshetra War.","Vrishaketu","en/d/vrishaketu.html"],["A disputed father","Pausanias presents him as the father of Procne and Philomela, but other authors give these daughters to an earlier Pandion, which has led scholars to suspect a single Pandion was split into two.","Pandion II","en/d/pandion-ii.html"],["A sea vocabulary","The names of Ægir and Rán&#x27;s daughters are poetic terms for waves, so the list works almost as a glossary of the sea.","Dúfa","en/d/dufa.html"],["A moon of Saturn","One of Saturn&#x27;s moons is named Gridr, in honour of this giantess.","Gríðr","en/d/grir.html"],["Citadel of Calydnus","Thebes was sometimes called &#x27;the citadel of Calydnus&#x27;, a reference to the fortifications attributed to him.","Calydnus","en/d/calidno.html"],["An Olympic boxer","Euthymus, the fighter in the story, won at the 74th, 76th and 77th Olympiads, according to tradition.","Licas","en/d/licas.html"],["Wagers with humans","The <em>Ördög</em> makes bets with people to see whether they become corrupted, and its long-term goal is to collect more human souls.","Ördög","en/d/ordog.html"],["Meaning of the name","The name Aglaope translates as &quot;with lambent voice&quot;, a direct reference to the song that characterized her.","Aglaope","en/d/aglaope.html"],["Formulas in visions","Ramanujan said the goddess proposed mathematical formulas to him in dreams and visions, which he then had to verify.","Namagiri","en/d/namagiri.html"],["Always last","In carved groups of Matrikas, Chamunda is always shown last on the right, though she is sometimes considered the leader of the group.","Chamunda","en/d/chamunda.html"],["Medicinal horns","The Muscogee used the horns of this being, called chitto gab-by, in medicine.","Horned Serpent","en/d/serpiente-con-cuernos.html"],["Coins with foxes","Early in the century, followers of Inari in Ginza minted coins as offerings. They bore images of two foxes and a jewel, symbols of long life and good luck.","Inari","en/d/inari.html"],["Sanskrit name","In Sanskrit he is called Krakucchanda, and in Tibetan Khorvadjig.","Kakusandha","en/d/kakusandha.html"],["From television to alligator","Brazil&#x27;s alligator-shaped Cuca came from the 1952 TV show Sítio do Picapau Amarelo. In Lobato&#x27;s novel she was described only as having an alligator face and clawed fingers.","Coco","en/d/coco.html"],["A debate that remains open","Heinrich Schliemann&#x27;s excavations at Hisarlık, in Turkey, place Troy where most scholars now accept the city stood. However, there is no proof that a Greek expedition like the one in the poems ever took place.","Trojan War","en/d/guerra-de-troya.html"],["Names with meaning","Skinfaxi means &#x27;shining mane&#x27; and Hrímfaxi means &#x27;frost mane&#x27;. The names describe the appearance of each horse.","Skinfaxi","en/d/skinfaxi.html"],["An asteroid with her name","Asteroid 385 Ilmatar, in the main belt, was named after the goddess.","Ilmatar","en/d/ilmatar.html"],["A shell for a horn","Ammonite fossils are rare in Egypt, so the association may have begun with fossil snail shells found in Mokattam limestone near Cairo.","Horns of Ammon","en/d/cuernos-de-amon.html"],["An Athens in Euboea","Tradition credits Dias with founding a city called Athens on the island of Euboea, distinct from the great Attic Athens.","Dias","en/d/diante.html"],["Daughters named after snow","Snær&#x27;s three daughters have names describing forms of snow: Fön means snowdrift, Drífa snowfall and Mjöl powdered snow.","Snær","en/d/snr.html"],["A saying of Ramakrishna","The mystic Sri Ramakrishna taught that the goddess carries the world and that, if she stopped, the world would be destroyed.","Jagaddhatri","en/d/jagaddhatri.html"],["A month named after her","The Anglo-Saxons called April <em>eosturmonath</em>, Ēostre&#x27;s month. Bede records this in the 8th century.","Ostara","en/d/ostara.html"],["A name meaning swift current","Her Greek name is usually rendered as &quot;swift current&quot;, a fitting label for an Oceanid, daughter of the great river that encircles the earth.","Ocyrhoe","en/d/ocirroe-q6048797.html"],["Iconography","She is often shown with dark blue or black skin and, in her four-armed form, holding an aruval, a bowl, a trishula or a lotus.","Kateri Amman","en/d/kateri-amman.html"],["A baptism from 1641","A researcher found the baptism record of Juan García Granado, the villager identified as the serpent&#x27;s slayer, dated 1641.","The Serpent of Pedro Bernardo","en/d/la-sierpe-de-pedro-bernardo.html"],["A sleep of ages","On waking, Muchukunda found that all living beings had shrunk while he slept, a sign that long ages had passed.","Muchukunda","en/d/muchukunda.html"],["Fire that goes for a walk","A household fire was blamed on an angry Gabija, whose flames spread until she was satisfied.","Gabija","en/d/gabija.html"],["Descent through the mother","Apollonius of Rhodes suggests that the Boeotian Minyans regarded themselves as descended from the daughters of Minyas, which points to an archaic matrilineal descent.","Alcimede","en/d/alcimede.html"],["2024 film","Paolo Sorrentino, a filmmaker born in Naples, directed a film called Parthenope whose main character shares the siren&#x27;s name.","Parthenope","en/d/partenope.html"],["An asteroid named after him","The trans-Neptunian object (53311) Deucalion, discovered in 1999, was named in his honor.","Deucalion","en/d/deucalion.html"],["Maya Sita","In some versions, Vedavati is not only Sita&#x27;s earlier life but the illusory double that Ravana carries off to Lanka.","Vedavati","en/d/vedavati.html"],["Twin births","Deion&#x27;s descendants were said to have frequently given birth to twins, as happened with his two daughters.","Deion","en/d/deyon.html"],["A queen without a name","The Vishnu Purana and the Harivamsa do not mention Bhadra in the list of eight queens, referring to her only as the daughter of Dhrishtaketu or the princess of Kekeya.","Bhadra","en/d/bhadra-q14589610.html"],["Telling name","Hesiod gives the three Cyclopes names linked to light and thunder: Arges (bright), Brontes (thunder) and Steropes (lightning).","Steropes","en/d/esteropes.html"],["A name with two origins","The word is easily confused with bereg, &#x27;river bank&#x27;, and with obereg, &#x27;protective spirit&#x27;, which explains its two modern meanings.","Berehynia","en/d/berehynia.html"],["A real fern","In 1698 Sir Hans Sloane linked the myth to a Chinese tree fern, Cibotium barometz. The lamb was made by removing the leaves from a section of its woolly rhizome, which when inverted resembled a lamb.","Vegetable Lamb of Tartary","en/d/cordero-vegetal-de-tartaria.html"],["The flying plum tree","Legend says a plum tree from the capital flew to Dazaifu to join the exiled poet. The tree is still on show at Dazaifu Tenman-gū.","Tenjin","en/d/tenjin.html"],["A genus of trees","Linnaeus took the name Thyia for the genus <em>Thuja</em>, which includes the white cedars.","Thyia","en/d/tuya.html"],["A composite name","Porphyry calls Hermanubis a &quot;composite&quot; and &quot;half-Greek&quot; name, a way of combining two gods that was uncommon in traditional Greek religion.","Hermanubis","en/d/hermanubis.html"],["Destroyed eyes","A later legend says that Virabhadra, created by Shiva, blinded him, an episode that does not appear in the oldest hymns.","Bhaga","en/d/bhaga.html"],["Twins fallen at Troy","Diocles&#x27; twin sons, Crethon and Orsilochus, died during the Trojan War at the hands of Aeneas.","Diocles of Pherae","en/d/diocles-de-feras.html"],["A Buddhist ending","In Buddhist texts, svaha appears in dharanis inserted into Mahayana sutras such as the Lotus Sutra and became a common ending for mantras.","Svaha","en/d/svaha.html"],["A plaque in the Louvre","The Neo-Assyrian &quot;Plaque des Enfers&quot; in the Louvre shows Lamashtu on a donkey in a boat, with a dog and a pig suckling at her breasts, while Pazuzu appears as an opposing protective force.","Lamashtu","en/d/lamashtu.html"],["An ancestor god","In Osraige tradition, Lóegaire Bern Búadach was regarded as an ancestor god, showing that the figure also had a religious role beyond the literary one.","Lóegaire Búadach","en/d/loegaire-buadach.html"],["A name that reached history","Imhotep is the earliest scientist whose name has survived to the present day, according to sources on his historical figure.","Imhotep","en/d/imhotep.html"],["A quest for immortality","According to the old accounts, Tajimamori was sent to Tokoyo by Emperor Suinin in search of a fruit or remedy that would grant eternal life.","Tokoyo no Kuni","en/d/tokoyo-no-kuni.html"],["A mistaken attack","The Coans took Heracles and his companions for pirates and pelted them with stones as they tried to land. The confusion ended in battle.","Eurypylus","en/d/euripilo.html"],["A meaningful name","Eurydice roughly means « wide justice », from the Greek words for « wide » and « justice ». Some sources also call her Henioche.","Eurydice of Thebes","en/d/euridice-de-tebas.html"],["A smile with a name","The Chinese name Niān huā wéi xiào literally means &#x27;picking up a flower and smiling&#x27;.","Flower Sermon","en/d/sermon-de-las-flores.html"],["A hieroglyph with a double use","The sign for Sia was used to write verbs such as to perceive or to know, so the god was tied to the writing of the very idea of knowledge.","Sia","en/d/sia.html"],["Tomb in Athens","According to Pausanias, the tombs of both Antiope and Molpadia were shown in Athens.","Antiope","en/d/antiope.html"],["An inspector of canals","Jeremy Black links Enbilulu to the management of irrigation, a vital matter in Mesopotamian agriculture.","Enlil and Ninlil","en/d/enlil-y-ninlil.html"],["Nymphs of Dodona","In Dodona, the Hyades were regarded as nymphs, which shows that their figure had a link with that sanctuary.","Ambrosia","en/d/ambrosia.html"],["A name meaning memory","His name is linked to a Proto-Indo-European root meaning to think or remember, the same family as the English word memory.","Mímir","en/d/mimir.html"],["A god who could be reinterpreted as female","In god lists from the first millennium BCE, Shara could be reinterpreted as a female deity.","Shara","en/d/shara.html"],["Coins of Troezen","Ancient coins of Troezen showed a trident and the head of Athena, symbols of the two gods who shared the protection of the city.","Althepus","en/d/althepus.html"],["Three colours","In the Prose Edda, Hár explains that the bridge has three colours and that its red is burning fire, which keeps the giants from climbing up to heaven.","Bifröst","en/d/bifrost.html"],["Statue on the seafront","A statue of Pania stands on Marine Parade in Napier, and the city has adopted her as an emblem.","Pania","en/d/pania.html"],["Asteria, the island that fled","Asteria threw herself into the sea to escape Zeus, and the myth says that her leap gave birth to the island of Delos.","Nesoi","en/d/nesoi.html"],["A name with several meanings","Crom can mean &#x27;bent&#x27; and cenn &#x27;head&#x27;. Cruach refers to a pile or heap, of grain or hay, so the name has been read as &#x27;head of the heap&#x27; or &#x27;bloody head&#x27;.","Crom Cruach","en/d/crom-cruach.html"],["Ninus the centaur","Some traditions credit him with training the first hunting dogs and taming horses, and for this he is sometimes shown as a centaur.","Ninus","en/d/nino.html"],["A river with several names","According to Tzetzes, the river was successively called Oceanus, Aegyptus, Nilus and Triton.","Nilus","en/d/nilo.html"],["A relief in India","A cave temple at Nashik, India, from the 2nd or 3rd century AD, shows two women, one lighting the circle of the Sun and the other waiting for it at sunset, a scene very similar to the Slavic miniature.","Zorya","en/d/zorya.html"],["Firearms","Indigenous peoples compared celestial fire with the Spanish arquebuses and muskets, and the term illapa came to designate these weapons.","Illapa","en/d/illapa.html"],["Lord of the nose","His name combines the Nahuatl words <em>yacatl</em>, nose, and <em>tecuhtli</em>, lord. The nose, which always goes in front of the body, symbolised the guidance of the traveler.","Yacatecuhtli","en/d/yacatecuhtli.html"],["Another name","In some manuscripts his name is spelled Thymoetes.","Dimoetes","en/d/dimetes.html"],["A star as a sign","In the late account of his wedding, a star falling on Eurythemista&#x27;s shoulder decides his choice. The mountain where it happens is named Asterion, meaning star.","Boeotus","en/d/beoto.html"],["Seven-headed horse","In the Devi Bhagavata Purana, Revanta rides Uchaishravas, a horse with seven heads.","Revanta","en/d/revanta.html"],["From the name to ‘hedonism’","The Greek word for pleasure is the root of ‘hedonism’ and ‘hedonist’, terms that today describe the pursuit of pleasure as a way of life.","Hedone","en/d/hedone.html"],["Fifty-one sites","According to the story, Sati&#x27;s body parts fell in 51 different places. Today they are called Shakti pithas and are considered sacred by Hindus.","Sati","en/d/sati.html"],["A variable mother","Ancient sources do not agree on Oeax&#x27;s mother, who appears as Clymene, Hesione or Philyra.","Oeax","en/d/ax.html"],["A cup named after him","A gold cup from Mycenae, now in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens, bears his name because it resembles the one described in the Iliad.","Nestor","en/d/nestor.html"],["Dragon bones","Ancient Chinese called fossil bones &quot;dragon bones&quot;. Modern analysis suggests they are most often remains of Cenozoic mammals, such as the extinct horse <em>Hipparion</em>. They are still used in traditional Chinese medicine.","Chinese dragon","en/d/dragon-chino.html"],["A rare mention","Alfheim appears only twice in the Old Norse texts, making it one of the least described worlds in the cosmology.","Alfheim","en/d/alfheim.html"],["A name with history","Miach is often translated as bushel, an agreed amount of grain. Irish law texts also use the word for a quantity of malted grain.","Miach","en/d/miach.html"],["The maqet ladder","The Egyptians used a symbol called maqet, a ladder, which according to tradition allowed Osiris to enter Nut&#x27;s heavenly sky. It was placed in tombs to protect the deceased.","Nut","en/d/nut.html"],["Pan and Pushan","The link between Pan and the Vedic god Pushan, both associated with goats, was first proposed in 1924 by the German scholar Hermann Collitz.","Pan","en/d/pan.html"],["A mine with her name","The Gumyoshevsky mine, the oldest in the Ural Mountains, was called &#x27;The Copper Mountain&#x27; or simply &#x27;The Mountain&#x27; by locals. It is now located in the town of Polevskoy, in Sverdlovsk Oblast.","The Mistress of the Copper Mountain","en/d/la-senora-de-la-montana-de-cobre.html"],["Omens at Minturnae","Livy reports that several buildings in the city, including Marica&#x27;s sacred grove, were struck by lightning, and that the inhabitants added an alleged trickle of blood at a gate to make the horror greater.","Marica","en/d/marica.html"],["A divine couple","At Grinario, Visucius appears beside the goddess Visucia, with whom he forms a couple. This kind of pairing, with a god bearing a Roman name and a goddess keeping a native one, was common in Gaul and the German provinces.","Visucius","en/d/visucius.html"],["A name with many forms","She is also known as Mamapacha or Mama Pacha, and her Quechua name combines pacha, meaning world, time and cosmos, with mama, mother.","Pachamama","en/d/pachamama.html"],["A name that changes","According to the myth, she received the name Ninlil after marrying Enlil; before that she was called Sud.","Ninlil","en/d/ninlil.html"],["A name with unknown meaning","The meaning of Aernus is still unknown. Scholars have compared it with the Proto-Indo-European roots <em>aper-</em>, &#x27;behind&#x27;, and <em>āpero-</em>, &#x27;bank&#x27;.","Aernus","en/d/aernus.html"],["Nurse of the stars","Claudian wrote that Tethys nursed Helios and Selene, the children of her siblings Hyperion and Theia, when their light was still weak.","Tethys","en/d/tetis.html"],["Twelve Adityas","The oldest legends mention seven or eight Adityas, but the number was later increased to twelve.","Amsha","en/d/amsha.html"],["A stone with his name","In Cornwall a 6th-century stone bears the inscription Drustanus, a Latin form of Drust, which is linked to the name Tristan.","Tristan","en/d/tristan.html"],["A jar, not a box","The Greek word pithos meant a large storage jar for wine, oil or grain. Erasmus of Rotterdam changed it to pyxis, meaning box, in his Latin version of the story.","Pandora's box","en/d/caja-de-pandora.html"],["A name with a double meaning","In Latin <em>lympha</em> can mean &quot;spring&quot; or simply &quot;water,&quot; and the Augustan poets played on its resemblance to <em>nympha</em>, &quot;nymph.&quot;","Lympha","en/d/linfa.html"],["An ancient insult","Hurling a bull&#x27;s leg at someone was an insult spread across the ancient Near East, and it reappears in the Odyssey.","Bull of Heaven","en/d/toro-celestial.html"],["Heads in Lerna","The Danaids buried the heads of their husbands in the city of Lerna, a place that appears in the story as the scene of the crime.","Pirene","en/d/pirene-q15631110.html"],["A palace as payment","According to the Kojiki, Ōkuninushi asked as a condition that a magnificent palace be built for him on Tagishi beach in Izumo.","Kuni-yuzuri","en/d/kuni-yuzuri.html"],["A citadel with a sanctuary","According to Pausanias, the citadel of Aegialea stood where the sanctuary of Athena at Sicyon was later built.","Aegialeus","en/d/egialeo-q1422648.html"],["Seven horses","The seven horses of Surya&#x27;s chariot are linked to the seven colours of the rainbow and the seven days of the week.","Surya","en/d/suria.html"],["A later life","According to the Bengali <em>Manasamangala Kavya</em>, Usha and Aniruddha are reborn as Behula and Lakshmindara and marry again.","Usha","en/d/usha.html"],["A name that passes to a hill","It has been proposed that the name of the Widdebierg derives from Veraudunus, making the god a possible echo in the local landscape.","Veraudunus","en/d/veraudunus.html"],["Mother of Eros","In Plato&#x27;s account, Eros is the son of Penia and Poros, inheriting his mother&#x27;s lack and his father&#x27;s resourcefulness.","Penia","en/d/penia.html"],["A son of Ares","The Iliad describes him as a son of Ares, which can be understood figuratively as a warrior.","Licymnius","en/d/licimnio.html"],["Zeus&#x27;s forge","The mountain was believed to be where Hephaestus and the Cyclopes made the thunderbolts of Zeus.","Aetna","en/d/etna.html"],["A dove as a test","The Argonauts did not risk their crew: they used a dove to time the rocks before attempting the passage, and the bird survived, losing only its tail.","Symplegades","en/d/simplegades.html"],["Christian chronology","Saint Jerome placed the Silver Age between 1674 and 1628 BC, in a chronology that tried to connect the myths with history.","Silver Age","en/d/edad-de-plata.html"],["Ten forms","The hymn lists ten appearances of the god, from an impetuous wind to a wild goat, which helps explain his shifting character.","Verethragna","en/d/veretragna.html"],["A treacherous herald","In one version, the herald whose task was to bring the bride handed Cydippe over to Cercaphus instead of Ocridion. From then on, no herald dared enter Ocridion&#x27;s small shrine.","Cercaphus","en/d/cercafo.html"],["A sacrifice in disguise","According to the story, Codrus approached the enemy dressed as a peasant so he would not be recognized as king.","Codrus","en/d/codro.html"],["A squadron with her name","A Turkish Air Force tanker squadron flying Boeing KC-135 Stratotankers is nicknamed Asena.","Asena","en/d/asena.html"],["Holy Mountain","In modern Greek the mountain is known as Agion Oros, meaning &#x27;Holy Mountain&#x27;.","Athos","en/d/atos.html"],["Eleven kinds of thunderbolt","The Etruscans distinguished eleven kinds of thunderbolt, and Tinia wielded three of them, according to sources on their religion.","Tinia","en/d/tinia.html"],["Swift feet","Her son Iphiclus was so fast that, according to the account, he ran over ears of grain without bending them.","Clymene","en/d/climene.html"],["Two fathers","Apollodorus makes her a daughter of Pheres in one passage and of Abas in another, without explaining the change.","Idomene","en/d/idomene.html"],["Clothes back to front","When he wears clothing, it is put on inside out and without a belt, and some versions say his shoes are worn on the wrong feet.","Lisovik","en/d/lisovik.html"],["A warrior with a man&#x27;s soul","Saxo Grammaticus describes Heiðr (Hetha) as having a woman&#x27;s body but a man&#x27;s soul. In his account, she commanded the right flank of Harald Wartooth&#x27;s army.","Heiðr","en/d/heir.html"],["Kushan coins","Kushan kings minted coins showing an armed, radiant deity identified by the Bactrian inscription ΦΑΡΡΟ (Pharro).","Khvarenah","en/d/khvarenah.html"],["A name meaning mist","Niflheim means &quot;home of mist&quot;. The word <em>nifl</em> survives in Icelandic with the sense of mist.","Niflheim","en/d/niflheim.html"],["Three legs","A folk tradition on the Isle of Man says the god moved like a wheel turning on his three legs.","Manannán mac Lir","en/d/manannan-mac-lir.html"],["Winged fairies","The only source for winged Aziza was recorded after contact with Europeans during the Atlantic slave trade. Their resemblance to European fairies raises the question of which tradition influenced the other.","Aziza","en/d/aziza.html"],["An Antarctic lake","Mneme Lake, in Antarctica, is named after the Muse.","Mneme","en/d/mneme.html"],["Possible origin of the name","The name may derive from <em>fúaimm</em>, &quot;noise, sound&quot;. A <em>dindsenchas</em> poem on Nás mentions the lamentations of the fair-skinned, vocal women of Fáil, using a related adjective.","Fuamnach","en/d/fuamnach.html"],["A name meaning no return","A late explanatory list interprets his name as &quot;lord of the netherworld, lord of no return.&quot; Some scholars link him with Ennugigi, although they probably were not the same deity.","Ennugi","en/d/ennugi.html"],["A meaningful name","Her name comes from the Akkadian verb šemû, &quot;to hear,&quot; which fits her role of listening to prayers.","Tashmetum","en/d/tasmetu.html"],["The ruins of his house","In Pausanias&#x27; time the foundations of his house on Mount Pontinus, near Lerna, were still shown to visitors.","Hippomedon","en/d/hipomedon.html"],["Possible identification","The bird Acanthus became has been identified as the siskin, the linnet or the blue tit, though with little certainty.","Acanthus","en/d/acanto.html"],["A Sicilian authoress","Samuel Butler argued that the Odyssey was written by a young Sicilian woman who presents herself in the poem as Nausicaa. He proposed that the coast of Sicily, especially the Trapani area, is reflected in the poem&#x27;s settings.","Homeric Ithaca","en/d/itaca-homerica.html"],["A ritual battle","The Ballêtus was a mock battle held at Eleusis in memory of Demophon.","Demophon","en/d/demofonte-q1475776.html"],["A god with a goat-fish form","Among his primary symbols was the goat-fish, a hybrid figure that identified him in Mesopotamian art and religion.","Enki (Ea)","en/d/enki-ea.html"],["An asteroid with his name","A potentially hazardous asteroid discovered in 2004 was named after this chaos serpent.","Apophis","en/d/apofis.html"],["A spring named after her","A spring near Marathon was named Macaria after her sacrifice, according to tradition.","Macaria","en/d/macaria.html"],["A warning plaque","At the entrance, a stone slab warns that visiting the spring while it is empty brings bad luck, and some superstitious visitors still take it seriously.","Fontes Tamarici","en/d/fuentes-tamaricas.html"],["Hares and rabbits","Both are called 兔 in Chinese, although the zodiac was devised when the hare was the only leporid known in China.","Rabbit (Chinese zodiac)","en/d/conejo-zodiaco-chino.html"],["A hook in the sky","According to Polynesian cosmogony, the gods placed Maui&#x27;s magic hook in the sky, in the constellation Scorpius, so that humans would remember his deeds.","Māui","en/d/maui.html"],["An island with her name","Oenone was also the ancient name of an island, which later took the name Aegina, daughter of the river god Asopus.","Oenone","en/d/enone.html"],["A Black Madonna","Erzulie Dantor is linked to the Black Madonna of Częstochowa, whose copies may have reached Haiti with Polish soldiers who fought in the Haitian Revolution from 1802.","Erzulie","en/d/erzulie.html"],["Confusion with the Salmon of Knowledge","Because he can turn into a salmon and bears the title &#x27;the Wise&#x27;, he is often confused with the Salmon of Knowledge, a similarly named animal figure linked to Fionn mac Cumhail.","Fintan mac Bóchra","en/d/fintan-mac-bochra.html"],["Song before battle","Tacitus says the Germans sang of Hercules before any other hero when they went into battle.","Hercules' Club","en/d/maza-de-hercules.html"],["Another name, another woman","According to the English reference, Lysithea is also another name for Semele, daughter of King Cadmus of Thebes and mother of Dionysus.","Lysithoe","en/d/lisitoe.html"],["A chained swan","In the Dolopathos it is hinted that the swan is the one that pulled an armed knight in a boat by a golden chain.","Knight of the Swan","en/d/caballero-del-cisne.html"],["Origin of philanthropy","The word philanthropy comes from <em>philánthrōpon</em>, the epithet with which Aeschylus describes Prometheus as the friend of humankind.","Prometheus","en/d/prometeo.html"],["Several Oechalias","Ancient writers placed this city in Euboea, Thessaly, Arcadia or Messenia. Strabo recorded all these options without settling the question.","Oechalia","en/d/ecalia.html"],["Meaningful names","Móði and Magni mean «Wrath» and «Strength», and their sister Þrúðr means «Power», names that reflect different facets of Thor.","Móði","en/d/moi.html"],["A rock with a tunnel","Bull Rock, the islet identified with Tech Duinn, has a natural tunnel the sea flows through, resembling a dolmen.","Donn","en/d/donn.html"],["A name with ancient roots","Her name appears to derive from the Proto-Celtic *Hek-to-landā, meaning roughly «cattle-adorned pasture».","Achtland","en/d/achtland.html"],["Three eyes","Some Puranas describe Narasimha with three eyes, like Shiva, and destroying with fire that comes from the third.","Narasimha","en/d/narasinja.html"],["Symbolic faces","Each king is identified by face color: white for the first, red for the second and black for the third. These colors are linked to virtues and to Five Elements symbolism.","Lords of the Three Mountains","en/d/los-senores-de-las-tres-montanas.html"],["Names with a moral","The guards&#x27; names carry a moral meaning: Xie Bi&#x27;an can be read as «those who make amends will always be at peace», and Fan Wujiu as «those who commit crimes will have no salvation».","Heibai Wuchang","en/d/heibai-wuchang.html"],["Name and birth","The name Uaithne probably means &quot;childbirth&quot;. In the Irish glossaries it is glossed as &quot;Orpheus&quot;.","Uaithne","en/d/uaithne.html"],["A kingdom named after a king","According to Herodotus, the land formerly called Maeonia took the name Lydia after the reign of Lydus.","Lydus","en/d/lido.html"],["A name split in two","According to Anatoly Liberman&#x27;s hypothesis, the names of Eikþyrnir and Heiðrún may come from a single term for the lowest heaven, divided into two halves.","Eikþyrnir","en/d/eikyrnir.html"],["The seven ceremonies","Besides the pipe, she is credited with seven sacred ceremonies, including the purification lodge and the crying-for-vision rite.","White Buffalo Calf Woman","en/d/mujer-bufalo-blanco.html"],["Mermaid","In art she is usually shown with a woman&#x27;s head and torso and a marine mammal&#x27;s tail, similar to a mermaid, although the story itself does not mention this.","Sedna","en/d/sedna.html"],["The baby&#x27;s name","On the <em>dies lustricus</em> the child&#x27;s name was chosen, a rite tied to the life that Nona was said to measure.","Nona","en/d/nona.html"],["A tortoise that gave rise to an insult","From the belief that tortoises mated with snakes comes the use of &#x27;tortoise&#x27; as an insult for a cuckolded husband, attested from the late Yuan dynasty.","Xuanwu","en/d/xuanwu.html"],["A famous painting","The painter Aristophon portrayed Nemea with Alcibiades seated on her arms, according to Plutarch, and the work was greatly admired.","Nemea","en/d/nemea.html"],["Coconut tree from the grave","On Mangaia, the white flesh of the coconut is called &#x27;Tuna&#x27;s brains&#x27;.","Hina","en/d/hina.html"],["A birth with a flower","Flora tested a magic flower on a heifer, which became pregnant at once. She then touched Juno&#x27;s belly with a flower, and Juno conceived Mars.","Mars","en/d/marte.html"],["A double-edged word","The term θράσος could mean both courage and insolence. Depending on the context, the same trait could be seen as a virtue or as an excess.","Thrasos","en/d/traso.html"],["A sword in an Italian abbey","In the Abbey of San Galgano, near Siena, a sword is fixed in the ground and no one has ever pulled it out. It is protected by a glass case, and only its hilt sticks out, close to the tomb of the saint.","Excalibur","en/d/excalibur.html"],["Meaning of the name","Hati comes from Old Norse and means &quot;he who hates&quot; or &quot;enemy&quot;.","Hati Hróðvitnisson","en/d/hati-hrovitnisson.html"],["Stone replicas","Rhodes commissioned stone replicas three times the size of the original to decorate the gates of his house in England.","Zimbabwe Bird","en/d/ave-de-zimbabue.html"],["A name with meaning","The name Iði is usually translated as &#x27;the active one&#x27; or &#x27;the hard-working one&#x27;. It is related to modern Scandinavian words such as Norwegian idig and Swedish idog, meaning &#x27;hard-working&#x27;.","Iði","en/d/ii.html"],["An ant hill as the goddess","In many Goan temples, the ant hill inside the sanctum is considered the goddess herself rather than a mere representation of her.","Shantadurga","en/d/shantadurga.html"],["A winning bull","In a bull contest, Paris pitted his champion against all rivals. The bull that finally beat it was Ares in disguise, and Paris handed over the golden crown without protest.","Paris","en/d/paris.html"],["A name with meaning","Pelops derives from the Greek <em>pelios</em>, &#x27;dark&#x27;, and <em>ops</em>, &#x27;face or eye&#x27;, so his name can be rendered as &#x27;dark eyes&#x27; or &#x27;dark face&#x27;.","Pelops (son of Agamemnon)","en/d/pelope-hijo-de-agamenon.html"],["The golden apples","The apples that decided the race were a gift from Aphrodite to Hippomenes. Atalanta could not resist stopping to pick them up.","Merope of Megara","en/d/merope-de-megara.html"],["A small divine court","Pater lists him alongside Vaticanus, who makes the baby cry at birth, and Cuba, who keeps the infant calm in its cot.","Fabulinus","en/d/fabulino.html"],["Dragon bones","In China, fossils of large prehistoric animals were identified as dragon bones and are still used in traditional medicine.","Dragon","en/d/dragon.html"],["Epithet of Artemis","According to Hesychius, Angelos was also an epithet of Artemis in Syracuse, and Artemis, as a moon goddess, was identified with Hecate.","Angelos","en/d/angelos.html"],["An inherited word","Her name lies behind the Spanish word fraude, which comes from Latin.","Fraus","en/d/fraus.html"],["A giant in Wolfe","In Gene Wolfe&#x27;s saga, the name Baldanders belongs to an enormous, intellectually gifted giant whose physical growth never stops.","Baldanders","en/d/baldanders.html"],["Twelve spirits","Later Russian legends describe twelve Likhoradkas, each with an individual name associated with a particular illness.","Likhoradka","en/d/likhoradka.html"],["A name with meaning","Banasura means &#x27;the demon Arrow&#x27;, combining bāṇa (arrow) and ásura (demon, enemy of the devas).","Banasura","en/d/banasura.html"],["A rank that grew with eruptions","According to tradition, each eruption of Mount Chōkai raised Omonoimi&#x27;s rank.","Omonoimi no Kami","en/d/omonoimi-no-kami.html"],["Salvadoran origin","In El Salvador, one story identifies her with Sihuehuet, a peasant woman who poisoned her husband, the prince Yeisun, and was cursed by the god Teotl to wander forever as the Sihuanaba.","Siguanaba","en/d/siguanaba.html"],["A giant in Tegea","Herodotus reports that the remains found in Tegea measured seven cubits, which would make Orestes a giant.","Orestes","en/d/orestes.html"],["Two accounts of Adonis&#x27;s birth","The sources record different versions: in one, Aphrodite urges Myrrha into incest; in another, Myrrha flees and Adonis is born from the tree when an arrow or a boar is involved.","Theias","en/d/tias.html"],["A people with a related name","The Nemetes, a Germano-Celtic people of the Middle Rhine, bore a name related to the goddess&#x27;s, and they are believed to have been linked to her.","Nemetona","en/d/nemetona.html"],["Mountains named after them","Two mountains in the Himalayas near Badrinath are named Nara and Narayana.","Nara and Narayana","en/d/nara-y-naraian.html"],["A name with two parts","The first element of her name means goddess, and the second is related to day. This is why she is understood as the goddess of daylight or the bright one.","Dea Dia","en/d/dea-dia.html"],["Kings on stones","Choosing a king by swearing over stones also appears in other Scandinavian sources, such as the <em>Chronicon Lethrense</em> and Olaus Magnus&#x27;s Swedish history.","Humblus","en/d/humblus.html"],["A tortoise beneath the altar","The Taittiriya Samhita prescribes burying a live tortoise beneath the fire altar. According to the text, the sacrificer gained merit for reaching heaven.","Kurma","en/d/kurma.html"],["Other names","Ereshkigal also appears as Irkalla and sometimes as Ninkigal, &#x27;Lady of the Great Earth&#x27;. Something similar happened with the Greek name Hades, which referred both to the place and to its ruler.","Ereshkigal","en/d/ereshkigal.html"],["An asteroid named after him","The asteroid 2893 Peiroos, a Jovian asteroid, is named after this Thracian war leader.","Peirous","en/d/peirous.html"],["A whale that turns into a reindeer","Nelson also described a white whale able to transform into a reindeer, another composite being from the same tradition.","Akhlut","en/d/akhlut.html"],["A horn for drinking","According to Snorri, Heimdallr uses the Gjallarhorn to drink from Mímir&#x27;s well, which makes him wise. The horn thus serves both as a signalling instrument and as a drinking vessel.","Gjallarhorn","en/d/gjallarhorn.html"],["A name with several explanations","Some etymologists link his name to the Greek verb <em>pérthein</em>, &#x27;to sack&#x27;, which would make him &#x27;the sacker of cities&#x27;.","Perseus","en/d/perseo.html"],["Statue at CERN","In 2004 a two-metre statue of the dancing Shiva was unveiled at CERN, the European centre for particle physics research in Geneva.","Nataraja","en/d/nataraja.html"],["Relatives in other mythologies","Her name comes from the same Indo-European root as Eos in Greece and Aurora in Rome.","Ushas","en/d/ushas.html"],["Kinship with Krishna","According to the Bhagavata Purana, Adhiratha descended from Yayati, so he was related to Krishna.","Adhiratha","en/d/adhiratha.html"],["A thousand eyes","According to the Mahabharata, Indra grew a thousand eyes across his body when he saw her, a striking image of his desire.","Tilottama","en/d/tilottama.html"],["An uncertain name","The meaning of her name is obscure, and the explanations proposed since the eighteenth century are not secure.","Pasithea","en/d/pasitea.html"],["A moon of Saturn","A moon of Saturn bears his name. Its jets of ice and water vapour make it one of the strongest candidates in the Solar System to harbour microscopic life.","Enceladus","en/d/encelado.html"],["A weighty offering","In the <em>tulabharam</em>, the scales do not move under all the wives&#x27; gold. Only a small tulsi leaf, offered with devotion, balances them.","Satyabhama","en/d/satia-bhama.html"],["A helmet for Hades","Apollodorus credits Arges and his brothers with making the Helmet of Invisibility for Hades, as well as Poseidon&#x27;s trident.","Arges","en/d/arges.html"],["Three names","The name appears in variants such as Sahrimnir in Old English, Særimner in Danish and Norwegian, and Särimner in Swedish.","Sæhrímnir","en/d/shrimnir.html"],["The necklace of Harmonia","Algos and some of her brothers took part in forging the necklace Hephaestus made for Harmonia, an object Greek tales link with misfortune.","Algos","en/d/algos.html"],["The river that boils","The River Barrow is named after the ashes of the serpents Dian Cecht burned. According to the story, the water boiled and killed every living creature in it.","Dian Cecht","en/d/dian-cecht.html"],["A name meaning ewe","His name was written with the logogram dU8, meaning &quot;ewe&quot;, and derives from the Akkadian <em>laḫru</em>, which has the same meaning.","Lahar","en/d/lahar.html"],["A namesake Polyctor on Ithaca","Another Polyctor, son of King Pterelaus of the Teleboans, gave his name to a place on Ithaca called Polyctorium. His story is separate from that of the sons of Aegyptus.","Polyctor","en/d/polictor.html"],["A skull on the hand","According to the myth, Brahma&#x27;s head stuck to Shiva&#x27;s hand, and he used it as a begging bowl until Varanasi.","Bhikshatana","en/d/bhikshatana.html"],["Protective peaches","Izanagi gave the peaches he used to drive off the Furies the name Augusto Ōkamuzumi, or great divine fruit. In ancient China, peaches were also used to repel evil spirits.","Yomi","en/d/yomi.html"],["Two Thoases","Some authors identify this king with another Thoas, king of Lemnos, son of Dionysus and Ariadne and father of Hypsipyle.","Thoas","en/d/toante-q1246185.html"],["A double face","As a divine midwife, Artemis helps in childbirth when she is well disposed, but if she is offended she can bring about the death of the mother.","Artemis","en/d/artemisa.html"],["A Greek proverb","Greeks said &quot;Tenedian human&quot; of people with a frightening look. The saying refers to a law of Tenes that placed a man with an axe behind the judge to strike the convicted man after his plea.","Tenes","en/d/tenes.html"],["A name with several readings","The name is read as &#x27;fate of the gods&#x27; or &#x27;twilight of the gods&#x27;, depending on how its second element is understood, a term philologists still debate.","Ragnarök","en/d/ragnarok.html"],["Open arms","Her pose with outstretched arms may have inspired her epithet “the Embracer” and recalled the shape of the Nile with its two tributaries.","Anuket","en/d/anuket.html"],["Goddess of the foam","Her name means &#x27;white goddess&#x27;, and it relates to the colour of sea foam.","Leucothea","en/d/leucotea.html"],["A very rich burial","His presumed tomb at Gamla Uppsala, in the Adils mound, held a man wrapped in a bearskin, with two dogs and rich grave goods, including a Frankish sword and Roman ivory gaming pieces.","Adils","en/d/adils.html"],["A grove made into a garden","According to Plutarch, Cimon turned a dry, barren spot into a well-watered grove with running tracks and shaded walks.","Academus","en/d/academo.html"],["A royal name","The god&#x27;s name appears in the titulary of the pharaoh Khufu in the form &quot;Khnum protects me&quot;, showing his importance in the age of the pyramids.","Khnum","en/d/jnum.html"],["Origin of the word cancer","The Greek term karkinos, meaning &#x27;crab&#x27;, was used by Hippocrates to describe certain tumors. Celsus translated it into Latin as cancer, which is where the modern word comes from.","Carcinus","en/d/carcinos.html"],["A grasping hand","Her afflictions were sometimes called the &#x27;hand of Aḫḫazu&#x27;, a way of describing being held by an invisible force.","Ahhazu","en/d/ahhazu.html"],["A wartime nickname","A group of B-29 bombers in the Second World War was nicknamed the Kagutsuchi Group and took part in the bombings of Tokyo.","Kagutsuchi","en/d/kagutsuchi.html"],["Another name","Diodorus Siculus calls her Eurydice, showing that ancient authors gave her different names.","Lysidice","en/d/lisidice.html"],["Festival in Holdia","In the village of Holdia in Joynagar, the Mandal family has organized a three-day fair in her honour for about 250 years.","Bonbibi","en/d/bonbibi.html"],["Twelve eyes","The goddess is born with twelve eyes, a feature that sets her apart from other deities in the story.","Bhutamata","en/d/bhutamata.html"],["Two births","Accounts of her origin do not agree: in one she comes from Shiva&#x27;s halo, in another from a spark on Parvati&#x27;s forehead.","Jyoti","en/d/jyoti.html"],["A name with meaning","The name Eunice comes from <em>Euníkē</em>, formed from <em>êu</em>, meaning &quot;positive&quot; or &quot;happy&quot;, and <em>níké</em>, meaning &quot;victory&quot;.","Eunice","en/d/eunice.html"],["A shared ending","In the legend, Hagbard and Signy die together, a tragic ending that the tradition passed on with force.","Hagbard","en/d/hagbard.html"],["Popular modern tale","One of the most favorable stories about the gumiho, known as &quot;The Gumiho&#x27;s 100 day wait&quot;, took its modern form in the 1800s.","Gumiho","en/d/gumiho.html"],["Milk from white cows","According to the Denkard, Zoroaster&#x27;s material self passed to his mother when she drank the milk of six white cows that had eaten grass nourished by the rain.","Ameretat","en/d/ameretat.html"],["Honey on the lips","Before he returns to Heaven, the paper image of Zao Jun is sometimes smeared with honey on its lips, to sweeten his report or keep his mouth shut.","Zao Jun","en/d/zao-jun.html"],["A dolphin in the sky","According to the story, the dolphin that searched for Salacia was rewarded with a place among the stars, where it forms the constellation Delphinus.","Salacia","en/d/salacia.html"],["A digging mouse","In the myth <em>Ezina and her Seven Children</em>, the youngest daughter is called the «digging mouse». Some scholars think the story may explain the origin of mice, which eat grain.","Ashnan","en/d/ashnan.html"],["An Antarctic beach","A beach in Antarctica bears the name of this mythical king.","Oeagrus","en/d/eagro.html"],["Pilgrimage in Pakistan","The three-day annual festival at the Rama Pir Temple in Tando Allahyar, Sindh, held in the Hindu month of Bhadrapada, is the second-largest Hindu pilgrimage in Pakistan.","Ramdev","en/d/ramdev.html"],["The festival of Holi","The Hindu festival of Holi is celebrated in memory of Holika, who died in the fire while Prahlada emerged unharmed.","Prahlada","en/d/prahlada.html"],["Star-like eyes","When drawn, he had black, curly hair and star-like eyes, because he dwelt in complete darkness.","Mictlantecuhtli","en/d/mictlantecuhtli.html"],["Children with city names","Among the children attributed to Macareus are Mitylene, Methymna, Antissa and Eresus, all of them names of cities on the island of Lesbos.","Macareus","en/d/macar.html"],["A borrowed name","The name Utu was used to write the names of many foreign solar deities logographically. The link with the Hurrian sun god Shimige is especially well attested.","Shamash (Utu)","en/d/samas-shamash.html"],["Many names","The Sumerians used many names for the underworld, including Arali, Kukku, Kur, Kigal and Ganzir, which later passed into Akkadian.","Irkalla","en/d/irkalla.html"],["The Tiger Dance","In some versions of the dance, the character starts drinking alcohol and keeps dancing until he is too drunk to continue.","Tsagaan Ubgen","en/d/tsagaan-ubgen.html"],["A humorous name","His name is usually interpreted as something like &quot;shabby young man&quot;, alluding to his scarecrow appearance.","Kuebiko","en/d/kuebiko.html"],["A disputed name","Scholars disagree on the meaning of his name. Some readings take it as “excellent” or as “many ploughs.”","Ajisukitakahikone","en/d/ajisukitakahikone.html"],["A whisker that gave the game away","According to the story, the small whisker sticking out from the gold forced Loki to hand over the ring Andvaranaut, which he had not planned for.","Otr","en/d/odder.html"],["One bird, many guesses","The species of the <em>u5</em> bird linked to Nanshe remains unresolved, with proposals ranging from swan to pelican.","Nanshe","en/d/nanshe.html"],["Paper pinwheels","Pinwheels are kept beside his effigy in his temples. Devotees believe that turning one in a particular direction brings good luck.","Che Kung","en/d/che-kung.html"],["Giants throwing rocks","In a tale from Vesilahti, giants threw rocks into the sea to stop people from travelling by boat to church. The cape of Hiidennokka was said to have been formed this way.","Hiisi","en/d/hiisi.html"],["Camels from Lanka","Pabuji promised Kelam camels seized from the demon king of Lanka, although camels were unknown in Rajasthan and Lanka was believed impregnable.","Pabuji","en/d/pabuji.html"],["A name that sounds like a howl","The name Hljóð has been translated as &#x27;howl&#x27;. It appears in Old Norse and is also transcribed as Hljod or Ljod.","Hljód","en/d/hljod.html"],["The Caryatids of the Erechtheum","The caryatids that support the porch of the Erechtheum on the Athenian Acropolis are female marble figures that serve as columns.","Caryatis","en/d/cariatis.html"],["A wig that produced treasures","According to the Prose Edda, the commission of Sif&#x27;s golden hair led the dwarfs to also forge Odin&#x27;s spear and Thor&#x27;s hammer.","Sif","en/d/sif.html"],["A father with a shrine","Iwa-no-ōkami, his father according to the Harima Fudoki, was the god of Iwa Shrine and was believed to dwell in Iseno.","Isetsuhiko","en/d/isetsuhiko.html"],["A cow in the name","Ptolemy&#x27;s 2nd-century Geography records the river&#x27;s ancient name as Bouvinda, which may derive from a Proto-Celtic root meaning &quot;white cow&quot;.","Boann","en/d/boann.html"],["Origin of the name","Pulaha is traced to the sense &#x27;destroyer of pula&#x27;, where pula can mean breadth or extent, bristling hair, or a particular gait of horses.","Pulaha","en/d/pulaja.html"],["A god without stories","No tales about the epithet survive; everything known comes from dedications carved in stone.","Cissonius","en/d/cissonius.html"],["The meaning of her name","Eurydice comes from Greek <em>eurys</em>, &quot;wide&quot;, and <em>dike</em>, &quot;justice&quot;, so her name means &quot;wide justice&quot;.","Eurydice of Argos","en/d/euridice-de-argos.html"],["Claws like knives","According to tradition, the lion&#x27;s claws cut through anything a mortal sword could, which allowed Heracles to skin it.","Nemean lion","en/d/leon-de-nemea.html"],["Two divine lineages","His father was Apollo, and his mother Aethusa was a daughter of Poseidon.","Eleuther","en/d/eleuter.html"],["A yellow oath","According to tradition, breaking an oath sworn in Veles&#x27;s name made the offender turn yellow, an image probably describing a condition such as jaundice.","Veles","en/d/veles.html"],["A Greek parallel","According to the description of her role, her Greek counterpart would be Semele, the mother of Dionysus.","Stimula","en/d/estimula.html"],["A bird protected by faith","Yazidis are forbidden to hunt, eat, curse or ill-treat the peacock, and its image adorns the Lalish sanctuary and many homes.","Melek Taus","en/d/melek-taus.html"],["The winds in a gourd","The winds of Hawaiʻi were kept in a gourd and called forth by chanting their names.","La'a Maomao","en/d/la-a-maomao.html"],["Two names for her father","The sources call her father Bebryx or Bebrycius, depending on the version. Both forms appear in ancient texts.","Pyrene","en/d/pirene-q1509554.html"],["A name tied to July","According to the sources, the Etruscan month of July bore her name, though only the Latin word Traneus is known.","Turan","en/d/turan.html"],["Trishanku","In the Ramayana, King Trishanku is expelled from Svarga by Indra and is left suspended in the air, held up by the sage Vishvamitra.","Svarga","en/d/suarga.html"],["Vishnu&#x27;s mount","Garuda earned from Vishnu the privilege of becoming his mount after defeating several celestial deities.","Vinata","en/d/vinata.html"],["Children of Poseidon","According to the sources, Tyro bore Pelias and Neleus to Poseidon, who later appear in other stories of Greek myth.","Salmoneus","en/d/salmoneo.html"],["An astronomical pairing","In a late astronomical commentary, Kishar appears as the spouse of the star Gudanna, described as the &quot;Enlil of Shuruppak&quot;.","Anshar","en/d/anshar.html"],["A golden apple","According to Lucian, the apple was solid gold and bore the inscription &#x27;for the queen of beauty&#x27;. Versions differ on the exact wording.","Eris","en/d/eris.html"],["Earliest appearances","The animal already appears on the ceremonial macehead of the ruler Scorpion II, and later on the serekhs of the Second Dynasty kings Peribsen and Khasekhemwy.","Set animal","en/d/animal-de-set.html"],["A sacred haircut","A child&#x27;s first haircut was celebrated as a rite in honor of Rod and the Rozhanitsy, to whom part of the food and the cut hair were offered.","Rod","en/d/rod.html"],["Names of Chinese origin","Many Japanese dragon names are loanwords from Chinese, and the word ryū is written with kanji of Chinese origin.","Japanese dragon","en/d/dragon-japones.html"],["A name with history","Before being known as the Barefoot Immortal, he was called Liu Hai, and he also appears as Liu Cao.","Barefoot Immortal","en/d/inmortal-descalzo.html"],["The crane bag","The <em>corrbolg</em>, or crane bag, is one of the Fianna&#x27;s great treasures and passed to Fionn after he defeated Liath.","Liath Luachra","en/d/liath-luachra.html"],["No footprints","In the Homeric Hymn to Hermes, the sandals let him steal Apollo&#x27;s cattle without leaving a trace.","Talaria (Winged Sandals of Hermes)","en/d/talaria-sandalias-aladas-de-hermes.html"],["Possible meaning of the name","The name &#x27;Ancasta&#x27; may be related to the Proto-Celtic *kasto-, meaning &#x27;swift&#x27;. This is only a possibility, not a certainty.","Ancasta","en/d/ancasta.html"],["Greek Hedone","The corresponding Greek goddess is Hedone, whose name means pleasure. Plato mentions her as the Greek equivalent of libido.","Voluptas","en/d/voluptas.html"],["A region named after him","A part of the region of Ambracia was called Dexamenas, in his honor.","Dexamenus","en/d/dexameno.html"],["Inscriptions in Sicily","In 1899 inscriptions dedicated to Anna and Apollo were found at Buscemi in Sicily, evidence of a local cult.","Anna Perenna","en/d/anna-perenna.html"],["Dice offered to Fortune","Pausanias says that in Argos there was a Temple of Fortune to which Palamedes dedicated the dice he had invented.","Palamedes","en/d/palamedes.html"],["An oracle and a sword","In 1928, the Panglung oracle twisted a sword of Mongolian steel into many loops during a public invocation witnessed by Joseph Rock.","Dorje Shugden","en/d/dorje-shugden.html"],["A stone map","In 2005, explorers studied the Pusharo petroglyphs and nearby geoglyphs, which some thought might show where Paititi lay.","Paititi","en/d/paititi.html"],["A founding by family","According to the account, Catillus and his three sons acted together: two of them carried the name Catillus, and another bore the name Tiburtus, who gave the city its name.","Tiburtus","en/d/tiburto.html"],["An inseparable sister","Torgerd usually appears alongside her sister Irpa, who shares with her the episode of arrows shot from the fingers during the battle.","Torgerd Hølgebrud","en/d/torgerd-hlgebrud.html"],["A lament named after him","The funeral genre <em>linos</em> took its name from the musician, and some authors think Linus himself may have been a personification of the lament.","Linus","en/d/lino.html"],["A mountain shaped like a lady","From certain viewpoints, the outline of the peaks looks like a reclining lady, with the nose, mouth and feet formed by different summits.","Mari","en/d/mari.html"],["A rival donkey","According to one legend, a donkey that Dionysus had given the power of speech challenged Priapus to a contest over who had the bigger member. Priapus won, but then killed the animal.","Priapus","en/d/priapo.html"],["Origin of the name","The Greek name Σφίγξ was linked by folk etymology to the verb meaning «to squeeze». Some connect it to the way lions kill their prey by strangling them. The historian Susan Wise Bauer proposes another origin: a corruption of the Egyptian «shesepankh», meaning «living image».","Theban Sphinx","en/d/esfinge-de-tebas.html"],["A log that decided a life","According to the Latin poets, the brand appeared in the palace rooms at the very moment Meleager was born, a detail absent from the older Greek accounts.","Althaea","en/d/altea.html"],["The Amazoneum","According to Kleidemus, the left wing of the Amazon army stood in a place in Athens later known as the Amazoneum.","Attic War","en/d/guerra-del-atica.html"],["Feigned thunder","According to the story, Romulus Silvius posed as someone able to make thunder so that his subjects would take him for a god.","Romulus Silvius","en/d/romulo-silvio.html"],["A spear turned into laurel","In a story recorded by Statius, an eagle snatched Amphiaraus&#x27;s spear and dropped it to the ground, where it took root and became a laurel tree.","Amphiaraus","en/d/anfiarao.html"],["Possible meaning of the name","Wilfred G. Lambert proposed that the element <em>a-zi</em> in her name could mean «water of life».","Azimua","en/d/azimua.html"],["A sacred crow","In one version, a crow stole a bone from the altar during a sacrifice and carried it to the Bosporus, indicating where Byzantium would rise.","Semystra","en/d/semistica.html"],["Festival of farces","During the Floralia, farces and mimes were performed, and women wore brightly coloured clothing that was normally forbidden to them.","Flora","en/d/flora.html"],["A falling anvil","Hesiod calculates that a bronze anvil would take nine days to fall from heaven to earth and nine more to reach Tartarus from the earth.","Tartarus","en/d/tartaro.html"],["A monster of many parts","Old Sinhalese illustrations describe it with parts of six or seven animals, such as an elephant&#x27;s trunk, crocodile jaws and lion&#x27;s feet.","Makara","en/d/makara.html"],["Many names","Babylonian god lists record around fourteen names and titles for Ninshubur, including Papsukkal and Sukkalanna, meaning &#x27;heavenly vizier&#x27;.","Ninshubur","en/d/ninshubur.html"],["Two Sarpedons","Some authors believed that Minos&#x27;s brother and the Trojan hero were the same person, but the difference in generations forced explanations such as the long life granted by Zeus.","Sarpedon","en/d/sarpedon.html"],["A name for dogs","In modern Russia, Polkan is a popular name for large dogs.","Polkan","en/d/polkan.html"],["The three precious children","Amaterasu, Tsukuyomi and Susanoo were born from Izanagi&#x27;s left eye, right eye and nose during his purification.","Izanagi","en/d/izanagi.html"],["Linked to Venus","Quetzalcoatl was associated with the planet Venus, which Mesoamerican peoples followed closely in the sky.","Feathered Serpent","en/d/serpiente-emplumada.html"],["Several names","She is known by several names, such as Mishimanomizokui-hime, Seyadatarahime, Katsutamayori-biyorihime or Kimikahihime.","Tamakushi-hime","en/d/tamakushi-hime.html"],["A disputed tomb","In the Middle Ages, Glastonbury Abbey claimed to have found Arthur&#x27;s oak coffin, bearing an inscription that placed him on the isle of Avalon. Today the find is considered a fraud, since the inscription seems to be in twelfth-century style and no contemporary records mention the discovery.","Avalon","en/d/avalon.html"],["Bone figurine in Crimea","In 2018, archaeologists found at the ancient Greek city of Tiritaka, in Crimea, a bone figurine of the god dating to the 1st century BC. It also shows two satyrs and a goose.","Harpocrates","en/d/harpocrates.html"],["Several spellings and names","In Tibetan she is called Lomagyuma, and her name is written in several forms, such as Parnashavari, Paranasavari or Parnasabari.","Parnashavari","en/d/parnashavari.html"],["An ambiguous cause","The account does not make clear whether the gods were angered by the names Mundilfari gave his children or by Sól&#x27;s marriage to Glenr.","Glenr","en/d/glenr.html"],["Named after the light","The name comes from the blue-paper lamps used at these gatherings, chosen to create an eerie atmosphere.","Aoandon","en/d/aoandon.html"],["A shrine with treasures","According to tradition, Izushi Shrine in Toyooka houses seven or eight treasures brought by Amenohiboko from Silla.","Amenohiboko","en/d/amenohiboko.html"],["Expressive name","Its Sumerian name, šar₂-ur₃, means &quot;smasher of thousands&quot;, a direct description of its power in battle.","Sharur","en/d/sharur.html"],["Tea from a falling leaf","According to tradition, tea leaves fell into his pot of boiling water and he tasted the drink, discovering tea.","Shennong","en/d/shennong.html"],["A sea name","Her name means &quot;wave of the deep sea&quot;, formed from the root <em>benthos</em>, which biologists use for the sea floor.","Benthesikyme","en/d/bentesicime.html"],["Other Eurylochi","The name belongs to several figures in Greek myth: a companion of Odysseus, a hero of Salamis, one of Penelope&#x27;s suitors and this Egyptian prince.","Eurylochus","en/d/euriloco.html"],["Twelve years of rule","According to Eusebius, Thymoetes ruled for twelve years, a detail that appears only in late chronologies.","Oxyntes","en/d/oxintes.html"],["A goddess on the road","In 2007, the Kumari of Bhaktapur, Sajani Shakya, travelled to the United States to attend a documentary screening. Temple authorities declared her tainted and removed her, but the decision was reversed after international media attention.","Kumari","en/d/kumari.html"],["Emblem of an office","In old Siam, the gajasimha identified the kalahom, one of the king&#x27;s two chief chancellors.","Gajasimha","en/d/gajasimha.html"],["A name with meaning","His Sanskrit name, <em>pāṇḍu</em>, means &quot;pale&quot;, and it is pronounced somewhat differently in Sanskrit than in several modern Indian languages.","Pandu","en/d/pandu.html"],["A Sorbian &quot;Faust&quot;","He is often called the &quot;Wendish Faust&quot;, comparing him to the German figure who learns magic from a sorcerer.","Krabat","en/d/krabat.html"],["A Hydra on the shield","A Hydra appears on his shield, the monster Hercules defeated. Some scholars see in this a hint that Aventinus himself represents a monster destined to fall before Aeneas.","Aventinus","en/d/aventino.html"],["Daikokuten, demon hunter","A legend tells that Daikokuten hung a sacred talisman from a branch in his garden and, using it as a trap, caught a demon.","Seven Lucky Gods","en/d/siete-dioses-de-la-fortuna.html"],["A name with three roots","Chicomoztoc comes from the Nahuatl chicome (“seven”), oztotl (“cave”) and the suffix -c (“place”).","Chicomoztoc","en/d/chicomoztoc.html"],["Gods without a Greek parallel","The Indigetes are a group of gods with no equivalent in Greek mythology, showing that Roman religion had its own deities.","Alemonia","en/d/alemonia.html"],["A rejected daughter","Iwanagahime, the daughter Ninigi rejected, was later venerated as a protective deity of longevity.","Ninigi no Mikoto","en/d/ninigi-no-mikoto.html"],["Folkloric comparison","The folklorist Kunio Yanagita compared Ne-no-kuni with the Nirai Kanai of Ryukyuan religion, a paradise located beyond the seas.","Ne-no-kuni","en/d/ne-no-kuni.html"],["Name of the spring","According to the myth, the spring where Callirhoë took her life was named after her, and the story may explain the name of the spring near Calydon.","Callirhoë","en/d/calirroe-q525524.html"],["Possible origin","Some scholars believe it was created by the tofu trade in the Edo period, perhaps as an advertising character.","Tōfu-kozō","en/d/tofu-kozo.html"],["Two readings of the name","The Sanskrit name is usually translated as &quot;the lord who looks down&quot;, but some scholars believe the original form, Avalokitasvara, meant &quot;the one who perceives sound&quot;.","Avalokiteśvara","en/d/avalokitesvara.html"],["A sign with several readings","The cuneiform sign for Ki means &quot;earth&quot;, but it was also read as GUNNI, &quot;hearth&quot;, or KISLAH, &quot;threshing floor&quot;. It shows how Mesopotamian writing combined meanings and sound values.","Ki","en/d/ki.html"],["A name that survives","A harbour near Taranto is still called Torre Saturo, a name derived from Satyrion, the mother or wife of the hero according to some accounts.","Taras","en/d/taras.html"],["Origin of Paeonia","According to tradition, Epeius&#x27; defeated brother Paeon settled beyond the River Axius, and that land took its name from him as Paeonia.","Epeius of Elis","en/d/epeo-de-elide.html"],["A hero who survives","According to Cath Gabhra, Caílte is one of only two members of the Fianna to survive the final battle, along with Oisín.","Caílte mac Rónáin","en/d/cailte-mac-ronain.html"],["A boon against strength","According to the Mahabharata, the sage Agastya granted Nahusha a boon that drained the strength of anyone stronger than him who touched him.","Nahusha","en/d/nahusa.html"],["A brother named after a citadel","According to one version, Pergamus was named after the citadel of Troy, the city from which his mother Andromache came.","Molossus","en/d/moloso.html"],["An eye on the mule","According to the myth, the king&#x27;s arrow struck the mule&#x27;s rump, and the goddess turned the wound into an eye that watches over the twenty-four regions.","Palden Lhamo","en/d/palden-lhamo.html"],["Longing for food","The word himeros can also refer to a desire for food, such as the craving a laborer feels after a long day at work.","Himeros","en/d/himero.html"],["Turquoise from afar","Some of the turquoise came to Mesoamerica from about 1,600 km to the northwest, from the Four Corners region, where the Ancestral Pueblo people mined the stone.","Double-headed serpent","en/d/serpiente-bicefala.html"],["A god who can be a whale","In some places Tangaroa is not shown in human form but as a whale.","Tangaroa","en/d/tangaroa.html"],["A secret union","In the <em>Iliad</em>, Ares couples with Astyoche secretly inside her father&#x27;s house, which explains the divine paternity of her sons.","Astyoche","en/d/astioque.html"],["A horse with a disputed tail","In the Mahabharata, the bet over the colour of the tail ends with the loser becoming the winner&#x27;s servant.","Uchchaihshravas","en/d/uchaisravas.html"],["Same name as her grandmother","According to some sources, she was named after her maternal grandmother, also called Pandora, a figure better known for the myth of the jar or box.","Pandora","en/d/pandora-q3085116.html"],["The Hounds of Annwn","The <em>Cŵn Annwn</em>, or Hounds of Annwn, ride the skies in autumn and winter. Their baying was linked to the calls of wild geese on migration.","Arawn","en/d/arawn.html"],["Name","His name is usually translated as &quot;mist-calf&quot;, an image that points to his origin in clay.","Mökkurkálfi","en/d/mokkurkalfi.html"],["A colour name","The name Glauce derives from the Greek glaukós, meaning something like &#x27;bluish-grey&#x27;.","Creusa","en/d/creusa-q269058.html"],["An Egyptian city","Ancient writers say that a town in Egypt, near Anthylla, was called Archandropolis, after this Greek hero.","Archander","en/d/arcandro.html"],["A name with wind in it","Its Old Norse name has been translated as &quot;storm pale&quot; or &quot;bleached by the weather&quot;, a reference that scholars interpret in several ways.","Veðrfölnir","en/d/verfolnir.html"],["A name that changes shape","The title of Gerður Kristný&#x27;s book is a variant of the horse&#x27;s name, with a small change in its spelling.","Blóðughófi","en/d/bloughofi.html"],["Tears of blood","The texts say that the god Dagda wept tears of blood over the death of his son.","Cermait","en/d/cermait.html"],["A misread name","Her name is often glossed as famed for her suitors, but this is a late reading based on a false link to the verb meaning to woo. Aeschylus played with another sense, that of a schemer.","Clytemnestra","en/d/clitemnestra.html"],["Landing at Dundrum","According to the texts, Genann arrived in Ireland through Dundrum Bay, in what is now County Down in the north of the island.","Genann mac Dela","en/d/genann-mac-dela.html"],["A name tied to speed","Her Greek name, Ἄελλα, is linked to a storm or whirlwind, and Diodorus Siculus explains that she was named for her swiftness.","Aella","en/d/aela.html"],["A coin with a public message","A denarius of the empress Julia Maesa shows Laetitia holding a globe, inscribed LAETITIA PVBL, &quot;joy of the public&quot;. It may suggest that the population rejoiced in the expansion of the Empire.","Laetitia","en/d/laetitia.html"],["A heart of steel","Hesiod is the only source that describes Eurybia as having a heart of steel, an unusual image for a sea deity.","Eurybia","en/d/euribia.html"],["A discus at the funeral games","Among the plunder from the raid on Thebe was a discus that, according to the Iliad, later appears in the funeral games held in honour of Patroclus.","Eetion","en/d/eetion.html"],["Day of the week","Záviš Kalandra proposed that Nezamysl was Sunday, the day when one does not think about working.","Nezamysl","en/d/nezamysl.html"],["A name for a strait","The Hellespont was named after Helle, Phrixus&#x27;s sister, who fell into the sea during the flight on the golden ram.","Nephele","en/d/nefele.html"],["Origin of the name","His name is linked to Old Turkic bay, &quot;rich&quot;, and ülgen, &quot;magnificent&quot;.","Ülgen (Bai-Ülgen)","en/d/ulgen-bai-ulgen.html"],["A staff as a god","Some historians believe Thietmar may have mistaken an ordinary ritual staff for a deity. This is one reason many doubt that Hennil was truly a god.","Hennil","en/d/hennil.html"],["An astronomical role","Plutarch gave these Muses a place in the order of the cosmos: Mese looked after the region of the planets.","Mese","en/d/mese.html"],["Fearsome look","Philostratus the Younger describes him as grim-looking, with thick hair, a beard and glaring eyes.","Phasis","en/d/phasis.html"],["The boots and the cap","The Cortona statuettes show Culsans naked except for boots, a torque and a cap. Scholars think these items were not special clothing belonging to one particular god.","Culsans","en/d/culsans.html"],["Joined at the waist","Eurytus and his brother are described as joined at the waist, which made them inseparable in battle and in their exploits.","Eurytus","en/d/eurito.html"],["A name of uncertain origin","He is known by dozens of names, such as bolotny dedko (&#x27;swamp grandpa&#x27;) or tsar bolotny (&#x27;the Swamp King&#x27;). Some of these come from Baltic languages or from Czech.","Bolotnik","en/d/bolotnik.html"],["His name","Kumbhakarna means «pot-eared», from <em>kumbha</em> (pot) and <em>karna</em> (ear).","Kumbhakarna","en/d/kumbhakarna.html"],["Many names","It is also known as Atvaras, Damavykas, Pūkis or Spirukas. It is identical to the Latvian <em>pūķis</em>.","Aitvaras","en/d/aitvaras.html"],["A city bearing his name","Teishebaini means &#x27;city of the god Teisheba&#x27; and was founded by King Rusa II. It shows that the god held an important place in the organisation of the kingdom.","Theispas","en/d/teisheba.html"],["An almond tree instead of a woman","In one version, Phyllis turns into an almond tree, which blossoms when Demophon embraces it.","Demophon","en/d/demofonte.html"],["Temples in Bali","Temples dedicated to Prajapati still exist in Bali, and many are linked to funeral ceremonies.","Prajapati","en/d/prayapati.html"],["The book and the brush","Her most characteristic attribute is a book with a brush, a symbol of recording newborns in the family genealogy.","Songzi Niangniang","en/d/songzi-niangniang-q24841560.html"],["A genjutsu with his name","In Naruto, Itachi Uchiha&#x27;s most famous illusion technique bears Tsukuyomi&#x27;s name and takes place under a blood-red moon.","Tsukuyomi","en/d/tsukuyomi.html"],["A statue to Modesty","According to the story, Icarius set up a statue of Aidos, Modesty, on the spot where he parted from his daughter.","Icarius","en/d/icario.html"],["A widely shared name","Bhadra means good, fortunate or auspicious, and several kings, sages and women in Hindu tradition carry it, including a Bhadrā who was the wife of Krishna.","Bhadrā","en/d/bhadra.html"],["An echo in the Romani language","The Romani word for god, <em>del</em>, descends directly from <em>devata</em>, the form the term takes in modern Hindi.","Deva","en/d/deva.html"],["A name that survives","The Antilles archipelago took its name from this legendary island after 1492, although the island itself was never found.","Antillia","en/d/antillia.html"],["A feverish artist","Shigeru Mizuki said he fell ill with a high fever while working on a kamishibai about the bake-kujira, and later wondered whether the creature had possessed him.","Bake-kujira","en/d/bake-kujira.html"],["Mother of Phaethon","According to some accounts, Clymene, daughter of Iphis, was the mother of Phaethon by Helios, and also had Iphiclus with her husband Philacus.","Iphis","en/d/ifis-q696330.html"],["Name for a sword","Snorri Sturluson includes Níðhöggr among the terms for swords, as well as in his list of serpents.","Níðhöggr","en/d/nihoggr.html"],["Name in Greek","In Greek it is written Χαρναβών. Hyginus, who wrote in Latin, calls him Carnabon.","Charnabon","en/d/carnabon.html"],["A sanctuary in Athens","The Kourotropheion was built in Athens in her name, honouring her local cult.","Kourotrophos","en/d/curotrofo.html"],["More than Ahura Mazda","In the Persepolis archives, Humban appears twenty-six times, compared with ten for Auramazdā, the Old Persian form of Ahura Mazda.","Humban","en/d/humban.html"],["Sweatbath name","As goddess of the sweatbaths, she was called Temazcalteci, &quot;Grandmother of sweatbaths&quot;.","Toci","en/d/toci.html"],["A name with character","His name means quarrelsome, a trait that fits his role as his brother&#x27;s rival.","Polynices","en/d/polinices.html"],["The Nebty title","Her name appeared beside Nekhbet&#x27;s in the royal title &quot;The Two Ladies&quot;, symbolising rule over the Two Lands.","Wadjet","en/d/uadyet.html"],["A stone with many names","It is also called the white stone or the blue stone, and some ancient tales speak of a white burning stone on Buyan, possibly the same stone.","Alatyr","en/d/alatyr.html"],["A hymn for dancing","A hymn associated with Amimitl survives with a Nahuatl gloss. Its message is cryptic, but it is understood as a song for dances.","Amimitl","en/d/amimitl.html"],["A bird and the stars","The Egyptians compared the shimmer of the ibis’s feathers to the twinkling of the stars, which may have strengthened its link with the spirits of the dead.","Akhty","en/d/akhty.html"],["An asteroid with his name","The asteroid 2357 Phereclos, a Jovian asteroid, keeps his name in the sky.","Phereclus","en/d/fereclo.html"],["A squid behind the legend","Cryptozoologist Bernard Heuvelmans thought the story may have arisen from the discovery of a mutilated Grimaldi scaled squid.","Bishop-fish","en/d/pez-obispo.html"],["A net in the ash","In the Prose Edda, Kvasir recognises the shape of Loki&#x27;s net in the ashes of the fire, something the other gods had not noticed.","Kvasir","en/d/kvasir.html"],["Four roads","Past the rivers, travellers reached a crossroads with four roads of different colours. Only the black one led to Xibalba.","Xibalba","en/d/xibalba.html"],["Sri-Aingini","At the Chausath Yogini Temple in Bhedaghat, one of her best-known images shows her with a bent left leg resting on an elephant-headed figure seated at her feet.","Vinayaki","en/d/vinayaki.html"],["Origin of the word fever","The Latin <em>febris</em> is the source of the English <em>fever</em>. Its Indo-European root relates to the idea of burning or warming.","Febris","en/d/febris.html"],["Name of the morning star","According to Jonas Vaiškūnas, her name gives rise to several terms in Lithuanian folk astronomy for the morning star.","Aušrinė","en/d/ausrine.html"],["A region named after him","Part of Arcadia was called Epitis, probably in his honor.","Aepytus","en/d/epito.html"],["Two serpents","Philostratus is the only author who describes two serpents guarding the apples instead of one.","Ladon","en/d/ladon.html"],["Many names","She is known by at least three different names, and her place of origin changes depending on which author mentions her.","Persian Sibyl","en/d/sibila-persa.html"],["Payments that change","In the United States, the average payout per tooth rose from $1.30 in 1998 to $6.23 in 2023, although it fell to $5.01 between 2024 and 2025. The first tooth lost usually earns more than the later ones.","Tooth fairy","en/d/hada-de-los-dientes.html"],["A rejected name","The proposal to call the body beyond Pluto Ataecina was not accepted. The IAU chose Haumea, a decision that raised suspicions because of the friendship between Brown and Marsden.","Ataegina","en/d/ataecina.html"],["The city of Larisa","Hyginus says that Larisa, daughter of Pelasgus, gave her name to an eponymous city. The passage is badly damaged, so the identification is uncertain.","Pelasgus","en/d/pelasgo-q6070881.html"],["From titan to map","The name of map collections does not come from the Titan himself, but from a king of Mauretania whom Mercator chose for his title page.","Atlas","en/d/atlas.html"],["A cave without human occupation","The cave associated with the serpent shows no evidence of human occupation.","Rupiana Serpent","en/d/sierpe-rupiana.html"],["Smoking mirror","His name is usually translated as &quot;smoking mirror&quot;, referring to the obsidian mirrors of Mesoamerica.","Tezcatlipoca","en/d/tezcatlipoca.html"],["Angel of Conscience","He is frequently called the &quot;Angel of Conscience&quot; or &quot;Voice of Conscience&quot;, a title that reflects his role as teacher of Daena.","Sraosha","en/d/sraosha.html"],["A Chinese guide and a statue","According to Kim Pusik, the guide who told him about Lady Saso said that he and his followers worshipped her.","Lady Saso","en/d/lady-saso.html"],["Double meaning of his name","The word <em>ek</em> can mean either &quot;black&quot; or &quot;star&quot;, which may explain his link to the North Star.","Ek Chuah","en/d/ek-chuah.html"],["Two names for the mother","Pausanias calls her Eurycyda, while Conon names her Eurypile. The two forms are treated as spelling variants of the same figure.","Eleus","en/d/eleo.html"],["A famous shoe","According to the Prose Edda, his shoe is made from the leather scraps people discard from their own shoes.","Víðarr","en/d/viarr.html"],["A dog called Diting","In China he is sometimes shown accompanied by a dog, which according to legend was his mother reborn.","Kṣitigarbha","en/d/ksitigarbha.html"],["A modern name","The word ichthyocentaur does not appear in classical antiquity; it was coined in the twelfth century by John Tzetzes.","Ichthyocentaur","en/d/ictiocentauro.html"],["One star, two names","The Greeks called the morning star Phosphorus or Eosphorus and the evening star Hesperus, without at first realising they were the same planet, Venus.","Hesperus","en/d/hespero.html"],["The Milky Way","Caer Wydion, the castle of Gwydion, was the traditional Welsh name for the Milky Way.","Gwydion fab Dôn","en/d/gwydion-fab-don.html"],["A goddess with a horse&#x27;s head","In Arcadia, according to one tradition, Demeter took the form of a mare to escape Poseidon. She was depicted with a horse&#x27;s head and called &#x27;Black Demeter&#x27;.","Demeter","en/d/demeter.html"],["X-ray vision","Lynceus&#x27;s ability to see through walls and skin has been compared with real X-ray photography and with Superman&#x27;s X-ray vision.","Lynceus","en/d/linceo.html"],["Trans-polytheism","The scholar Peter Harvey calls the Buddhist stance towards the many gods &#x27;trans-polytheism&#x27;, because it acknowledges them without placing them at the centre of practice.","God in Buddhism","en/d/dios-en-el-budismo.html"],["A name with meaning","The name Acontius is the masculine form of a Greek word meaning &quot;javelin&quot;, in its diminutive form.","Acontius","en/d/aconcio.html"],["A possible human origin","Some scholars link the tale to Konchak, a twelfth-century Polovtsian leader mentioned in Russian chronicles. Over the centuries his figure may have turned into that of a villain.","Koschei","en/d/koschei.html"],["Three names for one queen","Ancient sources give Polybus&#x27; wife different names: Merope, Periboea and, in some versions, Medusa.","Polybus","en/d/polibo.html"],["An unusual remedy","The oracle set a very specific condition: the urine of a woman faithful to her husband. None of the women tested, not even his own wife, met the requirement.","Pheron","en/d/feron.html"],["A name in many forms","He is called Ayyanar, Aiyanar, Sastha or Chattan depending on the language and the text, and scholars debate whether Aryankavu, a temple name in Kerala, derives from the same Tamil word.","Ayyanar","en/d/ayyanar.html"],["Cakes for a nymph","During the dry months of early summer, honey-cakes were offered to her at the small sanctuary beside the fountain.","Pirene","en/d/pirene.html"],["Aquae Bormonis","The Roman place name Aquae Bormonis, recorded on the Tabula Peutingeriana, is formed from the god&#x27;s name. It is disputed whether it refers to Bourbon-Lancy or Bourbon-l&#x27;Archambault.","Borvo","en/d/borvo.html"],["A wind with his name","Iapyx also names a minor north-west wind, which the ancients associated with sea voyages.","Iapyx","en/d/yapige.html"],["A horse form","Muslim commentators described Ya&#x27;uq in the shape of a horse, while other idols on the same list were imagined as a lion, an eagle or human figures.","Ya'uq","en/d/jauco.html"],["A pilgrimage tomb","According to Pausanias, the supposed tomb of Rhadine on Samos drew star-crossed lovers. The lost poem telling her story opened with an invocation to Erato.","Erato","en/d/erato.html"],["A popular proverb","In Russian, &quot;Anika the Warrior&quot; describes someone who takes on something as futile as fighting death.","Anika the Warrior","en/d/anika-el-guerrero.html"],["Tyrrhenian pirates","Hyginus calls the sailors of the story Tyrrhenian pirates and links them to the origin of the name of the Tyrrhenian Sea.","Acoetes","en/d/acetes.html"],["A name meaning soot","His Old Norse name means &#x27;the one exposed to soot&#x27;, formed from the elements <em>and-</em> and <em>hrīm</em>.","Andhrimnir","en/d/andhrimnir.html"],["A spider with a king&#x27;s name","Cocalus is also the name of a genus of jumping spiders. The match with the Sicilian king is purely in the name.","Cocalus","en/d/cocalo.html"],["The boys in the sky","According to the Popol Vuh, the four hundred boys were placed in the sky as the Pleiades, a star cluster visible to the naked eye.","Zipacna","en/d/zipacna.html"],["A name tied to memory","His name probably derives from a Proto-Indo-European root meaning to think or remember, the same base as Sanskrit smárati.","Mímir","en/d/mimir-q3566604.html"],["The grandchildren of Dazhbog","The ancient Slavs proudly called themselves &quot;the grandchildren of Dazhbog&quot;, showing how central this god was to their identity.","Dazhbog","en/d/dazhbog.html"],["Celestial iron","According to the myth, iron weapons on earth come from the body of the asura Loha, which turned to metal on contact with Vishnu&#x27;s sword.","Nandaka","en/d/nandaka.html"],["A name with a horn","Her name means &quot;the one of the horn,&quot; and the myth links her to the Golden Horn, the arm of the sea where, according to tradition, she was born.","Ceroessa","en/d/ceroesa.html"],["A word from nightmares","Part of her name, <em>mor</em>, may come from a root meaning terror, the same root behind Old English <em>maere</em>, the source of the word nightmare.","The Morrígan","en/d/la-morrigan.html"],["A river named for a serpent","The river that runs beside Mantineia was named Ophis, meaning &#x27;serpent&#x27;, in memory of the animal that guided the settlers.","Mantineus","en/d/mantineo.html"],["An old woman against a god","Thor, one of the most powerful gods of the Norse pantheon, could not defeat an old woman in the wrestling match and only fell to one knee.","Elli","en/d/elli.html"],["An island with her name","The island opposite the Sirens&#x27; cape was named after Leucosia and is today called Licosa.","Leucosia","en/d/leucosia.html"],["A name with meaning","Mehit means &quot;the complete one,&quot; and her name also appears in forms such as Mehyt or Mekhit.","Mehit","en/d/mehit.html"],["Winged sun","His image as a winged solar disk appears already on the panels at Saqqara and remained in royal iconography for centuries.","Behedety","en/d/behedety.html"],["A creation of Sekien","Scholars suspect that the aburakago Toriyama Sekien drew was inspired by a fire of stolen oil, and that Sekien himself turned it into a child.","Aburakago","en/d/aburakago.html"],["Another name","In Ghana she is known as Madam Moke, and some tales also call her Lady Koi Koi.","Madam Koi Koi","en/d/madam-koi-koi.html"],["Two different sphinxes","The Greek Sphinx is a proper name and should not be confused with the Egyptian sphinx, a common name for certain creatures.","The Riddle of the Sphinx","en/d/el-enigma-de-la-esfinge.html"],["Coins in the tombs","Archaeology confirms that in some graves low-value coins were placed in or near the mouth of the dead, known as Charon&#x27;s obols.","Charon","en/d/caronte.html"],["An uncertain name","Her name has been linked to &quot;cow,&quot; &quot;bean,&quot; or &quot;bee,&quot; but no proposal is certain.","Beyla","en/d/beyla.html"],["A ritual incense flower","The Mexican marigold, known to the Nahua as cempohualxochitl, was burned as incense in ceremonies honouring Tlaloc.","Tlaloc","en/d/tlaloc.html"],["A lake that claims the legend","Xinyu has a reservoir and resort called Fairy Lake, which presents itself as the hometown of the Seven Fairies legend.","Seven Fairies","en/d/las-siete-hadas.html"],["The divining hare","Dio Cassius says Boudica released a hare and read the omen from the direction of its run.","Andraste","en/d/andraste.html"],["The weight of a leaf","In a folktale from Odisha, Rukmini outweighs Satyabhama&#x27;s riches by placing a single tulasi leaf on the scale. This story does not appear in the main scriptures about Krishna.","Rukmini","en/d/rukmini.html"],["Flying serpent","Takshaka is described as the only snake able to fly among the great dragon kings of Chinese and Japanese tradition.","Takshaka","en/d/takshaka.html"],["Two names","Her name can be written Demonice (&quot;victory of the people&quot;) or Demodice (&quot;justice of the people&quot;), and some sources also give Demodoce.","Demonice","en/d/demonice.html"],["Mare pines","The dwarf, twisted pines growing on Swedish coastal rocks are called mare pines, because the creature was also believed to tangle trees.","Mare","en/d/mare.html"],["Horus the Elder","Plutarch distinguished between Horus, son of Osiris and Isis, and Horus the Elder, a primordial form that the Egyptians equated with the Greek Apollo.","Horus","en/d/horus.html"],["Water at sea","During a long calm, the water Dexicreon carried on his ship became the most valuable cargo, and he sold it at very high prices.","Dexicreon","en/d/dexicreonte.html"],["A name with two fathers","The sources disagree: Athenaeus makes him a son of Heracles, while Conon and Stephanus of Byzantium present him as a son of King Strymon.","Olynthus","en/d/olinto.html"],["The Museo del Oro raft","The Muisca raft, linked to the El Dorado tradition, is now displayed at the Museo del Oro in Bogotá, alongside other objects recovered from Lake Guatavita.","El Dorado","en/d/el-dorado.html"],["Origin of the name","Barong may derive from the local word bahruang, related to beruang, which means &#x27;bear&#x27; in Indonesian.","Barong","en/d/barong.html"],["An unexpected father","In one rare account, Poseidon, not Hellen, was said to be Dorus&#x27;s father.","Dorus","en/d/doro.html"],["A device with a Greek name","The doctor Jack Kevorkian named his euthanasia device the Thanatron, a reference to death.","Thanatos","en/d/tanatos.html"],["Rescue of Helle","According to the tradition, Cymothoe helped Glaucus rescue Helle when she fell from the golden ram.","Cymothoe","en/d/cimotoe.html"],["Differing versions","The Mahabharata survives in numerous manuscript versions. The Southern recension is longer than the Northern one, although both share the same thematic essence.","Kunti-Bhoja","en/d/kunti-bhoja.html"],["Protective figurines","Some Apkallu figurines have bird heads or other forms, not only fish shapes, and were used in protective rituals.","Apkallu","en/d/apkallu.html"],["Starry origin","The <em>Matrikas</em> were originally the seven stars of the Pleiades, before becoming goddesses linked to the gods.","Vaishnavi","en/d/vaishnavi.html"],["First appearance","The phrase first appears in 1823, in James Fenimore Cooper&#x27;s novel The Pioneers.","Happy hunting ground","en/d/territorio-de-caza-feliz.html"],["Healer and huntress","In the songs she heals animals hurt by traps, so her role is not limited to hunting.","Mielikki","en/d/mielikki.html"],["Name of victory","His name comes from a root meaning «to conquer» or «to prevail», and it was used in almost all Anatolian languages.","Tarḫunna","en/d/tarhunna.html"],["Only mention of the Rig Veda","Mahamayuri&#x27;s dharani, translated into Chinese by Kumārajīva, is the only place in the entire Chinese Buddhist canon that mentions the Rig Veda.","Mahamayuri","en/d/mahamayuri.html"],["A luminous name","Her Greek name, <em>Aïglê</em>, means &#x27;brightness&#x27; or &#x27;splendour&#x27;, a idea that fits her link with Helios, god of light.","Aegle","en/d/egle.html"],["A buffalo in Vietnam","In the Vietnamese zodiac, the water buffalo takes the place of the Ox.","Ox","en/d/buey.html"],["A name with a story","In the <em>Odyssey</em>, his grandfather Autolycus named him because he had been angered by many people. Other ancient authors linked the name to verbs meaning &quot;to lament&quot; or &quot;to perish&quot;.","Odysseus (Ulysses)","en/d/odiseo-ulises.html"],["An eye as payment","In the Völuspá, Odin leaves one of his eyes in the well in exchange for a drink from its waters.","Mímir's Well","en/d/manantial-de-mimir.html"],["A beetle with a mythic name","The cerambyx gave its name to the longhorn beetle family Cerambycidae, known for their long, slender bodies and long antennae.","Cerambus","en/d/cerambo.html"],["A name with trees inside","Hamadryas comes from the Greek words hama, meaning together with, and drys, which can mean holm oak or tree in general.","Hamadryas","en/d/hamadriade.html"],["Possible origin of the name","Her name may be connected with krithé, the Greek word for barley.","Critheïs","en/d/criteis.html"],["A house of demons","In <em>High School DxD</em> Gremory is one of the 72 devil noble families of the <em>Ars Goetia</em>, and the series&#x27; female lead belongs to it.","Gremory","en/d/gremory.html"],["A unique offering","At Kayatsu Shrine in Aichi Prefecture, she is offered tsukemono, pickled vegetables, a practice not recorded at any other site in Japan.","Kaya-no-hime","en/d/kaya-no-hime.html"],["The meaning of the name","Fodbgen is usually translated as &quot;the despoiler.&quot; Its modern Irish spelling is Foidhbhgen.","Fodbgen","en/d/fodbgen.html"],["A bowl for Anchises","Cisseus is remembered for giving Anchises an engraved bowl as a token of their friendship.","Cisseus","en/d/ciseo.html"],["A human bridge","According to the tale, Brân positioned himself across the river so his troops could cross over him, saying that the man who would lead his people must first become a bridge.","Brân the Blessed","en/d/bran-el-bendito.html"],["A city in Italy","The commentator Servius states that Corythus founded a settlement in Italy named after himself.","Corythus","en/d/corito.html"],["A sword from the Styx","According to the tradition recorded by Virgil, the sword Vulcan forged for Turnus was tempered in the waters of the Styx, the river of the underworld.","Daunus","en/d/dauno.html"],["A concept turned into a god","Soter and Ktesios were both personified beings and epithets of Zeus. An abstract idea such as salvation could thus become a title of the chief god.","Soter","en/d/soter.html"],["A dragon-shaped comet","The origin of the epithet Pendragon lies in a comet that, according to tradition, Uther saw on the way to battle. Merlin read it as an omen.","Uther Pendragon","en/d/uther-pendragon.html"],["The tornit","The tornit are another fabulous race in Inuit lore. In a tale recorded by Kroeber, two tornit escape from cannibalistic adlet by cutting the thongs that held their sledges together.","Adlet","en/d/adlet.html"],["Flayed body","She was depicted with a flayed body and an open jaw, as if ready to swallow the stars during the day.","Mictēcacihuātl","en/d/mictecacihuatl.html"],["A day of the week","English <em>Tuesday</em> comes from Tyr&#x27;s name rather than directly from Mars, although the Latin day was dedicated to the Roman god.","Tyr","en/d/tyr.html"],["A modern hypothesis","The scholar T.F. O&#x27;Rahilly suggested that Mug Nuadat might really be the god Nuada rather than a historical person. It is also possible that Éogan Mór was the earthly representation of that deity.","Mug Nuadat","en/d/mug-nuadat.html"],["A warning in the tongue","Katsumi Tada suggests the name hides a saying: the tongue is the gate to misfortune.","Aka-shita","en/d/aka-shita.html"],["Two centaur lineages","Chiron was immortal and of divine descent, while Pholus and the other centaurs were mortal. Their different ancestry explains why Pholus behaved so sensibly.","Pholus","en/d/folo.html"],["A pointing statue","His outstretched finger recalls the Shwedagon Pagoda legend, which is why nearly all his images show him this way.","Bo Bo Gyi","en/d/bo-bo-gyi.html"],["A sanctuary with wide reach","Votive offerings found at Samos include objects from Armenia, Babylon, Iran, Assyria and Egypt, showing the fame of her sanctuary beyond the Aegean.","Hera","en/d/hera.html"],["A frankincense tree","In Ovid&#x27;s version, Leucothoe&#x27;s body does not simply vanish; Helios transforms it into a tree that produces frankincense.","Thersanon","en/d/thersanon.html"],["A curious epithet","The Iliad calls him shieldless, a trait Pausanias attributes to a two-piece corselet that protected him in battle.","Phorcys","en/d/forcis-q3381470.html"],["Year and harvest","The kanji 年 (nen), which now means year, originally meant harvest, because the harvest happened once each year.","Toshigami","en/d/toshigami.html"],["Zheng He&#x27;s giraffes","According to the records, Zheng He&#x27;s fleet brought two giraffes to Nanjing in 1414, and the emperor took them for mythical creatures. The identification of the qilin with the giraffe has lasted since then.","Qilin","en/d/qilin.html"],["Sword ritual","In alagu seva, the veerakumars strike their bare chests with holy swords in a ritual dance, and the pandaram turmeric is applied to the wounds to prevent infection.","Sri Ramalinga Sowdeswari Amman","en/d/sri-ramalinga-sowdeswari-amman.html"],["A famous painting","The painter Jacek Malczewski depicted a drowned man in the arms of a dziwożona, one of these creatures.","Boginka","en/d/boginka.html"],["A male god","Some Akkadian prayers invoke a male Mamu, and at Imgur-Enlil the deity was viewed as male.","Mamu","en/d/mamu.html"],["Swedish runestones","The name Þjálfi appears as a personal name on more than twelve runestones in Sweden, including Sö 194 and U 56.","Þjálfi and Röskva","en/d/jalfi-y-roskva.html"],["A name meaning haste","According to James P. Allen, his name translates as He of Haste. His brother Imsety has been linked to dill, an herb.","Hapy","en/d/hapi-q1141220.html"],["Silver tears","Mama Quilla was said to weep tears of silver, a metal associated with the Moon.","Mama Quilla","en/d/mama-quilla.html"],["A name of Odin","Fjölnir appears as one of Odin&#x27;s many names in the <em>Grímnismál</em>, the <em>Reginsmál</em> and the <em>Gylfaginning</em>.","Fjölnir","en/d/fjolnir.html"],["Warming the grain","In the Kojiki-den, an alternative spelling of his name means that he warms the grain so that it ripens.","Amenohoakari","en/d/amenohoakari.html"],["A play on words","The <em>aka</em> of filth sounds the same as <em>aka</em>, meaning &#x27;red&#x27; in Japanese. For this reason, many modern artists tend to draw the akaname in red.","Akaname","en/d/akaname.html"],["A name meaning obstruction","Vritra literally means &#x27;cover&#x27; or &#x27;obstacle&#x27;, a reference to his holding back the waters.","Vritra","en/d/vritra.html"],["A name meaning thanks","Þökk means &#x27;thanks&#x27; in Old Norse and Icelandic, a name that contrasts with her refusal to help.","Þökk","en/d/okk.html"],["A Trojan asteroid","The asteroid 4063 Euphorbus shares an orbit with Jupiter and belongs to the Trojan asteroids, which travel along with the planet.","Euphorbus","en/d/euforbo.html"],["A fish named after her","In 2018, UNAM researchers described a freshwater fish from Oaxaca and Veracruz and named it Vieja coatlicue in her honor.","Coatlicue","en/d/coatlicue.html"],["A discarded theory","Authors of the 19th and early 20th centuries claimed Lahmu represented the zodiac, parent stars or constellations, an idea now disproven.","Lahmu","en/d/lahmu.html"],["A name with several readings","The meaning of Belisama is debated: &#x27;the very bright&#x27; and &#x27;the very strong&#x27; are the two most cited proposals among specialists.","Belisama","en/d/belisama.html"],["A sexual position","Rati-pasha, &quot;the noose of Rati&quot;, is the Sanskrit name of a position in which the woman crosses her legs behind her lover&#x27;s back.","Rati","en/d/rati.html"],["A name with several meanings","Robert Graves suggested that his name could mean &#x27;burner&#x27;, &#x27;woodpecker&#x27; or &#x27;sorcerer&#x27;.","Celeus","en/d/celeo.html"],["A mortar against Greece","In Aristophanes&#x27; Peace, Polemos threatens to grind Greece&#x27;s cities in a mortar, but the play puns on the cities&#x27; names until the threat sounds like a relish for a feast.","Polemos","en/d/polemo.html"],["The temple&#x27;s pillars","Strabo reports that many visitors to Gadir believed the two bronze pillars in the temple were the true Pillars of Heracles. He thought the claim was fraudulent, since their inscriptions mentioned only the cost of making them.","Melqart","en/d/melkart.html"],["A ship that fits in a pocket","According to several sources, the ship can be folded like cloth and kept in a pocket when it is not needed at sea.","Skíðblaðnir","en/d/skiblanir.html"],["A shared name","Rudraige was also the name of a son of Partholón, who led the first settlement of Ireland after the Flood, and of a Milesian High King of the 2nd or 3rd century BC. All three are associated with Ulster.","Rudraige mac Dela","en/d/rudraige-mac-dela.html"],["Yılankale in Adana","In Adana, in southern Turkey, the Yılankale («Snake Castle») is known locally as the home of Shahmaran.","Shahmaran","en/d/shahmaran.html"],["A word named after her","Snorri claims that the Old Norse word <em>sjafni</em> derives from her name. It means «love» and appears in the <em>þulur</em> word lists.","Sjöfn","en/d/sjofn.html"],["A stone with meaning","In Greek metamorphosis myths, stone usually does not reflect a person&#x27;s character. In Arsinoe&#x27;s case, however, the petrification does express the coldness and cruelty she showed before being transformed.","Arsinoe","en/d/arsinoe-q704969.html"],["Retreat at Pharping","According to Tibetan tradition, Padmasambhava, Vimalamitra and Śīlamañju systematized the <em>kīla</em> lore during a retreat at Yang-le-shod, today Pharping in Nepal.","Vajrakilaya","en/d/vajrakilaya.html"],["A false coin","In the Kuroe legend, the glint that lures the victim looks like a koban coin, which is why people reach out to touch it.","Tsurube-otoshi","en/d/tsurube-otoshi.html"],["A plant of her own","Meadowsweet, also called queen-of-the-meadow, is said to be her plant. It is one of the few concrete associations the popular tradition records for her.","Áine","en/d/aine.html"],["Two faces on one instrument","Some sistra showed the goddess on both sides of the instrument, with the rattles placed between her horns.","Bat","en/d/bat.html"],["Possible link with Tabiti","Her name, meaning something like &quot;the burning one&quot;, may be related to that of Tabiti, queen of the Scythian gods. Some scholars suggest that an early dominant fire goddess existed in ancient Proto-Indo-Iranian religion.","Tapati","en/d/tapati.html"],["Meaning of Shubhankari","Her epithet Shubhankari means &quot;the doer of auspicious deeds&quot;. It reflects her role as a benevolent mother to those who honour her.","Bhairavi","en/d/bhairavi.html"],["A name with many spellings","His name appears in several cuneiform spellings, two of which are already attested in the Early Dynastic period.","Pabilsaĝ","en/d/pabilsag.html"],["An eclipse in the prophecy","Some scholars believe Theoclymenus&#x27; prophecy in the Odyssey may describe a real solar eclipse visible over the Ionian Islands in 1178 BC.","Theoclymenus","en/d/teoclimeno.html"],["Six-headed coins","Numerous Kushan and Yaudheya coins, sculptures and inscriptions, produced from 500 BCE to 1200 CE, show Shashthi with six heads, often on the reverse, with six-headed Skanda on the obverse.","Shashthi","en/d/shashthi.html"],["A shared name","Greek mythology has other characters called Menoetius or Menoetes, such as a keeper of Hades&#x27; cattle who wrestles with Heracles during his twelfth labor.","Menoetius","en/d/menecio.html"],["A mispronounced spell","According to the Brahmanas, the mistake in the incantation used to create Vritra is what allowed Indra to defeat the dragon.","Tvashtr","en/d/tuastri.html"],["A dangerous beauty","According to the tales, her form was so beautiful that it robbed men of two-thirds of their valour when they saw her.","Medb","en/d/medb.html"],["Fist amulet","Fist-and-phallus amulets, widespread among soldiers, often show a hand making the figa, a sign of good luck. The largest known collection comes from Camulodunum.","Fascinus","en/d/fascino.html"],["A story that repeats","In the Aeneid, Virgil tells an almost identical ship-burning in Sicily, but with other protagonists, such as Beroe and Pyrgo.","Aethilla","en/d/etilla.html"],["A disputed name","Because the Etruscan alphabet had no letter G, the name Tages may be a Latinised form. Massimo Pallottino proposed reading the mirror inscription as <em>pava Tarchies</em>, &#x27;the child Tarchies&#x27;.","Tages","en/d/tages.html"],["The Quirinal Palace","The palace Pope Gregory XIII commissioned on the hill later took the name Quirinal Palace. Centuries later it housed the Italian monarchy, and today it is the residence of the President of the Republic.","Quirinus","en/d/quirino.html"],["Isla Mujeres","A small island north of Cozumel was named for the idols of goddesses such as Ixchel that the conquistadors found there.","Ixchel","en/d/ixchel.html"],["A name with many spellings","In Britain the name is written in several forms, such as Mogons, Moguns or Mogontis, and also without the g as Mounus.","Mogons","en/d/mogons.html"],["A name hard to read","According to Aleksander Brückner, the name Podaga cannot be read with certainty as Helmold wrote it.","Podaga","en/d/podaga.html"],["An error copied for centuries","Herberstein&#x27;s reading was repeated by Stryjkowski in 1582 and later by other authors, without being checked.","Uslad","en/d/uslad.html"],["An uncertain name","Medieval French romances record many different spellings of the name, and its origin remains unclear.","Camelot","en/d/camelot.html"],["An asteroid named for her","32 Pomona is a main-belt asteroid discovered in 1854.","Pomona","en/d/pomona.html"],["One sun too many","According to the story, all ten suns came out on the same day and the world began to burn until Houyi shot down nine of them.","Xihe","en/d/xihe.html"],["Three buddhas on the altar","In Chinese temples of the Pure Land school, the Medicine Buddha sits on the main altar alongside Shakyamuni and Amitabha, placed to the right of the group.","Bhaisajyaguru","en/d/bhaisajyaguru.html"],["Phaia, the sow and the robber","Plutarch relates that the sow was really a woman named Phaia, a thief and criminal who was nicknamed after the animal because of her character and way of life.","Crommyonian Sow","en/d/cerda-de-cromion.html"],["A name with two readings","The name is linked to the Latin <em>furfur</em>, &#x27;bran&#x27;, although some connect it to <em>furcifer</em>, an insult meaning &#x27;scoundrel&#x27;.","Furfur","en/d/furfur.html"],["The nine days","Folklore links the days from March 1 to March 9 with the nine coats Dochia sheds. The weather on those days was said to foretell how women would fare in old age.","Baba Dochia","en/d/baba-dochia.html"],["Stones from saliva","According to some traditions, the balaur&#x27;s saliva can form precious stones, a belief that Mircea Eliade links to cultures from England to China.","Balaur","en/d/balaur.html"],["A crown among the stars","According to the myth, Dionysus threw Ariadne&#x27;s wedding crown into the sky, where it became the constellation Corona Borealis.","Ariadne","en/d/ariadna.html"],["A tapestry of disguises","Ovid tells that Arachne wove into her tapestry Apollo&#x27;s disguise as a shepherd, alongside other disguises used by Zeus, Poseidon and Dionysus to seduce mortal women and nymphs.","Amphissa","en/d/anfisa.html"],["A name with an Egyptian echo","Some sources suggest his name derives from the Egyptian Prouti, and Diodorus notes that the Egyptians called him Cetes.","Proteus","en/d/proteo.html"],["A game with lost rules","A game called mehen existed, but nobody knows for certain how it was played. The rules used today are modern hypotheses based on surviving boards.","Mehen","en/d/mehen.html"],["A log from heaven","Along with the thunderbolt that struck Semele&#x27;s bridal chamber, a log is said to have fallen from the sky. Polydorus decorated it with bronze and named it Cadmeus.","Polydorus","en/d/polidoro-q1061554.html"],["A claimed ancestor","In folk tales and medieval literature, the Lusignan, Anjou and Plantagenet houses, along with the Limburg-Luxemburg dynasty, were said to descend from Melusine.","Melusine","en/d/melusina.html"],["A name with meaning","Aceso derives from a Greek verb meaning to cure an illness or ailment. Her name is therefore a description of her role.","Aceso","en/d/aceso.html"],["An easily confused name","In Lithuanian mythology Laima is often confused with Laimė, good fortune, and with Laumė, a fairy.","Laima","en/d/laima.html"],["Protector of children","His daughter Carna protected children&#x27;s intestines from striges, beings that Roman tradition associated with vampires.","Helernus","en/d/helernus.html"],["A river that made soldiers forget","According to Strabo, the Lima was known as the River Lethe. In 138 BC, the Roman general Decimus Junius Brutus crossed it and called his soldiers by name, one by one, to show that the myth was false.","Lethe","en/d/lete.html"],["Appearance in a video game","Nigihayahi appears in the video game Ghost of Tsushima, which draws on Japanese tradition.","Nigihayahi","en/d/nigihayahi.html"],["Two names","Besides Cleopatra, she was also called Alcyone, a name recalling the abduction of her mother Marpessa by Apollo.","Cleopatra","en/d/cleopatra-q1774178.html"],["No image","He was never represented with images or idols. His name was written on a tablet that the emperor carried to the Temple of Heaven.","Yuanshi Tianzun","en/d/yuanshi-tianzun.html"],["Origin of the name Alyssa","The feminine name Alyssa derives from Lyssa through the alyssum flower, which was named after rabies because its seeds were used, unsuccessfully, to treat the disease.","Lyssa","en/d/lisa.html"],["Children born from her body","According to tradition, several of Haumea&#x27;s children were born from different parts of her body, and only Pele was born in the normal way.","Haumea","en/d/haumea.html"],["A goddess compared to Hygieia","Some scholars propose that Eir is the Norse counterpart of the Greek goddess Hygieia, associated with health.","Eir","en/d/eir.html"],["A name with meaning","According to the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad, the name Rudra means &#x27;those who make cry&#x27;, because when they leave the body they cause death.","Rudras","en/d/rudras.html"],["An island with four faces","According to the Kojiki, the island of Iyo-no-futana-no-shima had four faces, and one of them was Ōgetsuhime.","Ōgetsuhime","en/d/ogetsuhime.html"],["Death tax","According to tradition, Libitina&#x27;s temple collected a coin for every death, a kind of funeral tax.","Libitina","en/d/libitina.html"],["A phoenix in Rome","Pliny says a specimen supposedly captured in Egypt was exhibited in the Comitium in Rome, but he himself considered it a fabrication.","Phoenix","en/d/fenix.html"],["Yellow and Cancer","The Agni Purana associates Dhata with the colour yellow and the zodiac sign of Cancer.","Dhata","en/d/dhata.html"],["Origin of the name","The name Prosymna derives from the Greek <em>prosymneô</em>, meaning &#x27;celebrate in song&#x27;. The nymph&#x27;s name thus carries a sense of musical praise.","Prosymna","en/d/prosimna.html"],["An unexpected birth","According to the Adi Parva, Drona, who later became teacher of the Pandavas and the Kauravas, was born from the involuntary union of the sage Bharadvaja with Ghritachi.","Ghritachi","en/d/ghritachi.html"],["Named after a boat","The island took its name from the buss, a type of small vessel used by its supposed discoverers.","Buss Island","en/d/isla-buss.html"],["A sun on a coffin","The earliest known winged sun appears on the coffin of Queen Hetepheres I, of the Fourth Dynasty, placed above the cartouche of Sneferu.","Winged sun","en/d/sol-alado.html"],["A temporary sex exchange","According to the story, the yaksha Stunakarna agreed to swap sexes with Śikhaṇḍī for a period, and Kubera&#x27;s curse made the change permanent.","Śikhaṇḍī","en/d/sikhandi.html"],["Two genealogies","Apollodorus presents him as a son of Cadmus and Harmonia, while Appian makes him a son of Polyphemus and Galatea, with brothers who gave their names to the Celts and the Galatians.","Illyrius","en/d/ilirio.html"],["A shared name","The name Tmolus belongs to several figures in Greek mythology, including a king of Lydia, a god of the mountain of the same name, and a son of Proteus.","Tmolus","en/d/tmolo.html"],["A moon of Jupiter","Jupiter&#x27;s irregular moon Harpalyke (Jupiter XXII) takes its name from this figure.","Harpalyce","en/d/harpalice-q7289051.html"],["Gatekeeper of Hades","One of the epithets of the god Hades was &#x27;gate fastener&#x27;, and the underworld was famous for its gates.","Greek underworld","en/d/inframundo-griego.html"],["The brothers&#x27; names","The names Morpheus and Phobetor first appear in Ovid, and some scholars think they may have been his own inventions.","Phantasos","en/d/fantaso.html"],["The horse tamer","Hector was known by the epithet <em>hippodamoio</em>, meaning «horse tamer».","Hector","en/d/hector.html"],["From goddess to word","The English word “hygiene” comes directly from the Greek name of the goddess, <em>hygieia</em>, meaning “health”.","Hygieia","en/d/higia.html"],["Singing swans","Philostratus the Elder says that after Phaethon fell from the sun-chariot, swans would sing the tale to Caystrus and Ister.","Caystrus","en/d/caistro.html"],["His driver","Leigong rides a chariot driven by a young boy named A Xiang.","Leigong","en/d/leigong.html"],["A reserved chariot","Camillus was sent into exile after driving a chariot drawn by four white horses, an honour reserved for Jupiter himself.","Jupiter","en/d/jupiter.html"],["The name of Rhodes","According to tradition, the island took its name from Rhodos, the daughter of Poseidon and Halia.","Halia","en/d/halia.html"],["A divine haircut","Loki cut off the golden hair of Sif, the wife of Thor, and had the Sons of Ivaldi make her a replacement. The text links these craftsmen to the black elves of this world.","Svartálfaheimr","en/d/svartalfaheimr.html"],["Colossal head monument","The name Coyolxauhqui-Chantico is given to a huge head found at the Templo Mayor, linking the goddess to that sculpture.","Chantico","en/d/chantico.html"],["Forms of depiction","Hu was shown as a man, as a hawk, or as a man with a ram&#x27;s head.","Hu","en/d/hu.html"],["A muse with a shepherd&#x27;s staff","Besides the comic mask, she is sometimes shown with a shepherd&#x27;s staff, a detail that links her to pastoral poetry.","Thalia","en/d/talia.html"],["A treasury at Delphi","The frieze of the Siphnian Treasury shows the Gigantomachy and is one of the best surviving examples of Archaic Greek art.","Asterius","en/d/asterion-q10420712.html"],["One woman, several names","Some accounts call Dardanus&#x27;s wife Arisbe, daughter of Teucer, and most writers consider her the same person as Batea.","Batea","en/d/batia.html"],["A very early inscription","The earliest known inscription mentioning Aglibol dates to around 17 BCE and links him with Malakbel.","Aglibol","en/d/aglibol.html"],["A region named after a woman","According to the myth, the region of Messenia was named after Messene following the conquest she carried out with her husband.","Messene","en/d/mesene.html"],["Flaming arrow","The arrow that burns in the air is read as a sign from Jupiter favouring Acestes, who is honoured by the prodigy during the games.","Acestes","en/d/acestes.html"],["Grandson of a river god","His mother, Callirrhoe, was the daughter of the river god Achelous, one of the river deities of Greek mythology.","Amphoterus","en/d/anfotero.html"],["Names that change","Ancient authors disagree on how many Hesperides there were: Apollonius gives three, Apollodorus four and Diodorus seven.","Lipara","en/d/lipara.html"],["An unusually hard skull","Hymir&#x27;s skull was so tough that the magic cup shattered when it struck him. That scene decides whether he hands over the cauldron.","Hymir","en/d/hymir.html"],["A town that never existed","Accounts of the ape-voiced amabiko place their appearance in Mana-kōri, a county of Higo that did not exist. Several locations in this group turn out to be fictitious place names.","Amabie","en/d/amabie.html"],["Ancestor of the Tyrians","Drosera and her two companions are presented as ancestors of the Tyrian people, the lineage to which Dionysos&#x27; ancestors belong.","Drosera","en/d/drosera.html"],["A god who sustains the others","In the Brihadaranyaka story, the other deities can function only while Vayu empowers them, which is why he is seen as the breath that keeps life going.","Vayu","en/d/vaiu.html"],["A god who lived three times","Tradition gives him three life stages, each with its own epithet, from childhood to death.","Maris","en/d/maris.html"],["A god in the form of a serpent","In some texts Atum appears as a serpent, the form he will return to at the end of the creative cycle and which, according to the Book of the Dead, he took when emerging from the waters.","Atum","en/d/atum.html"],["Sacred fees","A cult song describes her in erotic terms and states that she was believed to charge fees for sexual services.","Nanaya","en/d/nanaya.html"],["A wheel with four stages","The medieval Wheel of Fortune showed four figures labelled &quot;I shall reign&quot;, &quot;I reign&quot;, &quot;I have reigned&quot; and &quot;I have no kingdom&quot;, representing rise and fall.","Fortuna","en/d/fortuna.html"],["A fountain named after a monster","According to the myth, the fountain that sprang up where Sybaris fell bears the same name as the creature Eurybarus defeated.","Eurybarus","en/d/eurybarus.html"],["The name of the archipelago","According to some accounts the Aeolian Islands took their name from this figure, and they still bear it today.","Aeolus","en/d/eolo-q10396307.html"],["A woman with two fates","According to Pausanias, Laodice was not swallowed by the earth; instead she is depicted among the captive Trojan women in the Lesche at Delphi.","Laodice","en/d/laodice.html"],["An owl for mockery","According to the myth, Meropis was turned into an owl for ridiculing the name of Athena, the goddess who punished her.","Agron","en/d/agron-q397395.html"],["A name with many spellings","Her name appears in different cuneiform spellings, from Ebla to Ugarit.","Išḫara","en/d/ishara.html"],["Earth goddess","The Rigveda mentions an ancient goddess Sita, associated with fertile fields and good harvests. The Sita of the Ramayana may have taken her name from this earlier figure.","Sita","en/d/sita.html"],["Two honorific names","The sources give her the epithets Narendraputri, &quot;daughter of a king&quot;, and Kapindraputra, &quot;daughter of the king of the apes&quot;.","Jambavati","en/d/jambavati.html"],["A home in Japan","According to tradition, Kannon&#x27;s Japanese abode is Fudarakusan-ji, while in Tibet it is the Potala Palace.","Kannon","en/d/kannon.html"],["A mortal substitute","In the Rigveda, Saranyu&#x27;s substitute is called Savarna and is mortal, unlike the Puranic versions in which she is a mere shadow.","Chhaya","en/d/chhaya.html"],["The Golden Horn","According to some versions, the inlet that borders the city was named Khrysokeras in honour of Ceroessa, the mother of Byzas.","Byzas","en/d/bizas.html"],["Measure more than justice","On coins, the balance scale was more often a symbol of honest measure to the Romans than of justice.","Aequitas","en/d/aequitas.html"],["Ox head","His name gozu means &quot;ox-head&quot;, and his iconography shows him with an ox&#x27;s head.","Gozu Tennō","en/d/gozu-tenno.html"],["Black hen","When a new banya was raised, a black hen was suffocated, left unplucked and buried under the threshold as an offering to appease the spirit.","Bannik","en/d/bannik.html"],["Venus","Hirata Atsutane linked this god to the planet Venus, based on his reading of one part of the name.","Amatsu-Mikaboshi","en/d/amatsu-mikaboshi.html"],["A name that passed to an island","The island of Leucas, in the Ionian Sea, took its name from Leucadius, according to the tradition recorded in the sources.","Leucadius","en/d/leucadio.html"],["A calf with exact markings","To be recognised as Apis, the animal had to have a white triangle on its forehead and a crescent on its right flank, along with other markings.","Apis","en/d/apis.html"],["A hummingbird in his name","His name is usually read as hummingbird and left side or south, though some scholars propose a different translation.","Huitzilopochtli","en/d/huitzilopochtli.html"],["A disputed word","The word zimat in the 1722 French text is so hard to translate that some scholars suggest ponie meant horse dung, while others read it as Lapland sesame.","Hand of Glory","en/d/mano-de-gloria.html"],["Triform Hecate","At first Hecate was shown only in single form. She became triform in the late 5th century BC, although all three forms were usually shown at the same age.","Triple Goddess","en/d/triple-diosa.html"],["Very different dates","Keating places his reign in 64–63 BC, while the Four Masters date it to 111–110 BC.","Nuadu Necht","en/d/nuadu-necht.html"],["A lost original","Leonardo da Vinci&#x27;s 1508 version was lost, possibly deliberately destroyed, and is known only through copies.","Leda and the Swan","en/d/leda-y-el-cisne.html"],["A green goddess","Meditation descriptions paint her as emerald green or, in some texts, blue. Green is linked to deep knowledge.","Matangi","en/d/matangi.html"],["A boat that drifts downriver","In the Epic of Ziusudra, the boat does not come to rest on a mountain but floats down the Euphrates into the Persian Gulf.","Eridu Genesis","en/d/genesis-de-eridu.html"],["A notorious feast","The phrase &quot;Thyestean feast&quot; describes a meal at which human flesh is served, in direct reference to this myth.","Thyestes","en/d/tiestes.html"],["A goddess of many names","Roman sources linked her with Ceres, Diana, Nike, Minerva, Bellona, Venus and Victoria, which makes it hard to isolate her own identity.","Vacuna","en/d/vacuna.html"],["A broken wing","In the Epic of Gilgamesh, Dumuzid is pictured as a bird with a broken wing lamenting in the forest.","Dumuzid the Shepherd","en/d/dumuzid-el-pastor.html"],["Pillars of Heracles","According to one tradition, the famous Pillars of Heracles belonged to Aegaeon, placing him at the edge of the known world.","Aegaeon","en/d/egeon.html"],["A Mycenaean tablet","The name of mint is written in Linear B on a Bronze Age tablet, which shows that the word is very old.","Minthe","en/d/mente.html"],["Popular name","Children call him Heavenly Grandfather (Tiān Gōng), a name that originally meant &quot;Heavenly Duke&quot;.","Jade Emperor","en/d/emperador-de-jade.html"],["The best throw of the dice","In the dice game played with knucklebones, the luckiest roll possible was called Venus, a popular pastime among Romans of all classes.","Venus","en/d/venus.html"],["A name that describes weeping","Her name can be translated as &quot;weeping, wailing woman,&quot; a direct expression of her origin in Izanagi&#x27;s tears.","Nakisawame","en/d/nakisawame.html"],["Heads on necklaces","Large numbers of Pazuzu heads, mostly in terracotta, have been found with holes or loops so they could be hung from the neck.","Pazuzu","en/d/pazuzu.html"],["A name with two meanings","One etymology splits «bhadra» into «bha» and «dra», which can mean «delusion» and «the greatest». The name could thus be read as «great delusion».","Bhadrakali","en/d/bhadrakali.html"],["Possible origin of the name","It has been proposed that Tethra derives from a Proto-Indo-European root meaning &#x27;quacking sound&#x27;. In Old Irish the word means both sea and the scald-crow Badb.","Tethra","en/d/tethra.html"],["A fossil named after it","In 2022 a prehistoric giraffe-like artiodactyl from early Miocene China was named Discokeryx xiezhi, after a single bony plate on its skull that resembled the mythical creature&#x27;s horn.","Xiezhi","en/d/xiezhi.html"],["A celestial elephant","Airāvata, Śakra’s elephant mount, is among its residents. Pārileyyaka, an elephant that was reborn in the heaven after serving the Buddha, is also listed.","Trāyastriṃśa","en/d/trayastrimsa.html"],["A guiding star","Polaris, the brightest star of the Little Dipper, lies near the north celestial pole and was for centuries a reference for navigation.","Cynosura","en/d/cinosura.html"],["A disputed founding","Although Apollodorus credits Amphilochus with founding Amphilochian Argos, the city is usually attributed to his uncle, who bore the same name.","Amphilochus","en/d/anfiloco.html"],["Awards named after her","The Clio Awards for advertising take the muse&#x27;s name, honouring creative excellence.","Clio","en/d/clio.html"],["A two-faced god","He is usually shown with two faces, a beard and wings, and his usual attributes are a fish, a utensil and a rooster.","Bangpūtys","en/d/bangputys.html"],["A sea named after an island","Some researchers believe the Baltic Sea may have taken its name from this legendary island, although the hypothesis is not confirmed.","Baltia","en/d/baltia.html"],["A white rock","Spider Rock is a monolith in Canyon de Chelly. According to Navajo myth, its white color comes from bones left in the sun.","Spider Grandmother","en/d/abuela-arana.html"],["A name with a badger","One interpretation of his name links it to the badger, an animal whose secretion was used in Gaulish medicines.","Moritasgus","en/d/moritasgus.html"],["Origin of the name","One possible explanation is that Asgartha derives from Asgarth, another spelling of Asgard, the home of the Norse gods. The letter a was probably added to make the name sound closer to Sanskrit.","Agartha","en/d/agartha.html"],["A name with several forms","The god appears written as Abelio, Abellio or Abello, and in some sources as Abelionni.","Abellio","en/d/abelio.html"],["The oldest king of Greece","Pliny called him the oldest king of Greece, and the epic poem Phoronis called him &quot;the first of humans&quot;.","Phoroneus","en/d/foroneo.html"],["Meliboea turned Chloris","According to Pausanias, Meliboea was renamed Chloris, meaning “pale green”, because fear left her face ashen.","Niobe","en/d/niobe.html"],["Gold uraeus of Senusret II","In 1919 a solid gold uraeus of pharaoh Senusret II was found at Saqqara, with granite eyes and a lapis lazuli head. Before Tutankhamun&#x27;s tomb was discovered, it was the only such ornament known from an entombed pharaoh.","Uraeus","en/d/ureo.html"],["Many mothers","In one version, the sons of Aegyptus do not have a single mother but several, among them a naiad named Caliadne.","Eurryroe","en/d/eurriroe.html"],["A homophone princess","In a Breton legend, Princess Dahut of Ys is sometimes linked to the animal because their names sound alike, although she is a different figure.","Dahu","en/d/dahu.html"],["A name with symbolism","The name is often interpreted as a reference to writing and books, and the colours black and red are understood as symbols of wisdom.","Tlillan-Tlapallan","en/d/tlillan-tlapallan.html"],["A river of blood","In the Divine Comedy Phlegethon is not made of fire but of boiling blood, in the Seventh Circle of Hell.","Phlegethon","en/d/flegetonte.html"],["Confusion with medicine","Although the caduceus symbolises commerce, some countries use it as a medical emblem. The cause is its resemblance to the rod of Asclepius, which has a single snake and no wings.","Caduceus","en/d/caduceo.html"],["A prophecy at the origin","The oracle Acrisius feared foretold that his grandson would kill him, and that prediction guided all the king&#x27;s actions.","Danaë","en/d/danae.html"],["Founding brothers","Each of Dardanus&#x27;s brothers appears as the founder of an Illyrian people, such as the Enchelei, the Autariatae or the Taulantii.","Dardanus","en/d/dardano.html"],["A name with a Greek echo","Its resemblance to Horkos, the Greek god of oaths, led ancient writers to describe Orcus as a punisher of perjurers.","Orcus","en/d/orco.html"],["The tiger mount","In a popular legend, Gô͘ Tho healed a tiger with a bone stuck in its throat, and the grateful animal let him ride it. For this reason many of his temples feature tiger figures.","Baosheng Dadi","en/d/baosheng-dadi.html"],["A goddess even Zeus would not cross","In the Iliad, Hypnos escapes Zeus&#x27;s wrath by taking refuge with Nyx, since the king of the gods feared displeasing her.","Nyx","en/d/nix.html"],["A god who does not answer the summons","While the other Japanese gods gather at the Grand Shrine of Izumo in the tenth month, Ebisu does not respond to the call and stays with his worshippers.","Ebisu","en/d/ebisu.html"],["Two possible fathers for Iphitus","Hyginus records that Iphitus may have been a son of Naubolus, born in Phocis, or a son of Hypasus, a native of the Peloponnese.","Naubolus","en/d/naubolo.html"],["The Sun before dawn","In Vedic tradition, the Sun is called Savitr before rising and Sūrya from sunrise until sunset.","Savitr","en/d/savitr.html"],["A coin from Locri","A silver coin from around 275 BC, minted at Locri, shows a seated woman with a shield and weapons labelled Roma.","Roma","en/d/roma.html"],["Another name","In English her name is sometimes anglicised as Grania.","Gráinne","en/d/grainne.html"],["Laurel prize","Winners of the Pythian Games received a laurel wreath, and from this tradition came the custom of crowning poets and musicians with laurel.","Daphne","en/d/dafne.html"],["A shifting name","Over time, Itylus became Itys, and the story changed into a deliberate murder.","Itylus","en/d/itilo.html"],["Goddess of lineages","She is the kuldevi of communities such as the Rajputs and the Prajapati, that is, a protector of particular families and clans.","Brahmani","en/d/brahmani.html"],["Banyan tree","In Thailand his name, Chao Pho Ton Sai, means &#x27;lord of the banyan tree&#x27;.","Chun Kwan","en/d/chun-kwan.html"],["An Attic deme with a heroic name","The deme of the Paeonidae in Attica is said by Pausanias to have taken its name from this figure.","Paeon","en/d/paeon.html"],["A freed slave&#x27;s cap","The pileus was a felt cap that slaves received on becoming free, after their heads had been shaved. That is why it became an emblem of liberty.","Libertas","en/d/libertas.html"],["An inscription in Trier","On the Rotes Haus, in the central market square of Trier, an inscription commemorating the city&#x27;s founding by Trebeta has been preserved.","Trebeta","en/d/trebeta.html"],["Arrival at Wexford","Tradition places his landing at Wexford Harbour, at the mouth of the River Slaney in the southeast of the island.","Sláine mac Dela","en/d/slaine-mac-dela.html"],["A marble temple with 52 devakulikas","The Hutheesing Jain Temple in Ahmedabad is a two-storied white marble building known for its intricate stone carvings. It contains 52 devakulikas, small shrines dedicated to tirthankaras.","Dharmanatha","en/d/dharmanatha.html"],["Two Dardanuses","The Scythian king is sometimes confused with the Dardanus who was the son of Zeus and Electra and ancestor of the Trojans. They are different figures who share a name.","Dardanus","en/d/dardano-q58299980.html"],["Four heads","He was depicted with four ram heads, one for each of the four Bas of the sun god.","Banebdjedet","en/d/banebdyedet.html"],["Tears of resin","In the myth, the aromatic resin of the myrrh tree is identified with Myrrha&#x27;s tears.","Myrrha","en/d/mirra.html"],["A daughter turned into merchandise","Mestra could take different shapes thanks to a gift from Poseidon, which allowed her father to sell her again and again.","Erysichthon","en/d/erisicton.html"],["An arrow to the heel","According to one tradition, Poeas hit the heel of Talos, the bronze giant, with an arrow. The feat is more often credited to Medea, who was said to have drugged the giant.","Poeas","en/d/peante.html"],["Other names in the painting","Polygnotus painted the myth with the maidens named Cameiro and Clytie. They are shown crowned with flowers and playing dice.","Cleothera","en/d/cleotera.html"],["A dinosaur with his name","An extinct basal neornithischian dinosaur from Mongolia was named Haya griva, referring to its elongated, horse-like skull and the deity&#x27;s presence in Mongolian Buddhist art.","Hayagriva","en/d/hayagriva.html"],["A name with many spellings","The name appears in many spellings, such as Chinigchinix or Quaoar, and its exact pronunciation is not known for certain.","Chinigchinix","en/d/chingichngish.html"],["Swans of the well","According to the Prose Edda, two swans feed from the Well of Urd, and all other swans are said to descend from them.","Well of Urd","en/d/pozo-de-urd.html"],["A three-night festival","In Lyon a major three-night festival honoured Lugh. According to some studies, it was replaced by the festival of Saint Laurence, held from 10 August.","Lugh (Lugus)","en/d/lug-lugh-lugus.html"],["A region named after a wife","Pausanias explains that the territory where Perieres settled Melaneus was named Oechalia, said to derive from the hero&#x27;s wife.","Melaneus","en/d/melaneo.html"],["Ship&#x27;s figurehead","Sidonian coins show her standing on the prow of a galley, an image often seen as the precursor of ships&#x27; figureheads.","Astarte","en/d/astarte.html"],["Meaning of the name","The Old Norse name hróðr has been translated as &quot;glorious&quot; or &quot;famed&quot;.","Hróðr","en/d/hror.html"],["Russian Atlantis","Lake Svetloyar is sometimes called the Russian Atlantis because of the legends about the sunken city.","Kitezh","en/d/kitezh.html"],["Day of the week","The name of Odin lies behind Wednesday in English, meaning &#x27;day of Wōden&#x27;.","Odin","en/d/odin.html"],["Healing pools","According to Safaitic texts, Baalshamin was believed to give healing properties to the rainwater pools that formed.","Baalshamin","en/d/baalshamin.html"],["A treaty on ox hide","The agreement between Rome and Gabii was written on the hide of the ox sacrificed to the god and kept in his temple, perhaps the first international treaty recorded in writing in Rome.","Sancus","en/d/sancus.html"],["A broken statue","In 2016 a statue of the Taka-Onna in a park in Gobō was severed at its base; its torso was found at the bottom of the harbour and raised again. A high school student was charged after kicking it.","Taka-Onna","en/d/taka-onna.html"],["Alternative name","Helanicus of Mytilene calls him Polyxenus and considers him the son of Jason and Medea.","Medus","en/d/medo.html"],["A name that spread","According to the stories, Apis&#x27;s name was applied to the whole territory south of the Isthmus, a region far larger than his kingdom of Sicyon.","Apis","en/d/apis-q38366.html"],["A white tiger at court","In 1404 a captured animal was sent to Emperor Yongle and taken for a rare white tiger, a sign of good rule.","Zouyu","en/d/zouyu.html"],["Owls in the Tonalamatl","In the Tonalamatl the pair was painted as owls, one red like the day and another black like the night.","Oxomoco","en/d/oxomoco.html"],["A crocodile-like mount","His vahana, or vehicle, is the makara, a creature shaped somewhat like a crocodile.","Varuna","en/d/varuna.html"],["Shag Islands","In British historiography, James Sheffield rediscovered them and named them the Shag Islands.","Aurora Islands","en/d/islas-aurora.html"],["Paired image","In many representations she appears paired with the yaksha Sarvanubhuti on her right, with a small Tirthankara image above them.","Ambika","en/d/ambika-q4741456.html"],["A nymph without a tongue","According to Ovid, Jupiter tore out her tongue as punishment for her indiscretion, which explains her name as &quot;the mute one&quot; and her link with silence.","Lara","en/d/lara.html"],["A daily verse","A savaiya and a dohra from the Ram Avatar Bani are part of the Rehras Sahib, the prayer that many devout Sikhs recite every day.","Rama","en/d/rama.html"],["A shared name","Another Iphitus, son of Eurytus, also appears in some lists of Argonauts. That is why the two characters should be told apart.","Iphitus","en/d/ifito.html"],["A dwarf planet","The dwarf planet 225088 is named Gonggong, linking the Chinese myth to modern astronomy.","Gonggong","en/d/gong-gong.html"],["A name for death","In Ugaritic, <em>mot</em> simply means &#x27;death&#x27;, and the same root appears in languages such as Arabic and Hebrew.","Mot","en/d/mot.html"],["A parasol instead of a weapon","According to the story, the weapon Mara hurled at Siddhartha turned into a beautiful parasol that shaded him.","Mara","en/d/mara.html"],["A fly in the forge","Loki turned into a fly to pester the brothers at work. Each time the sting landed on a different part of Brokkr, from his hand to his eyelids.","Sindri","en/d/sindri.html"],["A luminous name","Stilbon means «the shining, glittering one», an allusion to the light of the heavenly body.","Stilbon","en/d/stilbon.html"],["A humbling foot wash","In the Mahabharata, Virochana&#x27;s punishment is to wash Sudhanva&#x27;s feet in front of the princess Kesini, instead of losing his life.","Virochana","en/d/virochana.html"],["Chained statue","In Sparta there was a statue of Enyalius in fetters, since it was believed that this way the god would never leave the city.","Enyalius","en/d/enialio.html"],["Fragrant name","Surabhi means &#x27;the fragrant one&#x27;. Indologist Jacobi linked the name to the distinctive smell of cows.","Kāmadhenu (Surabhi)","en/d/kamadhenu-surabhi.html"],["A yōkai that reads minds","In the Ashū Kiji Zatsuwa, a yamachichi appears near woodcutters and reads their thoughts, until a open flame startles it and it runs away.","Yamajijii","en/d/yamajijii.html"],["A star named after her","In Mesopotamia, Lisin&#x27;s name was used for the star now identified with Antares, in the constellation of Scorpius.","Lisin","en/d/lisin.html"],["A name tied to trance","Her name seems linked to lovi, a state of ecstasy or trance, expressed in Finnish by the phrase to fall into lovi.","Louhi","en/d/louhi.html"],["Two names, two words","Fand and Fann are not phonetic variants but two different words with different meanings. Fann means &quot;weak, helpless person&quot;.","Fand","en/d/fand.html"],["A body ten cubits long","Pausanias claims that the body of Asterius, buried on an islet near Miletus, measured no less than ten cubits.","Anax","en/d/anax.html"],["A disputed lineage","Herodotus and Pausanias give him different fathers, showing that the genealogy of Spartan kings was not fixed in antiquity.","Eunomus of Sparta","en/d/eunomo-de-esparta.html"],["A river with his name","According to Lucian, the river Adonis, today the Nahr Ibrahim, turned red each year at Byblos. He also recorded a natural explanation based on red soil blown into the water by storms.","Adonis","en/d/adonis.html"],["Two names depending on the speaker","Humans call him Phobetor, while the gods call him Icelus or Icelos, according to Ovid.","Phobetor","en/d/fobetor.html"],["A head on top of another","In some statues of Benzaiten, a male figure of Ugajin is visible on her head, with a torii shrine gate beside it.","Ugajin","en/d/ugajin.html"],["A garment with a name of its own","The women&#x27;s garment Nisus instituted in honour of Abrota was called <em>aphabroma</em>, derived from her own name.","Abrota","en/d/abrota.html"],["A name meaning tongs","In Old Norse, the name means &quot;iron grippers&quot;, while Járnglófar means &quot;iron gauntlets&quot;.","Járngreipr","en/d/jarngreipr.html"],["Humble offerings","Santoshi Mata receives raw sugar and roasted chickpeas, foods associated with ordinary people, which helped make her popular among the masses.","Santoshi Mata","en/d/santoshi-mata.html"],["A famous prisoner","The Welsh Triads name Llŷr one of the Three Exalted Prisoners of Britain for his captivity at Euroswydd&#x27;s hands, probably a lost tradition about the birth of Penarddun&#x27;s younger sons.","Efnisien","en/d/efnisien.html"],["A confessed hoax","A man who claimed to have shot the creature later admitted the whole episode was invented.","Lizard Man of Scape Ore Swamp","en/d/hombre-lagarto-de-scape-ore-swamp.html"],["Jet amulet","The black jet hand was a widely used protective charm in Asturias since ancient times.","Guaxa","en/d/guaxa.html"],["Famous forgeries","In 1914 a Boston museum bought a figure with an ivory body and gold snakes that is now considered a fake. Another piece, at the Walters Art Museum in Baltimore, is also regarded as a forgery.","Snake Goddess","en/d/diosa-de-las-serpientes.html"],["A boar on a crest","The Campbell coat of arms shows a boar&#x27;s head, which is read as a nod to Diarmuid&#x27;s death.","Diarmuid Ua Duibhne","en/d/diarmuid-ua-duibhne.html"],["A goddess with two genders","In different contexts she is referred to as both male and female. In other representations she takes the form of a fox.","Uke Mochi","en/d/uke-mochi.html"],["Three deaths","The asuras killed Kacha three times: they fed him to wolves, pounded his body into paste mixed with seawater, and in the third attempt burned him and gave his ashes, mixed with wine, to Shukra to drink.","Devayani","en/d/devayani.html"],["Father Time","Because of the phonetic similarity with Chronos, meaning time, Cronus merged with the personification of time and, in the Renaissance, with the figure of Father Time armed with a scythe.","Cronus","en/d/crono.html"],["Luohan in a dream","Guanxiu explained that he had painted what he saw in dreams, so he called these figures &#x27;Luohan in a dream&#x27;.","Eighteen Arhats (Luohan)","en/d/dieciocho-arhats-luohan.html"],["A ring with a Scottish echo","Webster noted that the ring motif has a parallel in Conall Cra Bhuidhe, a Scottish Gaelic tale published by John Francis Campbell.","Tartalo","en/d/tartalo.html"],["A name meaning caution","In Old Norse, Andvari is linked to a word meaning careful or cautious, a trait that fits his role as guardian of the treasure.","Andvari","en/d/andvari.html"],["Roses of the equinox","Tradition says that on the night of the spring equinox anjanas dance hand in hand until dawn, scattering roses. Whoever finds one with purple, green, blue or golden petals will be happy until death.","Anjana","en/d/anjana-q3848557.html"],["Different fathers","In tradition Magnes has Zeus as father in the Catalogue of Women and Aeolus in the Bibliotheca, showing that no single version was fixed.","Magnes","en/d/magnes.html"],["A statue with a bow","A 5th-century bronze statue, Riace B, was identified by two researchers as a likeness of Eumolpus. The position of its fingers suggests it once held a bow and arrow.","Eumolpus","en/d/eumolpo.html"],["An alternative name","Besides Pyroeis, the god is also called Mesonyx, meaning &#x27;midnight&#x27;.","Pyroeis","en/d/pyroeis.html"],["A number that changes","The lists of deities vary between manuscripts and regions of South Asia, especially regarding the Ashvins and the personified devas.","Thirty-three gods","en/d/treinta-y-tres-dioses.html"],["The path of Aryaman","In the Rigveda, the Milky Way is called <em>aryamṇáḥ pánthāḥ</em>, the path of Aryaman. The Germanic peoples called it the path of Irmin.","Aryaman","en/d/aryaman.html"],["An island of ash","According to Herodotus, Anysis built an island in the marsh of Elbo by piling up ash and earth that his visitors brought along with his food.","Anysis","en/d/anisis.html"],["Two capitals","The Mahabharata names two capitals of Kalinga, Dantapura and Rajapura.","Kalinga","en/d/kalinga.html"],["Surtsey island","The volcanic island Surtsey rose in 1963 off Iceland&#x27;s south coast and takes its name from the giant.","Surtr","en/d/surt.html"],["Varied forms","Harsomtus appears as a child, as a mummy, as a man with a falcon or serpent head, and as a serpent rising from a lotus, an image seen in reliefs at the temple of Dendera.","Harsomtus","en/d/harsomtus.html"],["The Hyantes","The Hyantes were the former name of the Boeotians. According to tradition, they were driven out of Boeotia by Phoenicians led by Cadmus.","Hyas","en/d/hias.html"],["An apsara created on purpose","In the story, Brahma created Mohini with the explicit task of testing the king&#x27;s devotion.","Rukmāṅgada","en/d/rukmangada.html"],["Another name","In many manuscripts Brian appears as Uar or Uair, a name that modern editors have corrected to Brian.","Iucharba","en/d/iucharba.html"],["Another Greek name","In Greek he also appears as Eumelus, a name that English-language sources often give as Eumelus.","Gadirus","en/d/gadiro.html"],["A troll wife","In the þulur, a list of poetic names, Járnviðja appears in the singular as a troll wife.","Járnviðr","en/d/jarnvir.html"],["A late name","The English word hippogriff was adopted shortly before 1615, while in 1591 John Harington translated it as “Griffith horse”.","Hippogriff","en/d/hipogrifo.html"],["An asteroid named for her","Asteroid 146 Lucina, discovered in the 19th century, is named after the Roman goddess.","Lucina","en/d/lucina.html"],["People of the fire thieves","The Kaqchikel refused the human sacrifices Tohil demanded in exchange for fire, so they stole it. Their name means &quot;fire thieves&quot;.","Tohil","en/d/tohil.html"],["A valkyrie outside the lists","Sigrún appears in the poems Helgakviða Hundingsbana I and II, outside the usual lists of valkyrie names.","Reginleif","en/d/reginleif.html"],["An asteroid named for her","The main belt asteroid 438 Zeuxo is named after this Oceanid.","Zeuxo","en/d/zeuxo.html"],["A throne of justice","Pittheus was said to dispense justice seated on a marble throne, which was still shown at Troezen together with his tomb in late times.","Pittheus","en/d/piteo.html"],["A rite that included men","Unlike many marriage rites for women only, Viriplaca&#x27;s service required the participation of husbands, as did the Matronalia.","Viriplaca","en/d/viriplaca.html"],["From Dutch to English","The name Santa Claus derives phonetically from Sinterklaas, the Dutch Saint Nicholas brought to New Amsterdam by Dutch settlers.","Santa Claus","en/d/papa-noel.html"],["A name with an echo","Cornutus, playing with the etymology of his name, describes him as a &#x27;spokesman&#x27; and makes him responsible for animals producing sounds.","Iapetus","en/d/japeto.html"],["A gene with her name","Geneticist Manyuan Long named a fruit fly gene jgw after her, because like the princess it is &#x27;reborn&#x27; with a new function and structure.","Jingwei","en/d/jingwei.html"],["A brother in theyyam form","Her brother Poomaruthan is performed in the theyyam ritual, but the goddess herself has no theyyam form.","Poomala Bhagavathy","en/d/poomala-bhagavathy.html"],["Pilgrimage to Girnar","Mount Girnar, where he retreated, remains one of the main Jain pilgrimage destinations in India.","Neminatha","en/d/neminatha.html"],["A name with several meanings","Alp means «brave» or «warrior», Er means «man» or «soldier», and Tunga or Tonga means «Siberian tiger». The name thus condenses the character of the hero.","Alp Er Tunga","en/d/alp-er-tunga.html"],["A name with a double meaning","Gestumblindi can be read as gestr blindi, meaning both Gestr the Blind and blind guest. This double reading fits the one-eyed Odin who visits the worlds disguised as a stranger.","Gestumblindi","en/d/gestumblindi.html"],["A name rooted in orphanhood","Her name derives from <em>orbus</em>, meaning bereft, the same root as the Greek ὀρφανός, meaning orphan.","Orbona","en/d/orbona.html"],["Lydian origin","Parthenius attributes his version to Xanthus of Lydia, Neanthes of Cyzicus and Simmias of Rhodes, though he follows Xanthus most closely.","Assaon","en/d/ason.html"],["A disputed name","The name Gibil is the conventional reading of a cuneiform sign, but scholars have not fully confirmed it from primary sources.","Gibil","en/d/gibil.html"],["A name that walks","The root of Gangr is related to words such as Icelandic <em>gangur</em> or Norwegian <em>gang</em>, meaning walking or hallway. The same root appears in Gothic and Old English.","Gangr","en/d/gangr.html"],["A name like the word for milk","The goddess&#x27;s name resembles an Egyptian word for milk. The more common word for milk may also have an etymological connection to her name.","Iat","en/d/iat.html"],["A name linked to fire","Her name comes from the Russian word for &quot;bonfire&quot;, connecting the goddess with the fires of the celebrations.","Kostroma","en/d/kostroma.html"],["A dog with many versions","Orthrus appears with one, two or three heads, and even with a snake tail, depending on the author or the artwork.","Orthrus","en/d/ortro.html"],["Buried head","After the war with Ireland, Pryderi and his companions buried Bendigeidfrân&#x27;s head on the Gwynfryn, facing France to ward off invasion.","Pryderi","en/d/pryderi.html"],["Play on sounds","The word al, meaning hoe, is repeated dozens of times in the poem, and many verb forms start with or contain that syllable.","Song of the Hoe","en/d/canto-de-la-azada.html"],["Lover of Apollo","A tradition noted in the English sources presents him as a lover of the god Apollo.","Phorbas","en/d/forbante.html"],["A name from a sash","Her name comes from the shesmet, a sash decorated with beads, shells or small heads of Hathor, which may originally have been worn by unmarried young women.","Shesmetet","en/d/shesmetet.html"],["A name that means fear","The plural mormones is rendered as &quot;the fearful ones&quot; or &quot;the hideous ones&quot;, a direct reflection of the creature&#x27;s role.","Mormo","en/d/mormo.html"],["A name with variants","The underworld is written in several forms, such as Tam, tamuk, tamu or tamag, depending on the language and the people describing it.","Tam","en/d/tam.html"],["Fifty sisters","Ploto was one of the fifty Nereids, daughters of Nereus and Doris, which makes her family one of the largest among the Greek sea deities.","Ploto","en/d/ploto.html"],["An invented name","Lönnrot created the name Aino for the <em>Kalevala</em>. The original oral poems do not use it.","Aino","en/d/aino.html"],["A spaceship","In Becky Chambers&#x27; 2018 novel <em>Record of a Spaceborn Few</em>, one of the Exodan fleet&#x27;s ships is named Al-Qaum.","Al-Qaum","en/d/al-qaum.html"],["A warrior named Autesion","In the epic poem Dionysiaca, another warrior named Autesion is killed by Corymbasos, a chief of the Indians.","Autesion","en/d/autesion.html"],["Coins with her head","Some ancient coins from the city of Chalcis bore the head of the nymph.","Chalcis","en/d/calcis.html"],["An asteroid named after him","The near-Earth asteroid 4486 Mithra is named after him.","Mithra","en/d/mitra-q12614595.html"],["A lettuce as origin","The First Vatican Mythographer says Hera conceived Hebe after eating a lettuce, a version that contrasts with the usual account of her birth.","Hebe","en/d/hebe.html"],["Telling names","The names of the Sirens allude to song, their most characteristic trait.","Sterope","en/d/esterope-q2346159.html"],["A fire mountain","Some rationalising versions say the Chimera was a mountain with burning gas vents that sailors used as landmarks.","Chimera","en/d/quimera.html"],["The widow&#x27;s son","The Bible describes a Hiram whose mother was a widow from the tribe of Naphtali. For this reason Freemasons often call him &#x27;the widow&#x27;s son&#x27;.","Hiram Abiff","en/d/hiram-abif.html"],["A seafaring name","Its Old Norse name relates to the word for ship, and the second element, tún, means town or enclosure.","Nóatún","en/d/noatun.html"],["Telling time by flowers","A Chinese text known as the Sse-kiau-tsih states that the gods of this world tell time by the opening and closing of flowers.","Yāma","en/d/yama.html"],["Tiny windows","According to tradition, some Leicestershire cottages had very small windows so that only one arm could get in.","Black Annis","en/d/black-annis.html"],["Sign of Sagittarius","In the usual astrological order, the Sun enters Sagittarius between November 22 and December 21, and this is when Buer is said to appear.","Buer","en/d/buer.html"],["Peerless beard","According to the Records of the Three Kingdoms, Zhuge Liang described him as having a &quot;peerless beard&quot;. His traditional red face may derive from the novel or from opera, where red symbolized loyalty.","Guan Di","en/d/guan-di.html"],["A snail named after her","A freshwater mollusc, <em>Myrtoessa hyas</em>, belongs to the mud snail family and was named in honour of the nymph.","Myrtoessa","en/d/mirtoesa.html"],["Closing formula","Many Sumerian texts end with the formula &#x27;praise to Nisaba&#x27;, a sign of her status among scribes.","Nisaba","en/d/nisaba.html"],["A lost piece","The leather-and-iron belt that Anton Pachinger found in Linz in 1889 has been lost, along with most of his collection.","Chastity belt","en/d/cinturon-de-castidad.html"],["A pope and liberty of conscience","Gregory XVI drew on the image of the bottomless pit from Revelation to criticise the idea of liberty of conscience, which he considered an absurd and erroneous proposition.","Abyss","en/d/abismo.html"],["Heidegger and truth","The German philosopher Martin Heidegger argued that the Greek idea of truth, aletheia, means &quot;unconcealment&quot; and differs from the Roman veritas, which is linked to rightness.","Aletheia","en/d/aletheia.html"],["A spirit passed down","In southern Kyushu, according to tradition, possession by a yako could pass from parents to children.","Yako","en/d/yako.html"],["A pair of Graces","Auxo and Hegemone appear together in Athenian worship, honoured as two Graces since early times.","Auxo","en/d/auxo.html"],["A name that is a realm","Gaueko literally means “of the night” in Basque, and his refrain proclaims that the night belongs to those of the night.","Gaueko","en/d/gaueko.html"],["Not the Buddha","Budha should not be confused with the Buddha, the founder of Buddhism, who lived centuries later and belongs to a different tradition.","Budha","en/d/budha.html"],["Rivers in the shape of oxen","The people of Stymphalus were said to honour the rivers Metope and Erasinus in the form of oxen, an unusual image in Greek religion.","Metope","en/d/metope.html"],["Bronze helmet","Among the objects found with the altars was a thin bronze helmet in Greek style, left as a votive offering.","Anvallus","en/d/anvalo.html"],["A reinterpreted name","In later times his name was read as “He who has great strength”, which helped link him with Heracles.","Herishef","en/d/herishef.html"],["A plague as punishment","According to the story, Dionysus drove the unmarried women of Athens into madness, and they hanged themselves in imitation of Erigone.","Maera","en/d/mera.html"],["A divine messenger","Her name comes from the mission she carried out: Shiva, sent by the goddess, delivered the ultimatum to the asuras, and she became associated with that errand.","Shivaduti","en/d/shivaduti.html"],["A name with meaning","Þrívaldi means &quot;thrice mighty&quot; in Old Norse, a name that stresses the giant&#x27;s strength.","Þrívaldi","en/d/rivaldi.html"],["Many names","Besides Indrajit and Meghanada, the sources give him the epithets Shakrajit, Ravani, Vasavajit, Varidanada and Ghananada.","Indrajit","en/d/indrajit.html"],["Noah&#x27;s daughter","A later tradition holds that Astghik was a daughter of Noah and was born after the flood.","Astghik","en/d/astghik.html"],["From Greece to Rome","The Greek equivalent is Aletheia, a name meaning something like &quot;unconcealment.&quot;","Veritas","en/d/veritas.html"],["Hwanung in Korea","In Korea the title is used for Hwanung, the legendary founder of Gojoseon.","Heavenly King","en/d/rey-celestial.html"],["A name meaning vine","Ampelos comes from ancient Greek and means grapevine, a name that sums up his link with the plant.","Ampelos","en/d/ampelo.html"],["Unexpected help","Despite their dangerous reputation, they are said to put out fires in villages near the river by raising its water with their paddles.","Les Llavanderes","en/d/les-llavanderes.html"],["An invented king","Modern historians believe Soos never existed as a historical king and was added to the list in the 4th century BC.","Soos","en/d/soos.html"],["Two kinds of arrows","Cupid carries golden arrows, which inspire desire, and leaden ones, which cause aversion. They explain why love can be returned or can flee.","Cupid","en/d/cupido.html"],["God of the gazelle","In bilingual Phoenician-Hittite inscriptions he is called the deer god or gazelle god, an animal with which he was also depicted in Egypt.","Resheph","en/d/reshef.html"],["A name from a sound","The phrase &quot;Carcas sonne&quot; plays on the sound of the city&#x27;s name, and according to the legend Carcassonne takes its name from it.","Lady Carcas","en/d/dame-carcas.html"],["A name of Vishnu","Aniruddha, meaning &quot;unstoppable&quot; or &quot;ungovernable,&quot; is also one of the names of Vishnu.","Aniruddha","en/d/aniruddha.html"],["Sun-facing eyes","His name combines k&#x27;in, meaning both sun and day, with ich, meaning face or eyes, and ajaw, meaning lord. The sun glyph even appears inserted into his body.","K'inich Ahau","en/d/kinich-ahau.html"],["Two kinds of wealth gods","Tradition separates civil wealth gods, such as Bi Gan and Fan Li, shown in court robes, from military ones, such as Zhao Gongming and Guan Yu, shown in armour.","Caishen","en/d/caishen.html"],["A month named after a rite","The month of February takes its name from the <em>Februa</em>, purification festivals held around the 15th day of the month.","Februus","en/d/februus.html"],["A golden palm tree","His battle banner was a golden palm tree, an emblem that distinguished him on the field of Kurukshetra.","Bhishma","en/d/bhisma.html"],["An Avestan hymn","The account of his struggle was incorporated by Ferdowsi into the Shahnameh.","Tishtrya","en/d/tishtrya.html"],["A rooster in the legend","In the Kamakhya story, the goddess makes a temple rooster crow early so that Naraka believes dawn has come and abandons his work.","Narakasura","en/d/narakasura.html"],["A tree with her name","In Greek, the linden tree is called <em>philyra</em>, the same name as the nymph, which explains her final transformation.","Philyra","en/d/filira.html"],["Squirrel turned into a bear","In a tale from the Saru region, the creature believed to be a bear turned out to be a dead squirrel, showing that Kenas-unarpe could transform one animal into another.","Kenas-unarpe","en/d/kenas-unarpe.html"],["A baby in the bed","In the Highland tale, Garb&#x27;s mistress mistakes Cú Chulainn, disguised as a herdsman, for a small baby when he crawls into her bed.","Garb mac Stairn","en/d/garb-mac-stairn.html"],["A god born from a fisherman","According to Ovid, Glaucus was a mortal fisherman who became immortal after tasting a magical herb that revived fish.","Glaucus","en/d/glauco.html"],["Differing versions","The accounts disagree on the timing: some mention fifty days of hunting, others a single week of unions or just one night.","Aeschreis","en/d/aeschreis.html"],["Twice-born","The Greek epithet diphyes means &#x27;of two natures&#x27; and was read both as his hybrid body and as a double citizenship, Greek and barbarian.","Cecrops","en/d/cecrope.html"],["Son of the sea","The Sanas Cormaic glosses Manannán&#x27;s name as &quot;son of the sea&quot;, since his father Lir was identified with the ocean.","Lir","en/d/lir.html"],["A hunting companion","In the folklore of Pernambuco he is described riding a deer, accompanied by a dog named Papa-mel.","Curupira","en/d/curupira.html"],["The last to arrive","At the Teotleco feast, Huehueteotl is the last god to appear at the gathering, while Tezcatlipoca arrives first.","Huehueteotl","en/d/huehueteotl.html"],["Butterfly","According to Seto tradition, Peko&#x27;s spirit can fly over the fields as a butterfly.","Peko","en/d/peko.html"],["A city named after him","The geographer Pausanias mentions an ancient Cretan city, Catreus, which the Cretans believed had been founded by the king.","Catreus","en/d/catreo.html"],["A word for horse","The Palaeo-Balkan root of the name is reconstructed as <em>Me(n)zana-</em>, related to Albanian <em>mëz</em> (&#x27;foal&#x27;) and Romanian <em>mânz</em>.","Thracian horseman","en/d/jinete-tracio.html"],["A Charun with a name","The Tomb of the Charuns shows four Charuns, and each seems to have a sub-name, such as Chunchules or Lufe.","Charun","en/d/charun.html"],["Born armed","According to Nonnus, Damasen was born with a spear and received a shield from Eileithyia, goddess of childbirth.","Damasen","en/d/damasen.html"],["Name meaning whale","According to the sources, the Greek name Ceto is rendered as &quot;whale&quot;, suggesting a link with the sea.","Ceto","en/d/ceto-q5065673.html"],["A Buddha who appears as a disciple","According to some sutras, although he is a Buddha, he appears as a disciple of Shakyamuni so that the same world does not have two main Buddhas.","Manjushri","en/d/manjushri.html"],["A lake instead of a river","In Dante&#x27;s Divine Comedy, Cocytus is described as a frozen lake rather than a river, although its source is said to be the tears of a statue on Mount Ida.","Cocytus","en/d/cocito.html"],["Her name in metaphors","In skaldic poetry, &quot;board of Skögul&quot; referred to the shield, an example of how a valkyrie&#x27;s name could become a poetic image.","Skögul","en/d/skogul.html"],["An enigmatic name","The meaning of her name remains uncertain. Scholars have proposed ideas such as wife or bark, and some connect it to an old yew goddess.","Bestla","en/d/bestla.html"],["Over a thousand votive figures","At the Es Culleram sanctuary on Ibiza, worshippers left more than a thousand terracotta figurines, whole and broken, depicting the goddess.","Tanit","en/d/tanit.html"],["A multiplied form","The Maha Pratyangira form expands her image with multiple lion faces and numerous hands holding different weapons.","Pratyangira","en/d/pratyangira.html"],["Another name","Some translations of the Iliad give his name as Lycophontes, a variant that can confuse readers.","Polyphontes","en/d/polifonte-q2993251.html"],["A mound for an enemy","The surviving companions of Gudlög raised a mound over the place where he was hanged, according to Snorri&#x27;s account.","Jorund","en/d/jorund.html"],["Seven spots","In its traditional description, the toad often displays seven diamond-shaped spots on its back.","Chan Chu","en/d/chan-chu.html"],["One figure, several names","Depending on the telling, the same figure is called Ila as a man or Ilā as a woman, and in some versions is named Sudyumna.","Ila","en/d/ila.html"],["A possible high-quality beer","In the first millennium BC she may also have been known as Kurunnītu, a name derived from a type of beer regarded as particularly high quality.","Ninkasi","en/d/ninkasi.html"],["Stone statues","In Kagoshima and parts of Miyazaki, farmers set up stone statues of Tanokansaa, shaped like a Buddha, a Shinto priest or a peasant.","Ta-no-Kami","en/d/ta-no-kami.html"],["A recent discovery","In June 2024 a set of circular ruins with a labyrinthine layout was announced at Papoura, northwest of Kastelli (Heraklion), covering about 1,800 square metres. Archaeologists suggested it may have been used for rituals.","Labyrinth of Crete","en/d/laberinto-de-creta.html"],["Other names","In the runic songs the sampo also goes by the names Samppu, Samppi or Kirjokansi.","Sampo","en/d/sampo.html"],["A disputed origin","The Heimskringla places Sölve in Jutland, while the older Historia Norvegiae describes him as a Geat, from a different region.","Sölve Högnesson","en/d/solve-hognesson.html"],["The Mygdonians","Some Phrygians were said to be called Mygdonians after Mygdon, according to the ancient account.","Mygdon","en/d/mygdon.html"],["A moon of Uranus","Titania is the largest of Uranus&#x27;s moons, and all of them are named after characters from Shakespeare or Alexander Pope.","Titania","en/d/titania.html"],["A wolf in the armour","Diodorus describes Makedon fighting with the forequarters of a wolf&#x27;s skin, an animal the Egyptians held in honour.","Makedon","en/d/macedon.html"],["Phoenix headdress","She is usually depicted wearing a headdress of three phoenixes, or more.","Bixia Yuanjun","en/d/bixia-yuanjun.html"],["A name meaning man","His name is linked to the Germanic root for &#x27;man&#x27;, the source of mann in German and man in English.","Mannus","en/d/mannus.html"],["A thief named Battus","In Rafael Sánchez Ferlosio’s novel Industrias y andanzas de Alfanhuí, one of the thieves hidden in the hayloft is called “Bato”, and his companion “Faulo” is, surprisingly, mute.","Battus","en/d/bato.html"],["Legendary stature","The sources give him a height of 58 cubits, about 87 feet, and he is often compared with the sun and the moon.","Phussa","en/d/phussa.html"],["Four animals of light","His solar chariot was pulled by four animals whose Sumerian names mean the abundant light of heaven, the terrifying great light of heaven, the dreadful great light and the noble light of heaven. The sources do not agree on their species, though equids are often mentioned.","Utu","en/d/utu.html"],["A modern coin","The head of Concordia on a 62 BC denarius served as the model for an Italian 1,000-lire coin issued in 1970.","Concordia","en/d/concordia.html"],["Two possible mothers","The sources do not agree on his mother, who appears as either Eurydice or Henioche depending on the account.","Lycomedes","en/d/lucomedes.html"],["Origin of the name","The term is attributed to Puerto Rican comedian Silverio Pérez, who coined it in 1995 during a radio program in San Juan.","Chupacabra","en/d/chupacabras.html"],["An echo of Homer","The episode of Tyr fighting in Neklan&#x27;s armour is strongly inspired by Homer, like Patroclus in the Iliad.","Neklan","en/d/neklan.html"],["Many spellings","According to the sources, his name is written in several forms, such as Ualac, Valak, Valax or Valic, depending on the grimoire and language.","Volac","en/d/volac.html"],["Other names","He is known as Pomberito, Pyrague (&quot;hairy feet&quot;) or Karai Pyhare (&quot;lord of the night&quot;). In the Esteros del Iberá he is called Kuarahy Jára, &quot;master of the sun&quot;.","Pombero","en/d/pombero.html"],["The name","Gungnir is usually read as the rocker or the swaying one, a reference to the violent shudder it was said to cause in whoever it struck.","Gungnir","en/d/gungnir.html"],["Nameless at first","In the earliest eleventh-century tales she appears only as an oni or an old female ogre, with no proper name.","Datsue-ba","en/d/datsue-ba.html"],["Mosaic in Mérida","She appears in the cosmos mosaic of the House of Mithras in Augusta Emerita, in the sea section beside Oceanus and Portunus.","Tranquillitas","en/d/tranquilitas.html"],["A god with almost no myth","Comus has almost no myths of his own in antiquity. His fame rests mainly on artistic descriptions and his role in the procession of Dionysus.","Comus","en/d/como.html"],["Bronze horse","At the Les Bolards shrine a bronze horse linked to the god was found, together with other images of horses and horsemen.","Segomo","en/d/segomo.html"],["A beetle that pushes the sun","The beetle rolls balls of dung along the ground, an image the Egyptians linked to the solar disk pushed across the sky.","Khepri","en/d/jepri.html"],["A people named after a king","The mythical Bebryces would take their name from this figure, though this is a presumed explanation rather than a firmly established fact.","Bebryx","en/d/bebrix.html"],["A wild fig tree","The signal was given from a wild fig tree, the tree that gives the festival its name, caprificus.","Philotis","en/d/filotis.html"],["Rooted in speech","Greek <em>pheme</em> is related to <em>phanai</em>, &#x27;to speak&#x27;, and Latin <em>fama</em> to <em>fari</em>, with the same meaning. English <em>fame</em> comes from the Latin word.","Pheme","en/d/feme.html"],["A shrine over a workshop","The shrine was built on the ruins of a Roman workshop destroyed during the barbarian incursions of AD 196.","Alaisiagae","en/d/alaisiagae.html"],["A bird with a pouch","It is described as a winged bird with a long beak and a pelican-like pouch.","Cikavac","en/d/cikavac.html"],["A collective name","For Berossus, the amphibious beings who followed Oannes were called Musarus, meaning « repulsive creatures ».","Oannes (Uanna)","en/d/oannes-uanna.html"],["The Amazon mounds","Myrina raised three large mounds over the graves of her fallen companions. Diodorus says they were called the Mounds of the Amazons, a name they still bore in his time.","Myrina","en/d/mirina.html"],["A name in several spellings","The form Elphin is a variant spelling of the same name, which also designates a village in Scotland and a town in Ireland.","Elffin ap Gwyddno","en/d/elffin-ap-gwyddno.html"],["A line of descent","The Íslendingabók lists Visburr, Dómaldr and Dómarr as successive kings, confirming that Domalde already appeared in early genealogical lists.","Domalde","en/d/domalde.html"],["A stone relief","One version of the story appears on a relief from Malatya, dated between 1050 and 850 BC, now displayed in Ankara.","Illuyanka","en/d/illuyanka.html"],["A swan messenger","The golden swan that Nala captures is the one who awakens Damayanti&#x27;s love, describing the king&#x27;s virtues before the two ever meet.","Damayanti","en/d/damayanti.html"],["A famous inscription","The apple thrown by Eris bore the Greek inscription &quot;for the most beautiful&quot;, which sparked the judgement of Paris.","Golden apple","en/d/manzana-dorada.html"],["Link to Lilith","A variant of the Al Basty myth appears in Sumerian tradition, as one of the names given to Lilith.","Al Basty","en/d/al-basty.html"],["Unnamed in Homer","The Homeric poems mention that Ajax took a captive as his prize, but they never name her or show her as a character.","Tecmessa","en/d/tecmesa.html"],["A reign among heroes","According to tradition, the deeds of Dionysus and Perseus took place during the reign of Cecrops II, two figures from very different generations.","Cecrops II","en/d/cecrope-ii.html"],["A genus of moths","A genus of moths in the family Geometridae is named Epione in honour of Asclepius&#x27;s wife.","Epione","en/d/epione.html"],["A Handel opera","George Frideric Handel composed the opera Faramondo, which takes the legendary Frankish king as its title character.","Pharamond","en/d/faramundo.html"],["An Argonaut","Staphylus and his brother Phanus are counted among the crew of the Argo, Jason&#x27;s ship.","Staphylus","en/d/estafilo.html"],["Meaning of the name","One proposed etymology links her name to the Newar word <em>tale</em>, meaning &quot;high&quot;, a reference to her tall temple in Kathmandu.","Taleju Bhawani","en/d/taleju-bhawani.html"],["An unusual appearance","Descriptions show him with a bird&#x27;s face and a tiger&#x27;s tail, an unusual combination among Chinese divine figures.","Dongwanggong (King Father of the East)","en/d/dongwanggong-rey-padre-del-este.html"],["A book of Herodotus","When later editors divided Herodotus&#x27;s Histories into nine books, each was named after a Muse. The fifth book bears the name Terpsichore.","Terpsichore","en/d/terpsicore.html"],["Twenty-one Taras","According to some schools, Tara emanates twenty-one forms with different colours and attributes, from the pacifying to the wrathful.","Tara","en/d/tara.html"],["Male song","Despite the tradition of the lament, in reality only the male nightingale sings; the female does not.","Aedon","en/d/aedon.html"],["Possible origin in Caesar","Many scholars propose that the name derives from Caesar, though the exact route to Scandinavia is unclear. A path through Gothic and, ultimately, Greek <em>kaisar</em> has been suggested.","Kjárr","en/d/kjarr.html"],["A swan that sings spells","The black swan guarding the river of Tuonela sings death spells, and hunting it is the task that costs Lemminkäinen his life.","Tuonela","en/d/tuonela.html"],["A name with several readings","His name could be written with a logogram that was also read as Enšadu, usually translated as the good-hearted lord. It is unclear whether this was simply another name for him or a separate deity.","Nusku","en/d/nusku.html"],["A town named after a woman","According to tradition, the town of Triteia took its name from the founder&#x27;s mother, not from the man who founded it.","Triteia","en/d/tritea.html"],["A nickname for poets","According to Snorri, those who excelled at poetry were called Bragi, so the god gave his name to a whole class of artists.","Bragi","en/d/bragi.html"],["Goose feet","One of the statues dedicated at Caerwent survives only as a pair of human feet and a pair of goose feet, so the full figure cannot be known for certain.","Ocelus","en/d/ocelus.html"],["Meaning of her name","Hippodamia means she who masters horses, from the Greek <em>hippos</em> (horse) and <em>damazein</em> (to tame).","Hippodamia","en/d/hipodamia-q2468258.html"],["Twins by two gods","Chione bore twins by two different gods: Autolycus, son of Hermes, a notorious thief and trickster, and Philammon, son of Apollo, famed for his voice and his skill with the lyre.","Daedalion","en/d/dedalion.html"],["A trio of sibyls","The Phrygian Sibyl often appears alongside the Hellespontine and Erythraean Sibyls, and some consider her a doublet of the first.","Phrygian Sibyl","en/d/sibila-frigia.html"],["A crocodile that brings rain","Among the Olmecs and Maya, crocodilians were linked to the rain-bringing wind. It was believed that rain clouds were breathed out of cave openings in the earth.","Cipactli","en/d/cipactli.html"],["A name that gives a word","According to Snorri, the goddess&#x27;s name gave rise to the word lof, meaning both permission and praise.","Lofn","en/d/lofn.html"],["Two namesakes","Sources distinguish between the nymph who was the wife of Hyrieus and an Amazon with the same name who fought at Troy.","Clonia","en/d/clonia.html"],["Origin of the word","The word sibyl comes via Latin from the Greek <em>sibylla</em>, meaning prophetess or oracle.","Hellespontine Sibyl","en/d/sibila-del-helesponto.html"],["Red Sea corals","According to Ovid, the corals of the Red Sea formed from Medusa&#x27;s blood that fell onto seaweed while Perseus rested her head on the shore.","Medusa","en/d/medusa.html"],["A Sanskrit name","Batara Guru derives from <em>bhattaraka</em>, a Sanskrit term meaning &quot;noble lord&quot;.","Batara Guru","en/d/batara-guru.html"],["A magazine with a mythic name","Pikker was the name of an Estonian satire and humor magazine.","Pikne","en/d/pikne.html"],["Cave paintings","Mackal cites anecdotal reports of cave paintings showing «elephant-like creatures with stegosaurus-like backs».","Mbielu-mbielu-mbielu","en/d/mbielu-mbielu-mbielu.html"],["A city beside the ravine","According to tradition, the Chaonians founded the city of Cichyrus around the ravine where the king&#x27;s son threw himself.","Epirus","en/d/epiro.html"],["Name and colour","Melinoë may derive from a Greek word meaning having the colour of quince, a yellowish green that the Greeks associated with the pallor of illness or death.","Melinoë","en/d/melinoe.html"],["A door that fell to earth","According to a legend of Togakushi Shrine, the cave door Tajikarao threw away landed on Earth and became Mount Togakushi.","Ame no Tajikarao","en/d/ame-no-tajikarao.html"],["Three valleys","According to Snorri, the force of Thor&#x27;s hammer blows left three square valleys in the landscape.","Útgarða-Loki","en/d/utgara-loki.html"],["A confusing name","Nuribotoke means “lacquered Buddha”, for its glossy black colour and its resemblance to the Buddha, though the word for Buddha can also refer to any deceased spirit.","Nuribotoke","en/d/nuribotoke.html"],["A creature of two forms","Some scholars see it as a shapeshifter: a horse that takes human form and gives itself away with pointed ears like a horse&#x27;s.","Glashtyn","en/d/glashtyn.html"],["Death by mercury","According to the account, Emperor Jiajing of the Ming dynasty died after ingesting a lethal dose of mercury in a supposed elixir of life prepared by alchemists.","Elixir of life","en/d/elixir-de-la-vida.html"],["A bow with its own name","Pinaka is the name of Shiva&#x27;s bow and the basis of one of his epithets, which literally means &quot;the wielder of the Pinaka&quot;.","Pinaka","en/d/pinaka.html"],["Two different Olofs","Olof Björnsson is not the same figure as Olof Skötkonung, although both appear in the Swedish sagas. The saga of Hervar makes Skötkonung a son of Eric who succeeded him after his death.","Olof Björnsson","en/d/olof-bjornsson.html"],["The triangular heart","According to the sources, his heart was triangular, which is why the valknut and the triquetra have been called &#x27;Hrungnir&#x27;s Heart&#x27;.","Hrungnir","en/d/hrungnir.html"],["The humming breath","In pranayama, the bhramari breath imitates the buzzing sound of a bee.","Bhramari","en/d/bhramari.html"],["A shuttle against Eurystheus","According to tradition, Alcmene, Hyllus&#x27;s paternal grandmother, tore out Eurystheus&#x27;s eyes with a weaving shuttle after receiving his head.","Hyllus","en/d/hilo.html"],["Flames that will not die","According to tradition, Emperor Akbar tried to extinguish the flame with an iron disk and by diverting water, but the fire resisted.","Jwala Ji","en/d/jwala-ji.html"],["A hook with a soul","In the story, Hoderi refused another hook because he believed every tool has its own spirit and is unique.","Hoori","en/d/hoori.html"],["Famous honey","In ancient times, the bees of Themiscyra were famous for their honey.","Themiscyra","en/d/temiscira.html"],["A party symbol","The Thai Democrat Party included her in its logo, alongside a Pali proverb meaning that truth is the word that never dies.","Phra Mae Thorani","en/d/phra-mae-thorani.html"],["A demon of the womb","In the Atharvaveda, Keshi appears as a demon that attacks female fetuses, before he is linked to Krishna.","Keshi","en/d/keshi.html"],["A plaque in a cathedral","A plaque in Linköping Cathedral names a King Björn as founder in 813, a date considered too early, so it may refer to this king or to a purely legendary figure.","Björn III of Sweden","en/d/bjorn-iii-de-suecia.html"],["Different chains","In another version of the tale, Caer is bound alone with 130 chains rather than chained to 149 other swans.","Caer Ibormeith","en/d/caer-ibormeith.html"],["A disputed name","Sophus Bugge proposed that the name came from Old English and means &quot;rat-tooth&quot;. Albert Sturtevant rejected this hypothesis for the element rata-.","Ratatoskr","en/d/ratatosk.html"],["A lost sandal","According to the tale, Jason lost a sandal while crossing a river, so he arrived in Iolcos wearing only one shoe, which alarmed his uncle Pelias.","Jason","en/d/jason.html"],["Telugu film versions","In 2012 the Telugu film Sri Vasavi Vaibhavam was released, depicting the goddess&#x27;s life.","Vasavi","en/d/vasavi.html"],["Buried palm trees","Excavations near Bushehr uncovered a custom of «burying» palm trees, which researchers tentatively link to her cult.","Kiririsha","en/d/kiririsha.html"],["A name meaning laughter","According to Robert Graves, the name Gelanor would mean &quot;laughter&quot;.","Gelanor","en/d/gelanor.html"],["Winged sons","According to the myth, her sons Zetes and Calais were born with wings like their father Boreas and joined the Argonauts.","Orithyia","en/d/oritia.html"],["Serpent nickname","Stephanus of Byzantium reports that Cychreus was known as “serpent” because of his irascible temper.","Cychreus","en/d/cicreo.html"],["A walrus by mistake","Several early Chinese-English dictionaries translated the name as &#x27;walrus&#x27;, although the Chinese term does not mean that. The mistake was copied from one dictionary to another.","Kui","en/d/kui.html"],["A god linked to the stork","Some scholars propose that Hœnir was connected with the stork, based on his epithets and his role in the ballad <em>Lokka táttur</em>. This idea is a modern interpretation rather than something stated in the ancient texts.","Hœnir","en/d/hnir.html"],["Born without a father","Hesiod presents her among the children of Night, produced without a father, a trait she shares with other descendants of Nyx.","Oizys","en/d/oizis.html"],["A temple on the Aventine","An inscription from a temple on the Aventine Hill in Rome lists the members of a cult community, with seven patrons and numerous candidates.","Jupiter Dolichenus","en/d/jupiter-doliqueno.html"],["Number of brothers","Some accounts add a fifth brother, Presbon, so the number of Phrixus&#x27;s sons varies from version to version.","Phrontis","en/d/frontis.html"],["A name that shifts","Pausanias uses Agraulos for both the mother and the daughter, while the forms with Aglauros appear mainly in late versions.","Agraulos","en/d/agraulo.html"],["A name for Achilles","Pyrrha was also the name under which Achilles was known while he hid, disguised as a maiden, at the court of King Lycomedes.","Pyrrha","en/d/pirra.html"],["A flower that becomes a god","In one of the stories, a simple flower that Hera held in her hand turned into Ares, the god of war.","Chloris","en/d/cloris.html"],["An offering with a signature","The inscription on the peplos at Tegea recorded both the gift and Laodice&#x27;s lineage, as quoted by Pausanias.","Laodice","en/d/laodice-q419120.html"],["Tehom","In Hebrew, the watery abyss of Genesis is called Tehom, a word that also appears among the aquatic monsters defeated by the Canaanite gods.","Cosmic ocean","en/d/oceano-cosmico.html"],["Her Latin equivalent","In Latin her counterpart was impietas, which also means impiety. It appears in a passage where she is named alongside other dark forces.","Dyssebeia","en/d/disebia.html"],["A honey-dripping staff","The thyrsus, his attribute, was a fennel staff that could be wound with ivy and drip with honey.","Dionysus","en/d/dioniso-q645312.html"],["Ullr&#x27;s ship","In skaldic poetry, a shield could be called &quot;Ullr&#x27;s ship&quot;. Snorri records the expression but does not explain why it was used.","Ullr","en/d/ull.html"],["Palaeolithic traces","Engravings and paintings of aurochs, bulls and oxen have been found in caves such as Isturits and Sare. Some interpret these images as pointing to a very ancient origin of the myth.","Aatxe","en/d/aatxe.html"],["Statue on a palm","In some free-standing statues, Victoria stands on the palm of a hand belonging to Jupiter, Mars or Roma, figures far larger than she is.","Victoria","en/d/victoria.html"],["A colour for the first time","Nennius&#x27;s Historia Brittonum is the first text that explicitly calls the dragon red, though Y Gododdin may contain an earlier hint.","Welsh Dragon","en/d/dragon-gales.html"],["A god who gave his name to the sky","In Latin caelum means sky, and the root gives English celestial. Caelus was, in a sense, the sky itself personified.","Caelus","en/d/caelus.html"],["A bird&#x27;s name","His Greek name, Epopeus, is linked to <em>epops</em>, the hoopoe, a bird the ancients associated with watchfulness.","Epopeus","en/d/epopeo.html"],["A name with several readings","The name can be understood as &#x27;stirrer of inspiration&#x27; or &#x27;stirrer of fury&#x27;, which suggests it refers to the mead.","Óðrerir","en/d/orerir.html"],["A rock with a hoofprint","According to local tradition, Bayard Rock near Dinant was split by the horse&#x27;s hooves.","Bayard","en/d/bayard.html"],["A river of forgetting","In the Underworld, souls drank from the waters of Lethe to forget their earlier lives before being reborn.","Lethe","en/d/lete-q1375734.html"],["A mountain named after him","A pyramidal peak in the Tian Shan range, between China, Kazakhstan and Kyrgyzstan, is called Khan Tengri.","Tengri","en/d/tengri.html"],["Dogs of gold and silver","According to Homer, the dogs guarding the entrance to Alcinous&#x27;s palace were forged by Hephaestus, the god of fire and metalworking.","Scheria","en/d/esqueria.html"],["A fruit-tree name","Her name, <em>ónkhnē</em>, means pear tree in ancient Greek, although its exact origin remains uncertain.","Ochne","en/d/ochne.html"],["A name for the bull","Asterion was also the name of the Minotaur and an epithet of Zeus, probably derived from the name of the goddess Aštar.","Europa","en/d/europa.html"],["Ancient names of Italy","According to ancient poets, Italy was successively called Artesa, Saturnia, Ausonia, Tyrrhenia and finally Italia.","Auson","en/d/auson.html"],["A constellation with a bird&#x27;s name","Merops, turned into an eagle by Hera, was carried to the sky and became the constellation Aquila.","Echemeia","en/d/echemeia.html"],["Rambha, the name of a banana","According to the Katha Sarit Sagara and kavya literature, rambha also means banana plant. In the same literary tradition it is the name of a courtesan.","Rambha","en/d/rambha.html"],["Opposite twins","Nisien and Efnisien are twins, yet their characters are completely opposed: one is peaceful and generous, the other vindictive and destructive.","Nisien","en/d/nisien.html"],["One hundred sons","The Bhagavata Purana gives him one hundred sons, including Kukshi and Nimi, and the eldest, Bharata, is said to have given the earth its name.","Rishabha","en/d/rishabha.html"],["A lost golden disk","In 1571 Spanish conquistadors seized a large golden disk representing Inti and sent it to the Pope via Spain. It has been lost since then.","Inti","en/d/inti.html"],["Hooved feet","Her most distinctive trait is that her feet are deer hooves, which usually give her away. It is said that looking at them breaks her spell.","Deer Woman","en/d/deer-woman.html"],["Sisters turned into birds","Artemis transformed several of Gorge&#x27;s sisters into birds because of their grief after Meleager&#x27;s death. Gorge and Deianira escaped this fate.","Gorge","en/d/gorge.html"],["A version of revenge","Propertius, in his Elegies, is the only source saying that Alphesiboea avenged Alcmaeon by killing her brothers. No other surviving source confirms this.","Alphesiboea","en/d/alfesiboya.html"],["Tripodiscium","According to Conon, after his exile Crotopus founded a city in the Megarid called Tripodiscium as penance for the plague.","Crotopus","en/d/crotopo.html"],["An underground altar","According to some sources, his altar was buried or covered with earth, and that earth was swept away during his festivals, when the races were held in the Circus Maximus.","Consus","en/d/consus.html"],["Lots with runes","To dissuade the Danes from attacking Birka, Anund had them draw lots, possibly using runes, about the city&#x27;s future.","Anund Uppsale","en/d/anund-uppsale.html"],["Heracles&#x27; descendants","According to the story, Heracles had children with both daughters of Phylas: Antiochus, born to Meda, and Tlepolemus, born to Astyoche.","Phylas","en/d/filanto.html"],["Fortress of Shadows","Her residence, Dún Scáith, means &quot;Fortress of Shadows&quot;. This is where her nickname &quot;the Shadow&quot; comes from.","Scáthach","en/d/scathach.html"],["A name with a geological root","The place-name Flinsberg comes from the Middle High German <em>vlins</em>, meaning flint or hard rock, a word used by local miners and woodcutters.","Flins","en/d/flins.html"],["Demonic twins","Khara and Dushana were twins, and together they ruled the Dandaka Forest before confronting Rama.","Khara","en/d/khara.html"],["Hearts of enemies","Egyptians compared Mafdet to cats, which leave their kills at their owners&#x27; feet. In this idea, she delivered the hearts of the pharaoh&#x27;s enemies.","Mafdet","en/d/mafdet.html"],["A name that travelled far","The name Aeshma is linked to Asmodeus, the demon of Jewish tradition, a connection often proposed from the Avestan aeshma.daeva.","Aeshma","en/d/aeshma.html"],["Calling out loud","Tradition held that if Hymen failed to attend a wedding, the marriage would be unhappy, so the guests called his name aloud during the celebration.","Hymen","en/d/himeneo.html"],["From chest to island","The island of Leucophrys, where the chest with the siblings washed up, later took the name Tenedos in honour of Tenes.","Philonome","en/d/filonome.html"],["The stork","The Romans believed the stork returned to the same nest every year and cared for its parents in old age, making it a symbol of filial piety.","Pietas","en/d/pietas.html"],["A golden shield","His temple at Vologošč housed a golden shield that was regarded as belonging to the god.","Yarovit","en/d/yarovit.html"],["Shifting names","According to some authors, Cinyras&#x27;s wife is called Metharme or Orithyia instead of Cenchreis.","Cenchreis","en/d/cenchreis.html"],["A warrior&#x27;s object","Its form comes from a stirrup, part of a rider&#x27;s saddle, which links the creature to the image of the fallen warrior.","Abumi-kuchi","en/d/abumi-kuchi.html"],["A feather at Tarsus","According to one tradition, when Zeus turned Pegasus into a constellation, a single feather fell to earth near the city of Tarsus.","Pegasus","en/d/pegaso.html"],["A primordial Euphrosyne","Hyginus presents her as a daughter of Night and Erebus, a very different version from the daughter of Zeus.","Euphrosyne","en/d/eufrosine.html"],["A serpent name","His name derives from the Greek <em>echis</em>, meaning serpent, linked to his origin from dragon&#x27;s teeth.","Echion","en/d/equion.html"],["Divine sculptor","One incantation links her with precious and semi-precious stones, while an inscription of Sennacherib mentions limestone as the material she worked with.","Ninkurra","en/d/ninkurra.html"],["A station named after it","The Yonago Airport station on the JR West Sakai Line is known locally as &quot;Betobeto-san Station.&quot;","Betobeto-san","en/d/betobeto-san.html"],["Hakgala Botanical Garden","The <em>Ashoka Vatika</em> is believed to be located at today&#x27;s Hakgala Botanical Garden, at the base of the Hakgala rock formations near the resort city of Nuwara Eliya.","Ashoka Vatika","en/d/ashoka-vatika.html"],["Hús-brenna","This is the name given to burning a house with its occupants inside, like the attack Ingjald ordered against Granmar and Hjörvard.","Granmar","en/d/granmar.html"],["Father and sons","According to the commentary, the Brahman and his two sons attained enlightenment together, and the father became the Medicine Buddha.","Sūryaprabha","en/d/suryaprabha.html"],["An offering of passage","The young man who received the toga virilis left a coin as an offering, a gesture tied to the passage from childhood to adult life.","Juventas","en/d/juventas.html"],["A plant remedy","According to tradition, a green hazel leaf smeared with fox blood was the only way to survive if a flying toad touched the ojáncanu.","Ojáncanu","en/d/ojancanu.html"],["A name with a double meaning","In heraldry, the word hippocampus now refers to the real seahorse, while the mythical creature is called a sea-horse.","Hippocampus","en/d/hipocampo.html"],["A name with several uses","Pergamus also names the citadel of Troy in Homer&#x27;s Iliad, a use separate from the hero.","Pergamus","en/d/pergamo.html"],["A name of frost","His name has been translated as &quot;frosty&quot;, &quot;the one covered with hoarfrost&quot; or &quot;the sooty one&quot;. It probably evokes the frost giants, the <em>hrímþursar</em>.","Hrímnir","en/d/hrimnir.html"],["A naked blue buddha","In Nyingma art, Samantabhadra appears naked and blue. The lack of robes symbolizes reality beyond any conceptual clothing.","Adi-Buddha","en/d/adi-buda.html"],["A female creator","In the story, the creator of the first human is a woman, Cura, not a god. Heidegger saw in this a contrast with the Christian myth, in which woman is created last.","Cura","en/d/cura.html"],["A lineage that reaches Troy","Through the Aeolid branch of her descendants, mythographers lead to Menoetius, father of Patroclus, the finest of the Myrmidons.","Pisidice","en/d/pisidice.html"],["A mortal name","The original word means &quot;mortal being&quot; and shares its root with Old Persian martya and Persian mard, both meaning &quot;man&quot;.","Mashya and Mashyana","en/d/mashya-y-mashyana.html"],["The root of his name","His name comes from a root that in archaic epic is linked to burning and shining, the same root found in Ethiopia, meaning «the one with the burnt face».","Aether","en/d/eter.html"],["Extreme offering","In the Lotus Sutra, his former life culminates in burning his own body as an offering to a buddha.","Bhaiṣajyarāja","en/d/bhaisajyaraja.html"],["A name with two meanings","In Japan, the word baku refers both to the mythical creature and to the Malayan tapir, and is used with either meaning.","Baku","en/d/baku.html"],["A red star in the texts","Several Greek and Roman authors describe Sirius as red, alongside Mars and Antares. In reality it is a blue-white star.","Sirius","en/d/sirio.html"],["Three faces","Trivia was shown with three heads: a horse, a dog, and a man or a pig, in an image that joined three divine aspects.","Trivia","en/d/trivia.html"],["Lord of the great city","His Sumerian name can be translated as &#x27;lord of the great city&#x27;, a euphemistic way of referring to the ruler of the underworld.","Nergal","en/d/nergal.html"],["First written reference","The first written mention of Bianor is attributed to Francesc Tarafa, a canon of Barcelona Cathedral.","Ocno Bianor","en/d/ocno-bianor.html"],["Sacred trees","The grounds hold rare ancient ginkgo and Magnolia compressa trees, considered sacred in Japan since ancient times.","Ama-no-Iwato","en/d/ama-no-iwato.html"],["A name with meaning","In Old Irish, nemed means something like privileged or holy, and it may be related to a Celtic root meaning sky.","Nemed","en/d/nemed.html"],["Ghosts without descendants","Some specialists connect these spirits to <em>mujagui</em>, ghosts with no descendants to remember them in ancestor memorial rites.","Egg ghost (dalgyal gwisin)","en/d/fantasma-huevo-dalgyal-gwisin.html"],["A name meaning bear","His name may come from the Gaulish word for bear, linking him to an animal of strong presence in many ancient cultures.","Matunus","en/d/matunus.html"],["Two namesakes","The name Chrysogenia also belongs to a naiad, daughter of the river-god Peneus and mother of Thissaeus by Zeus. She is not the same character.","Chrysogeneia","en/d/crisogenia.html"],["Bamboo strikes","In some Yulan Festival celebrations in Hong Kong, once the Guanyin image is removed, the paper effigy is struck with bamboo poles so that no wandering spirit stays attached to it.","Ulkāmukha Pretarāja","en/d/ulkamukha-pretaraja.html"],["Mirror of Diana","Poets called Lake Nemi speculum Dianae, meaning Mirror of Diana.","Diana Nemorensis","en/d/diana-nemorensis.html"],["A messenger bee","In one tale, God sends a bee to spy on the Devil. When the Devil tries to catch it and it escapes, he curses it, and God orders it to make honey from then on.","Slavic creation myths","en/d/mitos-eslavos-de-la-creacion.html"],["Massive pilgrimage","Sabarimala receives around 50 million devotees each year, making it the second largest pilgrimage in the world.","Ayyappan","en/d/ayyappan.html"],["A protective swan","Kára protects Helgi in the form of a swan, and an accidental blow from his sword breaks the spell.","Helgi Haddingjaskati","en/d/helgi-haddingjaskati.html"],["A statue without a blindfold","Atop London&#x27;s Old Bailey, the statue of Lady Justice has no blindfold, since her maidenly form is considered enough to guarantee impartiality.","Iustitia (Lady Justice)","en/d/iustitia-justicia.html"],["A saying with her name","Snorri records a saying that a woman becomes aware (<em>vor</em>) of something when she finds it out, a wordplay on the goddess&#x27;s name.","Vör","en/d/vor.html"],["The heron formation","Kritavarma was placed at the head of the flying-heron formation, a battle array that Bhishma had devised for the Kaurava side.","Kritavarma","en/d/kritavarma.html"],["Other names","Some sources call her Melanippe, Evippe or Hippe, showing that her name was not fixed in the tradition.","Ocyrhoe","en/d/ocirroe.html"],["Two genealogies","Hyginus presents him as the son of Schoeneus and king of Arcadia, while Parthenius makes him the son of Teleus, a man from Argos.","Clymenus","en/d/climeno-q4223629.html"],["The cup of Bellona","The earliest known image of the goddess appears on a Campanian cup from the 4th century BCE, inscribed with the phrase &#x27;cup of Bellona&#x27;. She is shown with a long neck and her hair tied up.","Bellona","en/d/belona.html"],["From fate to strangeness","The English word weird comes from wyrd, but its modern meaning of &#x27;odd&#x27; was first recorded in 1815.","Wyrd","en/d/wyrd.html"],["A day with a god&#x27;s name","Cipactli is the first day of the Mexica sacred calendar, and its name also means &#x27;crocodile&#x27;. This is why it is linked to the origin of all things.","Cipactonal","en/d/cipactonal.html"],["A statue in London","The British Museum holds a statue of Nabu that was originally erected in Nimrud, the Calah of the biblical sources, during the reign of Tiglath-Pileser III.","Nabu","en/d/nabu.html"],["Divine midwives","The Horae, goddesses of the seasons, were said to have helped at her birth.","Telete","en/d/telete.html"],["A city named for his mother","According to some accounts, Tangier was named in memory of Tingis, the mother of Sufax.","Sufax","en/d/sofax.html"],["A spring with her name","Near the city of the Lepreatai there is a spring called Arene, which was said to have been named after Aphareus&#x27;s wife.","Arene","en/d/arene.html"],["Double meaning","In Sanskrit, vajra means both &#x27;diamond&#x27; and &#x27;thunderbolt&#x27;, which explains its double symbolism of hardness and force.","Vajra","en/d/vajra.html"],["A proverb behind her name","The Chinese phrase <em>chaoyunmuyu</em>, morning cloud and evening rain, is an old proverb for secret encounters between lovers.","Ameonna","en/d/ameonna.html"],["A name with meaning","According to modern analysis of his name, Achilles might mean something like &quot;he who distresses the people&quot;, a theme repeated in the Iliad that contrasts with his fame as a hero of glory.","Achilles","en/d/aquiles.html"],["An island made by a throw","According to the myth, the islet of Nisyros was born from the piece of Kos that Poseidon tore off and hurled at the giant.","Polybotes","en/d/polibotes.html"],["Names among the Nuer","The Nuer, a people of South Sudan and Ethiopia, know her as Buk or Acol.","Abuk","en/d/abuk.html"],["White footprint","Her name is interpreted as &quot;white footprint&quot;. Legend says white trefoils sprang up from her steps.","Olwen","en/d/olwen.html"],["Rat Boys","His nickname, Rat Boys, refers to his looks: he is said to be fat, with dark, hairy skin, a long snout and a skinny tail.","Far darrig","en/d/far-darrig.html"],["A dream of serpents","Hecuba dreamed she gave birth to serpents instead of a child, an omen that tradition links to the fate of Troy.","Cilla","en/d/cila.html"],["A very ancient rock","In 1994 a rare formation called Sayagata-shūkyoku, a fold about 100 million years old nicknamed the Wrinkle of the Earth, was discovered on Nushima.","Onogoro","en/d/onogoro.html"],["National emblem","Indonesia and Thailand both have Garuda in their coats of arms, and in India his figure appears on the insignia of an Army guards brigade.","Garuda","en/d/garuda.html"],["A god with almost no stories","Condatis is known almost only from small votive altars, with no surviving mythic narratives.","Condatis","en/d/condatis.html"],["A divided face","According to the Prose Edda, Hel is half blue and half flesh-coloured, which makes her unmistakable.","Hel","en/d/hel.html"],["The boar emblem","He is identified by the boar carved or stamped on the pedestal beneath his images.","Vimalanatha","en/d/vimalanatha.html"],["An unusual name","The character used for his name is rarely found in personal names. In historical dictionaries it literally means «big fish».","Gun","en/d/gun.html"],["The gate of winter","The winter solstice was called Tabburt n Tagrest, literally &#x27;Gate of Winter&#x27;. It marked the yearly opening of a solar gate.","Magec","en/d/magec.html"],["Infant mortality","In antiquity, infant mortality could reach 30 or 40 percent, and childbirth remained dangerous for both mother and newborn.","Edusa","en/d/edusa.html"],["A son with his own name","Some traditions credit him with a son called Teuthranius, born to Auge, who died fighting in the Trojan War.","Teuthras","en/d/teutrante.html"],["Proverbial expression","The comparison «more naked than Ialemus» was used in Greek as a mockery of something poor or unadorned.","Ialemus","en/d/yalemo.html"],["A disputed coin","Some Phrygian coins show a nude warrior standing on a ship&#x27;s prow. B. V. Head identified him as Otreus, while Ernest Babelon believed he was Aeneas.","Otreus","en/d/otreo.html"],["A hundred heads","Aristophanes gives her a hundred heads, matching the hundred heads Hesiod gives her mate Typhon.","Echidna","en/d/equidna.html"],["A name with a pun","According to Apollodorus, the name Priam derives from <em>priamai</em>, &quot;to buy&quot;, referring to the veil his sister paid to ransom him.","Priam","en/d/priamo.html"],["Lakshmana&#x27;s limit","The line he drew around the hut, the <em>lakshmana rekha</em>, has given its name to a common expression in South Asia for a boundary one should not cross.","Lakshmana","en/d/lakshmana.html"],["Her festival&#x27;s name","Ochpaniztli means &#x27;sweeping&#x27;, and the festival&#x27;s rituals of cleaning linked it to purification.","Tlazolteotl","en/d/tlazolteotl.html"],["A misleading translation","The English word <em>rape</em> translates the Latin for «seized» or «carried off», and in this context it refers to the kidnapping of a bride, not to the sexual violence that may have followed.","Rape of Persephone","en/d/rapto-de-persefone.html"],["A calculation error","Columbus confused the Arabic mile with the Italian one and calculated the distance from the Canaries to Cipango as roughly a quarter of the real figure.","Cipango","en/d/cipango.html"],["Mizuki&#x27;s inventions","Mizuki said he created around 30 yōkai for his manga GeGeGe no Kitaro and other works, but he did not specify which ones.","Jubokko","en/d/jubokko.html"],["Name variants","Her name also appears as Dyomene and Dinamene, two older forms of the same figure.","Dynamene","en/d/dinamene.html"],["A god of roads","At Mannheim he appears as Mercurius Alaunus, a form linking him to Mercury, protector of roads.","Alaunus","en/d/alaunus.html"],["A god in horror fiction","H. P. Lovecraft used the name in his story “The Call of Cthulhu”, though he presented it briefly within his own invented mythology.","Torngarsuk","en/d/torngarsuk.html"],["Three Dianas","According to Cicero, tradition distinguished up to three goddesses called Diana, each with different parents.","Diana","en/d/diana.html"],["A telling name","Her name is a compound meaning &quot;brother killer&quot;. According to Stanford, it may have been a nickname applied later because of her actions in the myth, and she may originally have had another name.","Cassiphone","en/d/casifone.html"],["Bhanumati","Although she is one of the best-known wives of Duryodhana in popular tradition, she does not appear in any accessible Sanskrit version of the <em>Mahabharata</em>, where none of his wives is named.","Wives of Duryodhana","en/d/esposas-de-duryodhana.html"],["Founding twins","Their parallel with Romulus and Remus suggests Saxo wanted to place the Danes on a par with Rome, as an independent and equal people.","Angul","en/d/angul.html"],["A name possibly from Japan","Michael Jan de Goeje proposed that the name derives from a Cantonese form used for Japan. It is a hypothesis, not an established fact.","Wakwak (Waq Waq)","en/d/wakwak.html"],["A continent with a Lydian name","According to the Lydians, the continent of Asia took its name from Asies, grandson of Tyllus through his mother. Herodotus passes on this tradition.","Tyllus","en/d/tulo.html"],["Ship figureheads","Herodotus recalls that Phoenician ships carried figures of these protective dwarves on their prows.","Ptah-Patek","en/d/ptah-patek.html"],["A principle, not a god","Athenagoras makes clear that these are not distinct primordial beings but a single concept: water charged with what would later settle as earth.","Hydros","en/d/hydros.html"],["The Red One","A figure found in the 1980s at the Otterburn Training Area is known as the Red One. Its name may refer to the possibility that statues of the god were painted red.","Cocidius","en/d/cocidius.html"],["Lunar trinity","According to some sources, Mama Cocha formed with Mama Quilla and Pachamama an Inca lunar trinity, in which the three goddesses were linked.","Mama Cocha","en/d/mama-cocha.html"],["A sage with Phoenician letters","Syrus is said to have written arithmetic philosophy using the Phoenician alphabet.","Syrus","en/d/siro.html"],["Dangerous wishes","Scottish belief held that a wish made at night without invoking God&#x27;s protection might be granted in some terrible way. That is why the stories have the women arrive just after the hunters speak of female company.","Baobhan sith","en/d/baobhan-sith.html"],["Orion","A modern hypothesis links the three deities to the three stars of Orion&#x27;s Belt.","Sumiyoshi sanjin","en/d/sumiyoshi-sanjin.html"],["Etymology","Plato linked her name to the verb eireín, &#x27;to speak&#x27;, so that Iris would embody dialectic. Her father Thaumas is connected with thoûma, &#x27;wonder&#x27;.","Iris","en/d/iris.html"],["A Greek-rooted name","His name is linked to the Greek kakos, meaning bad, which fits his reputation as a thief and a man-eater.","Cacus","en/d/caco.html"],["An echo in the Americas","The story resembles the earth-diver creation myths of Indigenous peoples of the Americas, in which a creature brings up from the primordial waters a little magical soil that later expands into dry land.","Xirang","en/d/xirang.html"],["A Tatar ballet","The first Tatar ballet was based on a poem by Ghabdulla Tuqay inspired by this folklore figure.","Şüräle","en/d/surale.html"],["Outside the canon","The story does not appear in the Pali Canon, although his name does appear among the beings who honor the Buddha.","Apalāla","en/d/apalala.html"],["A maternal uncle","Several scholars see in Bolthorn a reflection of the bond with the maternal uncle, a tie that the Roman historian Tacitus had already noted as important in Germanic society.","Bolthorn","en/d/bolthorn.html"],["Perpetual flame","In the late 12th century Gerald of Wales described the perpetual fire at Kildare, kept by nineteen nuns and ringed by a hedge no man could cross.","Brigid","en/d/brigid.html"],["A name with clues","Himinbjörg translates as &quot;heaven&#x27;s castle&quot; or &quot;heaven mountain&quot;, and scholars debate which meaning is the original.","Himinbjörg","en/d/himinbjorg.html"],["One of the earliest dated stelae","The Hauberg Stela, dated to 199 AD in the Maya Lowlands, is one of the first dated monuments showing the serpent&#x27;s link to bloodletting.","Vision Serpent","en/d/serpiente-de-la-vision.html"],["A twin sister","According to the story, Theronice had a twin sister, Theraephone, and both sisters married the Molionides.","Theronice","en/d/teronice.html"],["A 1908 mural","The painter Frank Wilbert Stokes depicted the myth in a mural in the Eskimo Hall of the American Museum of Natural History in New York.","Aningaat","en/d/aningaat.html"],["Two genealogies","Herodotus makes him the son of Pritanis, while Pausanias presents him as the son of Eunomus. The difference shows how lists of Spartan kings varied among ancient authors.","Polydectes of Sparta","en/d/polidectes-de-esparta.html"],["Siwa Oracle","His oracle in the Siwa Oasis was the only oracle of Amun throughout Egyptian history.","Amun-Ra","en/d/amon-ra.html"],["Vandalised temple","In 2020, vandals attacked a Hindu temple dedicated to Rani Bhatiyani in Tharparkar district, Pakistan. They damaged her idol and set fire to holy books.","Rani Bhatiyani","en/d/rani-bhatiyani.html"],["A revealing name","The name Viðfinnr means &#x27;wood-Finn&#x27; or &#x27;forest of the Finns&#x27;, a hint at how the Norse named their neighbours.","Viðfinnr","en/d/vifinnr.html"],["A taro born from the earth","According to the myth, the first taro plant grew from the spot where Papa buried Hoʻohōkūkalani&#x27;s stillborn child.","Papa (Papahānaumoku)","en/d/papa-papahanaumoku.html"],["A name with ka","His name was written with the sign for ka, the Egyptian concept of vital force, suggesting the link between magic and that energy.","Heka","en/d/heka.html"],["A very popular god","Because so many dedications survive, some researchers believe Reo was the most popular deity in the Lusitanian pantheon.","Reo","en/d/reo.html"],["An Etruscan Hercules","Etruscan mirrors show Hercle in adventures not found in Greek or Roman myths.","Hercle","en/d/hercle.html"],["Other names","Her name is also rendered as Thea or Thia, and she is sometimes called Memnonis.","Theia","en/d/teia.html"],["Greek name for Edfu","The Greeks linked Hor-Behedety with Apollo, which is why the city of Edfu was called Apollinopolis Magna.","Hor-Behedety","en/d/hor-behedety.html"],["Proverbial wealth","Cinyras&#x27;s riches became famous in antiquity, to the point of being compared with those of Croesus.","Cinyras","en/d/ciniras.html"],["A name with meaning","Her Greek name, Σπειώ, means &quot;the dweller in the caves.&quot; It also appears spelled as Speio or Speo.","Speio","en/d/espeo.html"],["Oath on iron","Traditional Yoruba followers can swear to tell the truth in court by kissing a piece of iron in Ogun&#x27;s name.","Ogun","en/d/ogun.html"],["A lake bearing her name","Lake Bistonis, in Thrace, appears in a poem by Moschus as if it belonged to the nymph, filled with a population of nymphs.","Bistonis","en/d/bistonis.html"],["A name with a false etymology","The name Branchus was falsely linked to a Greek word meaning windpipe, based on his mother&#x27;s dream.","Smicrus","en/d/esmicro.html"],["A cobra in hand","In iconography he is often shown clutching a cobra, which in the Shaivite tradition symbolizes ahamkara.","Apasmara","en/d/apasmara.html"],["The letters on the hyacinth","According to tradition, the flower that sprang from Ajax&#x27;s blood bore the letters AI on its petals, the first two letters of his Greek name.","Ajax the Great","en/d/ayax-el-grande.html"],["An ambiguous character","She appears in Yazılıkaya reliefs both among the gods and among the goddesses, and one of her forms is shown as male, beardless and armed with a golden axe.","Šauška","en/d/sauska.html"],["Swivelling horns","According to ancient descriptions, the eale&#x27;s horns could turn in any direction, which was considered an advantage both in attack and in defence.","Eale","en/d/eale.html"],["Artemis&#x27;s two hounds","According to Callimachus, the goddess gave her two hunting dogs that helped her win a prize at the funeral games of Pelias.","Cyrene","en/d/cirene.html"],["A philosophical name","Berkeley&#x27;s Hylas takes his name from the Greek word hyle, meaning matter. He represents the materialist view that the author argues against.","Hylas","en/d/hilas.html"],["A different name for his wife","Pausanias, Lesches and the Cypria call Aeneas&#x27;s wife Eurydice, not Creusa.","Creusa","en/d/creusa.html"],["A modern city","Janasthana, the region&#x27;s former capital, is identified with the present-day city of Nashik in Maharashtra.","Danda","en/d/danda.html"],["A god in the form of a sword","In Scythia Ares was worshipped in the form of a sword, to which horses and cattle were sacrificed.","Ares","en/d/ares.html"],["A name with meaning","The name Nesaea comes from Greek and means &quot;the dweller on islands&quot;, a reference to her role as the Nereid of the islands.","Nesaea","en/d/nesaea.html"],["A name from his horns","The boy is born with deer horns, and his name literally means &#x27;horn sage&#x27;.","Rishyasringa","en/d/rishyasringa.html"],["Ozomene","Hyginus is the only author who calls the mother of Iris and the Harpies Ozomene, a name meaning &quot;of strong branches&quot; that appears in no other source.","Electra","en/d/electra-q1438693.html"],["A conquistador with a solar name","According to accounts of the conquest, the Mexica called the Spaniard Pedro de Alvarado Tonatiuh, because of his fair hair and a appearance that reminded them of the sun.","Tonatiuh","en/d/tonatiuh.html"],["Parallels in other cultures","Entities from Egypt, Greece, Britain and Siberia are also linked to sunset, fog or glory, showing common themes across different traditions.","Ayauhteotl","en/d/ayauhteotl.html"],["Devoured dreams","A Rigveda hymn asks Dawn to drive away bad dreams. Trita eats them and wears them on his body as ornaments.","Trita","en/d/trita.html"],["A early birth","Eurystheus was born after seven months, a premature birth that the stories attribute to Hera&#x27;s intervention.","Sthenelus","en/d/estenelo.html"],["A genus of sponges","The name Amphimedon also designates a genus of sponges.","Amphimedon","en/d/anfimedonte.html"],["Helmet with horns","The glossary Cóir Anmann places his horns on his helmet: two of silver and one of gold.","Furbaide Ferbend","en/d/furbaide-ferbend.html"],["Bells with their own voice","Each bell has a distinctive sound, so the racket, though chaotic, combines a wide range of tones.","Zarramaco","en/d/zarramaco.html"],["Born the same night","According to tradition, Polydamas was born on the same night as Hector, with whom he shared a close bond in the story.","Polydamas","en/d/polidamante.html"],["Vishnu&#x27;s names","The texts give him hundreds of epithets. The Vishnu Sahasranama, included in the Mahabharata, lists over a thousand names describing his qualities.","Vishnu","en/d/visnu.html"],["Dead skin","In Greek, gēras could also mean &quot;dead skin&quot; or &quot;the slough of a snake&quot;, a sense that captures the idea of something shed with time.","Geras","en/d/geras.html"],["Popular name","Dylan is one of the most popular traditional Welsh names for boys in Wales.","Dylan ail Don","en/d/dylan-ail-don.html"],["A defeated mountain","In Corinna&#x27;s account, Helicon, defeated in the singing contest, tears off a rock and smashes it into ten thousand stones.","Ourea","en/d/ourea.html"],["A visitor with news","In the Andes, the Ekeko figure played a similar role: he arrived with goods hanging from his body and announced a night of entertainment and trade.","Kokopelli","en/d/kokopelli.html"],["A name with two meanings","In Sanskrit, yama means &#x27;twin&#x27; but also &#x27;prohibition&#x27; and &#x27;restraint&#x27;, which explains his later role as king of prohibition.","Yama","en/d/iama.html"],["A town named after him","The Irish town of Maynooth takes its name from Nuada. In Irish it is called Maigh Nuad, the plain of Nuada.","Nuada","en/d/nuada.html"],["A toothless god","In later versions, Pushan lost his teeth in a dispute over a sacrifice with Rudra or Shiva. The Brahmanas say he eats a grain gruel because of it, and he is called karambhād.","Pushan","en/d/pushan.html"],["An animal left off the Ark","According to the legend, Noah decided not to take the creature aboard because it was dangerous to the other animals.","The Peluda (La Velue)","en/d/la-peluda.html"],["A Sumerian name","His name comes from Sumerian and means &#x27;lord of Susa&#x27;. It is a loanword with a shortened form that is rarely found in that language.","Inshushinak","en/d/inshushinak.html"],["Statue at Delphi","The Argives dedicated a statue of Baton at Delphi, placed beside one of Amphiaraus.","Baton","en/d/bato-q3636218.html"],["A fall and a marriage","In one version, Podalirius healed Syrna, daughter of King Damaethus, after she fell from a roof. In gratitude, the king gave him her hand and rule over the peninsula.","Podalirius","en/d/podalirio.html"],["A lucky find","In Greek, an unexpected stroke of luck was called hermaion, a term that reflects the god&#x27;s association with the unforeseen and the favorable.","Hermes","en/d/hermes.html"],["A cave with two stories","The Lupercal was the sanctuary of Lupercus and also the place where, according to legend, Faustulus found the twins Romulus and Remus.","Lupercus","en/d/luperco.html"],["A spring named after a bird","The nearby spring was named Cissa, meaning jay. The name is tied to the story of the bird that guided Heracles.","Phialo","en/d/fialo.html"],["A king who may never have existed","Eratosthenes argued that no king named Busiris ever existed and that the story was a mockery of the city bearing his name.","Busiris","en/d/busiris.html"],["Vaccination posters","The international smallpox programme produced posters showing Śītalā with a vaccination needle, so the campaign would be accepted by local people.","Śītalā","en/d/sitala.html"],["A bay with its name","Members of Magellan&#x27;s expedition are thought to have named a bay on the Argentine coast Samborombón, attributing its nearly semicircular shape to a detachment from the mainland.","Saint Brendan's Island","en/d/isla-de-san-brandan.html"],["A shrine in waiting","In the Vaishno Devi tradition, the goddess waits in a cave on Trikuta mountain for Kalki&#x27;s arrival.","Kalki","en/d/kalki.html"],["A literary precedent","Stories of captured fish-men already circulated in Spain long before, and the second part of Lazarillo de Tormes tells how Lazaro is exhibited as a sea monster after being rescued from a storm.","The Fish-Man of Liérganes","en/d/el-hombre-pez-de-lierganes.html"],["A goddess turned into a knight","Loomis noted that Dôn, mother of Gilfaethwy, appears in Arthurian romance as a male character, Sir Don, father of Griflet.","Gilfaethwy","en/d/gilfaethwy.html"],["Grandsons of the wind","In The Tale of Igor&#x27;s Campaign the winds are called Stribog&#x27;s grandsons, one of the few family references we have for this god.","Stribog","en/d/stribog.html"],["A name that gets confused","Her name resembles that of the river god Strymon, who gives his name to a river in Thrace. They should not be confused: Strymo is a nymph, daughter of Scamander.","Strymo","en/d/estrimo.html"],["The white cat","In Irish folklore, her sister Clíodhna transformed her into a white cat.","Aibell","en/d/aibell.html"],["A plant with his name","In the herbal of Dioscorides, asterion refers to Silene linifolia, a plant that, according to Pausanias, grew near the Heraion of the Argolid.","Asterion","en/d/asterion.html"],["A metaphor for gold","The expression &quot;the speech of Þjazi, Gangr and Iði&quot; served in Old Norse as a way of referring to gold, based on the sharing of Olvaldi&#x27;s inheritance.","Olvaldi","en/d/olvaldi.html"],["Eleven-year cycle","According to the Cantabrian tale, the monuca is born only once every eleven years, in spring.","Monuca","en/d/monuca.html"],["A figure on vases","Some Attic vases show him with wings, as one of the little Erotes that accompany Aphrodite.","Phthonus","en/d/ptono.html"],["Roots in southern China","Derk Bodde linked the myth to the ancestral traditions of the Miao and Yao peoples of southern China. A flood story in which a couple survives inside a gourd appears among the Zhuang, whose names for the pair mean whetstone and gourd.","Pangu","en/d/pangu.html"],["A name with several readings","Varaha comes from the Sanskrit word for boar, but the Rigveda also uses it for rain clouds. The grammarian Yaska, around 300 BCE, linked it to a root meaning to tear up roots.","Varaha","en/d/varaja.html"],["A month named after a child god","Her festival, linked to the birth of child gods and the new harvest, was held especially in honour of Khonsu. The month of Pashons was named after him.","Renenutet","en/d/renenutet.html"],["A name with history","The Finnish word &quot;pukki&quot; comes from the Swedish &quot;bock&quot;, meaning deer or billy goat.","Joulupukki","en/d/joulupukki.html"],["A predicting fish","In 1855 an eel fisherman noticed unusually restless catfish shortly before the Edo earthquake, an anecdote considered the earliest record of catfish predicting quakes.","Namazu","en/d/namazu.html"],["A possible Faunus","Some scholars propose that Agrius is in fact the Roman god Faunus, a son of Circe according to Nonnus of Panopolis.","Agrius","en/d/agrio-q2827213.html"],["An island under debate","Since ancient times scholars have debated which island in the Bay of Cadiz corresponds to Erytheia. Modern topographical and geological studies have confirmed the existence of several islands mentioned in ancient sources.","Erytheia","en/d/eritea-q1363689.html"],["Origin of the name","The Greeks explained the place name Pytho by the rotting of the serpent&#x27;s corpse. The Greek verb pythesthai means to rot.","Python","en/d/piton.html"],["Brothers in the Kalika Purana","The <em>Kalika Purana</em> presents him as a son of Bhagadatta alongside Pushpadatta, who shares the same lineage.","Vajradatta","en/d/vajradatta.html"],["A Tudor rose on the table","The Winchester Round Table was repainted in 1522 by order of Henry VIII, who had his portrait placed where King Arthur&#x27;s would be, with a red Tudor rose at the centre.","Round Table","en/d/mesa-redonda.html"],["Golden teeth","His name Gullintanni literally means the one with the golden teeth, and his smile was said to shine like the sun.","Heimdall","en/d/heimdal.html"],["Barnum&#x27;s rewards","The showman P. T. Barnum offered rewards for the monster in 1873 and 1887, hoping to exhibit it.","Champ","en/d/champ.html"],["A fatal peach tree","His fall from a peach tree, while reaching for fruit, is what opened the way to his immortality.","Han Xiangzi","en/d/han-xiangzi.html"],["Cauterizing the Hydra","Iolaus burned the severed necks of the Hydra with a torch to stop new heads from growing.","Iolaus","en/d/yolao.html"],["A famous crow","Pliny the Elder tells of a cobbler in the Roman Forum who had a tame crow that became a favourite among young Romans. When a rival of the cobbler killed it, the rival was executed and the bird received a public funeral in the field of Rediculus.","Rediculus","en/d/rediculus.html"],["A custom creation","Tilottama was made by Vishvakarma, the divine craftsman, at Brahma&#x27;s request, with the sole purpose of provoking rivalry between the two brothers.","Sunda and Upasunda","en/d/sunda-y-upasunda.html"],["A hero without known descendants","Pausanias notes that no inhabitant of Epidaurus claimed to descend from the hero who gave the city its name.","Epidaurus","en/d/epidauro.html"],["House of weapons","His temples were hung with weapons, which gave them the nickname by which they were known.","Ḫaldi","en/d/haldi.html"],["A single source","Molus appears only in Apollodorus, who mentions him in passing while discussing the family of Aeolus.","Molus","en/d/molo.html"],["Theft at Rocamadour","A replica sword embedded in a rock face at Rocamadour was reported stolen in June 2024.","Durendal","en/d/durandarte.html"],["A different name","Dionysius of Halicarnassus uses the name &quot;Tyrrhenus&quot; instead of Turnus, and makes him a nephew of Amata, Latinus&#x27;s wife.","Turnus","en/d/turno.html"],["Patron of a dynasty","Her worship was very popular in northern India during the Pala Empire, which adopted her as a protective deity. According to the Tibetan historian Tārānātha, Gopala I, the dynasty&#x27;s founder, was one of her devotees.","Cundī","en/d/cundi.html"],["Five bats","Zhong Kui often appears with five bats, which represent the five blessings of fortune.","Zhong Kui","en/d/zhong-kui.html"],["A whetstone in the head","According to Snorri, the whetstone lodged in Thor&#x27;s head is still there, which is why it is forbidden to throw a whetstone across the floor, since that stirs it in Thor&#x27;s skull.","Aurvandill","en/d/aurvandill.html"],["One hundred and eight heads","Her garland is often said to hold 108 demon heads, a sacred number in Hinduism and the same number of beads on a japa mala, the rosary used to repeat mantras.","Mahakali","en/d/mahakali.html"],["A living island","In the Legend of Saint Brendan, the monks celebrate Easter on the back of Jasconius, and the creature wakes when they light a fire.","Aspidochelone","en/d/aspidoquelonio.html"],["A Roman dedication","The altar was commissioned by a Roman centurion, not a local worshipper. It reveals the meeting of Roman tradition with the Celtic deities of the area.","Gontia","en/d/gontia.html"],["Cosmic pores","Tradition holds that many universes sprout from Mahavishnu&#x27;s pores, like seeds that later become cosmic eggs.","Mahavishnu","en/d/mahavisnu.html"],["A ‘round’ ring","Tolkien wrote to his publisher that the only similarity between his ring and Andvaranaut was that both were round, rejecting the usual comparisons.","Andvaranaut","en/d/andvaranaut.html"],["Disputed name","Epiphanius suggested Norea was a distortion of Pyrrha, wife of Deucalion, based on a link to <em>nura</em>, the Syriac word for fire.","Norea","en/d/norea.html"],["Four heavens","Pedro Cieza de León recorded that some Indigenous people spoke of &quot;four great heavens&quot;. Other sources mention stratifications of eight or even thirteen levels.","Hanan Pacha","en/d/hanan-pacha.html"],["The Bamiyan Buddhas","The largest of the Bamiyan Buddha statues, destroyed in Afghanistan by the Taliban, also depicted Vairochana.","Vairocana","en/d/vairochana.html"],["The constellation Hydra","The logogram dMUŠ was used for Nirah and other gods, and with a different determinative, mulMUŠ, it referred to the constellation Hydra.","Nirah","en/d/nirah.html"],["Two companions","The Mnevis bull was entitled to two cows as companions, representing Hathor and Iusaaset.","Mnevis","en/d/mnevis.html"],["A name tied to a trade","Sauti means &#x27;son of a suta&#x27;, though he was not one himself. The suta were charioteers and royal bards charged with remembering the deeds of the king.","Sauti","en/d/suta-goswami.html"],["Chariot of dragons","According to early commentary, the first illustrations showed He Bo on a chariot pulled by two dragons, riding through the clouds in every direction.","He Bo","en/d/he-bo.html"],["A name tied to Easter","The dwarf planet was discovered shortly after Easter 2005, and its code name was Easterbunny. The first European contact with the island also took place on Easter Sunday, in 1722.","Makemake","en/d/make-make.html"],["Identification with Durga","Kotavi is identified with the goddess Durga, linking her to one of the great female figures of Hinduism.","Kotavi","en/d/kotavi.html"],["A stone in the head","Shards of Hrungnir&#x27;s whetstone stayed lodged permanently in Thor&#x27;s head, according to the Prose Edda.","Gróa","en/d/groa.html"],["Silver hand","The epithet <em>llaw ereint</em> is usually translated as “of the silver hand”, though <em>ereint</em> has also been read as “silver cup”. Tolkien saw in the epithet an echo of the magic hand of Nodens.","Lludd Llaw Eraint","en/d/lludd-llaw-eraint.html"],["Fifty daughters","According to Pausanias, Selene had fifty daughters with Endymion, the menai, often read as the fifty lunar months of an Olympiad.","Selene","en/d/selene.html"],["A snake with his name","The Aesculapian snake, a non-venomous Mediterranean species, is named after the god.","Asclepius","en/d/asclepio.html"],["A name of height","Hyperion derives from the Greek preposition <em>hypér</em>, &#x27;above&#x27;, so his name roughly means &#x27;the one above&#x27;. Homer sometimes uses it as an epithet of Helios.","Hyperion","en/d/hiperion.html"],["One name, several meanings","Her Greek name, <em>steropē</em>, is linked to lightning, while Asterope means starred face, which fits her transformation into a star.","Sterope","en/d/esterope.html"],["Alternate kanji form","An alternate kanji spelling, 児啼爺, uses ji (児, infant) instead of ko, emphasizing the creature&#x27;s infant-like cry rather than its origin as a child spirit.","Konaki-jiji","en/d/konaki-jiji.html"],["Diamond pillars","According to the texts, the pillars of Amaravati are made of diamonds and its furniture is pure gold.","Amaravati","en/d/amaravati.html"],["A meaningful name","Her name is interpreted as &#x27;giver of reason&#x27; or &#x27;richness of mind&#x27;, a reference to thought rather than to the sea.","Polynoe","en/d/polinoe.html"],["A tea with a soul","According to Elison and Smith, Tsukumogami was the name of an animated tea caddy that Matsunaga Hisahide used to bargain for peace with Oda Nobunaga.","Tsukumogami","en/d/tsukumogami.html"],["A name with variants","Medieval manuscripts of the <em>Germania</em> record several forms of the name, such as Nertum or Herthum. The reading Hertha was popular until the Codex Aesinas, discovered in 1902, confirmed Nerthus.","Nerthus","en/d/nerthus.html"],["A name about ploughing","His name means something like &#x27;he of the plough handle&#x27;. He is compared with Bouzyges, a hero regarded as the founder of agriculture, who was shown with oxen and a plough.","Echetlus","en/d/equetleo.html"],["Food of the Arcadians","Pelasgus taught people to eat acorns from oak trees, the food by which the Arcadians were known.","Pelasgus","en/d/pelasgo.html"],["A luminous name","Phaenon means &#x27;bright&#x27; or &#x27;shining&#x27;, a fitting word for a star visible to the naked eye.","Phaenon","en/d/fainon.html"],["A female sun","In the Baltic languages the Sun is feminine, and in Latvian folk songs she appears as a mother who comforts orphans.","Saulė","en/d/saule.html"],["A place name","The town of Bargasa, in Caria, was said to have received its name from Bargasus, according to the tradition recorded by Apollonius of Aphrodisias.","Bargasus","en/d/bargasus.html"],["Fingernail hat","Vanatühi sometimes wears a hat made of fingernails that makes him invisible.","Vanapagan (Vanatühi)","en/d/vanapagan-vanatuhi.html"],["A proverb from a fable","The Spanish saying «la mentira no tiene pies» comes from Phaedrus&#x27;s story of Dolus&#x27;s figure without feet.","Dolus","en/d/dolos.html"],["The 1988 expedition","In 1988, a member of the village council of Shimokitayama, in Nara, organised an expedition to search for it, offering one million yen for a live one. It lasted until 1990 and found no proof that the creature existed.","Tsuchinoko","en/d/tsuchinoko.html"],["One name, several goddesses","Charis can refer to any of the Charites in general, but in the sources it also appears as a specific goddess with her own name.","Charis","en/d/charis.html"],["A possible contrast of colour","The scholar James Diggle notes that in Euripides&#x27; play Merops may have been dark-skinned, while Clymene and Phaethon appear as white. Another reading holds that the whole palace was portrayed as white in contrast with its dark-skinned subjects, a theory Diggle finds unconvincing.","Merops","en/d/merops.html"],["An armed jackal","At Dendera she is shown as an upright jackal brandishing knives, an unusual image in Egyptian funerary art.","Anput","en/d/anput.html"],["A name shared by kings","Three pharaohs of the Twelfth Dynasty took her name in the form Senusret, and one ruler of the Second Intermediate Period also incorporated it.","Wosret","en/d/usert.html"],["Telling name","Her Greek name is linked to the verb meaning to conceal, and the Etymologicum Magnum reads it as &quot;she who conceals knowledge&quot;.","Calypso","en/d/calipso.html"],["The good striker","The name combines the Gaulish prefix su- (&#x27;good&#x27;) with a root meaning &#x27;to strike&#x27;, which links it directly to his hammer.","Sucellus","en/d/sucellos.html"],["A king turned into a serpent","Movses Khorenatsi linked him with Astyages, king of the Medes, in an identification that mixes history with legend.","Azhdahak","en/d/azhdahak.html"],["A squeaky sandal","According to Philostratus, the only fault Momus found in Aphrodite was that her sandals squeaked, a joke about her chattiness.","Momus","en/d/momo.html"],["Scheuchzer&#x27;s dragons","Copperplate drawings of supposed Alpine dragons published by Johann Jakob Scheuchzer in 1723 are known as Scheuchzer&#x27;s dragons, although the original testimonies were recorded by Wagner.","Tatzelwurm","en/d/tatzelwurm.html"],["A name that describes her role","The Greek noun angelia means message, news, mission or proclamation, so her name is already a definition of her role.","Angelia","en/d/angelia.html"],["A hen to the conquistadors","During the conquest, the Spanish did not call this bird a guajolote but a gallina, or hen. They likened it to the pavón, a large bird of the gallinaceous family.","Chalchiuhtotolin","en/d/chalchiuhtotolin.html"],["A goddess with three faces","In Uruk she was worshipped in three forms: morning Inanna, evening Inanna and princely Inanna. The first two reflected the phases of Venus as morning and evening star.","Ishtar","en/d/ishtar.html"],["A large family","The accounts give Antenor and Theano fifteen children besides Polybus, making their line one of the largest in the Trojan War.","Polybus","en/d/polibo-q3266310.html"],["A necklace for the underworld","Gilgamesh does not offer the necklace for himself but for his dead friend, asking the gods to welcome him.","Hušbišag","en/d/husbisag.html"],["A name with several readings","The name has been translated as &#x27;anger striker&#x27; or &#x27;sudden striker&#x27;. In some Icelandic rune poems it appears as a poetic term for &#x27;arrow&#x27; or &#x27;bow&#x27;, with the sense of a striker that travels.","Fárbauti","en/d/farbauti.html"],["Five hundred hooks","To make up for the loss, Hoori broke his sword and forged its fragments into five hundred fishhooks, which Hoderi rejected.","Hoderi","en/d/hoderi.html"],["A king in Russian chronicles","Olof von Dalin linked Erik with Rurik, a Rus&#x27; chieftain. This identification is a modern hypothesis, not a fact from the sources.","Erik Björnsson","en/d/erik-bjornsson.html"],["A borrowed name","His name is probably derived from <em>Väinämöinen</em>, a figure of the Finnish <em>Kalevala</em>.","Vanemuine","en/d/vanemuine.html"],["Horns of Ammon","Images of Amun sometimes had small ram&#x27;s horns, known as the Horns of Ammon.","Amun","en/d/amon.html"],["Ten square feet","The term fangzhang, used in Chan monasteries for the abbot, means one square zhang, about ten square feet, a reference to Vimalakirti&#x27;s stone room.","Vimalakirti","en/d/vimalakirti.html"],["A heart of stars","According to this tradition, the stars below Orion&#x27;s Belt formed a heart that served as an image of the model of the universe.","Yohualtecuhtli","en/d/yohualtecuhtli.html"],["Muses at Delphi","At Delphi the three muses were named after the strings of the lyre: Nete, Mese and Hypate.","Melete","en/d/meletea.html"],["Seven protective scorpions","In the myth of Horus&#x27;s birth, seven scorpions considered emanations of Serket protect Isis and her unborn son from danger.","Serket","en/d/serket.html"],["Sons of Poseidon","Tyro had Pelias and Neleus with the god Poseidon before marrying Cretheus, who raised them as his own sons.","Cretheus","en/d/creteo.html"],["A long name","Other sources give him the full name Masakatsu-akatsukachi-hayahi-ame-no-oshihomimi, roughly &#x27;truly I have won with rushing might, ruling the grand rice ears of heaven&#x27;.","Amenooshihomimi","en/d/amenooshihomimi.html"],["A colony with his name","The Greek colony of Epizephyrian Locris in southern Italy bears a name linked to the west, the same root as the god&#x27;s name.","Zephyrus","en/d/cefiro.html"],["Heimdallr&#x27;s name","Snorri Sturluson calls Heimdallr the Seeker of Freyja&#x27;s Necklace, a title that comes from this theft episode.","Brísingamen","en/d/brisingamen.html"],["A name with many readings","Scholars have proposed that Rauni is a goddess, an epithet of Ukko, a fertility god, or even a name coined by Agricola.","Rauni","en/d/rauni.html"],["A rich and stingy brother","According to Hyginus, Plutus and Philomelus quarrelled because Plutus, the richer one, never shared his fortune.","Plutus","en/d/pluto.html"],["A name with a pun","The Greek name of the stone curlew, <em>óknos</em>, is linked to the idea that Autonous did not arrive in time to save his son.","Autonous","en/d/autonoo.html"],["A name with meaning","Oedipus means &#x27;swollen feet&#x27;, a reference to the injuries to his ankles when he was abandoned as a baby.","Oedipus","en/d/edipo.html"],["A violent ritual","During the Matralia, a single enslaved woman was beaten and driven from the area by the freeborn women.","Matuta","en/d/matuta.html"],["A name with real human use","The name Fjolvar also appears as a human personal name on the Viking Age runestone Hs 6 at Järvsö, Sweden, where a Fjolvar is named as the mother of Harlau. This is a historical usage, not the mythological figure.","Fjölvar","en/d/fjolvar.html"],["Steel against spirits","Among the Beni Ahsen it is believed that steel knives and needles drive her away, a belief that does not apply to other beings in the tradition.","Aisha Kandisha","en/d/aisha-kandisha.html"],["A lost play","Much of what is known about the character comes from Alcmaeon in Corinth, a tragedy by Euripides of which only fragments survive.","Tisiphone","en/d/tisifone-q2436714.html"],["Words that appear once","Both Kiyyun and Remphan are hapax legomena, meaning they occur only once in the surviving sources.","Remphan","en/d/renfan.html"],["A carved harbour","According to legend, two taniwha carved out Wellington&#x27;s harbour. The petrified remains of one of them formed the hill overlooking the city.","Taniwha","en/d/taniwha.html"],["Many spellings","The name is written in many ways, such as Njord, Njordr, Niord or Njördr, and in English it sometimes appears as Njorth.","Njörðr","en/d/njorr.html"],["Wife of an Athenian hero","An Athenian tradition made her the wife of Antiochus, the hero who gave his name to one of the ten tribes of Athens.","Pandia","en/d/pandia.html"],["A name with meaning","The name Telamon means “broad strap” in Ancient Greek.","Telamon","en/d/telamon.html"],["A dog on the altars","The dog appears on thirteen of the twenty-one altars catalogued by Ada Hondius-Crone, who described it as a kind of greyhound.","Nehalennia","en/d/nehalennia.html"],["Another name","Besides Coön, this Trojan prince is also called Cynon in some traditions.","Coön","en/d/ceon.html"],["Christian rímur","Some Icelandic rímur written after the Reformation, with Christian themes, still used kennings based on the mead to refer to poetry.","Mead of Poetry","en/d/hidromiel-de-la-poesia.html"],["A play on words","Ovid plays on the resemblance between stellio, the lizard, and stellatus, meaning starred, alluding to the creature&#x27;s spots.","Ascalabus","en/d/ascalabo.html"],["A name with meaning","His name is generally understood to mean “Iranian”, pointing to his role as the legendary progenitor of the Iranians.","Iraj","en/d/iraj.html"],["A river named after a son","The Theban river Ismenus took its name from Ismenus, the son that Pausanias attributes to Melia and Apollo.","Melia","en/d/melia-q785598.html"],["A name in Dalmatia","A dedication found in Dalmatia mentions «Jupiter Tanaris», confirming that a form of the name without the usual metathesis existed.","Taranis","en/d/taranis.html"],["Koschei and his egg","In the tales, Koschei the Deathless&#x27;s soul is not in his body but hidden in an egg kept in an oak on Buyan.","Buyan","en/d/buyan.html"],["King of the snakes","He was called “king of the snakes” and was invoked in incantations against snakebite, many of them written in Elamite and Hurrian.","Ninazu","en/d/ninazu.html"],["A famous grove","Homer calls Poseidon&#x27;s grove at Onchestus &#x27;bright&#x27; in the Catalogue of Ships in the Iliad.","Onchestus","en/d/onquesto.html"],["A name of Durga","According to some lexicographers, Sinivali is one of the names of the goddess Durga, showing how different deities came to share epithets.","Sinivali","en/d/sinivali.html"],["A recent discovery","In 2007 a white creature labeled as nurikabe was identified in a yōkai picture scroll held by Brigham Young University, which changed its popular image.","Nurikabe","en/d/nurikabe.html"],["The meaning of her name","Eurydice comes from Greek words meaning something like &quot;wide justice&quot;, formed from <em>eurys</em> (wide) and <em>dike</em> (justice).","Eurydice of Mycenae","en/d/euridice-de-micenas.html"],["Coins of the Social War","Coins depicting the event were minted in 89 BC, during the Social War between Rome and its Italian allies, suggesting the story regained relevance in that context.","Rape of the Sabine women","en/d/rapto-de-las-sabinas.html"],["God of the privy","An editor of An Encyclopaedia of Plants, published in 1836, claimed that Sterculius was the god of the privy, from the Latin word for excrement.","Sterquilinus","en/d/sterquilinus.html"],["A temple at Srirangam","According to regional legend, Vibhishana was carrying the image of Ranganatha to Lanka, but could not lift it after stopping on the banks of the Kaveri, and Vishnu chose to stay there.","Vibhishana","en/d/vibhisana.html"],["A name with several readings","The <em>Coffin Texts</em> offer two readings of his name: &quot;the sistrum player&quot; and &quot;calf&quot;.","Ihy","en/d/ihy.html"],["Bees named after him","A species of bee carries the name of Meliteus. The link comes from the episode in which a swarm fed him as an infant.","Meliteus","en/d/meliteo.html"],["A statue of Persian marble","The statue at Rhamnous was carved from Parian marble that the Persians had brought to raise a monument to their expected victory. In the end, the stone was used to honour the goddess instead.","Nemesis","en/d/nemesis.html"],["A constellation named after him","Mesopotamian astronomers recognised a constellation named Zababa, whose stars were linked to parts of his body. The &quot;Crown of Zababa&quot; and the &quot;Eye of Zababa&quot; are two of the documented examples.","Zababa","en/d/zababa.html"],["A ghostly procession","During the Greco-Persian Wars, a ghostly procession was supposedly heard advancing from Eleusis crying &quot;Iacchus&quot;. It was read as a sign of the Greek victory at Salamis.","Iacchus","en/d/yaco.html"],["Name of the transfer","His name Sankarshana means &#x27;completely dragged away&#x27; and refers to the transfer of his embryo from Devaki&#x27;s womb to Rohini&#x27;s.","Balarama","en/d/balarama.html"],["A name in a video game","The Galm Team squadron in <em>Ace Combat Zero</em> takes its name from Garm. The form Galm is a mistransliteration into English, due to the single liquid sound in Japanese.","Garm","en/d/garm.html"],["An Antarctic island","An island in Antarctica is named Ogygia after the mythical island.","Ogygia","en/d/ogigia.html"],["A long reign","According to the account, Saraṇaṅkara ruled for 7,000 years before giving up the throne to become an ascetic.","Saraṇaṅkara","en/d/saranankara.html"],["Sixty-year cycle","Combining the twelve animals with the five elements gives a sixty-year cycle. 2018 was an Earth Dog year, indicated by the characters 戊戌.","Dog","en/d/perro.html"],["Three deadly animals","Her body combines crocodile, lion and hippopotamus, the three most dangerous animals of the Nile to the ancient Egyptians, suggesting that no one could escape her even in the afterlife.","Ammit","en/d/ammit.html"],["A cursed umbrella","According to modern yōkai literature, whoever steals an amefurikozō&#x27;s umbrella will be unable to take it off afterwards.","Amefurikozō","en/d/amefurikozo.html"],["The Elarion cave","The cave through which Tityos was believed to have reached the surface lay on Euboea and was called the Elarion, in honour of his mother.","Elara","en/d/elara.html"],["Origin of lightning","Tsetse Bumba caused trouble on earth, so Mbombo chased her into the sky, where she became the thunderbolt.","Mbombo","en/d/mbombo.html"],["Many names","The custom goes by dozens of names depending on the people and region, such as Peperuda in Romania or Prporuša in Croatia.","Dodola and Perperuna","en/d/dodola-y-perperuna.html"],["Two possible roots","Scholar Xosé Lluis García Arias proposes the Arabic wahsh, &#x27;beast&#x27;, while Xulio Concepción links the name to Latin hostem, &#x27;enemy&#x27;.","Guajona","en/d/guajona.html"],["First words","According to some stories, his first words were that his feet had wandered through the purple palace and that his name was recorded in the capital of the Jade Emperor.","Zhongli Quan","en/d/zhongli-quan.html"],["The Donkeys","A part of the constellation Cancer is called the Donkeys. According to the story, when Jupiter declared war on the Giants, Liber, Vulcan, satyrs and silenoi answered his call riding donkeys.","Vulcan","en/d/vulcano.html"],["Origin of the name","The name combines the Greek words <em>peithō</em>, &#x27;to persuade&#x27;, and <em>dikē</em>, &#x27;custom&#x27; or &#x27;justice&#x27;.","Pisidice","en/d/pisidice-q1731974.html"],["Teeth like pomegranates","Her name refers to the colour of her teeth, likened to pomegranate blossoms. Some accounts say she lives in a pomegranate tree, and her teeth recall the red seed pods of its fruit.","Raktadantika","en/d/raktadantika.html"],["A name that is also a ship","The Prose Edda also lists Sessrúmnir among ship names. This has opened a debate about whether it refers to a hall, a ship, or both.","Sessrúmnir","en/d/sessrumnir.html"],["A helper with a past","In some stories from the Netherlands and Flanders, the Zwarte Piet themselves were kidnapped as children, and those kidnapped children become the next generation of helpers.","The Sack Man","en/d/el-hombre-del-saco.html"],["Cities named after twins","According to Hegesipus, the twin nymphs Palene and Retea, daughters of Anchirrhoe, gave their names to two cities.","Anchinoe","en/d/anquinoe.html"],["The Seven Hathors","Egyptian texts often speak of the goddess&#x27;s manifestations as the Seven Hathors, and sometimes of many more, up to 362.","Hathor","en/d/hathor.html"],["Several names","Orseis also appears as Otreis or Otreide in a scholium, and Vitruvius names her Phthia.","Orseis","en/d/orseis.html"],["Hattian name","In the Hattian language she was called Tappinu, meaning &#x27;her daughter&#x27;, a way of naming her through her relationship with the Sun goddess of Arinna.","Mezulla","en/d/mezulla.html"],["A presidential name","In 1942 President Franklin Roosevelt named his retreat Shangri-La, and Eisenhower renamed it Camp David in 1953.","Shangri-La","en/d/shangri-la.html"],["Wine jar seals","Old Kingdom wine jars carried seals reading &quot;I am refreshed by this Ash&quot;, one of the most common mentions of the god.","Ash","en/d/ash.html"],["A respected old coyote","In Aztec culture the coyote symbolized cunning, worldly wisdom and male beauty, so the nickname &quot;old coyote&quot; carried a positive meaning.","Huehuecoyotl","en/d/huehuecoyotl.html"],["A hole in the roof","In the Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus, Terminus&#x27;s stone sat beneath a small opening that let it face the sky.","Terminus","en/d/termino.html"],["A mistranslated name","For decades, missionaries and dictionaries rendered Èṣù as &quot;devil&quot; or &quot;Satan&quot;. The term was corrected after online campaigns and academic work.","Eshu","en/d/eshu.html"],["Another name","At least two ancient authors call her Perimede, an alternative form of her name.","Agamede","en/d/agamede.html"],["A vulnerability with his name","A privilege escalation flaw in the sudo program was named &quot;Baron Samedit&quot;, combining the loa&#x27;s name with sudoedit, the vulnerable application.","Baron Samedi","en/d/baron-samedi.html"],["A name meaning cloud","Her name comes from Old Norse mistr, meaning cloud or mist. This is the proposal of the scholar Rudolf Simek.","Mist","en/d/mist.html"],["A sacred oath","In the <em>Helgakviða Hundingsbana II</em>, the river <em>Leiptr</em> is where loyalty was sworn, and breaking that oath brings the curse of the valkyrie Sigrún.","Élivágar","en/d/elivagar.html"],["A Lydian people","The Meiones, whom Talaemenes&#x27; sons led, were a people of Lydia. Their city corresponds to today&#x27;s Sardis.","Talaemenes","en/d/talaemenes.html"],["The Lapwing Palette","A slate palette fragment found near Sais shows a barque with a lapwing on its bow. Its exact place of origin is unknown.","Rekhyt","en/d/rekhyt.html"],["Bristles that glow","According to the account, the bristles in the boar’s mane glowed in the dark, and their light lit even the darkest worlds.","Gullinbursti","en/d/gullinbursti.html"],["A second Parrhasius","Another Parrhasius, twin brother of Lycastus and son of Ares and Phylonome, was cast into the river Erymanthus and survived after a wolf suckled him. The shepherd Gyliphus raised him.","Parrhasius","en/d/parrasio.html"],["A prophetic dream","Croesus dreamed that his son would die from an iron spearpoint, so he removed all the weapons from his palace.","Adrastus","en/d/adrasto.html"],["The Distant Goddess","In one myth, the Eye of Ra flees Egypt for Nubia, where it turns into a lioness. When it returns to Ra, it either becomes Menhit or gives birth to her, depending on the version.","Menhit","en/d/menhit.html"],["A name with poison","In Sanskrit, sa means &#x27;with&#x27; and gara &#x27;poison&#x27;, which gives the king his name.","Sagara","en/d/sagara.html"],["A ladle with a hole","In many versions, the key to the tale is a simple wooden ladle. Punching out its bottom is enough to save the ship.","Umibōzu","en/d/umibozu.html"],["A ferryman in disguise","In Hárbarðsljóð, the name is applied to a supposed master who turns out to be Odin disguised as a ferryman.","Hildólfr","en/d/hildolfr.html"],["Variable name","Depending on the manuscript, her name may be written as Erinoma, Erínome, Eritoma or Eurínome.","Erinona","en/d/erinona.html"],["A rock at the isthmus","According to the sources, the boulder Alcyoneus hurled at Heracles could still be seen at the Isthmus of Corinth until fairly recent times.","Alcyoneus","en/d/alcioneo.html"],["A cannibal banquet","Nonnus recalls that Lycaon served Zeus a dish made from his own grandson Nyctimus, cut into pieces, and that the god ate it without knowing what it was.","Nyctimus","en/d/nictimo.html"],["A rabbit instead of a spider","Joel Chandler Harris rewrote several Anansi tales with a rabbit as the main character instead.","Anansi","en/d/anansi.html"],["A Norwegian island","The small island of Lauer, in southeastern Norway, owes its name to Laufey.","Laufey","en/d/laufey.html"],["A May feast","The Irish celebrated the &#x27;Baltené&#x27; on 1 May, one of their great feasts, which is believed to have come from the cult of this deity.","Belenus","en/d/belenus.html"],["A shrine for Hyrnetho","Deiphontes built a sanctuary to his wife at Epidaurus, and the place came to be known as Hyrnethion.","Deiphontes","en/d/deifontes.html"],["A name with several readings","The name could mean &#x27;rock&#x27; or &#x27;whitebeam&#x27;, and the same root appears in Alesia, a town further north in Burgundy.","Alisanos","en/d/alisanos.html"],["A whale that traps fish","In 2023 scientists observed whales that stay still at the surface with their jaws open, waiting for fish to swim in. They propose this behaviour may have inspired the hafgufa myth.","Hafgufa","en/d/hafgufa.html"],["Transcendent horses","Hofvarpnir and the eight-legged horse Sleipnir are cited as examples of horses that can move between worlds and link earth and sky.","Hofvarpnir","en/d/hofvarpnir.html"],["A sun with arms","The texts compare Lugh&#x27;s face to the sun, and Balor is astonished that the sun rises in the west, as the druids explain it is the radiance of Lugh&#x27;s face.","Lugh","en/d/lugh.html"],["A festival named after him","The Ecdysia of Phaistos took its name from the Greek verb meaning to undress, referring to the garment Leucippus could remove after his transformation.","Leucippus","en/d/leucipo-q1472590.html"],["Women&#x27;s games","Pausanias credits Hippodamia with founding the Heraean Games, competitions reserved for women and held in honour of Hera.","Hippodamia","en/d/hipodamia.html"],["A painting by Onasias","Pausanias cites a work by Onasias in which Euryganeia appears grieving over the feud between her two sons.","Hyperphas","en/d/hiperfante.html"],["A name with two readings","His name is usually translated as «mighty weaver», though some interpret it as «mighty in riddles».","Vafþrúðnir","en/d/vafrunir.html"],["A horse with an imperial epitaph","The emperor Hadrian wrote an epitaph for his horse, which he named Borysthenes. The text survives, a rare example of a written tribute to an animal.","Borysthenes","en/d/boristenes.html"],["A river that splits","Herodotus describes the river of Plataea dividing into two branches about three stadia apart before rejoining into a single channel.","Oeroe","en/d/oeroe.html"],["Spartan origin","A modern legend places Sabus at the head of colonists arriving from Sparta. It is an unproven tradition that does not appear in the ancient sources.","Sabus","en/d/sabo.html"],["A supreme name","Bahá&#x27;ís consider the highest name of God to be All-Glorious, or Bahá in Arabic. From this root come Bahá&#x27;u&#x27;lláh, meaning The Glory of God, and Bahá&#x27;í, meaning Follower of the All-Glorious.","God in the Bahá'í Faith","en/d/dios-en-la-fe-baha-i.html"],["Another name for his mother","In some accounts Euxantius&#x27;s mother appears under the name Dexione instead of Dexithea.","Euxantius","en/d/euxantio.html"],["Eagle on coins","The image of the Eagle of Zeus was widely used on coins of the Hellenistic period.","Eagle of Zeus","en/d/aguila-de-zeus.html"],["A visit in the Underworld","According to the <em>Odyssey</em>, Odysseus saw Jocasta during his journey to Hades, after her death.","Jocasta","en/d/yocasta.html"],["A protective she-bear","Gómez-Tabanera linked the figure to the ancient myth of the human bear, related to the legend of Doriga, in which a girl lost in the forest is cared for by a she-bear.","Busgosu","en/d/busgosu.html"],["The first funeral games","According to the account, the games held after Azan&#x27;s death were the first in history, and during them Aetolus accidentally killed Apis.","Azan","en/d/azan.html"],["The first gendered name","According to the Kojiki, Umashiashikabihikoji was the first deity of creation to have a gendered name.","Umashiashikabihikoji-no-Kami","en/d/umashiashikabihikoji-no-kami.html"],["Tiberius&#x27;s crown","In the Roman era, Emperor Tiberius appears on Egyptian temple walls wearing Onuris&#x27;s four-plumed crown.","Onuris","en/d/onuris.html"],["A mother who changes the story","In the Jaimini version, Chitrangada is the daughter of a Gandharva king and leaves her son to join Yudhishthira, so Ulupi raises him.","Babhruvahana","en/d/babhruvahana.html"],["Year of the Little Dragon","Snake years are sometimes called Little Dragon years, a nickname meant to make up for the Snake&#x27;s lower prestige compared with the Dragon.","Snake (Chinese zodiac)","en/d/serpiente-signo-del-zodiaco-chino.html"],["Liver or heart","The most widespread version says the eagle devoured Prometheus&#x27;s liver, but some accounts claim it was his heart.","Aethon","en/d/eton.html"],["One of the panchakanya","She is one of the panchakanya, five exemplary women whose names are believed, by tradition, to cleanse sin when recited.","Draupadi","en/d/draupadi.html"],["Celestial serpent","In the Pyramid Texts she appears as a serpent who washes Ra, an image of purification tied to the sky.","Kebechet","en/d/qebehut.html"],["A crocodile as emblem","His emblem is the crocodile or alligator, carved or stamped on the pedestal of his images.","Pushpadanta","en/d/pushpadanta.html"],["A name that describes its function","Hliðskjálf is usually translated as high seat with an expansive view, a name that sums up its role as a divine lookout.","Hliðskjálf","en/d/hliskjalf.html"],["Thirteen seats","Snorri says Gladsheim held thirteen high seats, one of them for Odin, reserved for the male Æsir.","Glaðsheimr (Gladsheim)","en/d/glasheimr-gladsheim.html"],["A furious name","Xavier Delamarre proposed that the name derives from a reconstructed earlier form <em>Lātu-biyo-</em>, meaning &#x27;Furious Striker&#x27;. The root refers to fury or ardour.","Latobius","en/d/latobio.html"],["A regional name","Emathia, the region said to derive from Emathus, is the Homeric name of Lower Macedonia.","Emathus","en/d/ematio.html"],["Other names","Vegoia also appears as Vecu, Vecui, Vecuvia, Vegoe, Begoe or Bigois.","Vegoia","en/d/vegoia.html"],["A mallet, not a rod","In some versions, Budayū does not use the divine rod but a wooden mallet given to him by the yōkai Sanmoto Gorōzaemon.","Inugami Gyōbu","en/d/inugami-gyobu.html"],["A character mix-up","The idea of Hwanguk arose from a misreading of the character 因 in the Samguk Yusa, which looked like a simplified form of 國, meaning &quot;nation&quot;.","Hwanguk","en/d/hwanguk.html"],["Rescued by a dolphin","According to a tradition in the sources, Phalanthus was shipwrecked before reaching Italy and a dolphin carried him ashore, just as it had done for the hero Taras.","Phalanthus","en/d/falanto.html"],["A simple root","Levana comes from the Latin verb levare, &#x27;to lift&#x27;, which explains her link to the gesture of raising the newborn.","Levana","en/d/levana.html"],["Old sayings","Expressions such as &#x27;go to Niye&#x27; or &#x27;dwell in Nya&#x27; were recorded in Polish folklore and are considered a hint of her cult.","Niya","en/d/niya.html"],["Body-part offerings","Some 1,500 stone, bronze and wooden offerings have been found, many depicting body parts such as eyes, limbs and internal organs.","Sequana","en/d/secuana.html"],["A name with two forms","The term can be used interchangeably with the longer form Toyoashihara no Nakatsukuni, built on the same elements of the middle land.","Ashihara no Nakatsukuni","en/d/ashihara-no-nakatsukuni.html"],["Gigaku masks","<em>Gigaku</em> was an ancient form of theatre in which performers wore masks. The menreiki takes its form from these pieces.","Menreiki","en/d/menreiki.html"],["A favourite of Buson","The haiku poet and painter Buson liked Shirime so much that he included it in many of his yōkai paintings.","Shirime","en/d/shirime.html"],["Wings at puberty","According to the myth, Calais and Zetes grew wings when they reached puberty. Some sources place them on the head and feet instead of the back.","Calais","en/d/calais.html"],["A genderless deity","At the start of the texts it is described as a kami without gender, although other passages treat it as a goddess.","Kami-musubi-no-kami","en/d/kami-musubi-no-kami.html"],["A boar with objects on its head","Between the animal&#x27;s ears were hidden a comb, a pair of scissors and a razor, and all of them had to be taken to complete the giant&#x27;s task.","Twrch Trwyth","en/d/twrch-trwyth.html"],["A snake with wings","According to a legend of the Bulgars, a snake living near the town of Bilär begged Allah for mercy and was given wings, with which it flew away.","Zilant","en/d/zilant.html"],["Nilachal","The temple stands on Nilachal hill, on the banks of the Brahmaputra, west of Guwahati.","Kamakhya","en/d/kamakhya.html"],["Yama in a judge&#x27;s cap","In China, Yama is shown wearing a judge&#x27;s cap and traditional robes. His image appears on the hell money burned in offerings to ancestors.","Death (personification)","en/d/la-muerte.html"],["A yōkai that was barely classified","It was not officially classified until the Edo period. Before that, the strange noises from the mountains were attributed to natural causes, such as birds.","Yamabiko","en/d/yamabiko.html"],["Three brothers, three capitals","Each of the three brothers founded a capital on the island and named it after himself, as happened with Lindos.","Lindus","en/d/lindo.html"],["A tarot with an Egyptian name","The Thoth tarot, designed by Crowley and Frieda Harris, takes its name from this god and the book attributed to him.","Book of Thoth","en/d/libro-de-thot.html"],["A name with history","The name «Tío» may derive from Spanish «Dios», since Quechua and Aymara have no D sound.","El Tío","en/d/el-tio.html"],["A star with her name","The star Gamma Virginis, in Virgo, bears the name Porrima, one of the sisters.","Antevorta","en/d/antevorta.html"],["Superhuman features","According to the tale, he had seven fingers on each hand and toe and seven pupils in each eye, features said to enhance his feats.","Cú Chulainn","en/d/cu-chulainn.html"],["A name of fire","His name comes from the Old Irish word for fire, which derives from a Proto-Indo-European verb meaning to burn or to kindle.","Aed","en/d/aed.html"],["A monkey made of clay","Boccaccio tells that Epimetheus was the first to model a human statue from clay, and that Jupiter turned him into an ape and chained him on the Pithecusae islands. Boccaccio reads this as a metaphor for someone who imitates without understanding.","Epimetheus","en/d/epimeteo.html"],["The corn dolly day","The last family to finish the harvest had to keep the Cailleach figure all winter. Nobody wanted to be that farmer.","Cailleach","en/d/cailleach-beirre.html"],["Golden garments","The skins of the victims were painted yellow and called teocuitlaquemitl, &quot;golden garments&quot;. Priests wore them to represent the god.","Xipe Totec","en/d/xipe-totec.html"],["The surviving cook","According to the story, the only one to survive the attack was the cook, who hid under the deck. This is why seafarers travelling abroad bring a cook along as a witness.","Xiongdi Gong","en/d/xiongdi-gong.html"],["The apple of discord","Eris, left out of the wedding of Thetis and Peleus, threw a golden apple marked &quot;for the fairest&quot;. The dispute it caused led to the judgment of Paris.","Thetis","en/d/tetis-q184437.html"],["A lineage down to Cinyras","Phaethon is the first link in a chain of three generations that ends with Cinyras, king of Cyprus.","Phaethon","en/d/faeton.html"],["A name with two meanings","Duryodhana is often translated as hard to conquer, while his alternative name Suyodhana means easy to conquer. The epic plays on both meanings.","Duryodhana","en/d/duryodhana.html"],["Many spellings","Her name was written in several ways, including Durtur, and in <em>emesal</em> as Zertu or Zertur. The etymology of the name remains unknown.","Duttur","en/d/duttur.html"],["An enigmatic name","His name has been interpreted as &quot;named in the depths&quot; or &quot;hidden name&quot;, though its meaning is not certain.","Ceuthonymus","en/d/ceutonimo.html"],["A white serpent","In the legend, the serpent that kills the outlaws with its breath is white, the colour that symbolises the White Emperor.","Baidi","en/d/baidi.html"],["A shared mantra","The same mantra, Oṁ Kurukulle Hrīḥ Svāhā, appears in Hindu and Buddhist sources with almost the same structure.","Kurukulla","en/d/kurukula.html"],["Several names","Ancient authors also give her name as Eriboea, Melibea or Phereboea, with variant spellings in Greek.","Periboea","en/d/peribea.html"],["Millions of albatrosses","Yamada&#x27;s report described several million albatrosses on the island, which was odd, since albatrosses did not fly there at that time of year.","Ganges Island","en/d/isla-ganges.html"],["A god perhaps seen as female","Some scholars think that in northwestern Mesopotamia Ilabrat may have been perceived as female, because of the -t suffix in some forms of his name. Most treat him as male.","Ilabrat","en/d/ilabrat.html"],["Two spellings","The name also appears as Sandacus in some Greek sources, differing by a single letter.","Sandocus","en/d/sandoco.html"],["Peacocks","According to the myth, Hera preserved Argus&#x27;s hundred eyes in the peacock&#x27;s tail to honour her faithful guardian.","Argus Panoptes","en/d/argos-panoptes.html"],["Child protection","Families sometimes place a Quran on a baby&#x27;s bed as a precaution against ghosts like this one.","Sundel bolong","en/d/sundel-bolong.html"],["A renamed goddess","According to the myth of Ninurta&#x27;s exploits, her son changed her name from Ninmah to Ninhursag to commemorate her creation of the hills and mountains.","Ninhursag","en/d/ninhursag.html"],["A biblical parallel","Samuel Noah Kramer considered this myth the closest Sumerian parallel to the biblical story of Cain and Abel.","Emesh","en/d/emesh.html"],["A lineage of soothsayers","The Galeotae were a family of soothsayers in Sicily who regarded Galeus as their ancestor, linking prophecy to a divine origin.","Galeus","en/d/galeo.html"],["A name of Persian origin","Virgil takes the name Achaemenes, of Persian origin, and reads it as Greek, giving it the meaning «he who waits with affliction».","Achaemenides","en/d/aquemenides.html"],["A name with double meaning","Sköll is usually translated as &quot;treachery&quot; or &quot;mockery&quot;. The noun is the feminine form of a word meaning bark or a far-carrying sound.","Sköll","en/d/skoll.html"],["A feather on the scales","The deceased&#x27;s heart was weighed against an ostrich feather, which represented Maat, order and justice.","Aaru","en/d/aaru.html"],["Mix-up with St Vitus","For a long time it was believed that his cult came from St Vitus. Today it is considered more likely that the saint was used to replace the god.","Svetovit","en/d/svetovit.html"],["A fruit whose meaning changed","The last character in the fruit&#x27;s name once meant simply &quot;fruit&quot;. Over time it came to refer to sweets.","Tajimamori","en/d/tajimamori.html"],["Two possible mothers","Apollodorus and a scholion on Euripides give Ecbasus different mothers: a daughter of the river god Strymon in one account and the Oceanid Peitho in the other.","Ecbasus","en/d/ecbasus.html"],["A name with a harp echo","Reconstructed Proto-Celtic lexica at the universities of Leiden and Wales suggest the name may derive from <em>Ad-bej-ānos</em>, possibly meaning something like &#x27;related to striking&#x27;, perhaps alluding to harp-strumming.","Abhean","en/d/abhean.html"],["Two versions of his origin","The Kojiki presents him as the direct son of Izanagi and Izanami, while the Nihon Shoki says he was born from the morning mist.","Shinatsuhiko","en/d/shinatsuhiko.html"],["A votive hand","Many offerings dedicated to this god take the form of a human hand with two raised fingers. Some have a small hole at the base, suggesting they were carried in processions on wooden poles.","Sabazios","en/d/sabacio.html"],["Meaningful name","Her Greek name, Hipponomes, is usually rendered as horse-driver, a meaning that reflects the sense of her name.","Hipponome","en/d/hiponome.html"],["Other Antheias","The name also appears for other figures in Greek mythology: one of the Hesperides on a lekythos by Asteas, and one of the fifty daughters of Thespius, who each spent a night with Heracles.","Antheia","en/d/antea.html"],["A name with history","The etymology of Oceanus remains unresolved. Some scholars propose a Semitic origin and others an Indo-European connection.","Oceanus","en/d/oceano.html"],["A television sketch","It appeared in a <em>Saturday Night Live</em> sketch titled &quot;Theodoric of York, Medieval Barber&quot;, where a medieval barber used it to diagnose a patient.","Caladrius","en/d/caladrio.html"],["A split tree","In the codices, its glyph is a cleft tree that flowers and bleeds, and from which a person falls.","Tamoanchan","en/d/tamoanchan.html"],["Three Silvani per estate","According to Higinio Gromatico, each estate had three Silvani: one for the household, one for the shepherds and one for the point where the property began.","Silvanus","en/d/silvano.html"],["Pigs at the Thesmophoria","The Athenians threw live piglets into sacred pits, and attendants later retrieved the remains, mixed them with seeds and placed them on altars.","Eubuleus","en/d/eubuleo.html"],["The golden-horned doe","Pindar recalls that the doe Taygete dedicated to Artemis was the same one Heracles later chased, though he did not kill it.","Taygete","en/d/taigete.html"],["An altar without a statue","Eleos&#x27;s altar in Athens had no image of the goddess, because she was believed to live in people&#x27;s hearts.","Eleos","en/d/eleos.html"],["Five pairs of twins","In the Critias, the offspring of Poseidon and Cleito include five pairs of male twins, an unusual detail even among mythological tales.","Autochthon","en/d/autoctono.html"],["Feast of Fools","Anyone who forgot which curia they belonged to and missed the Fornacalia was called a fool and had to make a special offering.","Fornax","en/d/fornax.html"],["A mound bearing his name","The burial mound at Lillhersby is called Agnehögen, or Agne&#x27;s mound, and was dated to around 400.","Agne","en/d/agne.html"],["Possible identity with Amphitrite","Some sources suggest Amphithoe may be the same figure as Amphitrite, the wife of Poseidon, although not all texts confirm this.","Amphithoe","en/d/anfitoe.html"],["A 16th-century description","In 1560 the Jesuit José de Anchieta described a shining beam moving toward its victims, attributed to this being and likened to a serpent&#x27;s undulating motion.","Boitatá","en/d/boitata.html"],["A disputed name","The spellings Hauron and Haurun both appear in Egyptological work, and it has been proposed that the name means &#x27;the deep one&#x27;.","Hauron","en/d/hauron.html"],["A lost camel","The first European account arose from the search for a stray camel, a common theme in tales of secret desert oases.","Zerzura","en/d/zerzura.html"],["A name with a double reading","The first two characters of Ōkuninushi&#x27;s name can be read as &quot;Daikoku&quot;, which helped identify him with Daikokuten in the medieval period.","Daikokuten","en/d/daikokuten.html"],["A name tied to hot water","Her name is linked to the Celtic root meaning &quot;to boil&quot;, which fits her association with thermal springs.","Bormana","en/d/bormana.html"],["A moon of Mars","Phobos, the larger of Mars&#x27;s two moons, was named after the god in 1877.","Phobos","en/d/fobos.html"],["A box never opened","According to legend, the <em>hundi</em> has received offerings for centuries, and no one has ever opened it.","Chelamma","en/d/chelamma.html"],["A paired name","In Sanskrit they are named together in the dvandva compound Mitra-Varuna, which joins the two names without a conjunction.","Mitra and Varuna","en/d/mitra-y-varuna.html"],["Origin of a name","The capital of Ulster, Emain Macha, takes its name from the twins the woman gave birth to during the race.","Cruinniuc","en/d/cruinniuc.html"],["Ghost stew","The rumour that a man from Yotsuya killed and ate a heron earned him the reputation of having eaten ghost stew.","Aosagi-no-hi","en/d/aosagi-no-hi.html"],["A day without fish","The Egyptian calendar forbade eating or offering fish on IV Akhet 28, in memory of the goddess leaving Mendes in fish form.","Hatmehit","en/d/hatmehit.html"],["A skull as model","It has been suggested that the head of the Lindwurm Fountain in Klagenfurt was inspired by a woolly rhinoceros skull found in a quarry in 1335, which makes it one of the earliest reconstructions of an extinct animal.","Lindworm","en/d/lindworm.html"],["A fire goddess by mistake","She is sometimes confused with a fire goddess due to a 1903 mistranslation: where the original says the volcano hurls fire, it was read as her hurling it.","Laon","en/d/laon.html"],["The Siete Cabrillas","This is the name given to a cluster of stars that, according to the myth, appeared in the sky at the birth of Luisón.","Luisón","en/d/luison.html"],["A name with many forms","In the ancient texts he receives dozens of names and variants, such as Ōanamuchi or Ōkunitama. Each reflects a different aspect of his role.","Ōkuninushi","en/d/okuninushi-q11470054.html"],["A name with a story","The name of the Teleboans is linked to the Greek phrase <em>telou ebē</em>, &quot;he went far&quot;, alluding to Taphius&#x27;s long journey away from his homeland.","Taphius","en/d/tafio.html"],["Eyes that see time","Each of the cuegle&#x27;s eyes corresponds to a dimension of time. The forehead eye looks to the future, the right eye to the present and the left eye to the past.","Cuegle","en/d/cuegle.html"],["An angel by another name","Martin Haug read the yazatas in Christian terms, and his reading spread so widely that they are now almost always translated as &quot;angels&quot;.","Yazata","en/d/yazata.html"],["Two-faced idol","Inside his temple, an algarrobo-wood idol with two faces looking in opposite directions was found, a symbol of duality.","Pachacamac","en/d/pachacamac.html"],["Plaques that shivered","Some silver plaques are so thin that, set upright, they would have trembled and glittered in any draught.","Senuna","en/d/senuna.html"],["A bog with her name","The Danish bog Huldremose, on Djursland, takes its name from these creatures and is famous for the discovery of the Huldremose Woman, a body preserved in the peat from about 55 BC.","Huldra","en/d/huldra.html"],["Two instead of three","Pausanias reports that the Lacedaemonians claimed the Charites were two, not three. This local version shows that their number could vary by region.","Phaenna","en/d/faena.html"],["A tomb in the marketplace","Her tomb stood in the agora of Argos, the centre of public life, near a temple dedicated to Asclepius.","Cerdo","en/d/cerdo-q5064045.html"],["Tears of gold","According to the Prose Edda, Freyja wept red gold tears for Óðr, a detail that gives color to his story.","Óðr","en/d/or.html"],["A fatal banquet","One version holds that Heracles killed three of Antimache&#x27;s sons during a sacrifice, over a dispute about the size of the meat portion served to him.","Antimache","en/d/antimaca.html"],["A throne for the god","After conquering Mari, Shamshi-Adad dedicated a throne to Itūr-Mēr and credited the god with his right to rule the city. The inscription does not mention Dagan.","Itūr-Mēr","en/d/itur-mer.html"],["A gruesome end","In some versions, Tydeus ate the brains of his enemy Melanippus, an act that cost him Athena&#x27;s favour.","Tydeus","en/d/tideo.html"],["Guardian of the north","According to the Garga Samhita, he guards the northern gate of Vaikuntha day and night.","Vishvaksena","en/d/vishvaksena.html"],["A name meaning &#x27;cold&#x27;","In Old Norse the name is linked to a verb meaning &#x27;to cool&#x27;, an intriguing choice for a shield meant to hold back the sun&#x27;s heat.","Svalin","en/d/svalin.html"],["Two lions with names","Aker&#x27;s lions were called Duaj, meaning &#x27;tomorrow&#x27;, and Sefe, meaning &#x27;yesterday&#x27;. They represented the past and the future that the sun passed through each day.","Aker","en/d/aker.html"],["A lost message","Some researchers propose that, when combined, the princes&#x27; names form a Slavonic phrase with a peaceful tone, perhaps addressed to the Franks.","Křesomysl","en/d/kresomysl.html"],["A heroic incarnation","According to the Popol Vuh, Ixbalanque, one of the Hero Twins, was an incarnation of Awilix.","Awilix","en/d/avilix.html"],["A name with several readings","Some scholars propose that feilian was a dialectal variant of <em>feng</em>, &#x27;wind&#x27;, and that its characters were used interchangeably with those of the phoenix.","Feilian","en/d/feilian.html"],["A forbidden name","Her name was considered unsafe to say aloud, so the Greeks referred to her indirectly, as &#x27;the Maiden&#x27;.","Persephone","en/d/persefone.html"],["Another name","A white-ground kylix in the British Museum has the name Anesidora inscribed beside her figure.","Pandora","en/d/pandora.html"],["A star&#x27;s name","Her name comes from the Greek word <em>astēr</em>, meaning star, and shares its root with Astraea, her cousin Astraeus, and his daughter.","Asteria","en/d/asteria.html"],["Confusion with Kavad I","In the Shahnameh, the &#x27;Kay&#x27; epithet does not always mean Kayanian origin: the Sasanian king Kavad I is also called Kay Qobad.","Kai Kobad","en/d/kay-kobad.html"],["Otherworld hounds","His hounds, the Cŵn Annwn, are described as a supernatural pack. In the Black Book of Carmarthen, Gwyn states that his finest hound is called Dormarch.","Gwyn ap Nudd","en/d/gwyn-ap-nudd.html"],["Egg with a serpent","He is depicted emerging from an egg entwined with a serpent, one of the most recognisable images of Orphism.","Phanes","en/d/fanes.html"],["Modern Persian name","In Middle Persian the plant was called hōm, a name that remains in modern Persian and other living Iranian languages.","Haoma","en/d/haoma.html"],["Two kinds of spirit","Hellois are distinguished from hingchabis: the former wander freely, while the latter live inside the bodies of some women.","Helloi","en/d/helloi.html"],["Shields of the Gauls","In Athena Itonia&#x27;s temple between Pherae and Larissa, shields taken from the Gauls were hung up after what was considered Greece&#x27;s last victory over barbarians.","Athena Itonia","en/d/atenea-itonia.html"],["An invisible cord","In the legend, the thread that binds couples is made of silk and stays invisible, yet nothing can break it.","Yue Lao","en/d/yue-lao.html"],["Two possible fathers","According to one version Acraepheus is a son of Apollo; according to another he is a son of Orion. The ancient sources do not agree.","Acraepheus","en/d/acrefeo.html"],["An uncertain name","No one knows for sure where the name Lua comes from. It has been linked to the Greek verb <em>luō</em> (&quot;to release&quot;), the Latin <em>luere</em> (&quot;to suffer&quot; or &quot;to atone&quot;), or <em>lues</em>, meaning &quot;plague&quot;.","Lua","en/d/lua.html"],["A horn with a history","According to Ovid, the cornucopia was born from a horn torn from a river god. Other myths give different origins for it.","Abundantia","en/d/abundancia.html"],["A name with several etymologies","Christoph Hartknoch linked it to a Sarmatian word for the underworld, while other scholars proposed Lithuanian roots tied to evil or hostility.","Peckols","en/d/peckols.html"],["A name with a story","His name means &quot;man with something to cut&quot;, referring to the mutilation of his daughter, which made her a goddess.","Anguta","en/d/anguta.html"],["A borrowed cuirass","Lycaon lent his cuirass to Paris for the duel against Menelaus, a detail that shows his ties to his Trojan brothers.","Lycaon","en/d/licaon.html"],["Achilles&#x27; heel","The Roman version of Thetis explains why Achilles&#x27; heel was his only vulnerable spot.","Styx","en/d/estigia-q8843276.html"],["Revealing name","Theandrios means &#x27;God-Man&#x27; in Greek, a name that points to a divine nature joined with a human one.","Theandrios","en/d/theandrios.html"],["Friday","The English weekday Friday comes from Old English Frīġedæġ, meaning &#x27;day of Frig&#x27;, a Germanic version of the Roman day of Venus.","Frigg","en/d/frigg.html"],["An avenger of a single night","According to the poems, Váli reached adulthood within a single day of his birth, a detail that emphasizes the urgency of his mission.","Váli","en/d/vali.html"],["Sri Yantra","A mystic diagram of nine interlocking triangles representing the union of divine masculine and feminine. It is central to worship in the Shri Vidya school.","Tripura Sundari","en/d/tripura-sundari.html"],["Eight banners","His name comes from the eight heavenly banners that signaled his birth. It is said that Empress Jingū set up eight flags on Tsushima as an offering after the expedition to Korea.","Hachiman","en/d/hachiman.html"],["Bowl-shaped box","A Japanese box with a cover shaped like a begging bowl displays the sixteen arhats.","Sixteen Arhats","en/d/dieciseis-arhats.html"],["The sword Higekiri","Kitano Tenmangū Shrine in Kyoto keeps a sword traditionally linked to the episode of the severed arm. Depending on the legend, it is also called Onikiri, Onimaru, Shishinoko or Tomokiri.","Ibaraki-dōji","en/d/ibaraki-doji.html"],["Wings from puberty","Zetes and Calais grew wings when they reached puberty, a trait inherited from their father, the north wind god Boreas.","Zetes","en/d/zetes.html"],["White bat","Ye Fashan claimed Zhang Guo was a white spiritual bat, and the bat is also a symbol of longevity in Chinese tradition.","Zhang Guo Lao","en/d/zhang-guo-lao.html"],["A yellow-eyed dog","In the Papyrus Jumilhac Babi appears as a red dog with yellow eyes, an animal form that contrasts with his identity as a baboon.","Babi","en/d/babi.html"],["A king who objected","The King of Denmark objected to using the Yule Lads as a disciplinary tool, and in 1746 their myths were officially banned from instilling excessive fear in children.","Yule Lads","en/d/jolasveinar.html"],["A primordial cannibal","According to the Tuamotu story, Tane becomes so hungry on earth that he eats a man, thus becoming the first cannibal.","Atea","en/d/atea.html"],["Sows and raiders","According to tradition, the abbess Samding Dorje Phagmo turned 80 novices into wild sows, prompting the Dzungars to flee and leave their plunder at the monastery.","Vajravārāhī","en/d/vajravarahi.html"],["Generic name","In Semitic languages, the word el simply meant &#x27;god&#x27;, and the same element appears in names such as Israel or Michael.","El","en/d/el.html"],["Latin origin","His name comes from the Latin sumere, meaning to take, appropriate or acquire, in keeping with his reputation for taking what is not his.","Sumiciu","en/d/sumiciu.html"],["A quarrel at a festival","The dispute broke out during a festival of Poseidon at Onchestus, over a matter of little importance.","Clymenus","en/d/climeno.html"],["A golden kite","The Nihon Shoki also says that a golden kite, <em>kinshi</em>, helped Jimmu in his battle against Naganomushihiko. This bird is associated with the god Amanohiwashi.","Kamotaketsunumi no Mikoto","en/d/kamotaketsunumi-no-mikoto.html"],["The Paps of Anu","The twin hills of Killarney in County Kerry bear her name in Irish, <em>Dá Chích Anann</em>, and their shape recalls two breasts.","Anu","en/d/anu-q612156.html"],["A king with her name","Her name appears in that of King Ur-Nammu, which for a long time was the only known theophoric name invoking her.","Nammu","en/d/nammu.html"],["Deadly waters","Turnus Herdonius was drowned in the sacred waters of the grove, a killing the story presents as ordered by King Tarquin.","Ferentina","en/d/ferentina.html"],["A disputed name","The meaning of her name is unclear. Many scholars link it to Old Norse words for &#x27;gravel&#x27; or &#x27;mud&#x27; and translate it as &#x27;gravel-offerer&#x27;, though another theory proposes &#x27;gold-bidder&#x27;.","Aurboða","en/d/aurboda.html"],["Mongoose and jewels","He is sometimes shown with a mongoose ejecting jewels from its mouth. The mongoose is the enemy of the snake, a symbol of greed or hatred, so the image expresses generosity.","Vaiśravaṇa","en/d/vaisravana.html"],["Three forms in one day","The goddess takes a different name depending on the hour: Saraswati in the morning, Lakshmi at noon and Parvati in the evening.","Mookambika","en/d/mookambika.html"],["First Dorian devotee of Zeus","According to tradition, Glaucus was the first Dorian to worship Zeus, king of the gods.","Glaucus of Messenia","en/d/glauco-de-mesenia.html"],["Two versions of the family tie","Ovid presents Dexithea as Macelo&#x27;s sister, while other accounts make her Macelo&#x27;s daughter.","Dexithea","en/d/dexitea.html"],["A plant&#x27;s name","In one account, Cleochus found him hidden in a bed of <em>smilax</em> and named him after the plant.","Miletus","en/d/mileto.html"],["A barrow on Munsö","In the early 18th century, antiquarians attributed a burial mound on the island of Munsö to Björn, a claim that still links his name to the site.","Björn Ironside","en/d/bjorn-ragnarsson.html"],["365 herbs","In the story, the herbs that sprang from Miach&#x27;s grave matched his joints and veins. The ancient Irish believed that losing this knowledge greatly increased human suffering.","Airmed","en/d/airmed.html"],["Facing the seven hills","The temple is designed so that Vakula Devi&#x27;s image faces the seven hills of Tirumala, where her foster son lives.","Vakula Devi","en/d/vakula-devi.html"],["A planet named after him","An extrasolar object, Fomalhaut b, is named in his honour.","Dagan","en/d/dagan.html"],["Colours","He is associated with blue, yellow and coral. In Candomblé, depending on the nation, his colour may be blue or green.","Oshosi","en/d/oshosi.html"],["Long beards","In the legend, the name of the Lombards comes from the long beards the women pretended to wear to trick Odin.","Gambara","en/d/gambara.html"],["A god with a slang name","Mutto and mūtōnium were slang words for penis in Latin, and Mutto was even used as a Roman cognomen.","Mutunus Tutunus","en/d/mutuno-tutuno.html"],["A goddess on a building","In the Finnish city of Kotka, a building is named after Vellamo.","Vellamo","en/d/vellamo.html"],["A place on the road","According to the sources, Romans travelling the Via Appia stopped at volcanic fissures to make offerings to the goddess.","Mefitis","en/d/mefitis.html"],["An olive tree against a horse","In the Athenian account, Athena and Poseidon competed for the city&#x27;s patronage. The god offered a horse and the goddess an olive tree, and the Athenians preferred her gift.","Athena","en/d/atenea.html"],["A goddess of distant lands","Ovid places her in Scythia, a cold and desolate region where almost nothing grows to eat.","Fames","en/d/fames.html"],["One name, two figures","Snorri Sturluson presents Gymir as another name for Ægir, although scholars debate whether they are the same figure or two different characters.","Gymir","en/d/gymir.html"],["A goat as emblem","The goat beside his images distinguishes him from other Tirthankaras in statues.","Kunthunatha","en/d/kunthunatha.html"],["Name","Its name is often glossed as meaning «two-winged», referring to its pair of wings.","Amphiptere","en/d/anfiptero.html"],["A nymph with two fathers","Ancient sources disagree about her origin: some make her a daughter of Achelous, while others say the water was a gift from the river Cephisus.","Castalia","en/d/castalia.html"],["A black bitch","According to Ovid, Hecuba was transformed into a black bitch and ran off howling, so her attackers, confused, withdrew.","Polymestor","en/d/polimestor.html"],["Two names","For a long time he was read as Zu because of confusion with the sign AN, and some researchers now argue that Imdugud was the original pronunciation.","Anzû","en/d/anzu.html"],["Objects fallen from the sky","According to the myth, a plough, a yoke, a sagaris and a golden cup fell in Scythia during the reign of the three brothers.","Targitaos","en/d/targitao.html"],["Coins called glaux","Athenian tetradrachms were known across the ancient world as glaux, meaning &#x27;little owl&#x27;, a name modern numismatics renders as &#x27;owl&#x27;.","Owl of Athena","en/d/mochuelo-de-atenea.html"],["The Bed of Boreas","The mountain where Boreas took Chione was said, according to the tradition, to have been named &#x27;The Bed of Boreas&#x27;.","Chione","en/d/quione-q1074468.html"],["A brother who was a son","The Roman mythographer Hyginus claims that Ganymede was not Assaracus&#x27; brother but his son, which changes their relationship entirely.","Assaracus","en/d/asaraco.html"],["A name of war","His name can be translated as warrior or battle, and it is related to the Old Norse word hǫð, meaning war or slaughter.","Höðr","en/d/hor.html"],["The name of Phocus","By tradition, the name of Phocus recalls his mother&#x27;s transformation into a seal, since phoke means &quot;seal&quot; in Greek.","Psamathe","en/d/psamate.html"],["A heavenly vine","Her name is usually translated as «grapevine of heaven». The word geštin may also have had the figurative sense of «sweet» or «lovely».","Geshtinanna","en/d/geshtinanna.html"],["The snake&#x27;s venomous fangs","According to the Kalevala, the <em>Kalman impi</em>, Kalma&#x27;s daughter, was responsible for giving the snake its venomous fangs.","Kalma","en/d/kalma.html"],["Names of her sons","Her sons had names that expressed qualities: Dub means darkness, Dother evil and Dian violence.","Carman","en/d/carman.html"],["Riding a dromedary","According to these sources, he is depicted as a crowned man riding a dromedary, preceded by musicians playing trumpets and cymbals.","Paimon","en/d/paimon.html"],["Sister of a cattle thief","Cacus stole some of Hercules&#x27; cattle, and it was his sister who revealed where they were hidden.","Caca","en/d/caca.html"],["A painting by Protogenes","The Rhodian painter Protogenes, of Carian origin, chose Cydippe as the subject of one of his works.","Cydippe","en/d/cidipe.html"],["A name of uncertain origin","The prefix niða- is unclear and has no known direct relative. It has been suggested that it may relate to the waning face of the Moon, which would give something like «waning mountains», though this remains only a hypothesis.","Niðafjöll","en/d/niafjoll.html"],["Pongal","The favourite offering to her is pongal, a dish of rice and green gram cooked in earthen pots.","Mariamman","en/d/mariamman.html"],["A name meaning fate","In Sumerian, <em>namtar</em> means &quot;fate&quot;. Texts distinguish him from the ordinary word by the divine sign placed before his name.","Namtar","en/d/namtar.html"],["A nameless wife","Antiphates&#x27;s wife, who raises the alarm, and his daughter, who guides the scouts to the palace, are never given names in the Odyssey.","Antiphates","en/d/antifates.html"],["A secret club&#x27;s cave","The Hellfire Club built a &quot;Cave of Trophonius&quot; with obscene wall paintings in which to hold its revels.","Trophonius","en/d/trofonio.html"],["Mountain of the tengu","Mount Atago and Kurama are counted among the most famous places associated with tengu.","Atago Gongen","en/d/atago-gongen.html"],["A name with several readings","His name was written with different signs depending on the period, and some sources suggest it was pronounced Marutuk.","Marduk","en/d/marduk.html"],["A name that became an adjective","Daedalus gave his name to Daedalic sculpture, an early style of Greek sculpture.","Daedalus","en/d/dedalo.html"],["Origin of the name","According to Robert Beekes, variants of the name point to a pre-Greek origin for the word.","Smilax","en/d/esmilax.html"],["Many spellings","The name appears in many forms, such as Pacaritambo, Paccarectambo or Paqariq Tambo, because of the mix of Quechua and Spanish spelling.","Paqariq Tampu","en/d/paqariq-tampu.html"],["A name with an echo","Þrymheim is usually translated as &quot;noisy home&quot; or &quot;thunder home&quot;. Some manuscripts of the Prose Edda alter the spelling slightly.","Þrymheim","en/d/rymheim.html"],["A nickname for hard labour","The nickname Bröt-Anund is linked to his work of opening roads and clearing forests so that Swedes could settle.","Anund","en/d/anund.html"],["A word for storms","The Finnish word <em>ukkonen</em>, meaning thunderstorm, derives from his name.","Ukko","en/d/ukko.html"],["Licensed hunting","Douglas issues jackalope hunting licenses to tourists. The official season lasts a single day, June 31, a date that does not exist.","Jackalope","en/d/jackalope.html"],["A name that changes meaning","The word <em>mergen</em> means archer in Turkic languages, but in Mongolian Khalkha it came to mean wise or genius, a striking shift in meaning between neighbouring peoples.","Mergen","en/d/mergen.html"],["An English village","The village of Bilsby, in Lincolnshire, has been proposed as a place named after Bil.","Bil","en/d/bil.html"],["A mistaken identity","For years he was described as the maize god, a label applied to god E of the Maya codices, but this identification is considered false.","Yum Kaax","en/d/yum-kaax.html"],["A tomb in a cave","Tradition identifies the Tama-no-iwaya cave as his grave, linking him to his role in the story of the sun goddess.","Tamanooya-no-Mikoto","en/d/tamanooya-no-mikoto.html"],["The moon and the sun","The <em>Amitāyurdhyāna Sūtra</em> assigns the moon to Mahāsthāmaprāpta and the sun to Avalokiteśvara, a pairing that presents the two bodhisattvas as complementary.","Mahāsthāmaprāpta","en/d/mahasthamaprapta.html"],["A sister with divine rank","Dakshina, Yajna&#x27;s wife, is described as a partial incarnation of Lakshmi, the goddess of fortune and Vishnu&#x27;s eternal consort.","Yajna","en/d/yajna.html"],["An insult to Freyja","In the Hyndluljóð, the giantess Hyndla uses the name Heiðrún as an insult against the goddess Freyja. Some translators have rendered it simply as &quot;she-goat&quot;.","Heiðrún","en/d/heirun.html"],["Famous hymn","The Tirupallantu, composed by Periyalvar, one of the twelve Alvars of the Sri Vaishnava tradition, is among the best-known verses dedicated to Perumal.","Perumal (Tirumal)","en/d/perumal-tirumal.html"],["Typhon and the giants","In Graeco-Roman art, Typhon and the giants are often depicted with serpent legs, just like the anguiped.","Anguiped","en/d/anguipedo.html"],["The name explained","The Mahabharata explains his name by his huge body, which the ascetic consumed through austerities.","Jaratkaru","en/d/jaratkaru.html"],["A cow&#x27;s head","After he cut off Isis&#x27;s head, Thoth gave her a cow&#x27;s head as a replacement.","Harsiesis","en/d/harsiesis.html"],["An insulting name","The term was originally used disparagingly for court ladies who failed to marry well or escape poverty, hence the comparison with the &quot;ugly stepsisters&quot;.","Ao-nyōbō","en/d/ao-nyobo.html"],["A disputed name","For a long time his name was read as &#x27;lizard house&#x27;, but that reading has been abandoned. Some studies propose a link to roots meaning secretions such as sap or dew.","Itzamná","en/d/itzamna.html"],["Cypresses of the Graces","According to the commentary, cypresses were sometimes colloquially called &quot;Graces&quot; because of the story of Eteocles&#x27; daughters.","Eteocles","en/d/eteocles-q337894.html"],["God or goddess","In some regions of East Asia she is depicted as a male god, revered among the warrior class.","Mārīcī","en/d/marici.html"],["Origin of the Peloponnese","The southern peninsula of Greece takes its name from Pelops, the immigrant who, according to myth, killed Stymphalus.","Stymphalus","en/d/estinfalo.html"],["Father of Oileus","Hodedocus is remembered mainly as the father of Oileus, a figure linked to Locris.","Hodedocus","en/d/hodedoco.html"],["A statue with another name","Pliny describes statues called Bonus Eventus that were actually renamed images of Greek gods, one by Praxiteles.","Bonus Eventus","en/d/eventus-bonus.html"],["A star bearing his name","Canopus is the second-brightest star in the night sky, surpassed only by Sirius. It lies in Carina, the keel, which in tradition is linked to the ship Argo.","Canopus","en/d/canopo.html"],["An epithet of Renuka","In a legend linked to the goddess Renuka, she is given the epithet Chhinnamasta, though she does not behead herself: her son Parashurama does it.","Chhinnamasta","en/d/chhinnamasta.html"],["Irish counterpart","Christian-Joseph Guyonvarc&#x27;h linked his name with Cichol, a Fomorian demon of Irish tradition. Patrice Lajoye considers the connection very weak.","Cicolluis","en/d/cicolluis.html"],["Almost always plural","In Snorri&#x27;s Edda the name always appears in the plural, megingjarðar, although the singular form megingjörð is the one that became common.","Megingjörð","en/d/megingjor.html"],["A name with a double meaning","His name is often translated as «the embracer», from a verb meaning to embrace. It has also been read as «the one of many fathoms», referring to his enormous length.","Fáfnir","en/d/fafnir.html"],["A name suggesting motion","The Old Norse name can be translated as &#x27;colliding rocks&#x27;, which may imply that the mountain could open and close.","Hnitbjörg","en/d/hnitbjorg.html"],["Pumpkin","The Sanskrit <em>kūṣmāṇḍa</em> means pumpkin, and the Nava Durga links the offering of this fruit to the goddess as another explanation of her name.","Kushmanda","en/d/kushmanda.html"],["Parallel with Prometheus","Like Prometheus, Amirani is punished for defying the divine, and in some versions an eagle eats his liver.","Amirani","en/d/amirani.html"],["A name with many variants","In Asturias he is called Trasgu, Cornín or Xuan dos Caminos in the west, and Gorretín Coloráu in the east, after his red hat.","Trasgo","en/d/trasgo.html"],["A four-faced altar","The altar in the temple built on the Tarpeian rock had four faces, according to the account.","Entoria","en/d/entoria.html"],["First gold","Tradition credits his reign with the first smelting of gold in Ireland, carried out by the craftsman Iuchadán.","Tigernmas","en/d/tigernmas.html"],["Sacred rats","At the temple of Karni Mata, devotees consider it a blessing to eat food that the rats have touched.","Rat","en/d/rata.html"],["A river named after her","The river Neda, in western Peloponnese, takes its name from this nymph according to tradition.","Neda","en/d/neda.html"],["A coin with two children","On a coin of AD 71 she appears as a woman accompanied by two children.","Tutela","en/d/tutela.html"],["A butterfly with his name","The butterfly Battus philenor is named after him.","Battus I of Cyrene","en/d/bato-i-de-cirene.html"],["The nine-headed lion","In some Daoist temples, a golden lion with eight smaller extra heads, known as the Nine-Headed Golden Lion, is usually placed beneath his statue.","Cihang Daoren","en/d/cihang-daoren.html"],["A head that sickens","According to the tales, the ōkubi can make people ill when they come near. Its breath turns the skin it touches yellow and swollen.","Ōkubi","en/d/okubi.html"],["English spelling","The spelling Hecat comes from Arthur Golding&#x27;s 1567 translation of Ovid, and for centuries the name was pronounced in two syllables.","Hecate","en/d/hecate.html"],["Lakes made by footprints","His enormous size led to the belief that countless lakes and ponds were formed by his footprints.","Daidarabotchi","en/d/daidarabotchi.html"],["A protective stone","Tradition held that a stone with a hole in its centre protected against the Nocnitsa. In Coraline, the heroine carries such a stone, which shields her from the false mother.","Nocnitsa","en/d/nocnitsa.html"],["White coat","According to tradition, a tiger turns white at five hundred years old. White tigers do exist, but their colour comes from a genetic mutation.","White Tiger (Byakko)","en/d/tigre-blanco-byakko.html"],["An author who was not Plutarch","The story survives only in a treatise attributed to Plutarch, but it is now known to have been written by another author in the second century. It is classed as paradoxography, or perhaps a parody of it, and may have been meant humorously.","Calydon","en/d/calidon-q11910914.html"],["A name with a soul","His name is linked to the <em>ba</em>, the part of the being that the Egyptians saw as mobile and personal.","Ba-Pef","en/d/ba-pef.html"],["Two Achaias","There were two regions called Achaia, one in the northern Peloponnese and another in Phthiotis, in Thessaly, both linked to the name of Achaeus.","Achaeus","en/d/aqueo.html"],["A cat cemetery","More than 300,000 mummified cats were found when the goddess&#x27;s temple at Bubastis was excavated. Many were buried next to their owners.","Bastet","en/d/bastet.html"],["Name with meaning","According to the Dehkhoda dictionary, her name means shining child, from <em>rud</em> (child) and <em>āb</em> (shining).","Rudaba","en/d/rudaba.html"],["An undersea volcano","In 2008 the French hydrographic service found an altimetry anomaly in the area that could correspond to an undersea volcano around 1474 metres deep.","Sandy Island","en/d/isla-sandy.html"],["Many names","Kangiten is known by names such as Shōten, Binayaka, or Tenson, used depending on context and devotion.","Kangiten","en/d/kangiten.html"],["Siblings of the river","The sources place her among a long list of siblings, all children of Asopus and Metope, such as Pelasgus, Thebe or Salamis.","Ornia","en/d/ornia.html"],["The first man","A Greek poetic fragment preserved in the Refutation of All Heresies says the Libyans believed the first man to rise from the earth was called Tarbas. Many scholars identify him with Hiarbas, though others prefer Garamas.","Hiarbas (Iarbas)","en/d/hiarbas-iarbas.html"],["An asteroid named after him","The Jupiter trojan asteroid 5144 Achates, discovered on 2 December 1991, was named in his honour.","Achates","en/d/acates.html"],["A name with variants","Texts mention forms such as Hesperetusa or Hesperusa. Editors of Apollodorus&#x27;s Library suggest splitting the name into Hesperia and Aretusa, although the manuscripts read Hestia Eretusa.","Hesperia","en/d/hespere.html"],["Meaning of the name","Her name is believed to mean a miko who draws divine spirits with rich pearls, since toyo means rich and tama means pearl.","Toyotama-hime","en/d/toyotama-hime.html"],["A meaningful name","His name derives from the Sanskrit roots <em>na</em> (not) and <em>much</em> (to release, let go), a meaning considered symbolic of his character.","Namuchi","en/d/namuchi.html"],["From Hel to &#x27;hell&#x27;","The English word hell shares a Germanic root with Hel. Its relative hall, meaning &#x27;great room&#x27;, is also related to Valhalla.","Helheim","en/d/helheim.html"],["An exotic name","The name Caphaurus means «camphor», presumably meant to suggest his somewhat exotic African origin.","Garamas","en/d/garamas.html"],["A discus accident","Acrisius died when a discus thrown by his own grandson at games struck him. A gust of wind that pushed the throw off course is the detail that fulfils the prophecy without anyone intending it.","Acrisius","en/d/acrisio.html"],["Goddess of the earth","Some sources identify her with the earth rather than the sky. Thorkild Jacobsen proposed that rain came from her breasts, imagined as clouds.","Antu","en/d/antu.html"],["Yellow Dragon Flag","The Chinese national flag used between 1889 and 1912 is also called the Yellow Dragon Flag, in reference to this figure.","Yellow Dragon","en/d/dragon-amarillo.html"],["Ifa and the babalawo&#x27;s Olokun","In Ifa, the babalawo&#x27;s Olokun is conceived without water, in the empty space between the earth&#x27;s core and the ocean waters.","Olokun","en/d/olokun.html"],["The first Nemean Games","According to the myth, the Argives founded the Nemean Games in honour of a child killed by a serpent, and Eteoclus won the stadion race at that first edition.","Eteoclus","en/d/eteoclo.html"],["A name with several roots","A folk etymology links it to Circassian words meaning roughly son of the fiery sword&#x27;s blow. Other theories point to Turkic or Persian roots.","Soslan","en/d/soslan.html"],["A bag of winds","In Ming mythology, Fengbo keeps the winds in a goatskin bag and releases them as he chooses.","Fengbo","en/d/fengbo.html"],["An Olympic precursor","According to Pausanias, Endymion settled his succession with a race between his sons at Olympia. Epeius won, and his subjects were then called Epeans.","Endymion","en/d/endimion.html"],["The lion&#x27;s head","Agave believed the head she carried back to Thebes belonged to a lion, and only realized it was her son&#x27;s when she spoke with her father.","Agave","en/d/agave.html"],["A mark turned into eyes","According to the stories, Indra was marked with a thousand vulvas that later became a thousand eyes.","Panchakanya","en/d/panchakanya.html"],["Name of the Palici","The twins&#x27; name is explained by the Greek <em>palin hikesthai</em>, &#x27;to come back again&#x27;, alluding to their return from the earth.","Thalia","en/d/talia-q3086495.html"],["A river named after him","Stephanus of Byzantium links the river Granicus to Graecus, whom he presents as a son of Thessalus.","Graecus","en/d/greco.html"],["A red crow","Although described as a crow, the Chinese golden crow is usually painted red rather than black.","Three-legged crow","en/d/cuervo-de-tres-patas.html"],["Brother on the mother&#x27;s side","In the Hárbarðsljóð, Thor makes clear that Meili is not his half-brother, which means the two share a mother.","Meili","en/d/meili.html"],["A name that changed","According to tradition, the name <em>Babbar Vahini</em> gradually changed over time until it became Bambar Baini.","Bambar Baini","en/d/bambar-baini.html"],["A name with meaning","Chocacíhuatl means &#x27;weeping woman&#x27;: the Nahuatl <em>choka</em> means &#x27;to cry&#x27; and <em>cihuatl</em> means &#x27;woman&#x27;.","La Llorona","en/d/la-llorona.html"],["The name","&quot;Abura&quot; means oil and &quot;sumashi&quot; means to press or squeeze. Literally, the name could be translated as &quot;oil squeezer.&quot;","Abura-sumashi","en/d/abura-sumashi.html"],["A name with a wider meaning","The historians Sasaki Kizen and Yanagita Kunio believe the name originally referred to any kind of haunted footwear.","Bakezōri","en/d/bake-zori.html"],["Two lineages","From Iadu descended the Yadava clan and from Puru the Puru clan, both of the lunar race.","Yayati","en/d/iaiati.html"],["Descent from Zeus","On her father&#x27;s side, Cleisithyra descended from Minos through Deucalion, which makes her a great-great-granddaughter of Zeus according to these genealogies.","Cleisithyra","en/d/cleisitira.html"],["A very ancient shrine","Her shrine at Nekheb is among the earliest temples in Egypt, and the city became the companion of Nekhen as the religious and political centre of Upper Egypt.","Nekhbet","en/d/nejbet.html"],["Five hundred and forty doors","According to the Grímnismál, the hall has five hundred and forty doors. Through each one, eight hundred einherjar march out at once to fight.","Valhalla","en/d/valhalla.html"],["A hyena&#x27;s name","The scientific name of the spotted hyena derives from the mythical creature.","Leucrota","en/d/leucrota.html"],["A shared name","Pallas was also the name of a Titan, and authors sometimes confused or identified the two figures.","Pallas","en/d/palas-q2463695.html"],["A goddess who is not one","Buddhists regard her as a bodhisattva, not a goddess, although in the West she is often called one through confusion with divinities.","Guanyin","en/d/guanyin.html"],["An asteroid named after him","The asteroid 4867 Polites takes its name from this Trojan prince.","Polites","en/d/polites.html"],["Two names for luck","Among the East Slavs good luck was called Dolya, and bad luck, its opposite, Nedolya. Sreća is the equivalent in the Serbian and Croatian sphere.","Sreća","en/d/sreca.html"],["The ephebes&#x27; oath","Young Athenians in civic and military training swore loyalty to the city, invoking Hegemone among others.","Hegemone","en/d/hegemone.html"],["A hidden word","English <em>hell</em> comes from a Germanic root meaning to cover or conceal, so etymologically Hell is a hidden place.","Hell","en/d/infierno.html"],["A name taken from the lemurs","Sclater chose the name after the animals whose distribution he was trying to explain. The lemurs of his time included a broader group of primates than the one recognised today.","Lemuria","en/d/lemuria.html"],["Sacred stone","Her Phrygian representation was a black stone, probably a meteoritic iron, which the Roman Senate moved to Rome.","Cybele","en/d/cibeles.html"],["A Roman estate","Titus Pomponius Atticus named one of his estates Amaltheum, and the phrase horn of Amalthea was later applied to fertile regions.","Amalthea","en/d/amaltea.html"],["Father of giants","Strabo calls him the harsh Pallas, father of giants, an epithet the stories also apply to his fifty children.","Pallas","en/d/palas-q429306.html"],["A name with a story","A folk etymology linked his name to <em>peritheein</em>, the verb that Zeus supposedly used to seduce Dia.","Pirithous","en/d/piritoo.html"],["A gulf with her name","The Gulf of Mexico was once called Chalchiuhtlicueyecatl in Nahuatl, in honour of the goddess.","Chalchiuhtlicue","en/d/chalchiuhtlicue.html"],["Sacred dogs","According to Aelian, around a thousand sacred dogs were kept near Adranus&#x27;s temple.","Adranus","en/d/adrano.html"],["Several Þrymrs","The <em>Hversu Noregr byggðist</em> mentions three figures named Þrymr, including a king and a jötunn of Verma, showing that the name was common in Norse genealogies.","Þrymr","en/d/rymr.html"],["A name with a play on words","The name Nun is linked to &#x27;inactivity&#x27; in a word play in the texts, and has been compared with a Coptic noun meaning &#x27;abyss&#x27;.","Nun","en/d/nun.html"],["A stone in Totnes","On Fore Street in Totnes, Devon, a stone known as the Brutus Stone marks the spot where, according to Geoffrey, Brutus and his companions landed.","Brutus of Troy","en/d/bruto-de-troya.html"],["Twice born","The name of Dionysus is linked to his double birth: from the womb of Semele and from the thigh of Zeus.","Semele","en/d/semele.html"],["A soul in the form of vapour","A cloud of vapour rises from his gourd, and inside it is his hun, the soul of the sage. It may be drawn as a shapeless form or as a miniature version of his own body.","Li Tieguai","en/d/li-tieguai.html"],["A name from thistles","The names of Autonous&#x27;s children come from plants that grew on his neglected land, such as rushes and thistles.","Acanthis","en/d/acanthis.html"],["Serpent dung","According to tradition, where the serpent spent the night a mountain formed from its excrement. For this reason, the term &#x27;serpent dung&#x27; is used in the Fon language for minerals and stones mined from underground.","Ayida-Wedo","en/d/ayida-wedo.html"],["One of the Graeae","In Hesiodic texts, Enyo is also the name of one of the three Graeae, grey-haired sisters who shared a single eye and a single tooth.","Enyo","en/d/enio.html"],["A city with his name","The Sinhalese chronicles identify the city where Parakramabahu reigned as Pulastinagara, a name that recalls the sage.","Pulastya","en/d/pulastia.html"],["A serpent-shaped sceptre","According to some authors, such as Aleister Crowley, the sceptre carried by Eligos can take the form of a serpent.","Eligos","en/d/eligos.html"],["Possible Persian origin","Some scholars link its name to the Huma bird of Persian mythology. Oleg Trubachyov derived it from the Avestan <em>hu-māiia</em>.","Gamayun","en/d/gamayun.html"],["Rocks of Ajax","According to Hyginus, the rocks where the Greeks were wrecked were named the Rocks of Ajax, after the hero whose impiety had provoked divine anger.","Nauplius","en/d/nauplio.html"],["A constellation linked to him","In Mesopotamian astronomy he was associated with the constellation Pig, which may have included stars now regarded as part of Draco.","Damu","en/d/damu.html"],["Dual nature","The name combines the characters for dragon and horse, reflecting two animals of great symbolic weight in China.","Longma (dragon horse)","en/d/longma-caballo-dragon.html"],["Origin of the name","Pratdip means meadow of the dips, and tradition links the village name to these dogs. They are said to have disappeared in the nineteenth century.","Dip","en/d/dip.html"],["A town named after him","Molorchia was an ancient city of Nemea which, according to the sources, took its name from the labourer.","Molorchus","en/d/molorco.html"],["Gate to Hell","The Cave of Cruachan was also known as Ireland&#x27;s Gate to Hell, the place where the sidhe army went in.","Nera","en/d/nera.html"],["Zeus as a dove","According to Aelian, Zeus took the form of a dove to seduce the young woman, a detail found in one of the most unusual versions of her story.","Phthia","en/d/ftia.html"],["A Canadian coin","In 2024 Canada issued a commemorative two-dollar coin showing Nuliajuk surrounded by a walrus, two beluga whales, a seal and an Arctic char.","Sedna","en/d/sedna-q1971830.html"],["A name with a mound","The epithet Haugi comes from the Old Norse word haugr, meaning mound or burial barrow.","Björn at Haugi","en/d/bjorn-at-haugi.html"],["A son named «spindle»","Pliny the Elder records that Arachne had a son, Closter, whose name means «spindle» in Greek, and that he introduced the spindle into wool-making.","Arachne","en/d/aracne.html"],["Different fathers","Ancient sources do not agree: depending on the author, he is the son of Broteas or of Thyestes.","Tantalus the Younger","en/d/tantalo-el-segundo.html"],["Longer lives in the Jatakas","In the Buddhist Jataka version of the Mahabharata, Prativindhya, Shrutakarma and Shatanika survive and have longer lives. Sutasoma appears among the mentors of Parikshit.","Draupadeyas","en/d/draupadeyas.html"],["The Mother of Otters","Hillervo, the name some link to Tellervo, was described by Ganander as an otter goddess, although it appears in only one song.","Tellervo","en/d/tellervo.html"],["Lunar birthday","His birthday falls on the third day of the sixth month of the lunar calendar.","Weituo","en/d/weituo.html"],["Earliest literary mention","The <em>Gatha Saptasati</em>, written in Prakrit in the 1st century CE, contains the earliest known literary mention of Radha alongside Krishna.","Radha-Krishna","en/d/radha-krishna.html"],["A mule&#x27;s name","Mullo is the Latin word for &#x27;mule&#x27;, which is why a link with horses or mules has been proposed, though it remains a hypothesis.","Mullo","en/d/mullo.html"],["Sea kings with poetic names","Many of the sea kings in Snorri&#x27;s list have names ending in -ill, which points to them being poetic inventions.","Refil Björnsson","en/d/refil-bjornsson.html"],["The Dog&#x27;s Tomb","In one version, Hecuba was turned into a dog in the Chersonese. Helenus buried her in the place later called the Dog&#x27;s Tomb.","Helenus","en/d/heleno.html"],["A confusion of names","Some researchers believe the name of the town of Rethra was mistakenly taken as the name of a deity, which may have created an apparently independent god.","Radegast","en/d/radegast.html"],["A father named after a river","Dictys Cretensis says his father was Axius, a name also borne by a river in Paeonia.","Pyraechmes","en/d/pyraechmes.html"],["A god of the streets","Many of his epithets refer to his role as night watchman, such as herald of the street or herald of the night.","Ishum","en/d/ishum.html"],["A name with meaning","Geirröðr is usually translated as &#x27;spear-reddener&#x27;, from the Old Norse <em>geirr</em>, &#x27;spear&#x27;.","Geirröd","en/d/geirrod.html"],["A son with two fathers","In the Iliad, Menesthius was publicly considered the son of Boros, but according to the same text his true father was Spercheus.","Spercheus","en/d/esperqueo.html"],["Mountain and river","According to the story, Mount Ithome and the Neda river take their names from these two nymphs.","Ithome","en/d/itome.html"],["The twenty-four lessons","According to the Puranas, Dattatreya listed twenty-four teachers, among them the bee, the spider and the moth, from whom he drew lessons on detachment and the mind.","Dattatreya","en/d/dattatreya.html"],["A striking number","The Historia Brittonum claims that Arthur killed 960 enemies with his own hands at Badon, a figure historians regard as unreliable.","King Arthur","en/d/rey-arturo.html"],["The Pillars of Briareus","According to Aristotle, the Strait of Gibraltar was once called the Pillars of Briareus, before they became known as the Pillars of Heracles.","Briareus","en/d/briareo.html"],["A name that is a color","His name literally means &#x27;the great green&#x27;, an expression that evokes the color of large bodies of water.","Wadj-wer","en/d/wadj-wer.html"],["A magical lock","His strength lay in a lock of purple hair. As long as he kept it, no harm could reach him.","Nisus","en/d/niso.html"],["His emblem","His icon is the deer, which gives him his name in iconography and distinguishes him from other tirthankaras.","Śāntinātha","en/d/santinatha.html"],["A seat given up","Alcinous offers Odysseus the seat of his son Laodamas, a detail the poem uses to show Phaeacian hospitality.","Laodamas","en/d/laodamante-q5969992.html"],["A name with a double reading","The name combines ying, meaning to respond, with lóng, dragon. Although ying can also be read as &quot;should&quot;, the correct meaning is &quot;responsive dragon&quot;.","Yinglong","en/d/yinglong.html"],["A planet named after him","The planet Uranus was discovered in 1781 and named after the Greek god of the sky, following the tradition of naming planets after mythological deities.","Uranus","en/d/urano.html"],["A moon of Jupiter","One of Jupiter&#x27;s moons carries her name, spelled Callirrhoe.","Callirhoe","en/d/calirroe.html"],["Stromboli, Aeolus&#x27; island","The geographer Strabo reports that Strongyle, today&#x27;s Stromboli and one of the Lipari Islands, was said to be the island of Aeolus.","Aeolia","en/d/eolia-q406345.html"],["The treasury of Minyas","Pausanias describes it as a round stone building with a not very pointed top, whose ruins could still be seen in his time.","Minyas","en/d/minias.html"],["A sword for a bride","To win Gerðr, Freyr had to give up his magic sword, leaving him defenseless against his enemies. Some accounts say he later fought with deer antlers.","Gerðr","en/d/gerr.html"],["Fish eyes","Several explanations have been proposed for her name: large, brilliant eyes, or the belief that fish never close their eyes, just as the goddess never stops watching over her devotees.","Meenakshi","en/d/meenakshi.html"],["Born early","According to the myth, Hera hastened his birth and delayed Heracles&#x27;, giving Eurystheus the throne of Mycenae.","Eurystheus","en/d/euristeo.html"],["Okame","In kyōgen farce she appears as Okame, a woman who revels in her sensuality.","Ame-no-Uzume","en/d/ame-no-uzume.html"],["A river on fire","According to tradition, after Phaethon&#x27;s fall the river was scorched by Zeus&#x27;s thunderbolt, and in the sky it still burns among the stars.","Eridanus","en/d/eridano.html"],["A real king","Gyges was a real king of Lydia and founder of the Mermnad dynasty. The ring story, however, comes from Plato&#x27;s imagination.","Ring of Gyges","en/d/anillo-de-giges.html"],["Multicolored llama","Tradition imagined him as a multicolored male llama, a very different image from that of a human-like god.","Urcuchillay","en/d/urcuchillay.html"],["Wayruru amulets","Red and black wayruru beans are used as amulets against the kharisiri. The traditional cure, besides killing the attacker, is to buy human fat and burn it with these beans and a white egg.","Pishtaco","en/d/pishtaco.html"],["No recorded children","The sources record no children of hers. Although her family&#x27;s story appears in the fragmentary <em>Catalogue of Women</em>, she is not included in it.","Chrysothemis","en/d/crisotemis.html"],["Chaos and Poros","A scholium on the Louvre Partheneion equates Poros with Hesiod&#x27;s Chaos, since both act as the first shaper of matter.","Poros","en/d/poro.html"],["A fragrant plant","According to the lexicographer Hesychius of Alexandria, <em>ameinasis</em> was another name for a sweet-smelling herb called duosmon, which could be dill, anise or cumin.","Ameinias","en/d/aminias.html"],["A moon with a Greek name","The moon of the dwarf planet Eris has borne the name Dysnomia since 2005, so a spirit of civil disorder now names a celestial body.","Dysnomia","en/d/disnomia.html"],["A month named after him","The seventh month of the Armenian calendar was dedicated to either Mher or Mithra and was called Mehekan.","Mher","en/d/mher.html"],["A name that passes to a place","Tradition credits Delphus, Hyamus&#x27;s grandson through his daughter, with giving his name to the city of Delphi.","Hyamus","en/d/hiamo.html"],["A name with meaning","His Hurrian name is usually translated as he of Kumar, possibly an early settlement that is poorly documented in the texts.","Kumarbi","en/d/kumarbi.html"],["Two versions","The better-known account of Cydippe&#x27;s marriage to Cercaphus is also told in an alternate version in which she is abducted.","Hegetoria","en/d/hegetoria.html"],["Thyrus of Terni","The green dragon on the emblem of the ancient Umbrian city of Terni is called Thyrus by its citizens.","Wyvern","en/d/guiverno.html"],["Seven sisters","Phul Mata belongs to a group of sisters whose list of names changes depending on the tradition.","Phul Mata","en/d/phul-mata.html"],["The prefix ve-","Aulus Gellius noticed that the prefix <em>ve-</em> in his name also appears in Latin words such as <em>vesanus</em>, meaning &quot;insane&quot;, and from this concluded that he was the opposite of Jupiter.","Vejove","en/d/vejove.html"],["A lunar name","According to the sources, Yaçi means moon in old Tupi, which explains the nocturnal character of the original being.","Saci","en/d/saci.html"],["A nickname with a double meaning","In late imperial China, &quot;rabbits&quot; was a slang term for homosexual men. That is why he is called the Rabbit God, although he has nothing to do with rabbits.","Hu Tianbao","en/d/hu-tianbao.html"],["Thunder hunting","In the Kantō region, villagers hunted raijū that damaged their rice paddies, a practice they called &quot;Kaminari-gari&quot;.","Raijū","en/d/raiju.html"],["Plants of the rite","The nabapatrika uses leaves from nine different plants, tied to a banana plant, during Durga Puja.","Navadurga","en/d/navadurga.html"],["Ægir&#x27;s fire","In the Skáldskaparmál, gold is called Ægir&#x27;s fire because his hall shines with gold as if it were flame.","Ægir","en/d/gir.html"],["A warrior lineage","Diodorus states that the sons of his brothers led the Boeotians on the expedition against Troy.","Alegenor","en/d/alegenor.html"],["A city with her name","In ancient Achaea there was a city called Argyra, located at the site of a spring.","Argyra","en/d/argira.html"],["A kissing contest","Among the trials of the Diocleia was a contest that rewarded the sweetest kiss. The referee had to pray that his lips could tell the best one apart.","Diocles of Megara","en/d/diocles-de-megara.html"],["A shared altar","Pausanias describes an altar divided into sections, one of them dedicated to Iaso alongside Aphrodite, Panacea, Hygieia and Athena Healer.","Iaso","en/d/yaso.html"],["Perky bat tower","In 1929 a creature resembling an ape was reported to have shaken the newly built Perky bat tower in the Florida Keys, scattering the bats before running into the woods.","Skunk ape","en/d/mono-mofeta.html"],["A king who was not a founder","Later Spartan authorities denied Eurysthenes and Procles the title of founder, reasoning that they had not established a state. The honour went instead to their son and grandson.","Procles","en/d/procles.html"],["Origin of the name","Evadne named her son Yamo because she found him lying among violets, according to the story.","Evadne","en/d/evadne-q1372039.html"],["Songs that crossed the continent","Some song lines travelled across Australia, passing through six to ten different language groups.","Dreamtime","en/d/tiempo-del-sueno.html"],["A translated term","In Navajo, the name translates roughly as &quot;by means of it, it goes on all fours&quot;, describing the animal form the witch takes.","Skinwalker","en/d/cambiapieles-skin-walker.html"],["A valkyrie according to Kauffmann","Friedrich Kauffmann classified her as a valkyrie in the nineteenth century, because the elements -gund and -hild are common in such names.","Sinthgunt","en/d/sinthgunt.html"],["An English word","The English verb “to pander” comes from the figure of Pandarus in the works of Chaucer and Shakespeare.","Pandarus","en/d/pandaro.html"],["A god of Rome and Greece","The Romans called him Somnus, and bronze and marble statues of the god survive in Rome, such as the head found at Hadrian&#x27;s Villa.","Hypnos","en/d/hipnos.html"],["A bronze ring in Durham","The sanctuary ring on the door of Durham Cathedral was inspired by medieval hellmouth images. It takes the form of a lion-like animal with a door knocker in its mouth.","Hellmouth","en/d/la-boca-del-infierno.html"],["Shared name","Búri and his supposed son Borr have names derived from the same Proto-Germanic root, meaning roughly &#x27;son&#x27; or &#x27;born&#x27;.","Búri","en/d/buri.html"],["Imperial coins","Fecunditas appears on coins minted for empresses such as Faustina the Elder, and on a gold coin of Julia Domna she is shown with a bunch of grapes, surrounded by children.","Fecunditas","en/d/fecunditas.html"],["Tyrrhenian dolphins","The legend explains that the pirates transformed into dolphins gave their name to the Tyrrhenians and to the Tyrrhenian Sea.","Liber","en/d/liber.html"],["A spindle with eight orbits","Plato describes the Spindle of Necessity with eight circles, which scholars identify with the spheres of the fixed stars, the five classical planets, the Sun and the Moon.","Myth of Er","en/d/mito-de-er.html"],["Buddhist link","Some scholars connect Houtu with Bhumi, the Buddhist goddess who personifies the Earth.","Houtu","en/d/hou-tu.html"],["Grey hair and victory","The Lemnian women mocked Erginus for his grey hair, but he went on to defeat the Boreads in the foot race.","Erginus","en/d/ergino.html"],["A ritual origin","According to the scholar Robert Graves, the myth may derive from a ceremony in which a person dressed in partridge wings was thrown from a cliff, called the pharmacos.","Icarus","en/d/icaro.html"],["Frösö Runestone","The Frösö Runestone is said to be the northernmost runestone in the world, and it bears a large serpent carved on it.","Storsjö Monster","en/d/monstruo-del-gran-lago.html"],["A steady spring","According to Pausanias, Hagno&#x27;s spring on Mount Lycaeum has the same volume of water in winter as in summer, like the river Ister.","Hagno","en/d/hagno.html"],["A name of chains","His name means «fetter» in Akkadian, and he probably began as a deification of a restraining object.","Birtum","en/d/birtum.html"],["A country named after him","According to Herodotus, the region of Lycia in Asia Minor took its name from Lycus, formerly known as Tremilis or Termilae.","Lycus","en/d/lico.html"],["A name with variants","In the Skáldskaparmál Snorri uses the form <em>Ónar</em>, which also appears in a poetic kenning for the Earth.","Annar","en/d/annar.html"],["A goddess with several names","She was also known as Salus Semonia, and some scholars link this name to the Semones, an ancient group of gods.","Salus","en/d/salus.html"],["A ring with a dedication","The Pietroassa ring, found in Romania, carries runic inscriptions that, in one reading, dedicate the piece to Ingwi.","Yngvi","en/d/yngvi.html"],["Inventor of the flute","Tradition credits him with inventing the flute, a detail that links him to the musical world of the Muses.","Ardalus","en/d/ardalo.html"],["Turtle place names","Place names with gui (turtle) are very common across China, often for hills shaped like a turtle, known as gui shan. Names with ao are more typical of the southeastern coast.","Ao","en/d/ao.html"],["A name with relatives","Ríg seems to derive from an oblique form of the Old Irish word rí, &#x27;king&#x27;, related to Latin rex and Sanskrit rajan.","Rig","en/d/rig.html"],["The 1903 attack","The first recorded sighting describes a buffalo-like creature that attacked three people.","Lake Tianchi Monster","en/d/monstruo-del-lago-tianchi.html"],["A Terry Pratchett character","In Terry Pratchett&#x27;s novel <em>Witches Abroad</em> (1991), a black cockerel named Legba appears in a voodoo-themed subplot set in a pseudo-New Orleans.","Papa Legba","en/d/papa-legba.html"],["Several characters with the same name","The name Eurypyle is given to different characters in Greek mythology, from an Amazon queen to a maenad in the retinue of Dionysus.","Eurypyle","en/d/euripile.html"],["A name with three readings","The term can be understood as &#x27;cunning&#x27;, &#x27;deception&#x27; or &#x27;injury&#x27;, which explains the different translations proposed.","Lævateinn","en/d/lvateinn.html"],["A double flute","She is linked to the aulos, a double flute widely used in ancient Greece at banquets, in theatre and at religious ceremonies.","Euterpe","en/d/euterpe.html"],["A name in a Hittite text","A Hittite text called The Cause Against Madduwata mentions a leader of Ahhiyawa named Attarsiya, a name some scholars connect with Atreus.","Atreus","en/d/atreo.html"],["Another author, another killer","In the late Dictys Cretensis version, Patroclus delivers the fatal blow instead of Teucer, changing who causes the death.","Gorgythion","en/d/gorgition.html"],["A kingdom without rain","During his reign no rain fell, only dew, and the harvests came back every year.","Eochaid mac Eirc","en/d/eochaid-mac-eirc.html"],["An elephant with a name","His mount, Supratika, is almost as famous as its rider. Ghatotkacha surrounded it with illusory elephants to attack it.","Bhagadatta","en/d/bhagadatta.html"],["An egg over the roof","To prevent a cockatrice from hatching, people believed the rooster&#x27;s egg had to be tossed over the house so that it landed on the other side without hitting the roof.","Cockatrice","en/d/cocatriz.html"],["Spider and mother","The myth explains the old common belief that spiders eat their mothers, which is mirrored in the punishment of the siblings.","Phalanx","en/d/falanx.html"],["The cornucopia","According to Ovid, the Naiads filled the horn the god had lost with fruit and flowers, and it became the horn of plenty.","Achelous","en/d/aqueloo.html"],["A threefold life","The idea of a character with three souls and three sets of arms reappears, according to some scholars, in Spenser&#x27;s Triamond in <em>The Faerie Queene</em>.","Erulus","en/d/erulo.html"],["Soul and butterfly","In Greek, <em>psyche</em> means both soul and butterfly, which is why she is often shown with butterfly wings.","Psyche","en/d/psique.html"],["Hierakonpolis","The Greeks called Nekhen Hierakonpolis. It was the centre of Horus worship and an ancient capital of Upper Egypt.","Souls of Pe and Nekhen","en/d/almas-de-pe-y-nejen.html"],["Work in the mountains","Yamawaro are said to help woodcutters, but only if the reward matches what was promised.","Yamawaro","en/d/yamawaro.html"],["A mirror of Pallas","Lausus is often seen as a counterpart to Pallas, son of King Evander: both are young, of royal blood, handsome and strong, and both die at the hands of greater heroes.","Lausus","en/d/lauso.html"],["A dinosaur named after him","The dinosaur Hagryphus was named in his honour, meaning «Ha&#x27;s griffin».","Ha","en/d/ha.html"],["Names in the sky","In Welsh tradition the Milky Way was called Caer Gwydion, the Castle of Gwydion.","Dôn","en/d/don.html"],["A pub with his name","In Puerto de la Cruz there is a pub named after Guayota, showing that his figure still lives on in Canarian social life.","Guayota","en/d/guayota.html"],["Name and justice","His name derives from a root meaning &quot;to move in a straight line&quot; or &quot;to direct&quot;, and its superlative, &quot;the straightest&quot;, is his standing epithet.","Rashnu","en/d/rashnu.html"],["A killing with two versions","Pindar describes Licymnius&#x27; death as deliberate and driven by anger. The Bibliotheca presents it as an accident that happened when the uncle stepped into a fight with a servant.","Tlepolemus","en/d/tlepolemo.html"],["Names in other languages","In Bengali it is called Khir Śagôr, in Tamil Tiruppāṟkaṭal and in Telugu Pāla Samudram.","Ocean of Milk","en/d/oceano-de-leche.html"],["A diverted course","The river originally flowed through the middle of Tarsus. After a flood, Justinian I changed its course so that it went around the city.","Cydnus","en/d/cidno.html"],["A king Pausanias leaves out","Pausanias does not include Messapus in the succession of Sicyon. According to him, the throne passed from Leucippus directly to his grandson Peratus.","Messapus","en/d/messapo.html"],["Divination with victims","The Lusitanians read the future in the innards of their victims and in the way they fell after being wounded.","Cariocecus","en/d/cariocecus.html"],["Inherited wings","According to the account, Arke&#x27;s wings passed from Zeus to Peleus and Thetis as a wedding gift, and from Thetis to Achilles, who wore them on his feet.","Arke","en/d/arce.html"],["A name meaning skill","His name is linked to a root meaning to be skilled or to achieve. A basic translation would be skilled and wise.","Kothar-wa-Khasis","en/d/kothar-wa-khasis.html"],["Naini Mountain","A footnote identifies the Naini Mountain of Ebliu with the Slieve Felim Mountains in County Limerick.","Fódla","en/d/fodla.html"],["Shangri-La","The name Shambhala may have inspired the hidden paradise of James Hilton&#x27;s novel Lost Horizon.","Shambhala","en/d/shambhala.html"],["Mother of the Danaides","According to Apollodorus, Atlantia and the hamadryad Phoebe were the mothers of Danaus&#x27;s daughters, without specifying which daughter came from which mother.","Atlantia","en/d/atlantia.html"],["Ostrich feathers","The ostrich feather he wears on his head symbolises lightness and emptiness, ideas tied to his role as the air.","Shu","en/d/shu.html"],["Tears of the karkadann","In Iraq it was believed that the animal wept on finding water in the desert, and that its tears turned into reddish beads.","Karkadann","en/d/karkadann.html"],["A dragon with eight legs","The abaasy ride two-headed, eight-legged, two-tailed dragons, a very different image from European dragons.","Abaasy","en/d/abaasy.html"],["An unusual treatment","According to the story, Fingín prepared a bath of bone marrow for Cethern and replaced some of his ribs with chariot parts.","Cethern mac Fintain","en/d/cethern-mac-fintain.html"],["A tourist spring","The spring linked to Egeria, in the Caffarella park, was a favorite picnic spot for nineteenth-century Romans.","Egeria","en/d/egeria.html"],["Ayurvedic physician","Some texts also describe him as an Ayurvedic physician skilled in surgery and in diseases of the eye and ear.","Satyaki","en/d/satyaki.html"],["Staff with birds","According to a quote from the Odu Ifa, there are sixteen styles of wrought iron staff, depending on the number of birds on its summit, from one to sixteen. The last is the most prestigious.","Osanyin","en/d/ossain.html"],["A Handel opera","Handel first set the story in Italy in 1708 and later reworked it in England as a pastoral with an English libretto.","Acis","en/d/acis.html"],["Resemblance to Wotan","Alexander von Humboldt compared the name Votan with Wodan or Odin and with the names of a day of the week in different regions.","Votan","en/d/votan.html"],["Heads in a stack","Older woodcuts show the nine heads clustered on one neck, sometimes facing three directions, unlike the modern hydra-like image.","Xiangliu","en/d/xiangliu.html"],["The most wanted year","Births rise noticeably in Dragon years in countries with Chinese influence, because children born then are thought to have better fortune.","Dragon (Chinese zodiac)","en/d/dragon-signo-del-zodiaco-chino.html"],["Divine queen","Her name, Δῖώνη, is usually read as &quot;divine queen&quot;. In its older form it is linked to Zeus&#x27;s genitive Diós, which is why she is seen as a feminine form of the god.","Dione","en/d/dione.html"],["A founding contest","His contest with Takemikazuchi is considered the first sumo bout, according to the tradition recorded in the Kojiki.","Takeminakata","en/d/takeminakata.html"],["Three spears","According to Herodotus, the messenger sent to Zalmoxis was flung into the air and caught on three spears held by other men.","Zalmoxis","en/d/zalmoxis.html"],["A Latin-derived name","His name seems to derive from Latin <em>barbatus</em>, &#x27;bearded&#x27;, which fits his image as an elderly philosopher.","Barbatos","en/d/barbatos.html"],["A name for kings","Five Assyrian kings, from Shalmaneser I to Shalmaneser V, carried his name as part of their own.","Shulmanu","en/d/shulmanu.html"],["The meaning of his name","His Old Norse name is usually translated as &#x27;Strength Yeller&#x27;. It is also written as Thrudgelmir.","Þrúðgelmir","en/d/rugelmir.html"],["A city named after her","The ancient polis of Lilaea took its name from the naiad, linking it directly to the spring she was associated with.","Lilaea","en/d/lilea.html"],["A Scooby-Doo monster","They appear in the episode «Mysteries On The Disorient Express» of Be Cool, Scooby-Doo!, among eight monsters from around the globe.","Tzitzimimeh","en/d/tzitzimime.html"],["Oak wreaths","According to Ovid, the first winners of the Pythian Games were crowned with oak wreaths, because the laurel did not yet exist.","Apollo and Daphne","en/d/apolo-y-dafne.html"],["The elephants","In Hinduism, elephants are associated with clouds and rain, linking them to fertility. Because of their strength, they also represent royal authority and divinity.","Kamalātmikā","en/d/kamalatmika.html"],["A relic cart","The cart attributed to Bouzyges was kept as a relic on the Acropolis of Athens.","Bouzyges","en/d/buzigues.html"],["Mythical confluence","In tradition, the Sarasvati joins the Ganges and Yamuna invisibly at the Triveni Sangam in Prayagraj.","Sarasvati","en/d/sarasvati-q177321.html"],["A crater on Io","One of the craters on Jupiter&#x27;s moon Io is named Camaxtli, one of the forms under which this god was known.","Mixcoatl","en/d/mixcoatl.html"],["A proverb attributed to her","Antianira is remembered for a saying attributed to her, a lame man makes the better lover, preserved in fragments of the poet Mimnermus.","Antianira","en/d/antianira.html"],["A name with meaning","His name translates as <em>Sky-Ba</em>, which can be understood as «soul of the sky» or «mood of the sky».","Petbe","en/d/petbe.html"],["A name with several readings","Her Sumerian name can be translated as &quot;radiant Baba&quot; or &quot;silver of Baba&quot;, although the exact reading of the last sign is still debated.","Kubaba","en/d/kubaba.html"],["Modern religion","Like Eris, Discordia is an important figure in Discordianism, a modern new religious movement.","Discordia","en/d/discordia.html"],["A tortoise with a name","A place called Chelone, meaning tortoise in Greek, was linked to the story of the animal that devoured Sciron&#x27;s victims.","Sciron","en/d/esciron.html"],["A dark god called white","Although he is shown with dark skin, his epithet Panduranga means whitish. Scholars explain this paradox by linking it to a form of Shiva worshipped at Pandharpur.","Vithoba","en/d/vithoba.html"],["A meaningful name","The name Lampetia means &#x27;bright&#x27;, in keeping with her status as a daughter of the sun god.","Lampetia","en/d/lampetia.html"],["Annual offering","King Ulmi-Teššup of Tarhuntassa donated a solar disc of gold, silver and copper each year, together with a bull and three sheep.","Arinnitti","en/d/arinnitti.html"],["A name with meaning","The name Protomedea is interpreted as &quot;the first to rule&quot; or &quot;first counsellor&quot;. It is one of the few individual traits attributed to her.","Protomedea","en/d/protomedea.html"],["An asteroid named after him","The asteroid 55576 Amycus is named after this centaur.","Amycus","en/d/amico.html"],["Shared honours","Pausanias reports that the Athenians honoured Thallo together with Pandrosos.","Thallo","en/d/talo.html"],["An airport bearing its name","Bangkok&#x27;s international airport, opened in 2006, is called Suvarnabhumi.","Suvarnabhumi","en/d/suvarnabhumi.html"],["Three figures share the name","In the sources, the name Celtus belongs to three different figures: the ancestor of the Celts and two minor characters from tales of ancient Greece.","Celtus","en/d/celto.html"],["Two faces of Venus","In this tradition, Venus had two aspects: Shalim, the Evening Star, and Shahar, the Morning Star.","Shalim","en/d/shalim.html"],["One name, several women","Idaea names several female figures in Greek mythology, linked to the Mount Ida of Crete or the one in Phrygia.","Idaea","en/d/idea.html"],["A truck with her name","The GAZ-66, a two-tonne Soviet army truck, is informally nicknamed Shishiga.","Shishiga","en/d/shishiga.html"],["A house on a pole","Her emblem, a house or shrine atop a long pole, is her most distinctive symbol and appears only in a specific area of Gaul.","Nantosuelta","en/d/nantosuelta.html"],["Founder of Erythrae","Strabo states that Nycteus founded the city of Erythrae, a detail that does not appear in the better-known Theban accounts.","Nycteus","en/d/nicteo.html"],["A play on words","His task in the myth, healing scalp pain, may be a pun on his name and the Akkadian word <em>abbuttu</em>.","Abu","en/d/abu.html"],["The Rällinge figurine","In 1904 a Viking Age figurine seven centimetres tall was found in Sweden and is interpreted as Freyr. It shows a bearded man seated cross-legged, wearing a pointed cap, with an erect phallus. It is now displayed at the Swedish National Heritage Museum.","Freyr","en/d/frey.html"],["A play on the plant","In one of the spells, the king is called by the name Weneg and also by that of the <em>wng</em> plant, which is linked to the god&#x27;s name.","Weneg","en/d/weneg.html"],["The origin of Sunday","According to the sources, Constantine decreed in 321 that the day of the Sun would be an official day of rest in the Empire. The name of Sunday in several Romance languages derives from this <em>dies Solis</em>.","Sol Invictus","en/d/sol-invictus.html"],["Ker on the shield","In the same scene appears Ker, the figure of violent death, dragging a newly wounded warrior. She is described as horrible, wearing a cloak reddened with human blood.","Palioxis","en/d/palioxis.html"],["A meaningful name","Hainuwele means &#x27;coconut branch&#x27;, a name that recalls the tree from which the tradition says she was born.","Hainuwele","en/d/hainuwele.html"],["A shifting north","According to scholars, Pohjola may have referred to different territories depending on the era and the people imagining it, from Lapland to regions of the Baltic.","Pohjola","en/d/pohjola.html"],["Variable eyes","The number of eyes of the hakutaku varies by source: nine eyes and six horns in some descriptions, eight eyes in others, and sometimes just one at the centre of the head.","Bai Ze","en/d/bai-ze.html"],["A king with a date","Dionysius of Halicarnassus places Amulius&#x27;s reign at 41 years, ending around 752 BC.","Amulius","en/d/amulio.html"],["A cave named after her","According to tradition, the Corycian Cave and its springs took their name from this nymph.","Corycia","en/d/coricia.html"],["Three brothers, three tribes","Tradition linked Pamphylus and his brothers to the three tribes into which the Dorians were divided, each with its own eponymous hero.","Pamphylus","en/d/panfilo.html"],["A bird of confused origin","The bird on the old seal was probably meant to be an eagle, the symbol of John the Evangelist, but over time it came to be read as a cormorant.","Liver bird","en/d/liver-bird.html"],["A telling name","Her name is linked to the Greek <em>pleíon</em>, meaning &#x27;more&#x27;, a fitting title for a figure associated with the increase of flocks.","Pleione","en/d/pleyone.html"],["Statue found on Popocatépetl","The sculpture was found on the slopes of the Popocatépetl volcano, near Tlalmanalco. It is now kept at the National Museum of Anthropology in Mexico City.","Xochipilli","en/d/xochipilli.html"],["A pair of limbs","In Kagawa and Fukushima the akateko appears in pairs, looking like moving feet or legs.","Akateko","en/d/akateko.html"],["A pre-Greek name","The name seems to come from a pre-Greek substrate, based on its suffix *-ssos, common in place names such as Knossos, Korinthos and Zakynthos.","Parnassus","en/d/parnaso.html"],["Letter to John Dee","Mercator described the island in a 1577 letter to the English mathematician John Dee.","Rupes Nigra","en/d/rupes-nigra.html"],["Eyes and noses","In Álava, Navarre, La Rioja and La Bureba, a man with as many eyes as days in the year visits on New Year&#x27;s Eve; in Larráun he was known as &quot;the man of the 366 eyes&quot;.","Olentzero","en/d/olentzero.html"],["A very long dynasty","Herodotus calculates that the Heraclid dynasty ruled Lydia for 22 generations, about 505 years, until Candaules.","Agron","en/d/agron.html"],["A borrowed name","A journalist named the creature after the couples&#x27; press conference. Many believe the name drew on the Batman comics, whose villain Killer Moth did not appear in the TV series.","Mothman","en/d/hombre-polilla.html"],["Intercidona","She was the goddess of axe blows struck against doors to drive out the demon Silvanus.","Pilumnus","en/d/pilumno.html"],["A name with destiny","In Euripides&#x27; tragedy, Dionysus himself points out that &#x27;Pentheus&#x27; means &#x27;man of sorrows&#x27;, an allusion to his death.","Pentheus","en/d/penteo.html"],["A stolen helmet","Áli&#x27;s helmet passed to the Swedish king Adils after a battle on Lake Vänern, in which Áli died.","Hildisvíni","en/d/hildisvini.html"],["A name with several readings","Her name has been read as &quot;the lady of Zarpan&quot; or &quot;creatress of seed&quot;, although today it is thought to come from the name of the town.","Sarpanitu","en/d/sarpanitu.html"],["A namesake priestess","Pausanias mentions a mountain nymph called Daphnis, to whom Gaia supposedly entrusted the prophecy at Delphi.","Daphnis","en/d/dafnis.html"],["Rays in his image","Macrobius says the image of this god was adorned with rays, a feature that brought him closer to the Roman Mars.","Neto","en/d/neto.html"],["Sun Mother","Her name means &#x27;Sun Mother&#x27; in Turkic languages, and in some sources she also appears as Yaşık Ana.","Gun Ana","en/d/gun-ana.html"],["A Titan of intelligence","Cornutus linked his name to the Greek verb <em>koeîn</em>, meaning to perceive or think, and so saw him as a personification of intelligence.","Coeus","en/d/ceo.html"],["A raven that was once white","According to Ovid, the raven was once silver, with feathers as white as doves. Its punishment for telling tales was to turn black for good.","Coronis","en/d/coronis.html"],["Name of a hummingbird","The calliope hummingbird of North and Central America takes its name from the Muse.","Calliope","en/d/caliope.html"],["Disputed name","Ancient writers already debated whether the correct form was Vaticanus or Vagitanus, and some modern scholars think Vagitanus was a vulgar Latin form.","Vaticanus","en/d/vaticano.html"],["Son of the sun","According to Toribio de Motolinia, the warrior sacrificed first in the ritual was called the «son of the sun».","Camaxtli","en/d/camaxtli.html"],["The eyed god","In Paracas and Nazca depictions he appears with a prominent head and large eyes, which is why he is called the “eyed god”.","Kon","en/d/kon.html"],["A famous phrase","The warning <em>Timeo Danaos et dona ferentes</em> comes from Virgil&#x27;s Aeneid and is still quoted as an example of distrust toward gifts.","Laocoön","en/d/laocoonte.html"],["Three names for one city","The city Aeneus founded also appears as Poltyobria or Poltymbria, depending on the tradition.","Aeneus","en/d/eneo-q4687813.html"],["Possible mothers of Heimdallr","Some scholars have identified these sisters with the nine mothers of the god Heimdallr, which would mean he was born from the waves. Others question this because the names do not match.","Bylgja","en/d/bylgja.html"],["A name without kanji","The original post never gave the kanji for the name, so it is written in hiragana. In Chinese it is often written 如月車站.","Kisaragi Station","en/d/estacion-de-kisaragi.html"],["A version about Hera","In a minority version, Eurymedon is said to have violated Hera when she was a girl, and Prometheus is said to have been born from that episode.","Eurymedon","en/d/eurimedonte.html"],["Nordendorf fibula","A brooch from around 600 CE bears the runic inscription <em>logaþore wodan wigiþonar</em>. Some read it as naming a third god alongside Odin and Thor, and Lóðurr or Loki have been proposed, though the etymological basis is weak.","Lóðurr","en/d/lourr.html"],["A second name","Some sources also call him Ornytus, and the city that gave him his name is the Arcadian Teuthis itself.","Teuthis","en/d/teutis.html"],["A name with history","Virgil popularised the name Iulus instead of Ascanius, linking the Julian family of Rome with older mythology.","Ascanius","en/d/ascanio.html"],["«Eye of the Sun»","She was given the epithet «Eye of the Sun», one of the arguments that led scholars to see her as a solar deity.","Cavatha","en/d/cavatha.html"],["Fifty brothers","Alipherus was one of the fifty sons of Lycaon, though sources give different mothers: the naiad Cyllene, the naiad Nonacris, or an unknown woman.","Alipherus","en/d/alifero.html"],["A name that may come from his daughters","Some scholars suggest the name Nereus may derive from that of the Nereids, since he is important mainly as their father.","Nereus","en/d/nereo.html"],["A disputed name","The name was once thought to be a corruption of kamae tachi, &quot;stance sword&quot;, but it is now read as kama (sickle) plus itachi (weasel).","Kamaitachi","en/d/kamaitachi.html"],["A nail as a calendar","At the Fanum Voltumnae a long nail was driven into a cell of the temple each year, fixing the passage of time in the Etruscan sacred calendar.","Voltumna","en/d/voltumna.html"],["Rooster and spirits","According to popular belief, spirits can appear only at night and scatter at dawn, so the rooster&#x27;s crow drives them away.","Rooster (Chinese zodiac)","en/d/gallo-signo-del-zodiaco-chino.html"],["A name with two meanings","Vajrasattva translates as Diamond Being or Thunderbolt Being. The vajra, a symbol of insight, is associated with Esoteric Buddhism.","Vajrasattva","en/d/vajrasatva.html"],["A name with an English echo","The Germanic word for the inhabited world became middelerde in Middle English, the source of the name «Middle-earth» that Tolkien made famous.","Midgard","en/d/midgard.html"],["A solitary god","The first seven gods in the Kojiki came into being one by one, without partners, and are described as hiding their presence once they existed.","Ame-no-Minakanushi","en/d/ame-no-minakanushi.html"],["Coins in the well","The well yielded around 13,487 coins, dating from Mark Antony to Gratian.","Coventina","en/d/coventina.html"],["Two cities, two versions","The people of Pellene linked their city&#x27;s name to Pallas, while the Argives attributed it to a local hero called Pellen.","Pallas","en/d/palas.html"],["Epithet of Apollo","The adjective Phoibos, from which her name derives, was applied to Apollo as a sun god, and from it came the Greek word for prophesying.","Phoebe","en/d/febe.html"],["A name with an uncertain pronunciation","His name is thought to have meant &#x27;father king&#x27;. Because Meroitic lacked the /p/ sound, it was probably pronounced abademak.","Apedemak","en/d/apedemak.html"],["Other mythologies","In Greek myth her counterparts are the Moirai, and in Germanic myth the Norns.","Decima","en/d/decima.html"],["A name that is a superlative","Akaniṣṭha means &quot;Nothing Higher.&quot; The Pali form, <em>Akaniṭṭha</em>, carries the same sense and reflects its status as the summit of the form realms.","Akaniṣṭha","en/d/akanistha.html"],["A name with two meanings","In German and Swedish, <em>Krake</em> and <em>krake</em> now mean octopus, so the word serves both the monster and the real animal.","Kraken","en/d/kraken.html"],["A local museum","A small museum in Adachigahara says it holds the remains of the woman behind the legend, along with the pot and knife she used on her victims.","Onibaba","en/d/onibaba.html"],["A name with meaning","His Greek name is usually translated as &quot;bold of thought&quot;, an etymology that fits his portrayal as a resolute young warrior.","Thrasymedes","en/d/trasimedes.html"],["A gift&#x27;s name","Doris is linked to a Greek word meaning gift, which the ancients associated with the generosity of the sea.","Doris","en/d/doride.html"],["Plural and singular","Hesiod uses the name in the plural, meaning the quarrels, while Empedocles uses the singular, meaning hatred.","Neikea","en/d/neikea.html"],["A name on the map","The town of Baiae, in the bay of Campania, takes its name from this steersman according to the tradition recorded by Lycophron.","Baios","en/d/baios.html"],["A wind that helped Athens","Herodotus says the Athenians believed Boreas sank the Persian fleet, and so they built him a shrine by the Ilissus.","Boreas","en/d/boreas.html"],["A rationalist reading","Palaephatus argued that Scylla was really a swift Tyrrhenian ship that bore her name on its prow. Procopius suggested instead that the name came from a fish abundant in the strait.","Scylla","en/d/escila.html"],["Ravana&#x27;s uncle","His sister Kaikasi was Ravana&#x27;s mother, making him the king&#x27;s maternal uncle.","Prahasta","en/d/prahasta.html"],["A princess sacrificed","In the <em>Nihongi</em>, the concubine Oto-tachibana-hime throws herself into the sea to calm a storm believed to be the work of the sea god. The storm stops and the ship reaches the shore.","Watatsumi","en/d/watatsumi.html"],["A dinosaur genus","The dinosaur Apatosaurus, meaning &#x27;deceptive lizard&#x27;, takes its root from this goddess&#x27;s name.","Apate","en/d/apate.html"],["Twenty nymphs","In Callimachus&#x27;s hymn, Artemis asks Zeus for twenty Amnisiades and sixty Oceanids for her retinue.","Amnisiades","en/d/amnisiades.html"],["A mistaken reading","The idea of Ixtab came from interpretations of Maya writing made before it had been deciphered. Researchers who reviewed them concluded that the codices do not support it.","Ixtab","en/d/ixtab.html"]];var d=new Date(),i=Math.floor(d.getTime()/864e5)%C.length,c=C[i],R=document.documentElement.getAttribute("data-raiz")||"";var el=document.getElementById("curio");if(el)el.innerHTML="<b>"+c[0]+".</b> "+c[1]+" <a href=\""+R+c[3]+"\">"+c[2]+" →</a>"})();